window.DEEP_STORIES["steam-engine"] = {"slug":"steam-engine","title":"The Steam Engine","emoji":"⚙️","tagline":"How boiling water learned to lift, spin and power the world","intro":"Put a lid on a pot of boiling water and watch it rattle. That little rattle is a clue to one of the biggest changes in human history. For thousands of years, almost all work was done by muscles, wind and flowing water. Then, step by step, people learned to turn heat into motion. In these 20 parts you will meet a Greek inventor with a spinning ball of steam, scientists who weighed the air, a blacksmith-like ironmonger who drained flooded mines, an instrument maker who took a Sunday walk and solved a puzzle, and the engineers who built mills, ships and roaring factories. You will see how a steam engine really works, why it changed cities, jobs and families (not always for the better), and how it helped create a whole new science. And you will discover a surprise: steam never really went away. Most power stations today still boil water to make your electricity.","outline":[{"n":1,"title":"A World Powered by Muscle, Wind and Water","when":"Ancient times – about 1700"},{"n":2,"title":"Hero's Spinning Ball","when":"About 1st century BCE – 1st century CE"},{"n":3,"title":"Spits, Pumps and Daydreams","when":"1500s – 1660s"},{"n":4,"title":"The Weight of the Air","when":"1640s – 1690s"},{"n":5,"title":"Savery and the Miner's Friend","when":"1698 – early 1700s"},{"n":6,"title":"Newcomen's Nodding Giant","when":"1712 – 1770s"},{"n":7,"title":"Watt's Sunday Walk","when":"1763 – 1769"},{"n":8,"title":"Boulton & Watt: Selling Power","when":"1769 – 1800"},{"n":9,"title":"Making It Turn","when":"1780 – 1790s"},{"n":10,"title":"Inside the Machine: How Steam Does Work","when":"Science explained (applies 1700s – today)"},{"n":11,"title":"High Pressure!","when":"1800 – 1850"},{"n":12,"title":"Mills, Factories and Smoky Cities","when":"1770s – 1870s"},{"n":13,"title":"The Industrial Revolution Picks Up Steam","when":"About 1760 – 1900"},{"n":14,"title":"Steam Takes to Water and Road","when":"1769 – 1920s"},{"n":15,"title":"The Dark Side of Steam","when":"1780s – 1900s (and later)"},{"n":16,"title":"The Science Born From Engines","when":"1824 – 1860s"},{"n":17,"title":"Parsons and the Spinning Turbine","when":"1880s – 1910s"},{"n":18,"title":"Steam in Power Stations Today","when":"1950s – today"},{"n":19,"title":"The Future of Steam","when":"Today – the coming decades"},{"n":20,"title":"Full Steam Ahead: The Whole Story","when":"1st century CE – today"}],"people":[["Hero (Heron) of Alexandria (about 10 – 70 CE, dates uncertain)","Greek mathematician and engineer in Roman Egypt who described the aeolipile"],["Vitruvius (about 80 – after 15 BCE)","Roman architect and writer who also described aeolipiles"],["Taqi al-Din Muhammad ibn Ma'ruf (1526 – 1585)","Ottoman astronomer and engineer who described a steam-turned spit in 1551"],["Jerónimo de Ayanz y Beaumont (1553 – 1613)","Spanish soldier and inventor who patented a steam water-raising device in 1606"],["Salomon de Caus (1576 – 1626)","French engineer who described using steam pressure to raise water (1615)"],["Giovanni Branca (1571 – 1645)","Italian engineer who published a steam-jet wheel design in 1629"],["Edward Somerset, 2nd Marquess of Worcester (1601 – 1667)","English nobleman who described a steam 'water-commanding engine' in 1663"],["Evangelista Torricelli (1608 – 1647)","Italian physicist who made the first mercury barometer (1643)"],["Blaise Pascal (1623 – 1662)","French scientist who showed air pressure falls with height"],["Otto von Guericke (1602 – 1686)","German scientist and mayor of Magdeburg who built a vacuum pump and the Magdeburg hemispheres"],["Robert Boyle (1627 – 1691)","Irish-born scientist who studied air and gases with an air pump"],["Robert Hooke (1635 – 1703)","English scientist who built Boyle's air pump"],["Christiaan Huygens (1629 – 1695)","Dutch scientist who designed a gunpowder engine (1673)"],["Denis Papin (1647 – about 1713)","French physicist who invented the steam digester with a safety valve and proposed the steam piston-and-cylinder idea"],["Thomas Savery (about 1650 – 1715)","English military engineer who patented the first commercially sold steam pump (1698)"],["Thomas Newcomen (1664 – 1729)","English ironmonger who built the first successful piston steam engine (1712)"],["John Calley (died 1717)","Newcomen's partner, a plumber/glazier who helped build the early engines"],["John Smeaton (1724 – 1792)","English civil engineer who tested and improved Newcomen engines"],["Joseph Black (1728 – 1799)","Scottish chemist who discovered latent heat and supported Watt"],["James Watt (1736 – 1819)","Scottish instrument maker and engineer who invented the separate condenser and many engine improvements"],["John Roebuck (1718 – 1794)","Scottish industrialist, Watt's first financial backer"],["Matthew Boulton (1728 – 1809)","Birmingham manufacturer, Watt's business partner from 1775"],["John Wilkinson (1728 – 1808)","English ironmaster whose boring machine made accurate cylinders"],["William Murdoch (1754 – 1839)","Scottish engineer at Boulton & Watt, credited with the sun-and-planet gear and gas lighting"],["James Pickard (dates uncertain)","Birmingham button maker who patented the crank for steam engines (1780)"],["Richard Arkwright (1732 – 1792)","English inventor and mill owner whose Cromford Mill helped begin the factory system"],["Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot (1725 – 1804)","French military engineer who built a steam-powered vehicle (1769–1770)"],["Claude de Jouffroy d'Abbans (1751 – 1832)","French nobleman whose Pyroscaphe steamboat ran in 1783"],["John Fitch (1743 – 1798)","American inventor who ran an early steamboat on the Delaware River (1787)"],["William Symington (1764 – 1831)","Scottish engineer who built the steam tug Charlotte Dundas (1803)"],["Robert Fulton (1765 – 1815)","American engineer whose 1807 steamboat began the first commercially successful service"],["Richard Trevithick (1771 – 1833)","Cornish engineer who pioneered high-pressure steam engines"],["Oliver Evans (1755 – 1819)","American inventor who built high-pressure engines and automated mills"],["Arthur Woolf (1766 – 1837)","Cornish engineer who developed the compound engine"],["Henry Maudslay (1771 – 1831)","English engineer who improved machine tools like the screw-cutting lathe"],["Friedrich Koenig (1774 – 1833)","German inventor of the steam-powered printing press"],["James Nasmyth (1808 – 1890)","Scottish engineer who invented the steam hammer"],["Isambard Kingdom Brunel (1806 – 1859)","English engineer who designed the steamships Great Western and Great Britain"],["George Corliss (1817 – 1888)","American engineer whose valve gear made very efficient factory engines"],["Friedrich Engels (1820 – 1895)","German writer who described working-class life in Manchester (1845)"],["Sadi Carnot (1796 – 1832)","French engineer whose 1824 book founded the science of heat engines"],["James Prescott Joule (1818 – 1889)","English physicist who showed heat and work are forms of energy"],["Rudolf Clausius (1822 – 1888)","German physicist who helped state the laws of thermodynamics and named entropy"],["William Thomson, Lord Kelvin (1824 – 1907)","Belfast-born physicist who helped found thermodynamics and the absolute temperature scale"],["Gustaf de Laval (1845 – 1913)","Swedish engineer who built an early high-speed impulse steam turbine"],["Charles Parsons (1854 – 1931)","Anglo-Irish engineer who invented the practical multi-stage steam turbine (1884)"],["Thomas Edison (1847 – 1931)","American inventor whose Pearl Street Station (1882) used steam engines to make electricity"],["Francis E. Stanley (1849 – 1918) and Freelan O. Stanley (1849 – 1940)","American twin brothers who built Stanley Steamer cars"]],"glossary":[["steam","water in its invisible gas form; the white cloud we see is tiny water droplets that have cooled back into liquid"],["boiler","a strong closed container where water is heated to make steam"],["pressure","how hard something (like steam or air) pushes on each bit of a surface"],["atmospheric pressure","the push of the air all around us, caused by the weight of the air above"],["vacuum","a space with little or no air (or other gas) in it"],["condense","when steam cools and turns back into liquid water, shrinking hugely"],["condenser","a separate chamber where used steam is cooled back into water, creating a vacuum"],["latent heat","the 'hidden' heat needed to turn boiling water into steam without making it hotter"],["cylinder","a tube, closed at the ends, inside which a piston moves"],["piston","a tight-fitting disc or plug that slides inside a cylinder when pushed by steam or air"],["beam engine","an engine with a big rocking beam (like a seesaw) linking the piston to a pump or wheel"],["atmospheric engine","an engine (like Newcomen's) where air pressure, not steam pressure, pushes the piston"],["double-acting","an engine where steam pushes the piston in both directions"],["compound engine","an engine that uses the same steam twice or more in cylinders of increasing size"],["governor","a device that automatically keeps an engine at a steady speed"],["horsepower","a unit of power invented by Watt, equal to about 746 watts"],["efficiency","how much of the fuel's energy becomes useful work instead of wasted heat"],["turbine","a machine where a fluid (like steam) pushes on rows of blades to spin a shaft directly"],["thermodynamics","the science of heat, energy and work"],["generator","a machine that turns spinning motion into electricity"]],"parts":[{"n":1,"title":"A World Powered by Muscle, Wind and Water","when":"Ancient times – about 1700","summary":"Long ago there were no engines at all. People, horses, rushing rivers and windy hills did every bit of work. But deep under the ground, mines were filling up with water, and nobody could pump it out fast enough.","sections":[{"heading":"A World Without Engines","paragraphs":["Close your eyes and imagine a world with no engines of any kind. No cars, no trains, no electric motors humming inside fridges, no power station somewhere far away sending electricity down a wire. If you wanted something to move, something alive or something natural had to move it. That was the world almost everyone lived in for thousands of years, right up until about 300 years ago.","In that world, the word 'power' meant muscles first of all. Farmers dug fields with spades and hoes. Women and children ground grain into flour by rubbing it between heavy stones, a job that could take hours every single day. Builders carried stone on their backs, sawyers cut planks by pulling a long saw up and down a pit, and sailors hauled ropes until their hands were raw. Rowers pushed great warships across the Mediterranean Sea with oars.","How much power is in a human body? Scientists today measure power in watts, the same unit printed on a light bulb. A healthy adult can keep working steadily all day at only about 70 to 75 watts. That is roughly enough to keep one old-fashioned light bulb glowing. For a short burst, such as sprinting up a staircase, a person might manage close to 900 watts, but nobody can keep that up for more than a few seconds. Muscles get tired, and tired people need food, water, sleep and rest.","Because one person is so weak, big jobs needed many people, and clever tools to multiply their strength. Levers, ramps, pulleys and wheels let a team move things far heavier than any one of them could lift alone. Roman engineers built cranes with a system of pulleys called a polyspastos. Worked by four men at a winch, one of these cranes could lift about 3,000 kilograms, about the weight of two small cars.","Even better was the treadwheel. Picture a giant wooden hamster wheel, taller than a house, with people walking inside it. As they walked, the wheel turned, winding a rope around its axle and lifting a heavy stone into the air. Because the wheel was so big compared with the axle, a couple of walkers could raise loads that would have needed a whole crowd pulling on ropes. In the Middle Ages, treadwheel cranes came back into use to build Europe's great cathedrals. They were set up inside the half-built walls and moved higher as the building grew.","You can still see real ones today. A treadwheel crane in the English harbour town of Harwich was built in 1667 and has two wheels side by side. Another, at Guildford in Surrey, dates from around 1700. They look like strange, enormous toys, but for centuries they were some of the most powerful lifting machines in the world, and every bit of their power came from people walking."]},{"heading":"Horses in Circles: Gins and Whims","paragraphs":["Animals were the next step up. Oxen pulled ploughs and heavy carts in slow, steady lines. Donkeys and mules carried loads along mountain paths. Horses were faster and stronger, and over the centuries farmers and miners found clever ways to borrow their strength for jobs that had nothing to do with pulling a cart.","The trick was to make the horse walk in a circle. Imagine a tall wooden post standing upright in the middle of a round, flat track. A long wooden arm sticks out sideways from the post, and a horse is harnessed to the end of the arm. As the horse plods round and round the track, the post turns with it. Gears or a big drum on the post can then turn that slow circular motion into almost anything: grinding grain, crushing apples for cider, threshing wheat, or winding a rope.","This machine was called a horse engine, a horse mill, or, in Britain, a horse gin. The word gin is short for engine, which once simply meant any clever device. Many farms in northern England and Scotland built round or many-sided sheds, called gin-gangs, to keep the horses and machinery out of the rain. A lot of these little buildings still stand next to old farmhouses today.","At mines, the horse gin had a special name: the whim. A whim had a wide wooden drum on top of its upright post. A rope wrapped around the drum ran out over pulleys and down the mine shaft. As the horse walked one way, the drum let one bucket down into the darkness while winding a full bucket up to the surface. Then the horse turned around and walked the other way. Whims hauled coal, rock and ore, and they could also be hooked up to pumps to lift water. Horse whims were used at some coal mines until the end of the 1800s. A wooden gin wheel from 1844, which once worked at Langton and Pinxton collieries (coal mines) in the East Midlands, is kept today at the Nottingham Industrial Museum.","In 1556 a German doctor and mining expert named Georg Bauer, who wrote under the Latin name Georgius Agricola, published a famous book called De re metallica, which means 'On the Nature of Metals'. It was packed with 292 woodcut pictures showing how miners worked. Some of them show horses walking in circles beside a mine, turning machines that pulled up water. Agricola explained that one of his pumps could only lift water about 24 feet (about 7 metres), so deep mines needed whole chains of pumps, one above the other. His book stayed the standard guide to mining for about 180 years.","Horses were strong, but they were not magic. Scientists who measured horses at a fair in Iowa in 1925 found that a big horse could produce nearly 15 horsepower for a few seconds. Over a whole working day, though, a horse can only keep up about one horsepower, a small fraction of its short-burst strength. And every horse needed hay and oats, water, a stable, a blacksmith for its shoes, and people to look after it. A busy mine might need dozens of horses working in shifts, eating their way through fields of crops. Keeping all those animals was expensive, and the deeper a mine went, the more of them it needed."]},{"heading":"The Mighty Waterwheel","paragraphs":["If you have ever held your hand in a fast stream, you have felt how hard moving water pushes. People realized long ago that a river never gets tired. It flows day and night, summer and winter, and it never needs feeding. All you have to do is put a wheel in its way.","Greek engineers were building waterwheels and toothed gears in the 3rd century BCE, more than 2,200 years ago. Greek and Roman writers described water-powered mills for grinding grain. The Roman architect Vitruvius gave the first known technical description of a watermill in the 1st century BCE. By about 70 CE, the Roman writer Pliny the Elder mentioned water-powered hammers working across much of Italy.","The most impressive ancient example was at Barbegal, near the town of Arles in southern France. In the 2nd century CE, the Romans built a hillside there with 16 waterwheels arranged in two long rows, like a staircase of wheels. Water from an aqueduct poured from one wheel down to the next. Experts estimate the mills could grind about 4.5 tonnes of flour a day, perhaps enough bread for around 12,500 people. Historians have called it the greatest known concentration of mechanical power in the ancient world.","Waterwheels came in different shapes. An undershot wheel dips its paddles into a flowing stream and is pushed along by the current. An overshot wheel has water poured onto it from above, into little buckets around its rim, so the weight of the falling water turns it. Overshot wheels are usually more efficient, but they need a waterfall or a channel bringing water down from higher ground. Breastshot wheels take the water about halfway up.","How does a turning wheel grind flour? With gears. In a typical mill, the waterwheel turns slowly on a thick wooden axle. A big wooden gear wheel on that axle, with pegs sticking out like teeth, meshes with a much smaller gear. Because the small gear has fewer teeth, it has to spin several times for every single turn of the big one. That smaller gear turns an upright shaft, and on top of that shaft sits the runner stone, a heavy round millstone spinning just above a fixed bed stone. Grain trickles into a hole in the middle of the runner stone and comes out at the edges as flour. Some clever mills, called tide mills, were built on the coast. They trapped seawater in a pond at high tide and let it flow out through the wheel as the tide went down.","In Europe in the Middle Ages, watermills spread almost everywhere. In 1085, William the Conqueror, the Norman king who had taken over England, sent officials to every county to record who owned what. The result, finished around 1086, was the Domesday Book. It lists 5,624 mills, and because the record is incomplete, the real number was probably higher. By about 1300, England may have had 10,000 to 15,000 mills. They ground grain, but water power was also used for sawing wood, pounding cloth to thicken it (called fulling), making paper and hammering iron.","But waterwheels had a big weakness: they could not move. A mill had to be built exactly where the right river was, and the work had to come to it. In a dry summer, the stream might shrink to a trickle and the wheel would stop. In a hard winter it could freeze solid. After heavy rain, floods could smash the wheel to pieces. And many of the places where power was needed most, such as the tops of hills where miners were digging, had no fast river nearby at all."]},{"heading":"Catching the Wind","paragraphs":["Wind was the other great natural power. Sailors had used it for thousands of years to push ships across seas and oceans. Turning wind into power on land took longer.","Hero of Alexandria, a Greek engineer you will meet properly in the next part, described a little wind-wheel that worked an organ in the 1st century CE, but it was more of a clever demonstration than a working mill. The first windmills that we know were really used for work appeared in Persia (modern Iran) and the neighbouring region of Khorasan, roughly between 700 and 900 CE. These early mills had sails that spun around an upright post, a bit like a revolving door, and they were used to grind grain and pump water.","In north-west Europe, windmills with sails turning on a horizontal shaft (the kind you probably picture) appeared in the 1100s. One of the earliest certain records is from 1185, at Weedley in Yorkshire, England. The first common type was the post mill. The whole wooden body of the mill, with its sails and millstones, sat balanced on one massive upright post, and the miller pushed a long tail pole to swing the entire building round so the sails faced into the wind. Later came tower mills, where only the cap on top of a stone or brick tower turned, and smock mills with wooden sides.","The Dutch became famous masters of wind power. Much of the Netherlands is low, flat land, some of it below sea level, and windmills were used from the 1400s onwards to pump water out of marshes and lakes and into drainage channels. Bit by bit, wind-powered pumps helped turn soggy wetland into farmland. Around 1850 the Netherlands had about 10,000 windmills, and roughly 1,000 still stand today. Across Europe, by about 1850 there may have been around 200,000 windmills and roughly 500,000 waterwheels working.","Wind power had its own problems, though. The wind does not blow when you want it to. On a calm day, the sails hang still. In a storm, a miller had to stop the sails before they spun so fast that the machinery caught fire from friction or the sails tore off. Windmills had to be built on open, exposed ground, and like waterwheels, they could not be picked up and moved to wherever a new job was waiting. Nobody could promise a mine owner that the wind would keep his pumps running through a still, foggy week."]},{"heading":"Running Short of Wood, Turning to Coal","paragraphs":["For heat, people mostly burned wood. Wood, and charcoal made by slowly baking wood, cooked food, warmed homes, fired pottery and bricks, boiled salt out of seawater, brewed beer and smelted iron. Forests also supplied timber for houses and, very importantly, for building ships.","In Britain, by the middle of the 1500s, wood was getting scarcer and more expensive, especially near growing towns like London. The population was rising, and forests had been cut down for farmland and fuel over many centuries. Historians still argue about how serious this 'wood crisis' really was. Some think it was a true national shortage, while others think it was mostly a local problem of prices in big towns, made worse by the cost of carrying heavy wood long distances. But everyone agrees on what happened next: more and more people switched to burning coal.","Coal was not new. The Romans had dug coal in Britain and burned it to heat bath houses and forts along Hadrian's Wall. But many people disliked it. It was smoky, smelly and dirty, and its critics mocked it with rude names, including 'the devil's excrement'. Still, coal had one huge advantage: a lump of coal holds much more heat energy than a lump of wood of the same weight, and Britain was sitting on enormous amounts of it.","So the coal trade boomed. Ships carried coal from Newcastle down the east coast to London in such numbers that the phrase 'carrying coals to Newcastle' later came to mean doing something pointless. In 1575 a Scottish businessman, George Bruce of Carnock, opened a remarkable mine at Culross on the Firth of Forth. He even built an artificial island out in the water and sank a shaft from it, with extra shafts for drainage and fresh air. By 1700 Britain was producing just under 3 million tons of coal a year, far more than any other country.","Coal did not only warm homes. It boiled seawater in huge iron pans to make salt along the coasts of Scotland and north-east England. It heated kilns for burning limestone into lime, which farmers spread on fields and builders used in mortar. Brewers, dyers, soap makers, glassmakers and brick makers all learned to use it in place of wood. Smelting iron with coal was much harder, because impurities in the coal spoiled the metal, and that problem would not be properly solved until the 1700s. Still, more and more of Britain's daily life was running on the black rock dug out from under its fields.","There was a catch. The easy coal, lying near the surface, was dug out first. To keep up with demand, miners had to follow the seams deeper and deeper underground. Before 1800, pits around the River Tyne in north-east England already reached about 300 to 1,000 feet (about 90 to 300 metres) down. It was not only coal, either. In Cornwall, in the far south-west of England, miners were chasing veins of tin and copper deep into the rock, and in Derbyshire and the north of England they dug for lead."]},{"heading":"Mines That Kept Filling With Water","paragraphs":["Dig a hole on a beach, and after a little while its bottom fills with water. The same thing happens underground, on a much bigger scale. Rainwater soaks down through soil and cracks in the rock, and much of the ground below us is full of this hidden groundwater. When miners dig a shaft and tunnels, water trickles, seeps and sometimes gushes in from the walls. The deeper the mine, the more water tends to pour in, and every bucket of it has to be lifted all the way back to the surface.","If the water is not removed, the mine floods. Tools, tunnels and valuable coal or ore disappear under a black, icy lake. Sometimes miners broke into old flooded workings by accident and the water rushed in suddenly, which was extremely dangerous. For mine owners, water was the enemy that could end a mine forever.","Miners fought back in several ways. The cheapest was to let gravity do the work. Where a mine was on a hillside, they dug a long, gently sloping drainage tunnel called an adit from the low ground into the hill, so water could run out by itself. In the lead mines of Derbyshire these tunnels were called soughs (said 'suffs'). In Cornwall, a huge network called the Great County Adit was dug during the 1700s and early 1800s, eventually stretching about 40 miles (about 64 kilometres) and draining more than 100 mines. But an adit can only drain a mine down to its own level. Anything dug deeper than the adit still floods.","Below that level, the water had to be lifted. Miners used buckets on ropes wound up by whims, chains of buckets on a loop, and pumps worked by people, horses or waterwheels. In the 1600s many British collieries used chain pumps driven by waterwheels. But each of these methods had a ceiling. Horses were expensive and slow. Waterwheels needed a good river nearby. And pumps had a strange limit that puzzled everyone.","Most simple pumps of the time were suction pumps, which work a bit like sucking a drink up a straw. However strong the pump and however hard you worked it, a suction pump could not raise water more than about 10 metres (about 33 feet) in one go. A measurement made in Italy around 1635 put the limit at about 18 Florentine yards, which is about the same. The great scientist Galileo was asked to explain it and got it wrong. The real answer, which involves the weight of the air itself, is so important to the steam engine story that it gets a whole part of its own later on. For now, the key point is this: to drain a deep mine with suction pumps, you needed a whole staircase of pumps, one above the other, each lifting water to the next. That meant lots of machinery, lots of horses and lots of money.","As mines went deeper in the 1600s, some simply had to be abandoned with plenty of coal or metal still left in them, because the water won. The miners knew exactly where the treasure was. They just could not keep it dry."]},{"heading":"The Big Question: Could Fire Do the Work?","paragraphs":["Look at the situation from a mine owner's point of view around the year 1690. You are sitting on a fortune in coal or tin. Your problem is water. Muscles, human or horse, are weak, tired and hungry. Rivers and wind are strong but stuck in one place and unreliable. You need a source of power that is strong, works day and night in any weather, and can be built right next to your mine shaft, wherever it is.","Now notice something odd. At a coal mine, the one thing you have plenty of is fuel. Fire was everywhere in this world. Blacksmiths' forges, brick kilns, glass furnaces, bakers' ovens and kitchen hearths burned all day long. Everyone knew fire made heat. Everyone had seen a pot of water boil, and seen the lid jiggle and rattle as steam escaped. But that heat simply drifted away up the chimney. Nobody had a machine that could turn the heat of a fire into steady, useful pushing and pulling.","That is the big question at the heart of this whole story: could heat be turned into motion? Could the burning of coal, instead of the eating of oats, lift water out of a mine, turn a millstone, or one day pull a cart? It sounds simple, but answering it would take the work of dozens of people in many countries over centuries. They would need to understand air, water, steam and a mysterious thing called a vacuum. They would need better metalworking, more accurate tools and a lot of courage, because steam can be dangerous.","When the answer finally came, it changed nearly everything about how people live and work, from the clothes we wear to the cities we live in and the electricity in our homes. But the idea did not appear out of nowhere in the 1700s. In fact, the idea that steam could make something move was already very old. Nearly two thousand years earlier, in a busy harbour city in ancient Egypt, an inventor had already made a ball spin with nothing but the steam from boiling water."]}],"deepDive":"How much power did the pre-industrial world really have? Historians and engineers have tried to put numbers on it. A person working steadily all day delivers only about 75 watts of useful mechanical power. A draught horse can sustain roughly ten times that, about one horsepower (around 750 watts), although it can briefly produce far more: measurements at the 1925 Iowa State Fair recorded a peak near 15 horsepower. Animals, however, come with hidden costs. A horse eats the produce of several acres of land a year, and for every hour of pulling it needs many hours of rest and care. Water and wind were the heavyweights. A large medieval watermill might deliver a few horsepower, and by about 1850 Europe may have had roughly half a million waterwheels and 200,000 windmills. The best ancient site, Barbegal in Roman Gaul, combined 16 wheels. Yet all of these sources shared a basic limitation that economists call being 'site-bound': the power was available only where the river ran or the wind blew, and only when nature cooperated. Mines exposed this limitation most painfully. Water flowing into a shaft must be lifted against gravity, and the work needed grows with both the amount of water and the depth. Doubling the depth doubles the work for each litre lifted, while deeper mines usually also let in more water. Suction pumps added a hard physical ceiling of about 10 metres per stage, because atmospheric pressure can support a water column only about that high. Historians debate the 'timber crisis' that is often said to have pushed Britain towards coal. Older accounts described a nationwide shortage; more recent research suggests that wood remained available in many regions and that high prices were concentrated in growing towns where transport costs were heavy. Either way, Britain's coal output rose to just under 3 million tons a year by 1700, and the problem of draining ever-deeper mines became a pressing economic issue. That is why the first commercial steam engines were not built to drive factories or vehicles, but to pump water.","funFact":"When scientists measured working horses at the 1925 Iowa State Fair, one horse produced a burst of nearly 15 horsepower, even though over a whole day a horse can keep up only about one.","check":{"q":"About how high can a simple suction pump lift water in one go?","a":"About 10 metres (about 33 feet)","wrong":["About 1 metre (3 feet)","About 100 metres (330 feet)","There is no limit if you pump hard enough"]},"sources":[{"title":"Watermill","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watermill"},{"title":"Windmill","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Windmill"},{"title":"Horsepower","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horsepower"},{"title":"Whim (mining)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whim_(mining)"},{"title":"History of coal mining","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_coal_mining"},{"title":"De re metallica","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/De_re_metallica"},{"title":"Suction pump","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Suction_pump"},{"title":"Treadwheel crane","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Treadwheel_crane"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/01-1.jpg","alt":"Woodcut from Agricola's De re metallica (1556): a big geared wooden machine lifting water out of a mine with a chain of buckets.","file":"Agricola, De re metallica libri XII. Wellcome L0006608.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Agricola,_De_re_metallica_libri_XII._Wellcome_L0006608.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/01-2.jpg","alt":"The 1667 treadwheel crane at Harwich, England. Men walked inside its two big wheels to lift loads.","file":"Treadwheel Crane 20180506 4.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Treadwheel_Crane_20180506_4.jpg","author":"Helmut Seger","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/01-3.jpg","alt":"Saxtead Green post mill in Suffolk, England: a white wooden windmill with four big sails.","file":"Saxtead Green Post Mill - geograph.org.uk - 4011287.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saxtead_Green_Post_Mill_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4011287.jpg","author":"Kim Fyson","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3789},{"n":2,"title":"Hero's Spinning Ball","when":"About 1st century BCE – 1st century CE","summary":"About 2,000 years ago, in the great city of Alexandria in Egypt, a clever teacher called Hero heated water in a pot. Steam shot out of a metal ball through two bent pipes, and the ball whirled round and round. It was a wonderful trick, but nobody used it to do real work for a very, very long time.","sections":[{"heading":"The City of Books and Big Ideas","paragraphs":["Imagine stepping off a wooden ship onto a busy stone quay about 2,000 years ago. Sailors shout in Greek, Egyptian, Latin and a dozen other languages. Sacks of wheat, jars of olive oil and bales of papyrus are being hauled ashore. Behind you, out in the harbour, a gigantic tower rises far above the masts: the Pharos, the famous lighthouse that was counted among the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World. Welcome to Alexandria, on the Mediterranean coast of Egypt.","Alexandria was founded in 331 BCE by Alexander the Great, the Macedonian king who conquered a vast empire stretching from Greece to India. After Alexander died, one of his generals, Ptolemy, took control of Egypt. Ptolemy and his descendants, the Ptolemies, ruled for almost 300 years, and they made Alexandria one of the largest and richest cities in the world. The last of the family to rule was the famous queen Cleopatra. After her death in 30 BCE, Egypt became part of the Roman Empire, but Alexandria stayed a Greek-speaking city full of scholars, merchants and craftspeople.","The Ptolemies wanted Alexandria to be the brainiest city on Earth. They set up an institution called the Mouseion, which means 'shrine of the Muses', the Greek goddesses of the arts and knowledge. Our modern word museum comes from it. But it was not a building full of objects in glass cases. It was more like a research university, where scholars were paid by the state, had their meals provided and did not have to pay taxes, so they could spend their time thinking, writing, teaching and arguing.","Linked to the Mouseion was the Library of Alexandria, the most famous library of the ancient world. Nobody knows exactly how many scrolls it held; modern estimates range anywhere from about 40,000 to 700,000. The kings were so hungry for books that, according to ancient writers, any book found on a ship arriving in the harbour could be taken to the library and copied. The library kept the original and gave the copy back to the owner.","Some of the greatest minds of the ancient world worked or studied in Alexandria. Eratosthenes, one of the library's head librarians, worked out the size of the Earth surprisingly accurately using shadows and a bit of geometry. Astronomers mapped the stars, doctors studied the human body, and mathematicians wrote books that students would read for more than a thousand years. Alexandria also had a strong tradition of engineers who loved building clever machines. One of the earliest was Ctesibius, who lived in the 3rd century BCE. He experimented with compressed air and water, and he is credited with inventing a force pump, a water organ and very accurate water clocks.","This was the world of our inventor: a city where books, ideas and gadgets were taken seriously, where people asked how nature worked and then tried to show it with real devices you could watch and touch."]},{"heading":"Who Was Hero?","paragraphs":["His name in Greek was Heron, so you will see him called either Hero or Heron of Alexandria. Strangely, for someone so famous, we know almost nothing about his life. We do not know when he was born, who his parents were, or what he looked like. No portrait of him survives from his own time. Everything we know comes from his writings.","For a long time, historians could not even agree which century he lived in. Guesses ranged across about 400 years, from around 150 BCE to around 250 CE. Then, in 1938, the historian of science Otto Neugebauer noticed something clever. In one of his books, the Dioptra, Hero describes how to use a lunar eclipse, seen at the same moment from two different cities, to work out the distance between them. The eclipse he describes matches one that happened on 13 March in the year 62 CE. Most historians now think Hero was working in the 1st century CE, and his dates are often given as roughly 10 to 70 CE. Some scholars are careful to point out that the book does not actually say Hero watched the eclipse himself, so even this is not completely certain.","Hero seems to have been a teacher. Many of his books read like lecture notes or textbooks, explaining step by step how to do things. He probably taught at the Mouseion. He wrote about an impressive range of subjects. His Metrica explains how to measure areas and volumes, and it contains what is still called Heron's formula, a way to find the area of a triangle when you know only the lengths of its three sides. His Dioptra describes a surveying instrument for measuring land and planning tunnels and aqueducts. His Belopoeica is about war machines such as catapults. His Mechanica, about lifting heavy loads with levers, pulleys, wedges and screws, survives only in an Arabic translation. Another book, Automata, describes small self-moving theatre shows driven by falling weights and ropes.","Hero did not claim that he had thought up everything himself. He openly built on the work of earlier engineers, especially Ctesibius, and some of the devices he describes may have been invented by others. For a long time, some historians dismissed him as just a copier of other people's ideas. Today, most see him as a well-educated and often ingenious applied mathematician and engineer, someone who collected, improved and clearly explained the best mechanical knowledge of his age. That is a hugely valuable job. Without writers like Hero, much of that knowledge would have been lost forever."]},{"heading":"A Book About Air, Water and Steam","paragraphs":["Hero's most famous book for our story is the Pneumatica. The name comes from the Greek word pneuma, meaning breath or air, which is also where we get words like pneumatic tyres and pneumonia. The Pneumatica describes dozens of devices that work using air, water, heat and steam.","Hero begins with a big idea. Many people thought that an 'empty' jar was really empty. Hero argued that air is a real substance made of tiny particles, even though you cannot see it. To prove it, he suggested a simple experiment you can try in a sink or a bath with an adult's permission. Push an empty cup upside down straight into water. The water will not fill it, because the cup is already full of air, and the air has nowhere to go. Make a hole in the bottom of the cup, and the air rushes out and the water flows in. Hero also believed that the spaces between those tiny particles could be squeezed, which is why air can be compressed, and that heat makes air expand.","Then he describes gadget after gadget. There are drinking vessels that pour wine and water in turn, and a wine bowl that keeps itself topped up using a float valve, a little like the float in a modern toilet cistern. There are mechanical birds that seem to sing when water flowing into a chamber pushes air out through whistles. There is a fountain, still called Heron's fountain, that seems to flow by magic, powered by water and air pressure moving between hidden containers. There is a force pump with pistons and valves that could squirt a jet of water, which Hero suggests could be used to fight fires. There is even a wind-wheel that pumps air into a musical organ, one of the earliest known machines driven by the wind.","Some devices were for temples, some for entertainment at feasts, and some seem to be teaching demonstrations, showing students how air and water behave. Many feel more like magic tricks than tools. But hidden among them is the device that has made Hero famous in the history of engines: a hollow ball that spins using nothing but steam."]},{"heading":"The Aeolipile: A Ball That Spins on Steam","paragraphs":["Here is how Hero's spinning ball worked. At the bottom is a closed metal pot, a little like a covered cauldron, filled partly with water and sitting over a fire. Two pipes rise up from the lid. At the top of the pipes, held between them on pivots, is a hollow metal ball that is free to turn, rather like a globe on its stand. One of the pipes is hollow and carries steam up from the pot into the ball through the pivot. Sticking out from opposite sides of the ball are two small bent tubes, shaped like the letter L, one pointing one way and the other pointing the opposite way.","Light the fire and wait. As the water heats up and boils, it turns into steam. Steam takes up far more room than the water it came from, so it rushes up the pipe, fills the ball, and has only one way out: the two bent nozzles. Two jets of steam hiss out, one forwards on one side and one backwards on the other. And the ball begins to turn, slowly at first, then faster and faster, until it is whirling round with a hiss.","Today this device is usually called an aeolipile. The name joins Aeolus, the Greek god of the winds, with the Latin word pila, meaning ball. So an aeolipile is a 'ball of Aeolus', or a wind-ball. Hero gave clear instructions for building one, and because his description survived, he is often called the inventor of the first known steam engine. To be fair, it is better to say that Hero's aeolipile is the earliest known device that turned steam into spinning motion. As you will see, he was not the first person to write about steam-filled balls, and his version may well have been built on earlier ideas.","Why does it spin? The answer is one of the most important rules in physics. When something pushes stuff out in one direction, the stuff pushes back on it in the opposite direction. Every action has an equal and opposite reaction. Isaac Newton wrote this down as his third law of motion in 1687, more than 1,600 years after Hero, but the aeolipile already shows it beautifully. Each jet of steam shoots out of a nozzle, and in return the nozzle gets shoved the other way. Because the two nozzles point in opposite directions on opposite sides of the ball, their two shoves work together to twist the ball around its pivots.","You have probably seen the same idea at work. A spinning garden sprinkler with bent arms whirls around because water squirts out of its nozzles. Let go of a blown-up balloon and it zooms off as air rushes out of the neck. A rocket blasting into space is pushed upwards by the hot gas it throws downwards. Engineers call the aeolipile a simple kind of reaction turbine: a machine that spins because of the push-back from a jet leaving it.","Modern people have built many working copies of the aeolipile. A well-made one can spin very fast indeed, with a loud hiss. But if you tried to make it do a job, such as lifting a weight on a string wound round its axle, you would be disappointed. The steam escapes so freely and the jets are so small that it produces only a tiny turning force. Most of the heat from the fire simply escapes into the air with the steam. It is a brilliant demonstration and a terrible machine for doing work."]},{"heading":"Vitruvius and the Wind-Balls","paragraphs":["Hero was not the only ancient writer to mention steam-filled balls, and he was not the first. About a century earlier, a Roman architect and engineer named Marcus Vitruvius Pollio wrote a large work called De architectura, meaning 'On Architecture', in roughly 30 to 20 BCE. It is the only complete book on architecture to survive from ancient Rome, and it covers everything from building temples and town walls to water supplies, sundials and siege machines. He dedicated it to the emperor Augustus.","In the first of its ten books, Vitruvius discusses how to plan a town so that its streets are not battered by strong winds. While explaining where winds come from, he describes devices called aeolipiles. They were, in an English translation, 'hollow brazen vessels': small hollow balls of bronze with a tiny hole, through which they could be filled with water. Placed by a fire, they gave out little at first. But once the water began to boil, a powerful blast of vapour came roaring out of the hole.","Vitruvius's aeolipile did not spin. It was a fixed ball with a single jet. And he was not interested in using it as a machine at all. To him it was a scientific demonstration. He thought that wind in the sky was produced when heat acted on moisture, and the blasting wind-ball seemed to prove it on a small scale. By studying such little devices, he argued, people could understand the great laws of nature. We now know that weather winds are mostly caused by differences in air pressure and temperature across the Earth, not by boiling water, but his instinct to test big ideas with small experiments was a very scientific one.","Putting Vitruvius and Hero side by side tells us something important. Steam-blowing balls were known in the Greek and Roman world for at least a century, and probably longer, perhaps going back to the circle of Ctesibius in Alexandria. Educated people found them interesting. They used them to explain nature and to amaze audiences. But none of them, as far as we know, ever used one to do a useful job."]},{"heading":"Coin Machines and Temple Wonders","paragraphs":["The aeolipile was far from Hero's only crowd-pleaser. Some of his most famous inventions were designed for temples, where a little mechanical mystery could make visitors feel they were in the presence of the gods.","One is often called the world's first vending machine. In some temples, worshippers washed with holy water before entering. Hero describes a sealed jar with a slot in the top. Inside, a coin dropped through the slot landed on a little flat pan at one end of a lever, like a tiny seesaw. The coin's weight tipped the lever down, and the other end of the lever lifted a plug, letting holy water trickle out of a spout. As the pan tipped further, the coin slid off into the jar, the lever swung back, the plug dropped into place, and the water stopped. One coin, one measured splash of water. It is a simple and clever idea, and the same basic principle of a weight working a valve is used in many machines today.","Even more theatrical were Hero's automatic temple doors. In his description, priests light a fire on an altar outside a small temple. Hidden underneath the altar is a closed metal container of air connected to another container holding water. As the fire heats the air, the air expands and pushes water through a bent tube, called a siphon, into a bucket hanging from a rope. As the bucket fills, it gets heavier and sinks, pulling ropes wound around the pivots of the temple doors, and the doors swing slowly open as if by magic. When the fire dies down, the air cools and shrinks, the water is drawn back, the bucket gets lighter, and a counterweight pulls the doors closed again.","It is important to be careful about two things here. First, these temple doors were worked by fire-heated air and the weight of water, not by steam. Some popular books have mixed them up with the aeolipile, but they are different devices. Second, this is a device Hero describes in writing. Historians do not know whether anyone ever built it at the full size of real temple doors, or whether it existed only as a description or perhaps a small model. Hero's books include both proven tricks and clever designs on paper, and it is often impossible to tell which is which.","Hero also described entire puppet shows that ran by themselves. In his book Automata, a cart could roll onto a stage on its own, stop, perform a little show with moving figures and sound effects, and roll away again, all driven by falling weights, ropes wound around axles and pegs that changed direction at set moments. Some historians describe this as a simple kind of programming, because the sequence of actions was set in advance by the way the ropes and pegs were arranged."]},{"heading":"Why Didn't Hero Start a Steam Revolution?","paragraphs":["This is one of the great 'what if' questions in history. If people in Alexandria already knew that steam could make something spin, why did it take until the 1700s for steam engines to do real work? Why didn't the Romans have steam-powered mills, ships or carts? Historians have suggested several reasons, and most think it was a combination of them.","The first is metal and making. A powerful steam engine needs a strong, sealed boiler that will not leak or burst, and a cylinder and piston that fit together extremely tightly. Ancient metalworkers could make beautiful bronze statues, coins and tools, but making large, strong, airtight vessels and accurately shaped metal parts was a different challenge. Joints were usually soldered or hammered, and they would have leaked or failed under real steam pressure. Even 1,700 years later, as you will discover, the first successful engine builders struggled with exactly these problems.","The second is that nobody had a clear job for it. The aeolipile spins very fast but with almost no force. To make it useful, someone would have had to work out how to connect that weak, fast spinning to a slow, strong job like grinding grain, and nobody seems to have tried. The ancient world had the waterwheel for heavy work, and it was reliable and well understood.","The third reason is about people. In the Roman world, a great deal of hard physical labour was done by enslaved people and poorly paid workers. Some historians argue that when human labour is that cheap, there is little reason to invest in expensive, unfamiliar machines. Others think this explanation is too simple, since the Romans did happily build watermills and cranes to save effort. It is still a lively debate.","The fourth reason is missing science. Hero knew that heated air expands and that steam rushes out of a hot vessel. But nobody yet understood air pressure, the vacuum, or how much work a certain amount of heat could do. As later parts of this story show, the first engines that actually worked did not even rely on steam pushing. They relied on a surprising discovery about the weight of the air, which would not be made until the 1600s.","Finally, scholars in Hero's world often valued ideas and demonstrations more highly than practical improvements. A device that revealed a law of nature or delighted a crowd was admired in its own right. Turning it into a money-making machine was simply not what many educated people were trying to do."]},{"heading":"Asleep for 1,500 Years","paragraphs":["In the centuries after Hero, the Roman world changed enormously. The great days of the Mouseion faded; the last known references to it come from the 260s CE, and the royal quarter of Alexandria was badly damaged in fighting in 272 CE. The Library of Alexandria did not vanish in one single dramatic fire, as legends sometimes claim, but declined over a long time.","Hero's ideas, however, did not disappear. His books were copied again and again by hand. Scholars of the Byzantine Empire, which continued in the eastern Mediterranean, kept Greek texts alive. Scholars writing in Arabic translated and studied many Greek works, and Hero's Mechanica survives today only because of an Arabic translation. Later, in the Renaissance of the 1500s, Italian scholars printed Latin and Italian translations of the Pneumatica, and inventors across Europe read about the spinning ball again.","Yet for about 1,500 years after Hero, steam stayed mostly a curiosity. The aeolipile was remembered in books and sometimes built as a toy or a demonstration, but it did not grind grain, pump water or move ships. The world kept running on muscle, wind and water.","Then, in the 1500s and early 1600s, something began to stir. In several very different countries, from the Ottoman Empire to Spain, France and Italy, curious inventors began to look at steam again and to dream of putting it to work. Some wanted it to turn a roasting spit, some to raise water, some to drive mills. Their ideas were sketchy and their machines were mostly experiments, but the long sleep of Hero's steam was finally coming to an end."]}],"deepDive":"Historians have argued for a long time about why the ancient Mediterranean world, which knew of the aeolipile, never developed practical steam power. One classic answer points to slavery: where human labour was cheap and plentiful, the incentive to invest in labour-saving machinery was weak. Critics reply that Roman society did adopt labour-saving technology when it made sense, such as the watermills at Barbegal or treadwheel cranes, so cheap labour alone cannot explain the gap. A second answer is technical. The aeolipile is a reaction turbine with no way to recover energy from the steam other than the push of an unguided jet. Its torque is tiny and almost all the fuel's energy leaves as hot vapour, so its efficiency would have been a very small fraction of one percent. Turning such a fast, weak rotation into useful work would require high-speed gearing and bearings that ancient workshops could not make, and in any case the real breakthrough of the 1700s, the piston engine driven by atmospheric pressure, depended on understanding the vacuum, which arrived only in the 1640s. A third answer is about the dates themselves. Hero's lifetime is not securely known: estimates once ranged from about 150 BCE to 250 CE. In 1938 Otto Neugebauer connected a lunar eclipse described in Hero's Dioptra with the eclipse of 13 March 62 CE, which is why most modern accounts place him in the 1st century CE. But the text does not say Hero saw it, so the dating is strong rather than certain. There is also the question of credit. Vitruvius described fixed, non-spinning aeolipiles in De architectura around 30 to 20 BCE, so steam-blowing balls predate Hero, and the Alexandrian engineer Ctesibius, whose writings are lost, may have influenced both. What Hero's text gives us is the earliest surviving description of steam producing continuous rotation, an idea that would return, in vastly more refined form, in the steam turbines of the 1880s.","funFact":"Our word museum comes from the Mouseion of Alexandria, which was not a building full of exhibits but a community of paid scholars who lived, studied and taught there.","check":{"q":"What made Hero's aeolipile spin?","a":"Jets of steam shooting out of bent nozzles pushed the ball the opposite way (action and reaction)","wrong":["A falling weight on a rope","A hidden waterwheel underneath it","Wind blowing on sails attached to the ball"]},"sources":[{"title":"Hero of Alexandria","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hero_of_Alexandria"},{"title":"Heron of Alexandria (biography)","publisher":"MacTutor History of Mathematics, University of St Andrews","url":"https://mathshistory.st-andrews.ac.uk/Biographies/Heron/"},{"title":"Aeolipile","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeolipile"},{"title":"Vitruvius, De architectura, Book I (English translation)","publisher":"LacusCurtius, University of Chicago","url":"https://penelope.uchicago.edu/Thayer/E/Roman/Texts/Vitruvius/1*.html"},{"title":"Mouseion","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mouseion"},{"title":"Library of Alexandria","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Library_of_Alexandria"},{"title":"History of the steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_steam_engine"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/02-1.jpg","alt":"An 1870s engraving of Hero's aeolipile: a ball on a boiling cauldron, spun round by jets of steam.","file":"PSM V12 D025 Grecian idea of steam engine.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PSM_V12_D025_Grecian_idea_of_steam_engine.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/02-2.jpg","alt":"Hero's automatic temple doors: fire on the altar heats air, pushing water into a bucket that pulls the doors open.","file":"Heron - automatische Tempeltür.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heron_-_automatische_Tempelt%C3%BCr.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/02-3.jpg","alt":"A big copper replica of Hero's aeolipile at the Kotsanas Museum of Ancient Greek Technology in Athens.","file":"Αιολόσφαιρα Μουσείο Κοτσανά Αρχαίας Ελληνικής Τεχνολογίας.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:%CE%91%CE%B9%CE%BF%CE%BB%CF%8C%CF%83%CF%86%CE%B1%CE%B9%CF%81%CE%B1_%CE%9C%CE%BF%CF%85%CF%83%CE%B5%CE%AF%CE%BF_%CE%9A%CE%BF%CF%84%CF%83%CE%B1%CE%BD%CE%AC_%CE%91%CF%81%CF%87%CE%B1%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82_%CE%95%CE%BB%CE%BB%CE%B7%CE%BD%CE%B9%CE%BA%CE%AE%CF%82_%CE%A4%CE%B5%CF%87%CE%BD%CE%BF%CE%BB%CE%BF%CE%B3%CE%AF%CE%B1%CF%82.jpg","author":"Aga39memnon","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":3722},{"n":3,"title":"Spits, Pumps and Daydreams","when":"1500s – 1660s","summary":"About 450 years ago, clever people in Turkey, Spain, France, Italy and England started playing with steam again. One used it to turn a roast on a fire, one used it to pump water out of a mine, and one dreamed of a water fountain driven by fire. None of their machines changed the world yet, but each one was a step on the road.","sections":[{"heading":"Steam Wakes Up Again","paragraphs":["Imagine walking into a busy workshop in the middle of the 1500s. There are no electric lights and no engines. Every hammer is swung by an arm, every bellows is squeezed by hand, and every heavy load is dragged by people or animals. Yet the air is full of new ideas. Printing presses have been spreading books across Europe for about a hundred years, and old Greek books, including Hero of Alexandria's Pneumatica, are being copied, translated into Latin and Italian, and studied by curious engineers.","For roughly 1,500 years after Hero, steam had mostly been a party trick. Cooks knew that a lid would rattle over boiling water. Metalworkers knew that a sealed pot thrown on a fire could burst with a frightening bang. But almost nobody had tried to make steam do a steady, useful job. Between about 1550 and 1665, that slowly began to change.","In this part of the story, we will travel across a whole continent and beyond. We will visit Istanbul, the glittering capital of the Ottoman Empire, where an astronomer wrote about a spit that turned itself. We will go to Spain, where a soldier who looked after the king's mines received official permission to use a steam device for raising water. We will cross into France and the gardens of German princes with a fountain designer, peek into an Italian book of imaginary machines, and finally stop in London, where a nobleman who had once been locked in the Tower wrote about a 'water-commanding engine'.","These people did not know each other, and most of them could not have read each other's books. They lived in different countries, spoke different languages and followed different religions. But they shared one big question that you met at the end of Part 1: could fire, through water and steam, be made to do work that muscles, wind and rivers usually did?","As you read, notice two things. First, many of these machines were described in books or drawings, but we often do not know how well, or even whether, they worked in real life. A drawing is not the same as a machine that runs every day for years. Second, history books written in Britain have sometimes told the steam story as if it began in England. It did not. Some of the earliest steam ideas of this period came from the Ottoman world and from Spain, and it is only fair to give them their place."]},{"heading":"Taqi al-Din and the Self-Turning Spit","paragraphs":["Our first stop is with a man named Taqi al-Din Muhammad ibn Ma'ruf. He was born in 1526 in Damascus, in what is now Syria, which was then part of the huge Ottoman Empire. That empire stretched from Hungary to Egypt and from Iraq to Algeria, and its capital, Istanbul (then usually called Constantinople), was one of the largest cities on Earth.","Taqi al-Din was what we would now call a polymath, which means a person who is expert in many subjects. He studied religion and law, but he also loved mathematics, optics (the science of light), astronomy and machines. Over his life he wrote more than ninety books. He worked as a judge and teacher in Egypt and Syria, and in 1571 he became the chief astronomer to the Ottoman sultan.","In 1551, while he was living in Egypt, he finished a book about clever mechanical devices. It was written in Arabic, and its title is usually translated into English as 'The Sublime Methods of Spiritual Machines'. The word 'spiritual' here does not mean ghosts. It was an old way of describing machines that seemed to move by themselves, powered by hidden things like air, water or weights.","One of the machines in the book was a roasting spit. A spit is a long metal rod pushed through a piece of meat so it can be turned slowly over a fire. For thousands of years, somebody had to sit by the hot fire and turn it by hand, hour after hour, so the meat cooked evenly and did not burn on one side. In some places, people even trained small dogs to run inside a wheel to turn the spit.","Taqi al-Din described a way to let the fire do the turning. A closed vessel of water was heated until steam rushed out of a narrow opening. The jet of steam was aimed at vanes, small flat blades arranged around the edge of a wheel. As the steam struck the vanes, it pushed them round, and the wheel turned the spit. The hotter the fire, the faster the steam came out, and the faster the meat would rotate.","Look closely at that idea, because it is important. Hero's aeolipile spun because steam rushed out of the ball itself and pushed it backwards, like a lawn sprinkler. Taqi al-Din's spit worked in a different way: a jet of steam from a fixed nozzle struck the blades of a separate wheel and shoved them along. Engineers today call this an impulse design. Centuries later, in the 1880s, the Swedish engineer Gustaf de Laval would build fast-spinning impulse turbines that used the same basic principle, with far better metal and much more precise blades.","Taqi al-Din's book also described several water-raising machines, including pumps driven by animals and by water wheels, one of them with six cylinders. He later wrote about mechanical clocks and, in the 1570s, ran a new observatory in Istanbul, where he watched the Great Comet of 1577. Sadly, the observatory was torn down in 1580 after political quarrels at the sultan's court. His steam spit, meanwhile, stayed a kitchen curiosity. It saved a little work for a cook, but nobody tried to build it into a giant machine. Still, it is one of the earliest known written descriptions of steam turning a wheel by striking blades."]},{"heading":"Jerónimo de Ayanz: A Soldier Who Fought Floods","paragraphs":["Our next traveller is a Spanish nobleman with a very long name: Jerónimo de Ayanz y Beaumont. He was born in 1553 in Navarre, in the north of Spain. Like many young noblemen of his time, he became a soldier. He fought in North Africa, in Italy and in the Low Countries (today's Belgium and the Netherlands), and he was known as a brave and very strong man. He was made a knight of the Order of Calatrava, one of Spain's old military orders, and later served as governor of the town of Martos.","In 1587, King Philip II gave Ayanz a very different job. He was made administrator general of the kingdom's mines. Spain at that time ruled a vast empire, and silver from mines in Spain and the Americas helped pay for its armies and palaces. Ayanz was responsible for hundreds of mines, about 550 according to one count, scattered across Spain and its American lands.","That job showed him the same terrible problem that English coal miners faced, which you read about in Part 1. The deeper miners dug, the more groundwater seeped into the tunnels. Water had to be lifted out with buckets, hand pumps and horse-driven machines, and it was slow, costly and exhausting. One of the mines he knew well was at Guadalcanal, in southern Spain, a famous source of silver that suffered badly from flooding.","Ayanz was an inventor at heart. In 1606, King Philip III granted him a 'privilege of invention', a royal document that gave him the sole right to use and profit from his ideas for a set period. In that sense it was like a modern patent. The privilege covered close to fifty different inventions. They included improved windmills, better furnaces, new scientific instruments, a type of pump, and, most importantly for our story, a device that used steam to help raise water. Historians describe it as one of the earliest known patents for any steam device anywhere in the world.","Exactly how his steam water-raiser worked is still studied by historians, because it is known mainly from written descriptions and drawings in the privilege documents rather than from a surviving machine. The basic idea was to use the force of steam from a heated vessel to push water upward, so that a mine could be drained without endless teams of animals. Sources say it was tried at the Guadalcanal mines. How long it worked there, and how much water it really moved, is not well recorded.","Ayanz also loved testing ideas in public. On 2 August 1602, in front of King Philip III and his court, he arranged a demonstration on the Pisuerga River near Valladolid. A man wearing a special diving suit, fed with fresh air through tubes, went under the water and stayed there for about an hour, until the king himself told him he could come up. People watching on the riverbank must have been astonished.","Ayanz died in Madrid in 1613. For a long time afterwards, his name was almost forgotten outside Spain, while English-language books talked mostly about later British engineers. Only in the late 1900s did historians of Spanish engineering carefully study his papers and bring his work back into the light. Today he is often called a pioneer of steam, a reminder that the history of invention can lose people simply because their papers sat unread in an archive."]},{"heading":"Salomon de Caus and the Fountain That Ran on Fire","paragraphs":["Now let us travel north to France. Salomon de Caus was born in 1576 in Dieppe, a port town on the coast of Normandy. He was a Huguenot, which means a French Protestant, at a time when Protestants and Catholics in France often distrusted and even fought each other. Partly for that reason, de Caus spent much of his life on the move, working wherever a rich patron would hire him.","His speciality was something that sounds like pure fun: gardens full of moving water. Wealthy princes of the early 1600s loved grottoes (artificial caves), splashing fountains, mechanical birds that sang when water flowed through hidden pipes, and statues that seemed to play music. De Caus designed these wonders. He worked in England for the royal family, building a fountain shaped like a rocky mountain, Mount Parnassus, at one of their London palaces. Later he moved to Heidelberg in Germany, where he designed the famous Hortus Palatinus, a huge terraced garden for the ruler of the Palatinate and his English wife, Elizabeth Stuart. Parts of its walls and terraces can still be seen beside Heidelberg Castle today.","To make all those fountains work, de Caus had to think hard about how to move water uphill. In 1615 he published a beautifully illustrated book in French, Les Raisons des forces mouvantes, which means roughly 'The Reasons for Moving Forces'. It was full of pumps, water wheels, organs and garden machines. Among them was a simple but striking experiment with steam.","Here is the idea. Take a strong, hollow metal ball and fill it partly with water. Fit a narrow pipe through the top of the ball, so that the bottom end of the pipe dips almost to the floor of the ball, under the water, while the top end sticks out into the air. Seal everything else tightly. Now put the ball over a fire. As the water heats up, steam collects in the space above the water inside the ball. The steam has nowhere to go, so its pressure rises and presses down on the surface of the water. The only way out is up the pipe, so the water is forced up and spurts out of the top like a fountain.","De Caus did not invent this trick entirely on his own. About fourteen years earlier, in 1601, an Italian scholar named Giambattista della Porta had described a similar way of using steam pressure to raise a column of water. But de Caus's book was widely read, and his drawing became one of the best-known early pictures showing steam pressure pushing water.","There was a big problem with his fountain, though. It could only run once per filling. When the water in the ball was used up, you had to let it cool, open it and refill it. It also needed the ball to be very strong, because steam pressure that can lift a jet of water can also split a weak seam. Metalworkers of the time could not easily make large, safe, pressure-proof vessels.","In the 1800s, some French writers, including the scientist François Arago, claimed that de Caus was the true inventor of the steam engine. That went too far: he never built a working engine that ran on its own. A dramatic story also circulated at that time that he had been locked in a madhouse in Paris for pestering the king's minister about steam power. That tale came from a letter that later turned out to be made up, so it should be treated as a legend. The real de Caus was a respected engineer and garden designer, and he died in Paris in 1626."]},{"heading":"Giovanni Branca's Machine on Paper","paragraphs":["Our fifth stop is the Italian hill town of Loreto, near the Adriatic Sea. It was, and still is, a famous place of pilgrimage, built around a church called the Holy House. Giovanni Branca, born in 1571, worked there from 1616 as an architect and engineer, looking after the great church buildings.","In 1629 Branca published a book in Rome called Le Machine, which simply means 'The Machines'. It was a collection of illustrated plates showing all kinds of devices: lifting gear, mills, pumps and other contraptions. Picture books of machines like this were popular in Renaissance Europe. They were partly engineering manuals and partly showcases, a bit like a catalogue of amazing ideas.","One plate in Le Machine has become famous in the history of steam. It shows a closed vessel of water sitting on a fire. Steam escapes from a pipe at the top and blows straight at a horizontal wheel fitted with flat vanes around its rim, almost like a small paddle wheel lying on its side. As the steam hits the vanes, the wheel turns. Through a set of gears, its turning motion lifts and drops a pair of heavy pestles, the club-like tools used to pound things in a mortar. Branca suggested that machines like this could grind materials, raise water or saw wood.","If that sounds familiar, it is because it uses the same principle as Taqi al-Din's spit: a jet of steam striking blades. For that reason, Branca's drawing is often shown as an early ancestor of the steam turbine. But there is no evidence that Branca actually built a full working version. Even if he had, it would have been very weak. A loose jet of steam blowing across open air quickly spreads out and slows down, so most of its energy is lost before it ever touches the vanes. Real impulse turbines, built two and a half centuries later, needed carefully shaped nozzles, tight casings and precisely made blades spinning at high speed.","One modern historian of technology, Alex Keller, described many of the machines in books like Branca's as 'armchair inventions', designs that seldom had any working three-dimensional version. That does not make Branca foolish. Drawing an idea is an important first step, and books like his spread ideas to other engineers. But it reminds us that a picture of a machine in a book is not proof that the machine ever ran."]},{"heading":"The Marquess and His Water-Commanding Engine","paragraphs":["Our final traveller lived through one of the most dangerous periods in English history. Edward Somerset was born in about 1601 into one of the richest Catholic families in England. His family's great home was Raglan Castle in Monmouthshire, on the border between England and Wales. He later became the second Marquess of Worcester.","When the English Civil War broke out in 1642, between supporters of King Charles I and supporters of Parliament, Somerset sided with the king. He raised troops in Wales and was sent on a secret mission to Ireland to try to win soldiers for the royal side. The deal he made there was so generous that the king later disowned it. After the king lost the war, Somerset spent years in exile in France. When he returned to England in the 1650s, he was arrested and held in the Tower of London for a time.","Through all this turmoil, Somerset loved inventing. He kept a skilled mechanic named Caspar Kaltoff (also spelled Kaspar Kalthoff) working with him, and together they built and tested machines. In 1663 he published a little book with a long title, usually known as A Century of Inventions. Here 'century' means 'a hundred', not 'a hundred years': the book lists about one hundred inventions, from secret codes and combination locks to calculating devices and floating gardens.","Most of the entries are only a few lines long, and many are so vague that historians still argue about what he meant. But one entry, about a way of driving water upward by fire, has attracted enormous attention. Somerset called it a 'water-commanding engine'. From his descriptions, it seems that steam from a separate boiler was led into closed vessels of water and its pressure forced the water up a pipe. While one vessel was being emptied by steam, another could be refilled, so the flow could keep going. That idea of separating the boiler from the vessels where the pumping happened was an important step beyond de Caus's single ball.","Somerset claimed big things for his engine. He wrote that it could send water up like a fountain many feet high, around 40 feet (about 12 metres) by his account, and that a small amount of water turned to steam could push up many times its own amount of cold water. Visitors recorded seeing a water-raising machine at his workshop at Vauxhall in London around 1663, and an old account says that, before the Civil War, he tested the idea on the walls of Raglan Castle. In 1663 Parliament even passed an Act allowing him to keep the profits from his water-commanding engine, with a tenth going to the king.","Yet the engine never became a business. Somerset was deep in debt, he struggled to find investors, and some scientists doubted him. Robert Hooke, a brilliant experimenter you will meet in the next part, dismissed the engine as one of the 'perpetual motion fallacies'. Somerset died in 1667. It was said that he wanted a model of his engine buried with him, and in 1861 a group led by the engineer and historian Bennet Woodcroft opened the family vault at Raglan to look for it, but found nothing. Whatever the water-commanding engine really was, it vanished with its inventor."]},{"heading":"So Many Ideas, So Few Machines","paragraphs":["Let us line up our travellers. In 1551, Taqi al-Din described a steam jet turning a spit. In 1601, della Porta described steam forcing water up a tube. In 1606, Ayanz received a royal privilege that included a steam device for raising water from mines. In 1615, de Caus published his steam fountain. In 1629, Branca drew a steam jet spinning a wheel. In 1663, the Marquess of Worcester described an engine that commanded water with fire. That is more than a century of clever thinking about steam, from Egypt to England.","So why, by the 1660s, was there still not a single steam machine quietly working day after day in a mine or a mill? There were several reasons, and they all fitted together.","First, materials. Steam pressure is powerful, and early metal vessels were often hammered or cast with weak spots and leaky seams soldered with soft metals. If you push too hard, they split or explode. Engineers of the time did not have reliable ways to make big, strong, accurately shaped containers, and they had no good gauges for measuring pressure.","Second, efficiency. Most of these designs wasted huge amounts of heat. A jet of steam blowing on a wheel, or steam pressing directly on cold water, loses heat and energy very quickly. To pump a meaningful amount of water, you would have needed a great deal of fuel, and in many places wood and coal were expensive.","Third, nobody yet understood the science. People did not know what steam really was, how air pressure worked, or why water behaved as it did when heated and cooled. Without that knowledge, every improvement was guesswork. Building a better machine was a bit like trying to fix a bicycle in the dark.","Fourth, there was not always a clear customer who desperately needed the machine and could pay for it. Garden fountains and turning spits were nice to have, but they did not justify building expensive, dangerous equipment. That would change once mines grew deeper and their owners grew desperate, as you will see in Part 5.","It is also worth noticing who was doing this thinking: an Ottoman astronomer, a Spanish soldier and mine official, a French Protestant garden designer, an Italian church architect and an English Catholic nobleman. Invention was not the property of one country. Ideas were scattered all across the world, and many people built small pieces of the puzzle without ever seeing the whole picture.","And here is the surprise. When the first truly practical steam engines finally appeared, about forty to fifty years after Worcester's book, they did not mainly use steam to push. The real breakthrough came from something stranger: the discovery that the empty space called a vacuum is real, and that the invisible air around us is heavy enough to press with enormous force. To understand that discovery, we need to visit a glassblower's tube full of shining mercury, a mountain in France, and a town where teams of horses tried and failed to pull two metal bowls apart. That is the story of Part 4."]}],"deepDive":"Who invented the steam engine? The honest answer is that there was no single moment and no single inventor, and the period from 1550 to 1665 shows why the question is slippery. Historians usually separate four stages: a written idea, a drawing, a working model and a machine that runs reliably for paying customers. Taqi al-Din (1551) and Branca (1629) give us the first two stages for the steam jet striking vanes, which is the principle of the impulse turbine. Della Porta (1601) and de Caus (1615) describe steam pressure forcing water up a pipe, the principle behind the 'pressure' half of Thomas Savery's later pump. Ayanz (1606) and Worcester (1663) seem to have gone further, toward working water-raisers, but surviving evidence is thin: for Ayanz it is mainly privilege documents and drawings, and for Worcester it is a short, deliberately mysterious book, a few visitors' reports and an Act of Parliament. Priority claims have also been shaped by national pride. In the 1800s, French writers championed de Caus, British books celebrated Worcester and Savery, and Spanish historians later rediscovered Ayanz. Even older claims exist: a story says a Spanish sea captain, Blasco de Garay, showed a steam-driven ship to Emperor Charles V in 1543, but the evidence is doubtful and most historians reject it. Why did none of these designs take off? Look at the physics. A free steam jet in open air loses most of its kinetic energy to turbulence before reaching the vanes, so Branca-type wheels deliver only a tiny fraction of the fuel's energy as useful work. Pressure-displacement devices like de Caus's ball heat the water they are pushing, wasting heat, and they demand vessels that can safely hold pressure, which was hard with 1600s metalworking. The missing ingredient was not cleverness but understanding: until scientists explained atmospheric pressure and the vacuum in the 1640s to 1690s, inventors lacked a reliable model of what steam and air were actually doing.","funFact":"In 1602, a diver wearing an air-supplied suit designed by Jerónimo de Ayanz stayed under the Pisuerga River for about an hour, until King Philip III himself told him to come up.","check":{"q":"What did Taqi al-Din's 1551 steam device do?","a":"It turned a roasting spit, using a jet of steam striking vanes on a wheel","wrong":["It pumped water out of silver mines in Spain","It pulled a cart through the streets of Istanbul","It rang the bells of a mosque every hour"]},"sources":[{"title":"Taqi ad-Din Muhammad ibn Ma'ruf","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Taqi_ad-Din_Muhammad_ibn_Ma%27ruf"},{"title":"Jerónimo de Ayanz y Beaumont","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jer%C3%B3nimo_de_Ayanz_y_Beaumont"},{"title":"Salomon de Caus","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Salomon_de_Caus"},{"title":"Giovanni Branca","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Giovanni_Branca"},{"title":"Edward Somerset, 2nd Marquess of Worcester","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Edward_Somerset,_2nd_Marquess_of_Worcester"},{"title":"History of the steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_the_steam_engine"},{"title":"Steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steam_engine"},{"title":"Somerset, Edward (1601-1667), Dictionary of National Biography","publisher":"Wikisource","url":"https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Dictionary_of_National_Biography,_1885-1900/Somerset,_Edward_(1601-1667)"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/03-1.jpg","alt":"Branca's 1629 idea: steam from a head-shaped boiler blows on a vaned wheel, whose gears work two pounding pestles.","file":"Dampfmaschine Branca 1629.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dampfmaschine_Branca_1629.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/03-2.jpg","alt":"Salomon de Caus (1576-1626), the French engineer who showed steam could push water up a pipe.","file":"Salomon-de-Caus04.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Salomon-de-Caus04.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/03-3.jpg","alt":"The ruined Great Tower and moat of Raglan Castle in Wales, home of the Marquess of Worcester.","file":"Raglan Castle, The Great Tower - geograph.org.uk - 5743671.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Raglan_Castle,_The_Great_Tower_-_geograph.org.uk_-_5743671.jpg","author":"Michael Garlick","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3936},{"n":4,"title":"The Weight of the Air","when":"1640s – 1690s","summary":"Air feels like nothing, but it is actually pressing on you all the time. In the 1600s, scientists proved it with tubes of shiny mercury, a trip up a mountain, and teams of horses that could not pull two metal bowls apart. Then an inventor named Denis Papin had a brilliant idea: use steam to make an empty space, and let the air do the pushing.","sections":[{"heading":"The Pump That Would Not Pull","paragraphs":["Try this the next time you have a drink with a straw. When you suck, it feels as if you are pulling the drink up into your mouth. For most of history, that is exactly what people thought was happening. The suction seemed to grab the liquid and drag it upward.","Since the time of the ancient Greek thinker Aristotle, most learned people in Europe also believed that 'nature abhors a vacuum'. In other words, nature supposedly hates empty space so much that it will rush to fill any gap. When you pull the handle of a pump and make a space inside it, water was thought to leap up to fill that space because a true emptiness was impossible.","But practical people kept running into a puzzle that did not fit. As you read in Part 1, a suction pump on top of a well or mine shaft could only lift water so far. In Italy, pump makers and well diggers had found that no matter how well they built a suction pump, the water would not rise more than about 10 metres (roughly 33 feet). Pull harder, build the pump better, and still the water stopped at the same height, as if it had hit an invisible ceiling.","The great Italian scientist Galileo Galilei wrote about this limit in his last major book, published in 1638. He thought the column of water might be like a rope that snaps when it gets too long under its own weight. It was a clever guess, but it was wrong. The real answer would come from one of his students, and it would turn the whole idea of suction upside down.","The answer, in short, is this: suction does not pull at all. When you drink through a straw, you lower the air pressure inside the straw, and the air pressing down on the surface of your drink outside the straw pushes the liquid up. Air has weight. It is a vast invisible ocean, many kilometres deep, sitting on top of everything. That weight can push water up a pipe, but only so far: a column of water about 10 metres tall weighs as much as the whole column of air above it, and at that point the push runs out. That discovery would become the key to the first practical steam engines."]},{"heading":"Torricelli's Shining Tube","paragraphs":["Evangelista Torricelli was born in 1608 and grew up in a poor family in Italy. He was a gifted mathematician, and in 1641 he went to help the elderly Galileo, who by then was blind and living under house arrest near Florence. After Galileo died in early 1642, Torricelli took his place as mathematician to the Grand Duke of Tuscany.","Torricelli thought hard about the pump puzzle. If the weight of the air was pushing water up to about 10 metres, then a heavier liquid should be pushed up a much shorter distance. Mercury, the silvery metal that is liquid at room temperature, is about 13.6 times heavier than the same amount of water. So the air should hold up a column of mercury roughly 13.6 times shorter than a column of water.","In 1643, Torricelli and his colleague Vincenzo Viviani tested this. They took a glass tube about a metre long, sealed at one end, and filled it to the brim with mercury. Then, holding a finger over the open end, they turned it upside down and dipped that end into a dish of mercury before letting go. The mercury in the tube began to sink, but it did not all pour out. It stopped when the column was about 76 centimetres (about 30 inches) tall.","Above the mercury, at the sealed top of the tube, was a gap. Nothing had been let in. No air could have got past the mercury. The space was, as nearly as anyone could tell, empty. It was a man-made vacuum, and it is still sometimes called a Torricellian vacuum. Torricelli had made the first barometer, an instrument for measuring air pressure.","He also noticed that the height of the mercury changed a little from day to day. The air itself was getting slightly heavier or lighter, which we now know is linked to the weather. In a famous letter written in 1644 to his friend Michelangelo Ricci, Torricelli summed up his view: 'We live submerged at the bottom of an ocean of air.'","Torricelli died young, in 1647, at just 39. But his tube spread quickly across Europe. In honour of his work, a small unit of pressure used in vacuum science is still called the torr. And weather forecasters still talk about air pressure in terms that trace back to his experiment: standard air pressure at sea level is often given as 760 millimetres of mercury, or about 29.92 inches of mercury."]},{"heading":"A Barometer Climbs a Mountain","paragraphs":["Not everyone was convinced. Perhaps, some scholars argued, the space at the top of the tube was not really empty but filled with some invisible, airy substance. Perhaps the mercury was held up by nature's dislike of emptiness, not by the weight of the air. The famous French philosopher René Descartes, for example, believed that space was always filled with some kind of matter.","In France, a brilliant young mathematician named Blaise Pascal, born in 1623, came up with a way to settle the question. If the mercury really was held up by the weight of the air above it, then carrying the tube up a mountain should make a difference. At the top of a mountain there is less air above you, so it should weigh less and push less. The mercury column should get shorter.","Pascal was often ill and could not make the climb himself. So he asked his brother-in-law, Florin Périer, who lived in Clermont-Ferrand in central France, close to a tall old volcano called the Puy de Dôme. Its summit stands about 1,465 metres (around 4,800 feet) above sea level.","On 19 September 1648, Périer and a group of local witnesses carried out the experiment. First they set up mercury tubes in the garden of a monastery in the lower part of town, where the mercury stood at about 26 inches and 3.5 'lines' in the old French units (a line was a twelfth of an inch). One tube was left behind in the garden, with a monk to watch it all day and check that it did not change. Then the rest of the group climbed the mountain, about 500 fathoms (roughly 1,000 metres) higher.","At the summit, the mercury stood at only about 23 inches and 2 lines, more than 3 French inches lower, which is roughly 8 centimetres. They repeated the test several times, in different spots, and always got the same result. On the way down, the column grew taller again. Back at the monastery, the mercury in the watched tube had stayed the same all day.","Pascal then tried a smaller version in Paris, taking a barometer up the tall bell tower of the church of Saint-Jacques-de-la-Boucherie, about 50 metres high. Even there, the mercury dropped slightly. The conclusion was hard to argue with: the air really has weight, the weight gets smaller as you go higher, and the gap at the top of the tube is a vacuum. Today the official scientific unit of pressure, the pascal, is named after him."]},{"heading":"The Mayor, the Bowls and the Horses","paragraphs":["Meanwhile, in Germany, a very different kind of scientist was at work. Otto von Guericke was born in 1602 in Magdeburg, a wealthy trading city on the River Elbe. As a young man he lived through one of the worst events of the Thirty Years' War: in 1631 an army stormed Magdeburg, and most of the city was burned and thousands of its people were killed. Guericke and his family survived. Afterwards he helped rebuild the city as an engineer and official, and from 1646 he served as its mayor for more than thirty years.","Guericke wanted to know whether a vacuum could really exist, and he decided to make one himself. His first attempts used a wooden barrel full of water. He tried to pump the water out with a pump like those used by fire brigades, hoping to leave emptiness behind. But air kept whistling in through the wood. Then he tried pumping the air directly out of a sealed copper globe. Around 1650 he built one of the first true vacuum pumps, or air pumps: a cylinder with a piston and valves that, stroke by stroke, removed a little air each time.","Once he could pump air out of containers, Guericke found that the outside air pressed on them with astonishing force. One copper vessel suddenly crumpled with a bang as the air was pumped out, crushed by the air outside. In another test, according to the reports, he fitted a large cylinder with a piston attached to a rope, and a number of strong men took hold of the rope. When the air was pumped out from under the piston, the outside air pushed it down into the cylinder, dragging the men along with it. Keep that picture in your mind: a piston in a cylinder, pushed by the air into a vacuum. It is exactly the idea that later steam engines would use.","Guericke's most famous show used two hollow copper half-spheres, each about 50 centimetres (20 inches) across. Their edges fitted together and were sealed with grease. When the two halves were just touching, they could be separated easily. But when Guericke pumped out the air inside, they held together as if welded.","On 8 May 1654, at the meeting of the Imperial Diet (a great assembly of German rulers) in Regensburg, Guericke showed this in front of Emperor Ferdinand III. Teams of horses were hitched to rings on each half and whipped to pull in opposite directions. According to the reports, thirty horses, fifteen on each side, could not drag the halves apart. He later repeated the show in Magdeburg with sixteen horses, and the demonstration became known around the world as the Magdeburg hemispheres. Yet when a small tap was opened to let air back in, the halves simply fell apart in a person's hands.","Why was it so strong? Air at sea level presses with a force of about 1 kilogram on every square centimetre of surface, about 14.7 pounds on every square inch. A circle 50 centimetres across has an area of nearly 2,000 square centimetres. If all the air inside had been removed, the outside air would have pressed the halves together with a force equal to the weight of about 2 tonnes, more than a large car. Guericke's pump could not make a perfect vacuum, so the real force was smaller, but still more than the horses could overcome. The original hemispheres can still be seen today at the Deutsches Museum in Munich.","The news first spread through a book by a Jesuit scholar named Gaspar Schott in 1657, and Guericke later described his experiments himself in a book of 1672. Across Europe, people who had never thought about air before were suddenly talking about its weight and power."]},{"heading":"Boyle, Hooke and the Air Pump","paragraphs":["One of the people who read Schott's description was Robert Boyle, born in 1627 in Ireland, the fourteenth child of one of the richest men in the British Isles. Boyle was free to spend his life doing experiments, and he moved to Oxford in 1654, where he gathered a group of talented helpers.","Boyle thought Guericke's pump was clumsy. It needed, as Boyle put it, the hard work of two strong men for several hours. So he asked his young assistant, Robert Hooke, to build a better one. Hooke, born in 1635 on the Isle of Wight, was a mechanical genius who would later become famous for his microscope drawings and his studies of springs. By 1659 he had built Boyle an improved air pump. It had a large glass globe on top, which meant that experimenters could put objects inside and watch what happened as the air was pumped out.","And watch they did. They put a ticking watch inside the globe and found that, as the air was removed, the sound grew fainter, showing that sound needs air to travel through. They watched candle flames shrink and go out, and saw that burning coals faded as the air was taken away. They also placed small animals inside, which suffered as the air disappeared. Such experiments seem cruel to many people today, and they helped show that animals, like fire, need something in the air to stay alive.","In 1660 Boyle published a book describing these experiments, with a title that begins New Experiments Physico-Mechanical, Touching the Spring of the Air. By 'spring', Boyle meant that air behaves like a spring: squash it and it pushes back harder; let it expand and it pushes back more gently. In 1662 he described the rule we now call Boyle's law: if you keep the temperature the same and squeeze a gas into half the space, its pressure doubles. (Other researchers, including Henry Power and Richard Towneley, were working on the same idea, so credit is shared.)","Boyle's air pump became one of the most famous scientific instruments of its century, so famous that it was nicknamed the 'machina Boyleana', Boyle's machine. Air pumps became star attractions at the new Royal Society of London, founded in 1660, where scientists met to watch and argue about experiments. It all added up to a powerful new understanding: air has weight, air has springiness, and a vacuum can be made and used."]},{"heading":"Huygens's Gunpowder Engine","paragraphs":["If the air can push so hard on an empty space, could that push be turned into useful work? One of the first scientists to try was the Dutch genius Christiaan Huygens, born in 1629. He had already invented the pendulum clock and discovered Saturn's largest moon, Titan. In the 1670s he was working in Paris at the new French Royal Academy of Sciences.","Huygens designed an engine that used gunpowder, of all things. It is often dated to 1673, though Huygens described it in detail in 1678, and a model seems to have been built around 1678 or 1679. Picture a tall metal tube, standing upright, with a piston inside that could slide up and down. A tiny charge of gunpowder was placed at the bottom and lit. The bang drove the hot gases outward and pushed the piston up. Near the top, the hot gases escaped through holes covered by leather flaps that worked like one-way valves: they flapped open to let gas out but fell shut again so air could not get back in.","Then came the clever part. The little bit of gas left inside the tube cooled down and shrank, leaving a partial vacuum under the piston. Now the air outside pushed the piston back down with great force. By tying a rope from the piston over a pulley to a weight, the downward push could lift a load. By 1682, according to one report, a dram of gunpowder, about one-sixteenth of an ounce, could raise seven or eight boys, a load of hundreds of kilograms.","The gunpowder engine never became practical. Each shot needed a fresh charge, loading it was slow and dangerous, the leather valves leaked, and the vacuum was never very good because some burnt gas always remained. Historians still debate how well it could ever have worked. But it pointed the way to a crucial idea: use the push of the air on a piston, once you have made an empty space underneath it. Huygens's young assistant in Paris had been watching closely. His name was Denis Papin."]},{"heading":"Denis Papin's Bone Digester","paragraphs":["Denis Papin was born in 1647 near Blois in France, into a Protestant family. He trained as a doctor, but he was fascinated by machines and experiments. In 1673 he was in Paris, working with Huygens on air pumps and on experiments with gunpowder. Two years later, in 1675, he moved to London, where he became an assistant to Robert Boyle.","In London, Papin invented something that you might have in your kitchen today. He called it a 'digester', and it was the ancestor of the pressure cooker. It was a strong metal pot with a lid that could be clamped down tightly so that steam could not escape. Inside, water could be heated well above its usual boiling point. That was the secret: when steam is trapped, the pressure rises, and at higher pressure water does not boil until it is much hotter than 100 °C. Food cooked in that extra-hot water became tender much faster. Papin's digester could even soften bones until they could be crushed, and he showed how they could be turned into a nourishing jelly.","Of course, trapping steam in a sealed pot is risky. If the pressure gets too high, the pot can explode. Papin's solution was one of his most important inventions: the safety valve. A small hole in the lid was plugged by a valve held shut by a lever with a weight on it. If the steam pressure grew too strong, it lifted the weight and let some steam escape, then the valve dropped shut again. By sliding the weight along the lever, Papin could set the highest pressure he wanted. The same basic idea protected steam boilers for the next two centuries, and versions of it are still used today.","Papin presented his digester to the Royal Society in 1679. But his life was unsettled. He worked for a few years in Venice from 1681, then returned to London. In 1685, the French king Louis XIV withdrew the rights of Protestants in France, so Papin could not go home safely. In about 1687 he took a post teaching mathematics at the University of Marburg in Germany, and he also worked for the ruler of Hesse-Kassel, a German prince."]},{"heading":"1690: A Piston, a Cylinder and a Drop of Water","paragraphs":["Papin had not forgotten the gunpowder engine. In Germany he kept thinking about how to make a good vacuum under a piston. Gunpowder left dirty gases behind. What else could fill a cylinder and then disappear almost completely? The answer was steam.","Here is the simple science behind his idea. When water boils at normal air pressure, it turns into steam that takes up roughly 1,600 to 1,700 times as much space as the water did. One small cup of water can become enough steam to fill a bathtub many times over. But if you cool that steam, it condenses, turning back into a few drops of water. All that space it was filling is suddenly almost empty. You have made a near-vacuum, without any pump at all.","In 1690 Papin published a description of a small engine using this trick, in a scientific journal printed in Leipzig called the Acta Eruditorum. He took a vertical metal cylinder, closed at the bottom and open at the top, with a piston that fitted snugly inside. He put a little water in the bottom and heated it with a fire underneath. As the water boiled, the steam pushed the piston gently up to the top, where a catch held it in place. Then he took away the fire and let the cylinder cool. The steam inside condensed back into water, leaving a near-vacuum below the piston. When the catch was released, the weight of the air outside, about 1 kilogram on every square centimetre, drove the piston down hard. A rope tied to the piston and run over a pulley could lift a weight on the other side.","Notice what Papin's engine did. The steam did not push the piston down. The steam was only a way to create an empty space. The real force came from the atmosphere, just as it had in Guericke's cylinder and Huygens's gunpowder tube. This is why later engines of this type are called 'atmospheric engines'. Papin's piston-and-cylinder layout is the basic arrangement that Thomas Newcomen and James Watt would later build on, which you will meet in Parts 6 and 7.","Papin's model was slow. For every single stroke, the fire had to be brought in and taken away again and the whole cylinder heated and cooled, so it moved only now and then, and it was nowhere near strong enough for a mine. He realised it might be used to pump water or even to drive a boat, and he worked on other designs, including a steam pump that used steam pressure, described in 1707. A story spread in the 1800s that he had built a steam-powered boat in Germany, but historians showed that his boat was actually driven by paddles turned by hand. That steam boat story is a legend.","Sadly, Papin never found the money and support to turn his ideas into big working engines. He returned to London in 1707 and struggled to earn a living, complaining that his work was used without credit. He died in poverty, probably in 1713; for a long time nobody even knew where he was buried, until a burial record at St Bride's Church in London was found in 2016. Yet the key ideas were now on the table: steam can be made in a boiler, it can fill a cylinder, it can be condensed to make a vacuum, the air will then push a piston with tremendous force, and a safety valve can keep the boiler from bursting.","All that was missing was someone who could put those ideas into a machine big enough, tough enough and simple enough to run all day at the bottom of a flooded mine. In England, mine owners were becoming desperate. Their shafts were getting deeper, the water kept pouring in, and horses could not keep up. In 1698, a military engineer named Thomas Savery would step forward with a patent for a fire-powered water pump, which he soon advertised under a very hopeful name: 'the Miner's Friend'."]}],"deepDive":"The scientific battle over the vacuum was not just about pumps. For about two thousand years, following Aristotle, most European scholars held that a true void was impossible. Torricelli's 1643 tube, Pascal's 1648 Puy de Dôme test and Guericke's pumps shifted the explanation from nature 'pulling' into a void to the surrounding air 'pushing'. Pascal's design is admired for including a control: a tube left at the bottom of the mountain and watched all day, so the drop at the summit could not be blamed on the weather. The numbers are easy to check. Standard sea-level pressure is 101,325 pascals, which is the weight of about 10.3 metres of water or 760 millimetres of mercury, and works out to about 1.03 kilograms-force per square centimetre or 14.7 pounds per square inch. That is why no suction pump can lift water more than about 10 metres, even in theory; in practice leaks and dissolved air keep it lower. For Guericke's 50-centimetre hemispheres, a perfect vacuum would give a holding force of roughly 20 kilonewtons, about the weight of 2 tonnes; modern estimates suggest his pump reached only a partial vacuum, so the real force was lower. The power of condensing steam comes from the huge change in volume. At 100 °C and normal pressure, one gram of liquid water becomes about 1.67 litres of steam, roughly 1,670 times its liquid volume. Condense it and the volume collapses, which in a closed cylinder means the pressure collapses too. In practice, early engines never reached a perfect vacuum: the hot cylinder walls kept re-boiling some of the condensed water, and air leaked in. Historians also debate the role of social factors. Robert Boyle's style of public, witnessed experiments, with detailed reports so others could repeat them, became a model for modern science, but it was challenged at the time by the philosopher Thomas Hobbes, who doubted that air pumps proved anything about the vacuum. Finally, a note on credit: Papin's 1690 piston-and-cylinder engine had every key element of the atmospheric engine except a practical boiler and automatic valves, but he lacked funding and skilled workshops, a reminder that inventions need money and makers as well as ideas.","funFact":"The original Magdeburg hemispheres, which in 1654 held together against teams of horses using nothing but air pressure, are still on display at the Deutsches Museum in Munich.","check":{"q":"In Papin's 1690 idea, what actually pushed the piston down?","a":"The pressure of the outside air, after the steam under the piston condensed and left a near-vacuum","wrong":["High-pressure steam forced in from a boiler","An exploding charge of gunpowder","A team of horses pulling on a rope"]},"sources":[{"title":"Evangelista Torricelli","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Evangelista_Torricelli"},{"title":"Blaise Pascal","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blaise_Pascal"},{"title":"Magdeburg hemispheres","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Magdeburg_hemispheres"},{"title":"Otto von Guericke","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Otto_von_Guericke"},{"title":"Robert Boyle","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_Boyle"},{"title":"Gunpowder engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gunpowder_engine"},{"title":"Denis Papin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denis_Papin"},{"title":"Air Pressure","publisher":"NOAA (JetStream)","url":"https://www.noaa.gov/jetstream/atmosphere/air-pressure"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/04-1.jpg","alt":"Guericke's engraving: two teams of horses fail to pull apart the empty Magdeburg hemispheres, shown above them.","file":"Teams of horses trying unsuccessfully to pull apart vacuum-filled copper spheres, also depicted in detail above, in Magdeburg demonstration for Emperor Ferdinand III LCCN92518531.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Teams_of_horses_trying_unsuccessfully_to_pull_apart_vacuum-filled_copper_spheres,_also_depicted_in_detail_above,_in_Magdeburg_demonstration_for_Emperor_Ferdinand_III_LCCN92518531.jpg","author":"Guericke, Otto von, 1602-1686.","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/04-2.jpg","alt":"Engraving of Robert Boyle's first air pump: a big glass globe on a brass pump worked by a crank.","file":"Boyle's first air pump. Wellcome M0010676.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boyle%27s_first_air_pump._Wellcome_M0010676.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/04-3.jpg","alt":"Papin's steam digester, the first pressure cooker. Fig. 1 shows the weighted lever safety valve on top.","file":"Papin's digester - the first form of pressure cooker. Wellcome M0011059.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Papin%27s_digester_-_the_first_form_of_pressure_cooker._Wellcome_M0011059.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"}],"words":4012},{"n":5,"title":"Savery and the Miner's Friend","when":"1698 – early 1700s","summary":"In 1698 an English army engineer named Thomas Savery built a machine that used fire and steam to suck water up and push it higher. It had no moving piston at all, just pipes, taps and big metal vessels. It was not quite strong enough for deep mines, but it pumped water for grand houses and gardens, and it started the age of steam for real.","sections":[{"heading":"A Captain from Devon","paragraphs":["Picture the southwest corner of England around the year 1690. In Devon and neighbouring Cornwall, men dig down into the hills for tin and copper. Further north, in the Midlands and around Newcastle, others dig for coal. And everywhere the same enemy creeps in: water. It trickles through cracks in the rock, drips from the roof of every tunnel and gathers in black pools at the bottom of every shaft. The deeper the miners go, the faster it comes.","This was the world that Thomas Savery grew up in. He was born in about 1650 at Shilstone, a manor house near the small town of Modbury in Devon. His family were landowners, comfortable but not famous, and young Thomas seems to have had a good education in mathematics and mechanics. Like many clever young men of his day, he went into military engineering, the job of designing forts, bridges, harbours and the machines an army or navy needed. Later he became widely known as Captain Savery.","Savery was a natural tinkerer. Long before steam made him famous, he was playing with ideas. In the 1690s he worked on a clock design and on a way of moving ships in calm weather using paddle wheels turned by men working a capstan, which he described in a little book called Navigation Improv'd in 1698. The navy officials he showed it to were not interested. That disappointment did not stop him. He was the kind of person who believed that a better machine was always waiting to be built, if only someone would try.","Remember the scientists we met in the last part: Torricelli and his tube of mercury, von Guericke and his horses straining at the Magdeburg hemispheres, Papin and his piston pushed down by air. By the 1690s, educated people in London talked about air pressure and vacuums at coffee houses and at meetings of the Royal Society, England's famous club of scientists founded in 1660. They knew that if you fill a closed container with steam and then cool it, the steam shrinks into a few drops of water and leaves an almost empty space behind. They knew that the air outside, pressing down with about 1 kilogram on every square centimetre, would rush to fill that space if it could.","Savery's great insight was not a new law of nature. It was practical. He asked: what if that empty space could be filled not with air, but with water from a flooded mine? And once the water was inside, what if steam itself could shove it out again, higher still? Put those two tricks together, suction and pushing, and you might have a machine that raised water using nothing but fire."]},{"heading":"Raising Water by the Impellent Force of Fire","paragraphs":["On 2 July 1698, Thomas Savery was granted an English patent. A patent is a promise from the government: for a set number of years, only the inventor (or people who pay him) may make and sell the invention. In return, the invention is written down so others can learn from it. Savery's patent described, in the long and curly language of the time, 'a new invention for raising of water and occasioning motion to all sorts of mill work by the impellent force of fire'.","Look closely at those words, because they are bolder than they first seem. 'Impellent' means pushing or driving forward. Savery was claiming the right to use fire to drive things, not just to lift water but to make 'all sorts of mill work' move. In theory you could use his pump to lift water onto a waterwheel, and the waterwheel could then grind corn or turn machinery. So the patent quietly covered a huge area. Almost any way of using fire to make water move could be argued to fall under it. That broad wording would turn out to matter enormously, as we will see at the end of this part.","Savery did not keep his machine secret. According to accounts written at the time, he showed a working model to King William III at Hampton Court Palace, the great royal home beside the River Thames, around the time of the patent. On 14 June 1699 he demonstrated a model to the Royal Society in London. The Society's members, who loved experiments, watched it work and recorded the event in their papers. Their journal, the Philosophical Transactions, later printed a picture and a description of the engine.","For an inventor, this was a big moment. Being seen by a king and by the cleverest men in the country gave Savery's machine a kind of stamp of approval. It also made him a public figure. Newspapers and gossip spread the idea that a Devon captain had found a way to make fire do the work of horses. People called his device a 'fire engine', which back then meant an engine worked by fire, not a machine for putting fires out.","It is worth being fair here. Savery was not the first person to dream of raising water with steam. In Part 3 we met Jeronimo de Ayanz in Spain, Salomon de Caus in France and the Marquess of Worcester in England, who had all described ways of using heated water or steam to lift water. Historians still argue about how much Savery knew of these earlier ideas, especially Worcester's. What Savery did, and what makes him important, is that he built engines, patented them, advertised them and sold them. He turned an idea into a product."]},{"heading":"Inside the Fire Engine","paragraphs":["Let us imagine we are standing beside one of Savery's engines, at the bottom of a mine shaft or in the cellar of a grand house. What do we see? There is no great wheel and no swinging arm. There is no piston sliding in and out. Instead there is a brick furnace with a fire roaring inside it, and on top of the furnace sits a round metal boiler, a strong closed container where water is heated to make steam. Pipes lead from the boiler to one or two large egg-shaped metal vessels, which Savery called receivers. A pipe runs down from the receivers into the water we want to remove, and another pipe runs up from them towards the surface. Between all of these are valves and taps, little gates that can be opened and closed by hand.","Now let us watch it work, one step at a time.","Step one: the operator opens the tap between the boiler and a receiver. Hot steam rushes in and fills the vessel, pushing out the air that was inside. Then he closes the steam tap. The receiver is now full of nothing but steam.","Step two: the operator pours or sprays cold water over the outside of the receiver. The metal cools, and the steam inside cools with it. Steam that cools condenses, which means it turns back into liquid water. Because steam takes up roughly 1,600 to 1,700 times more room than the water it came from, the steam shrinks almost to nothing. A big vessel that was full of steam is suddenly almost empty. It now holds a partial vacuum, a space with very little gas in it.","Step three: the operator opens the valve on the pipe that leads down into the flooded water below. The air pressing down on the surface of that water is now much stronger than the weak pressure inside the receiver, so the air pushes water up the pipe and into the vessel. It looks as if the vessel is sucking the water up, but really the air outside is shoving it in. A one-way valve, called a non-return valve, stops it from falling back.","Step four: the operator closes the lower valve and opens the steam tap again. This time the steam coming from the boiler is at higher pressure than the air outside. It presses down on the surface of the water in the receiver and forces it out through another one-way valve and up the delivery pipe, higher and higher, until it spills out at the top.","Step five: once the vessel is empty of water and full of steam again, the whole cycle starts over. In the improved version Savery described in 1702, there were two receivers side by side. While one was filling with water by suction, the other was being emptied by steam pressure, so water flowed upward in a fairly steady series of gulps.","Think about what the person running this engine had to do. Every single cycle, he had to turn the steam tap, start the cooling water, open the suction valve, close it again and turn the steam back on, in exactly the right order and at the right moment. There was no clockwork and no linkage doing it for him. If he opened the steam tap too early, the vacuum was lost; if he forgot to cool the vessel, no water came up at all. Meanwhile someone had to keep the fire burning evenly and keep the boiler topped up with water, because a boiler allowed to run dry could overheat and be ruined. Running a Savery engine was a skilled, tiring, hour-after-hour job, and it is easy to see why later engineers worked so hard to make engines that opened and closed their own valves.","So the Savery engine used steam in two different ways. First, condensing steam made a vacuum so that air pressure could lift water from below. Second, pressurised steam pushed that water up to a height above. The suction half was clever physics borrowed from the vacuum scientists. The pushing half was the dangerous part, as Savery's customers soon discovered."]},{"heading":"A Little Book Called The Miner's Friend","paragraphs":["Having a patent is one thing. Persuading people to buy your machine is another. In 1702 Savery published a booklet with a catchy title: The Miner's Friend; or, An Engine to Raise Water by Fire. Today we might call it a sales brochure. It contained a description of the engine, a drawing, and a lot of enthusiastic promises.","Savery wrote directly to the 'gentlemen adventurers' who invested money in mines. He told them his engine could drain deep workings that had been abandoned because of water, and so bring new wealth out of old mines. He argued that it would cost less to run than the teams of horses used to work pumps, and that it was simple enough for an ordinary workman to operate after a little training. He even suggested other uses: supplying towns with drinking water, filling ponds and fountains in noble gardens, draining marshes, and putting out fires in big buildings.","He also answered his critics in advance. Some people feared that a boiler full of steam might burst. Savery insisted that his engine was safe if it was properly made and properly handled. Many inventors of his time wrote in this confident style, and Savery was trying to raise money and win customers, so it is no surprise that he made his machine sound as good as possible.","The booklet is interesting to historians for another reason. In it Savery compared the work of his engine with the work of horses. Mine owners understood horses. They knew how many animals it took to keep a pump going and how much it cost to feed them. By describing his engine in terms of the horses it could replace, Savery was doing something that James Watt would later make famous with the unit called horsepower. The idea of measuring a machine against an animal was already in the air.","Imagine being a mine owner in 1702, reading this little book by candlelight. Your mine is losing money because water has drowned the richest seams. Here is a gentleman, approved by the Royal Society and shown to the king, promising a fire-driven servant that never gets tired. You would be tempted to try it. And some owners did."]},{"heading":"Why the Mines Were Disappointed","paragraphs":["Sadly for Savery, his engine ran into three stubborn problems when it went underground.","The first problem was the limit of suction. The suction half of the engine works only because air pressure pushes water up into the empty vessel. As we learned in Part 1, air pressure can only hold up a column of water about 10 metres (about 33 feet) tall, and that is with a perfect vacuum. Savery's condensing vessels never made a perfect vacuum, so in practice the lift by suction was less, around 6 to 9 metres. That meant the engine could not sit comfortably at the top of a mine shaft and suck water up from far below. It had to be placed down inside the mine, no more than a few metres above the water it was pumping. Building a furnace and boiler down a deep, damp shaft, and keeping them supplied with fuel and fresh air, was difficult and risky.","The second problem was the pushing half. To force water up a tall pipe, the steam had to press much harder than the air does. Every extra 10 metres of height needs roughly one extra atmosphere of pressure. A mine that was 50 or 60 metres deep would need steam at several times normal air pressure. The boilers of 1700 were made of hammered copper or iron plates, and the joints between pipes and vessels were often sealed with soft solder, a metal mixture that melts at a fairly low temperature. Hot, high-pressure steam softened the solder and tested every weak seam. At York Buildings, a waterworks beside the Thames in London where a Savery engine was installed, there were reports of steam blowing open joints which had to be soldered again. One clear answer would have been to use several engines stacked one above another, each lifting water part of the way, but that multiplied the cost and the trouble.","There were frightening failures too. In the early 1700s, a scheme to drain a flooded area of coal workings called Broad Waters, near Wednesbury in Staffordshire, ended when the engine was reported to have been torn apart by the force of its own steam. The project was abandoned. Stories like that travelled fast among mine owners, who were already nervous about the idea of a pressurised boiler near their workers.","The third problem was fuel. Every time the vessel was filled with steam, much of that steam was wasted simply heating up the cold metal and the cold water inside. Then the vessel was cooled again, and the next charge of steam had to heat it all up again. The hot steam also touched the water it was pushing, and some of its heat went into warming that water instead of moving it. The result was an engine that burned a great deal of coal for each tonne of water it lifted. At a coal mine that might not matter so much, but at a tin mine in Cornwall, where coal had to be shipped in from Wales, it was expensive.","Put together, these problems meant that the Miner's Friend was not really a friend to most deep miners. Historians are unsure exactly how many Savery engines were ever installed in mines, and there seem to have been few. The dream of a fire engine that could drain any mine was real, but Savery's design was not the machine to do it."]},{"heading":"Fountains, Gardens and Waterworks","paragraphs":["If the story stopped there, Savery might be a footnote. But his engine found a happier home somewhere else: above ground, in places where water only needed to be lifted a modest height.","Think about a wealthy family living in a large country house around 1710. They want water for the kitchens and the laundry, for the stables, for a bathhouse perhaps, and above all for their garden, where fashionable owners loved fountains, pools and cascades. Carrying water in buckets took lots of servants. A Savery engine could sit beside a well or a stream and lift water into a tank on the roof or at the top of a garden, and from there it could flow down through pipes wherever it was needed. Because the lift was only a few metres, the steam did not need dangerously high pressure.","Records show Savery-type engines doing just this. One was used in the water supply at Hampton Court, the palace where the king was said to have seen his model. Another, at Campden House in Kensington, west London, reportedly kept working for about 18 years. Others supplied water to houses and estates in different parts of England. For jobs like these, the engine had real advantages: it had very few moving parts, nothing that swung or slid, and it could be worked by a servant who had been shown which taps to turn and in what order.","The design also kept being improved by others. In the years after Savery, engineers, including, according to some accounts, the scientist and lecturer John Theophilus Desaguliers, built versions that sprayed cold water inside the vessel instead of pouring it over the outside, which condensed the steam much faster. Later in the 1700s, some engineers used Savery-style pumps to lift water back up onto a waterwheel, so that the wheel could keep turning factory machinery even when the river was low. In this roundabout way, Savery's original promise of 'occasioning motion to all sorts of mill work' actually came true, at least in a small way.","So Savery's machine was not a failure. It was the first steam-powered device that was built, sold and used for real work by paying customers, and that is a genuine milestone. It was simply not strong enough or efficient enough for the giant job that most needed doing: emptying the deep mines that fed Britain's hunger for coal and metal."]},{"heading":"A Patent That Lasted 35 Years","paragraphs":["Now we come back to that bold wording in Savery's patent, because it shaped the next chapter of our story.","A normal English patent at the time lasted 14 years. Savery's would have run out in 1712. But in 1699, just a year after it was granted, Parliament passed a special law, often called the Fire Engine Act, that extended his patent by another 21 years. This meant that Savery, and the people who later bought shares in his rights, controlled the use of fire to raise water until 1733. That is 35 years in total, a very long time in an age when new ideas were starting to appear quickly.","Why did Parliament do this? Savery had friends in high places, and he argued that his invention was so important and had cost him so much to develop that he deserved a long reward. Lawmakers may also have hoped that a strong reward would encourage him to keep improving the engine. Whatever the reasons, the extension was unusual and very valuable.","Because the patent covered raising water 'by the impellent force of fire' in general terms, it was hard to build any steam pump in England without stepping on it. If somebody else invented a completely different steam engine that worked in a new way, they could still find that, legally, they had to share their profits with Savery. Some historians think this held back other inventors for years. Others point out that patents also gave inventors a reason to try in the first place, and that the people involved often reached business deals rather than fighting. Both views have some truth, and you will see the same argument come up again when we reach James Watt's patent later in the story.","Thomas Savery himself did not live to see how it all turned out. He died in London in May 1715, aged about 65. By then, a very different kind of fire engine was already lifting water out of a coal mine in the West Midlands. It was larger, slower, and in many ways cleverer than his. And because of that long patent, its inventor would have to work under Savery's name.","That inventor came from Savery's own county. About 20 kilometres east of Modbury lies the busy harbour town of Dartmouth, where an ironmonger who sold tools to the tin miners of Devon and Cornwall had seen their flooded workings up close. He had a different plan. Instead of letting steam push water directly, he would use a piston moving inside a cylinder and a giant wooden beam rocking like a seesaw. His name was Thomas Newcomen."]}],"deepDive":"Engineers judge a pump by how much water it lifts, how high, for how much fuel, and how safely. Savery's engine scored poorly on three of these for deep mining. The physics explains why. A suction lift depends on the difference between atmospheric pressure (about 101 kilopascals at sea level) and the pressure inside the vessel. Even a perfect vacuum can only hold up about 10.3 metres of water, and because Savery's condensing vessels contained leftover air and warm vapour, the real lift was smaller. The pressure lift had no such ceiling in theory, but each 10 metres of extra height required roughly one more atmosphere of steam pressure, so a 60-metre lift would have meant steam at perhaps six or seven atmospheres, far beyond what soldered copper vessels of 1700 could hold reliably. Later engineers who reached such pressures, such as Richard Trevithick a century later, had much better iron, rivets and machine tools. The fuel problem is about heat transfer. In a Savery engine, hot steam came into direct contact with cold metal and cold water at every cycle, so a large share of its latent heat was lost in reheating the vessel and warming the pumped water rather than doing work. Modern estimates of its efficiency are tiny, well under 1 percent, though exact figures vary because we have few reliable measurements. Historians also debate Savery's originality. The Marquess of Worcester had described a steam 'water-commanding engine' in 1663, and some writers have accused Savery of copying it, while others note that Savery's arrangement of condensing vessels and non-return valves goes beyond what Worcester published. Then there is the patent question. The 1699 Act that extended protection to 1733 is often cited in debates about whether strong patents speed up or slow down innovation. Newcomen's engine, which worked on a quite different principle, still had to be licensed under Savery's patent, and after Savery's death the rights were managed by a group of investors called the Proprietors of the Invention for Raising Water by Fire. For Newcomen this meant less money and less fame than his engine deserved, but the arrangement also gave his engine a ready business structure to be sold through.","funFact":"Back in Savery's time, the words 'fire engine' meant an engine worked by fire, so a steam pump draining a mine and a hand pump for putting out house fires could both be called fire engines.","check":{"q":"How did Savery's engine lift water up from below into its vessel?","a":"Steam in the vessel was condensed by cooling, leaving a partial vacuum, and air pressure pushed the water up into it","wrong":["A piston slid up inside a cylinder and pulled the water along","A large wooden beam rocked up and down to work a pump rod","Horses walked in a circle to turn the steam valves"]},"sources":[{"title":"Thomas Savery","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Savery"},{"title":"Newcomen atmospheric engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newcomen_atmospheric_engine"},{"title":"Thomas Newcomen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Newcomen"},{"title":"John Theophilus Desaguliers","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Theophilus_Desaguliers"},{"title":"Horsepower","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horsepower"},{"title":"Thomas Savery","publisher":"Grace's Guide to British Industrial History","url":"https://www.gracesguide.co.uk/Thomas_Savery"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/05-1.jpg","alt":"Savery's engine 'for raising water by the help of fire', printed in 1699: furnace, boiler, two vessels and pipes.","file":"The miner's friend, a pump using steam to create a vacuum to draw water from flooded mines; known as \"Mr. Savery's engine for raising water by the help of fire\" LCCN2006690468.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_miner%27s_friend,_a_pump_using_steam_to_create_a_vacuum_to_draw_water_from_flooded_mines;_known_as_%22Mr._Savery%27s_engine_for_raising_water_by_the_help_of_fire%22_LCCN2006690468.jpg","author":"Savery, Thomas, 1650?-1715.","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/05-2.jpg","alt":"Diagram of Savery's pump: boilers C and D send steam to vessels A and B; valves V1 to V4 steer the water.","file":"Lantern Slide - Thomas Savery's Mine Dewatering Pumping Engine Diagram, circa 1698.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lantern_Slide_-_Thomas_Savery%27s_Mine_Dewatering_Pumping_Engine_Diagram,_circa_1698.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/05-3.jpg","alt":"Hampton Court Palace seen across the River Thames.","file":"Hampton Court Palace, across the Thames - geograph.org.uk - 4877473.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hampton_Court_Palace,_across_the_Thames_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4877473.jpg","author":"Christopher Hilton","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3754},{"n":6,"title":"Newcomen's Nodding Giant","when":"1712 – 1770s","summary":"Thomas Newcomen sold tools to miners and watched their mines fill with water. In 1712 he built a giant engine with a wooden beam that nodded up and down like a seesaw, slowly pumping water out of a coal mine. It was the first steam engine with a piston that really worked, and soon hundreds of these nodding giants were working all over Britain and Europe.","sections":[{"heading":"The Ironmonger of Dartmouth","paragraphs":["Dartmouth is a steep little harbour town on the south coast of Devon, where the River Dart meets the sea. Around the year 1700 its quays were busy with fishing boats and trading ships, and its narrow streets were full of craftsmen's shops. In one of them worked a man named Thomas Newcomen.","Newcomen was born in Dartmouth in February 1664. He became an ironmonger, but that word meant more than it does today. His business did not just sell nails and pots; it designed, made and sold iron tools and equipment, including gear for the tin and copper mines of Devon and Cornwall. That meant Newcomen often travelled to the mines and talked with the men who ran them. Again and again he would have heard the same complaint: the water. Mines were being abandoned not because the metal had run out, but because the pumps could not keep up.","Newcomen was also a deeply religious man. He was a Baptist, part of a Christian group that worshipped outside the official Church of England, and he became a lay preacher and later a leader of the local Baptist congregation. Through his church he knew other craftsmen, and one of them became his partner: John Calley, sometimes spelled Cawley, who worked as a plumber and glazier, which meant he worked with lead pipes and with glass. Lead-working skills turned out to be very useful when you are trying to build something that must not leak.","We do not have Newcomen's notebooks or letters describing exactly how he came up with his engine, and that is one of the frustrating gaps in this story. What we do know is that he and Calley spent years experimenting, probably about ten years, before they had a machine they trusted. People have long wondered whether Newcomen knew about Denis Papin's idea of a piston pushed down by air after steam condensed, which we met in Part 4, or about Savery's engine just a short ride along the coast. It seems likely that he knew something of both, since these ideas were discussed in print and Savery's engine was famous. But there is no proof of exactly what he read or who he met, so it is fairest to say that Newcomen combined ideas that were in the air with a great deal of hands-on trial and error.","His key decision was to avoid Savery's biggest weakness. Savery's engine used steam to push water directly, which demanded dangerous pressures. Newcomen would never ask steam to push hard at all. Instead, he would let steam make a vacuum and let the ordinary pressure of the air do the heavy work, and he would pass that work to the pump through a piston and a rocking beam."]},{"heading":"The Engine Near Dudley Castle","paragraphs":["The first Newcomen engine that we know for certain worked successfully was built in 1712 at the Coneygree (or Conygree) Coalworks, near the town of Tipton in the area now called the Black Country, in the West Midlands of England. It stood not far from the ruins of Dudley Castle, and it is often called the Dudley Castle engine. Its job was to pump water out of a coal mine.","We know what it looked like thanks to a wonderful engraving made in 1719 by a man named Thomas Barney, with the title The Steam Engine near Dudley Castle. It shows a tall brick engine house, with a huge wooden beam sticking out of the top of one wall. Inside, on the ground floor, sits a boiler over a fire. Above the boiler stands an upright cylinder, open at the top, with a piston inside. From one end of the beam hang chains attached to the piston; from the other end, outside the building, hang long rods that drop straight down the mine shaft to the pumps below.","Imagine walking up to this building in 1712. You would hear it before you saw it: a slow sigh and hiss, a gurgle, then a creak and a deep thump as the giant beam tipped. About twelve times a minute, roughly once every five seconds, the beam rocked. On the outside end, the pump rods rose and fell, and with each stroke a gush of water poured out of a pipe at the top of the shaft and ran away in a channel. Day and night the engine kept going, as long as the stoker kept the fire burning.","People who saw it were amazed. The engine did steadily the work that had needed teams of horses, and it did not get tired, hungry or ill. According to later accounts it lifted roughly ten gallons of water with each stroke, which would add up to hundreds of tonnes of water a day. At a coal mine, the fuel to feed it lay right there underground, so it could literally burn the coal it was helping to dig.","A full-size working replica of this engine stands today at the Black Country Living Museum in Dudley, close to where the original once worked. Visitors can watch its beam nod and hear it breathe, just as the miners of 1712 did."]},{"heading":"How the Nodding Giant Works","paragraphs":["The Newcomen engine is called an atmospheric engine. That name is the key to understanding it: the atmosphere, the air all around us, does the pushing, not the steam. Let us go through one complete stroke, step by step.","Start with the beam. Picture a giant seesaw, made from a tree trunk of solid oak, resting on a pivot on top of the engine-house wall. On the inside end, a chain hangs down to the piston, a tight-fitting disc that can slide up and down inside an upright iron or brass cylinder. On the outside end, chains hang down to the heavy pump rods that reach all the way down the mine shaft. Those rods are heavier than the piston, so when nothing else is happening, the outside end of the beam sinks down and the inside end lifts the piston to the top of the cylinder.","Step one: steam fills the cylinder. As the heavy pump rods sink and the piston rises, a valve opens between the boiler and the bottom of the cylinder. Steam from the boiler flows in underneath the piston. This steam is at very low pressure, only a tiny bit above the pressure of the air, perhaps 1 or 2 pounds per square inch more. So the steam is not shoving the piston up. The weight of the pump rods is doing the lifting; the steam just fills the space and keeps the air out.","Step two: the cold-water spray. When the piston reaches the top, the steam valve closes. Then a second valve opens and a jet of cold water squirts up into the cylinder from a tank placed high above it. The cold spray hits the steam, and the steam instantly condenses into a few spoonfuls of water. The cylinder, which was full of steam a moment ago, is now nearly empty, a partial vacuum.","Step three: the air pushes. The top of the cylinder is open to the air. Air at sea level presses with about 1 kilogram on every square centimetre. Above the piston there is the full push of the atmosphere; below it there is almost nothing. So the air drives the piston down with tremendous force. In a cylinder about half a metre across, that adds up to well over a tonne of push. The piston plunges down, the inside end of the beam is dragged down, and the outside end swings up, lifting the pump rods and hauling a load of water up the shaft.","Step four: start again. At the bottom of the stroke, the water from the spray and the condensed steam drain out through a pipe. The steam valve opens once more, the pump rods sink again under their own weight, the piston rises, and the cycle repeats.","Notice what is clever here. The dangerous part of Savery's engine, high-pressure steam, has disappeared. Newcomen's boiler could be a simple copper or iron vessel like a giant kettle with a domed lid, because it never had to hold much pressure. The force comes from the sky itself, from the weight of all the air above. That is why the glossary for this story says an atmospheric engine is one where air pressure, not steam pressure, pushes the piston. A boiler leak might scald someone, but it would not usually blow the engine house apart.","There was also a little valve with a funny name: the snifting valve. Air dissolved in the water could build up inside the cylinder and spoil the vacuum. The snifting valve let this air escape with a sniffing, snorting sound at each stroke, which is said to be how it got its name. Engineers have always enjoyed giving machines nicknames, and the Newcomen engine collected plenty, including the one in this part's title: the nodding giant."]},{"heading":"A Lucky Leak and a Boy with String","paragraphs":["The cold-water spray inside the cylinder was one of Newcomen's most important improvements. In Savery's engine, and probably in Newcomen's own early trials, the steam was cooled from outside, by pouring water over the vessel or by surrounding the cylinder with a jacket of cold water. That works, but slowly, because the heat has to soak through thick metal. Squirting cold water straight into the steam condensed it almost instantly, which made each stroke faster and the vacuum stronger.","There is a famous story about how Newcomen discovered this. According to the tale, his early engine had a cylinder surrounded by cold water, and one day the piston suddenly crashed down with such force that it broke the chain or damaged the engine. When the builders investigated, they found a tiny hole in the cylinder wall, perhaps a flaw that had been patched with solder, through which a jet of cold water had squirted inside. The leak, so the story goes, showed them that cold water inside the cylinder worked far better than cold water outside.","Is it true? Nobody can be sure. The story was written down years later, and it is the kind of neat, lucky-accident tale that people love to tell about inventions. It might be based on a real event, or it might be a simple way of explaining a discovery that really came from patient experiment. So it is best to treat it as a traditional story rather than proven history. Either way, the internal spray became a standard part of every Newcomen engine.","The next problem was the valves. In the earliest engines, a person had to open and close the steam valve and the injection valve by hand at exactly the right moment in every stroke, about twelve times a minute, hour after hour. That was tiring, boring and easy to get wrong. If the spray came too early or the steam valve stayed open too long, the engine would stumble.","This is where another famous story comes in. It is said that a boy named Humphrey Potter, whose job was to work the valves, grew tired of his task and wanted to go off and play. So he tied strings from the valve handles to the moving beam, so that the beam itself would open and close the valves at the right moments. The engine ran on its own, and the boy got his break. It is a delightful story, and it has been repeated in books for nearly 300 years.","But it is a legend, and historians are very doubtful about it. The real solution was a carefully designed system called valve gear, and in the Newcomen engine the main part was the plug tree (or plug rod), a wooden rod hanging from the beam with pegs fixed along it. As the beam moved up and down, the plug tree moved with it, and its pegs knocked against levers and catches that opened and closed the valves at the right points in the stroke. Evidence suggests that this kind of automatic valve gear was already in use within a few years of 1712, and it was the work of engineers refining the machine, not a single bored boy. Still, the legend reminds us of something true: an engine that runs itself is far more useful than one that needs a person standing over it every second.","With automatic valves, the Newcomen engine became what engineers call self-acting. A small team, a stoker to feed the fire and an engineman to watch over everything, could keep a huge machine pumping day and night. That was a giant step towards the factory machines of the century to come."]},{"heading":"Working in Savery's Shadow","paragraphs":["Newcomen's engine worked in a completely different way from Savery's. It had a piston, a cylinder and a beam, none of which Savery's engine had. You might think Newcomen could simply patent it and collect the rewards. But remember Savery's patent from Part 5. It covered raising water by the 'impellent force of fire' in very broad terms, and Parliament had extended it until 1733. Newcomen's engine raised water using fire, so legally it fell inside Savery's rights.","As a result, Newcomen had to work under Savery's patent. The two men seem to have reached an agreement while Savery was still alive, and after Savery died in 1715 the rights passed to a group of investors called the Proprietors of the Invention for Raising Water by Fire. This group, run by a London secretary and treasurer named John Meres, licensed the building of engines and collected payments. Mine owners who wanted an engine often paid large yearly sums to the Proprietors. Some of this money reached Newcomen, but historians think he received far less money and recognition than his invention deserved.","In fact, for many years Newcomen's name was barely attached to his own machine. People simply called it the fire engine, or sometimes linked it with Savery. Only later did historians fully recognise that Newcomen had built the first practical piston steam engine, a machine whose basic layout of cylinder, piston and condensing steam would lead straight on to James Watt.","Newcomen died in London on 5 August 1729, at the house of a Baptist friend, and was buried at Bunhill Fields, a burial ground used by people from churches outside the Church of England. The exact spot of his grave is unknown. He died four years before Savery's patent finally ran out. Today, the Newcomen Society, a society for the history of engineering and technology founded in Britain in 1920, is named in his honour."]},{"heading":"Nodding Giants Everywhere","paragraphs":["Once people saw what the Dudley Castle engine could do, orders followed. Newcomen engines were built at coal mines in the Midlands, around Newcastle upon Tyne and in other coalfields, and at the tin and copper mines of Cornwall. By the time Savery's patent ran out in 1733, roughly 100 Newcomen engines, perhaps around 125, had been built in Britain and across Europe. After that, anyone could build one without paying the Proprietors, and the numbers grew much faster. Over the whole 1700s, hundreds and hundreds were built.","The engine also travelled abroad, carried by English engineers and by foreign visitors who came to study it. In the early 1720s an English engineer named Isaac Potter is said to have built one in the mining region of Upper Hungary, at a town the Germans called Königsberg (today Nová Baňa in Slovakia). In France, an engine was set up at Passy, near Paris, in 1726 to pump water for the city. In Sweden, the engineer Mårten Triewald, who had worked in England and seen the engines there, built one at the Dannemora iron mines after returning home in 1726. Engines also appeared in Belgium, Spain, the Netherlands and other countries. It was the first time a steam-powered machine became an international technology, copied and adapted from country to country.","Why did it spread so widely when it was so wasteful? A Newcomen engine was terribly inefficient by modern standards. Each stroke, the cold spray chilled the whole cylinder, and then the next charge of steam had to heat it all up again before it could fill the space. Most of the fuel's energy went into that endless heating and cooling. Historians estimate that the engines turned only around 1 percent of the energy in their coal into useful work, or even less. That is like buying a hundred sweets and getting to eat only one.","But at a coal mine, that did not matter much. The coal that could not be sold, the small broken pieces called slack, could be shovelled into the engine's firebox for almost nothing. What mattered to the mine owner was that the engine kept the water down, so the miners could keep digging. In Cornwall, where coal had to be brought by ship, the fuel bill hurt much more, which is why Cornish mine owners would later be so eager to buy James Watt's more efficient engines.","Engines also got bigger. Builders learned to cast larger cylinders, first in brass and later in cheaper iron, and to build longer beams and stronger pumps. By the 1770s, the largest cylinders were about 1.8 metres (72 inches) across. One engine nicknamed Fairbottom Bobs, built for a colliery near Ashton-under-Lyne in about 1760, worked for many decades and is now preserved at the Henry Ford Museum in Dearborn, Michigan, in the United States."]},{"heading":"Smeaton Takes Measurements","paragraphs":["For about sixty years, Newcomen engines were built mostly by rule of thumb. Engine builders copied what had worked before and guessed at the rest. That changed with John Smeaton, a Yorkshireman born near Leeds in 1724, who is often called the father of civil engineering in Britain. He built bridges, harbours, canals and the famous Eddystone Lighthouse off Plymouth, and he loved to test things scientifically.","In the late 1760s and early 1770s, Smeaton studied Newcomen engines carefully. He measured how much coal they burned and how much water they lifted, and he built a small experimental engine at his home so that he could try out different proportions, such as the size of the cylinder, the length of the stroke and the amount of injection water. He found that many engines were badly made and badly adjusted, and that just by getting the details right, the same basic design could do far more work with the same coal.","Engineers measured an engine's performance with a number called its duty: how many pounds of water it could lift one foot high by burning a set amount of coal, usually a bushel. Smeaton's improved engines roughly doubled the duty of typical older engines. In 1775 he built a large engine with a 72-inch cylinder at Chacewater in Cornwall, described as one of the most powerful of its time. His work showed something that would become a theme of the whole steam story: careful measurement can be as important as a brilliant idea.","Yet even Smeaton's best engines still had the same basic flaw. They still cooled their cylinders with cold water every stroke and then reheated them. Smeaton could make the Newcomen engine as good as it could be, but he could not escape its design. That needed a completely new idea.","Newcomen-style engines did not vanish quickly, though. In coalfields, where cheap fuel made efficiency less important and the simple design was easy to repair, they kept being built for decades. One was put up in 1795 at Elsecar, near Barnsley in South Yorkshire, for Earl Fitzwilliam's colliery. It kept pumping water until 1923, more than 120 years, when electric pumps replaced it. The Elsecar engine still stands in the very place where it was built, the oldest steam engine anywhere still on its original site, and it has been restored so that visitors can watch its beam move again."]},{"heading":"A Broken Model in Glasgow","paragraphs":["Let us end by travelling north, to Scotland, in the early 1760s. The University of Glasgow owns a small working model of a Newcomen engine, used to show students how the famous fire engine works. But the model does not work very well. It runs for a few strokes and then stops, out of steam. The professor of natural philosophy sends it to London for repair, but it comes back no better.","So the model is handed to the university's own instrument maker, a quiet, careful young man in his twenties who makes and mends scientific instruments in a small workshop on the university grounds. He has never built a full-size engine. But he is curious, he is good with his hands, and he is the kind of person who will not leave a puzzle alone. Why does the model keep running out of steam? Where is all the heat going?","His name is James Watt, and the answer he finds will change the world."]}],"deepDive":"The Newcomen engine is a perfect case study in the difference between force and efficiency. Its force came from atmospheric pressure: about 101 kilopascals, or roughly 10 tonnes per square metre. The working replica of the 1712 engine has a cylinder about 52 cm (roughly 21 inches) across, with an area of about 0.21 square metres, so a perfect vacuum would give a push of over 2 tonnes; real engines achieved perhaps two-thirds of that because the vacuum was never complete. Engine builders kept the piston speed and stroke modest, and most early engines delivered only a few horsepower, around 5 is one estimate for the 1712 engine, though such numbers are estimates since horsepower had not yet been defined. Efficiency was the weak spot. Each cycle, the injection water cooled the cylinder walls to well below boiling, and the next charge of steam had to reheat that metal before the cylinder could fill. Much of the steam simply condensed on the walls without doing anything. Overall thermal efficiencies of around 0.5 to 1 percent are commonly cited. John Smeaton's careful tests in the 1760s and 1770s introduced the idea of measuring 'duty', and he raised typical figures from around 5 or 6 million to roughly 9 million or more foot-pounds per bushel of coal; Watt's engines would later more than double that again. Historians debate several points. One is how much Newcomen owed to Papin and Savery: the piston-and-cylinder vacuum principle was published by Papin in 1690, but Papin never built a working full-size engine, and Newcomen's practical solutions, from the internal spray to the valve gear and the large-scale beam, were his own. Another is the economic impact of Savery's patent: it diverted money from Newcomen, but the Proprietors also created an organised way to license and spread engines. Economic historians also stress that the engine's importance was not just technical. By letting mines go deeper, it helped Britain's coal output rise, which fed ironworks and later more engines, the beginning of the coal, iron and steam loop described later in this story. Finally, the long survival of engines like Elsecar, working until 1923, shows that 'old' technology often stays in use for generations where its strengths, such as simplicity and cheap fuel, still matter.","funFact":"The Elsecar Newcomen engine near Barnsley was built in 1795 and kept pumping water out of a coal mine until 1923, so it was still working in the age of aeroplanes and radio.","check":{"q":"In a Newcomen engine, what actually pushed the piston down?","a":"The pressure of the air (the atmosphere), after the steam under the piston was condensed","wrong":["High-pressure steam from the boiler","The weight of the pump rods in the mine","A spring hidden inside the cylinder"]},"sources":[{"title":"Newcomen atmospheric engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Newcomen_atmospheric_engine"},{"title":"Thomas Newcomen","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Newcomen"},{"title":"Thomas Savery","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thomas_Savery"},{"title":"Elsecar Heritage Centre","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Elsecar_Heritage_Centre"},{"title":"John Smeaton","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Smeaton"},{"title":"Black Country Living Museum","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Country_Living_Museum"},{"title":"Fairbottom Bobs","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fairbottom_Bobs"},{"title":"Mårten Triewald","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M%C3%A5rten_Triewald"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/06-1.jpg","alt":"A full-size working replica of the 1712 Dudley Castle Newcomen engine, with its brick engine house and pump frame, at the Black Country Living Museum.","file":"Newcomen Engine Replica at Black Country Museum 2012.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Newcomen_Engine_Replica_at_Black_Country_Museum_2012.jpg","author":"IMechE1","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/06-2.jpg","alt":"A cutaway drawing of a Newcomen atmospheric pumping engine: round boiler below, cylinder above it, and the big rocking beam on top.","file":"Lantern Slide - Tangyes Ltd, Diagram,Newcomen's Atmospheric Steam Pumping Engine, circa 1910.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lantern_Slide_-_Tangyes_Ltd,_Diagram,Newcomen%27s_Atmospheric_Steam_Pumping_Engine,_circa_1910.jpg","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/06-3.jpg","alt":"The Elsecar Newcomen engine house near Barnsley, still standing on its original colliery site, with the engine's beam sticking out the top.","file":"Elsecar Newcomen engine - geograph.org.uk - 3144326.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Elsecar_Newcomen_engine_-_geograph.org.uk_-_3144326.jpg","author":"Ashley Dace","license":"CC BY-SA 2.0"}],"words":3839},{"n":7,"title":"Watt's Sunday Walk","when":"1763 – 1769","summary":"A young Scottish instrument maker was asked to fix a tiny model engine that kept stopping. He measured everything and discovered the engine was wasting most of its steam heating and cooling itself. On a Sunday walk he had a brilliant idea: cool the steam in a separate box so the cylinder could stay hot!","sections":[{"heading":"A Quiet Boy from Greenock","paragraphs":["Picture a busy harbour town on the River Clyde in the west of Scotland in the 1740s. Tall-masted ships creak at the quays, ropes slap against wood, and the air smells of tar, salt and fresh-cut timber. This is Greenock, and in a house not far from the water lives a small, thin boy who often has headaches and is frequently kept home from school because he is unwell. His name is James Watt, and he was born here on 19 January 1736.","James's father, also called James Watt, was a shipwright, which means a builder and repairer of ships. He also traded goods, owned shares in ships, and helped run the town as one of its officials. His workshop was full of tools, wood, metal fittings and the delicate navigation instruments that sailors relied on to find their way across the ocean. His mother, Agnes Muirhead, came from a well-educated family and taught him a great deal at home. Because young James was so often ill, much of his early learning happened at the kitchen table and in his father's workshop rather than in a classroom.","That workshop turned out to be a perfect school. James was given his own small set of tools and a little forge, and he took things apart and put them back together: models, cranes, pulleys, barrel organs. Family stories say he was quiet, patient and unusually good at mathematics once he got to grammar school. He liked to understand exactly why things worked, not just that they worked. Hold on to that habit, because it is the real hero of this chapter.","Life was not easy for the Watt family. Several of James's brothers and sisters died young, which was sadly common in the 1700s, and his father's business ran into money trouble when some trading ventures failed. When James was still a teenager, his mother died. A teenager who was good with his hands needed a trade, and James decided on one that matched his talents perfectly: he would become a maker of mathematical instruments.","What was a mathematical instrument maker? Today we might call it a precision engineer. These craftspeople built quadrants and sextants for navigation, compasses, surveying tools, drawing instruments, scales, rulers marked with tiny accurate divisions, and scientific apparatus for universities. The work needed steady hands, sharp eyes, careful measuring and a deep understanding of geometry. All of those skills would matter enormously when Watt later faced a giant, clumsy, wasteful machine and asked how to make it better."]},{"heading":"London Lessons and a Workshop in a University","paragraphs":["In 1755, aged 19, Watt travelled to London, a long and tiring journey that took more than a week on horseback. London was the biggest city in Europe, with hundreds of thousands of people, and it was the best place in Britain to learn the instrument-making trade. The problem was that the craft was controlled by guilds, organisations of master craftsmen who normally expected a young person to serve a long apprenticeship of about seven years before working independently.","Watt did not have seven years, or much money. After some searching, he found a master, an instrument maker named John Morgan in Cornhill, who agreed to teach him for about a year in return for a fee and his labour. Watt worked extremely long days, often from early morning until late at night, learning to cut brass, engrave fine scales and build complicated instruments. He lived very cheaply and kept out of sight as much as possible, partly because in wartime young men on the streets of London could be grabbed by press gangs and forced into the navy. The hard work and cold, cheap lodgings damaged his health, and in 1756 he returned to Scotland, tired but highly skilled.","Back in Scotland he wanted to set up shop in Glasgow, the growing trading city upriver from Greenock. According to the traditional account, Glasgow's own craft guild objected: Watt had not served a full apprenticeship, so in their eyes he was not entitled to open a business in the town (historians still debate how strongly the guild really opposed him). This might have stopped his career right there. Instead, a lucky escape appeared. The University of Glasgow was not under the control of the town guild, and some of its professors needed someone to care for a collection of astronomical instruments that had recently arrived from Jamaica.","In 1757 the university gave Watt a small workshop inside its buildings and allowed him to call himself mathematical instrument maker to the university. He repaired and built instruments, sold drawing tools and spectacles, and even made musical instruments such as flutes and organs, even though he was not especially musical himself. He simply studied the science of sound and worked it out.","Being inside a university turned out to be even more valuable than the workshop itself. Watt's little room became a meeting place for curious minds. Among his friends were the chemist and doctor Joseph Black, the student (and later professor) John Robison, and the philosopher Adam Smith, who would go on to write The Wealth of Nations, a famous book about economics. Imagine a small, cluttered room full of brass tubes, glass, half-finished instruments and lively conversation about heat, chemistry, mathematics and trade. That was the world in which Watt's great idea would grow."]},{"heading":"A Little Broken Engine","paragraphs":["The university owned a small working model of a Newcomen engine, the kind of atmospheric engine you met in Part 6, which pumped water out of mines using a rocking beam, a piston and the push of the air. Professors used the model to show students how a real engine worked. Unfortunately, this one hardly worked at all. It had even been sent away to London to be fixed, and it came back still disappointing.","In the winter of 1763 to 1764, the professor of natural philosophy, John Anderson, asked Watt to repair it. Watt was about 27. The model was small, roughly the size of a tabletop machine: by most accounts its brass cylinder was only about 5 centimetres (2 inches) across, and its piston moved about 15 centimetres (6 inches) each stroke. Everything else was in proportion, with a little boiler, a little beam and little pump rods.","Watt cleaned it, adjusted it, and got it going. But he found that even when properly repaired, the engine would make only a few strokes and then stop. The boiler simply could not make steam fast enough to keep it running, even though the boiler looked big enough compared with the size of the cylinder. Many people would have shrugged, written it off as a toy that does not scale down well, and handed it back. Watt did not. Something about the numbers bothered him.","He started measuring. He worked out how much water the boiler turned into steam each minute, and how much steam it should take to fill the cylinder once. The answer was startling. The engine was using several times more steam per stroke than the volume of the cylinder could possibly hold. Steam was disappearing. Where on earth was it going?","This is a wonderful moment in the history of invention, because it shows a kind of thinking that anyone can learn. Watt did not start with a grand plan to change the world. He started with a small, specific question about a machine on a workbench: why does this little engine stall, and why do the measurements not add up? Following that question carefully, step by step, would lead him to one of the most important improvements in the history of engineering."]},{"heading":"Hot, Cold, Hot, Cold: Where the Fuel Went","paragraphs":["To find the missing steam, remember how a Newcomen engine works. Steam fills the cylinder. Then a spray of cold water is squirted inside. The steam condenses (turns back into a few drops of water), leaving a near-vacuum, and the air outside pushes the piston down. Then fresh steam comes in for the next stroke.","Now think about the cylinder itself. Every time the cold spray goes in, it does not only cool the steam. It also cools the metal walls of the cylinder and the piston. So when the next lot of steam arrives, the first thing it meets is a chilly cylinder. Much of that new steam immediately condenses on the cold walls, warming them back up, before the cylinder is hot enough to stay full of steam. Only after the cylinder has been reheated can steam fill it properly. Then the spray cools everything again, and the whole wasteful cycle repeats, ten or twelve times every minute, all day and all night.","Watt realised that most of the steam, and therefore most of the coal burned under the boiler, was being used not to work the engine but simply to reheat the cylinder over and over. In his little model the problem was even worse than in a big engine, because a small cylinder has a lot of metal surface compared with the amount of steam inside it. He estimated that around three quarters of the steam was being wasted this way.","He tried the obvious fixes first. He experimented with making cylinders out of materials that do not soak up heat so easily, including wood treated with oil, so that the walls would not need as much reheating. That helped a little but created other problems. He also tried using less cold water in the spray, so that the cylinder did not cool down so much. But then the steam did not condense completely, the vacuum was weak, and the piston was pushed down with much less force.","Here was the puzzle in its sharpest form. For a strong vacuum, the place where steam condenses must be cold. For no wasted fuel, the cylinder must stay hot. One part of the machine seemed to need to be hot and cold at the same time. Every engineer who had built Newcomen engines for the past fifty years had lived with this contradiction. Watt was the one who decided it must be possible to escape from it."]},{"heading":"Joseph Black and the Hidden Heat","paragraphs":["While Watt puzzled over his engine, he had an expert friend just down the corridor. Joseph Black, born in 1728, was a doctor and chemist who taught at the University of Glasgow. He is famous in chemistry for studying the gas he called fixed air, which we now call carbon dioxide. But it was Black's work on heat that mattered most to Watt.","Around 1761, Black noticed something odd. If you heat a block of ice that is sitting at its melting point, its temperature does not go up while it melts. All the heat you pour in goes into turning solid ice into liquid water. The same thing happens when water boils: you can keep heating boiling water, but its temperature stays at the boiling point while it slowly turns into steam. The heat seems to vanish into the water without showing up on a thermometer. Black called this heat latent, from a Latin word meaning hidden. Latent heat is the hidden heat needed to turn boiling water into steam without making it any hotter.","Black also studied what we now call specific heat: the idea that different substances need different amounts of heat to warm up by the same number of degrees. Together, these ideas helped scientists see that heat and temperature are not the same thing. Temperature is how hot something is; heat is a quantity of energy that can flow, be stored, and even hide.","Watt, meanwhile, was doing experiments of his own. In one of them he led steam from a kettle-like boiler through a tube into a container of cold water and measured how much the water warmed up. He was amazed to find that a small amount of steam could heat about six times its own weight of cold water all the way up to boiling. Steam was carrying an enormous load of heat, far more than its temperature suggested. When he told Black, his friend explained his theory of latent heat, and the surprising result suddenly made sense.","Watt also measured how much bigger steam is than the water it comes from, getting, by one account, a figure of roughly 1,800 times by volume, close to the modern value of about 1,700 times at normal air pressure. With these numbers he could calculate how much fuel an engine ought to need, and compare it with how much it actually used. The gap was huge. Black's science gave Watt a way to understand exactly why the Newcomen engine was so hungry: every time the cold spray condensed steam inside the cylinder, all that hidden heat was thrown away, and then had to be put back in again by burning more coal.","Black did more than share ideas. He became a loyal supporter of Watt's engine research and lent him money for his experiments in the early years. Historians still discuss exactly how much Black's theory shaped Watt's breakthrough; Watt himself said he had noticed the effect in his own experiments and that Black then explained it to him. Either way, it is a lovely example of a scientist and a craftsman learning from each other."]},{"heading":"The Sunday Walk on Glasgow Green","paragraphs":["Now comes the famous moment. On a fine Sunday afternoon in May 1765, Watt went for a walk across Glasgow Green, a large open park beside the River Clyde where townspeople strolled, washed and dried their linen, and grazed animals. In strict Scotland of the 1700s, Sunday was a day of rest, so he could not go to his workshop. Instead his mind kept turning over the problem of the hot-and-cold cylinder.","As he walked, an idea arrived. Steam is elastic, he reasoned: like any gas, it will rush into an empty space. So if there were a separate vessel, joined to the cylinder by a pipe and emptied of air, steam from the cylinder would rush into it. If that separate vessel were kept cold, the steam would condense there, creating a vacuum. And that vacuum would pull more steam out of the cylinder, emptying the cylinder too. The cylinder itself never needed to be cooled at all.","In other words: do the condensing somewhere else. Keep the cylinder hot all the time, and keep a second chamber, the condenser, cold all the time. Hot stays hot, cold stays cold, and the contradiction disappears. Watt later said that before he had walked much further, the whole arrangement was clear in his mind.","We know about this walk mainly because Watt described it to friends many years later, and their account was written down after his death. So, as the story outline for this Deep Dive says, it is how Watt later remembered it, and memories can tidy up the past. But unlike the kettle legend, this account comes from Watt himself, and it fits what we know from his notes and models. The date, 1765, is widely accepted.","Watt was eager to test the idea immediately, but he had to wait for Monday. Soon afterwards he built a small experimental model. Stories say he used a large brass syringe as the cylinder, a quick and clever way to try out the principle without making new parts. The model worked. With a separate condenser, a small cylinder could make stroke after stroke with far less steam than before."]},{"heading":"Condenser, Air Pump, Jacket: Building the Better Engine","paragraphs":["A good idea is not the same as a working machine, and Watt soon found there was more to solve. First, the condenser would gradually fill up with the water from condensed steam, plus the cold water used to cool it, plus air that leaked in or came out of the water. Water and air in the condenser would spoil the vacuum. So Watt added an air pump: a small pump, worked by the engine itself, that pulled the warm water and air out of the condenser at every stroke. The warm water could even be sent back to the boiler, saving a little more fuel because it was already partly heated.","Second, in a Newcomen engine the top of the cylinder was open to the air. Cold air resting on top of the piston cooled the cylinder every stroke. Watt closed the top of the cylinder with a cover, letting the piston rod slide through a tight seal. Then, instead of using the weight of the atmosphere to push the piston down, he used low-pressure steam from the boiler to push on top of it, while the condenser created a vacuum underneath. This was a big step: steam now did some of the pushing, although at fairly low pressure.","Third, he wrapped the cylinder in a steam jacket: an outer case around the cylinder, filled with steam, like a warm coat. With hot steam all around it, the cylinder walls stayed close to steam temperature, so less steam condensed on them by mistake. He also tried lagging, or insulating, parts of the engine with materials such as wood to keep the heat in.","Put together, the separate condenser, the air pump, the closed cylinder and the steam jacket made a very different machine from Newcomen's, even though it still looked like a beam engine with a rocking beam and a pump. Fully developed, Watt's engines used roughly a quarter of the coal of a Newcomen engine doing the same work. For a mine owner who had to pay for coal, that was like discovering that three out of every four sacks of fuel had been thrown away for decades.","Building a full-size engine, though, was a struggle. Watt needed money for materials and time. In the late 1760s he found a backer, the Scottish industrialist John Roebuck, who had founded the Carron ironworks and who was struggling with flooded coal mines of his own near Bo'ness. Roebuck paid off Watt's debts, including money owed to Joseph Black, in exchange for a large share of the future invention. Watt built a test engine in a workshop beside Roebuck's home, Kinneil House, near Bo'ness on the Firth of Forth. The ruin of that little workshop still survives.","On 5 January 1769 Watt was granted his famous patent, for, as its title put it, a new method of lessening the consumption of steam and fuel in fire engines. (Fire engine was the old name for a steam engine.) A patent gave him the right, for a limited time, to stop others from using his invention without permission. The idea was protected. But the engine at Kinneil still did not work properly. Its cylinder was not round and smooth enough, and steam hissed out around the edges of the piston, which Watt tried to seal with materials such as cork, oiled rags, felt and even horse dung, with little success."]},{"heading":"The Kettle Legend, and Giving Fair Credit","paragraphs":["You may have heard a different story about James Watt. In it, a young Watt sits by the fire at home, staring at a boiling kettle, and watches the lid jiggle as steam escapes. An aunt scolds him for wasting time, but in that moment, the story says, he realises the power of steam and goes on to invent the steam engine.","It is a charming scene, and it has appeared in countless books and paintings. But it is a legend. There is no good evidence that it happened, and the story seems to have grown up after Watt's death; it may even have been created or embellished by his son, James Watt Junior, to polish his father's legend. The truth is more interesting. Watt did use kettles, but as pieces of scientific apparatus in measured experiments on how much heat steam carried, not as a sudden flash of inspiration from a rattling lid. And his great insight came not as a child, but as a grown man of 29, after months of careful measuring.","The other half of the myth is even more important to correct. James Watt did not invent the steam engine. Steam pumps had been working since Savery's time around 1700, and Newcomen's atmospheric engines had been pumping mines since 1712, more than fifty years before Watt's walk on Glasgow Green. What Watt invented was a way to make the steam engine far more efficient, starting with the separate condenser. Efficiency means how much of the fuel's energy becomes useful work instead of wasted heat, and Watt's changes improved it dramatically.","That might sound like a smaller achievement than inventing the whole thing, but it is not. An idea that cuts fuel use by about three quarters can turn a machine that only makes sense next to a coal mine into one that can be used almost anywhere. Over the next decades, Watt and his partners would keep improving the engine in many other ways, which you will meet in Parts 8 and 9.","So the fair way to tell this story is to say that the steam engine was built by many people over a long time: Papin with his piston idea, Savery with his pump, Newcomen and Calley with the first practical engine, Black with his science of heat, and Watt with his brilliant, patient improvements. Watt's real lesson for young inventors is not to stare at kettles. It is to notice waste, measure carefully, ask why the numbers do not add up, and keep thinking about a problem until the answer arrives, even on a Sunday stroll.","And yet, in 1769, Watt's story seemed stuck. He had a patent, a brilliant idea and a test engine that leaked. He had no money of his own, no proper factory, and no way to make a large cylinder accurate enough to hold its steam. He took jobs surveying routes for canals in Scotland to earn a living, and his engine sat waiting. Then, through friends, he came to know a lively, ambitious businessman in Birmingham who owned one of the most famous factories in Britain, and who believed that Watt's engine could change the world."]}],"deepDive":"Why was the Newcomen engine so wasteful, and how much did Watt really save? Modern numbers make the problem clear. Heating 1 kilogram of water from 0 °C to 100 °C takes about 419 kilojoules of energy, but turning that same kilogram of boiling water into steam takes about 2,260 kilojoules more: the latent heat Joseph Black described. So most of the energy in steam is hidden heat, and in a Newcomen engine it was thrown away every stroke when the cold spray hit the cylinder. Watt's own experiment, in which steam heated about six times its weight of cold water to boiling, matches this well: 2,260 divided by roughly 400 is between five and six. The cylinder walls made it worse. A large cast-iron cylinder weighs tonnes, and cooling and reheating even its inner surface ten or twelve times a minute swallowed steam that never pushed the piston at all. Watt estimated that about three quarters of the steam in his small model was wasted this way. His solution is a beautiful example of separating functions: one part of the machine (the cylinder) stays as hot as the steam, while another (the condenser) stays as cold as possible. The air pump keeps the condenser free of water and air, which would otherwise spoil the vacuum. In thermodynamic terms, Watt reduced the losses that come from heat flowing back and forth inside the engine, decades before Sadi Carnot (Part 16) wrote the theory down. Historians still debate how much Black's science drove Watt's invention. Watt credited Black generously, but he also said he found the key facts through his own measurements and only then learned Black's theory. Many historians now see it as a two-way exchange between university science and craft skill, rather than a simple case of theory being applied. Another debate concerns the Glasgow Green story itself. It reached print through recollections recorded long after 1765, so details like the exact spot may have been polished. But Watt's notebooks and early models confirm the timing broadly. Finally, the efficiency gain: fully developed Watt engines used roughly a quarter of the coal of comparable Newcomen engines. Yet in absolute terms they were still inefficient, turning only a few percent of the coal's energy into useful work. There was plenty of room left for later engineers.","funFact":"Watt's first test of the separate condenser idea is said to have used a large brass syringe as its cylinder, and an early model of his separate condenser is kept today at the Science Museum in London.","check":{"q":"What did Watt's separate condenser allow the main cylinder to do?","a":"Stay hot all the time","wrong":["Run without any water","Burn steam as a fuel","Work without a piston"]},"sources":[{"title":"James Watt","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Watt"},{"title":"Watt steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watt_steam_engine"},{"title":"Joseph Black","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joseph_Black"},{"title":"Matthew Boulton","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthew_Boulton"},{"title":"Kinneil House","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kinneil_House"},{"title":"Replica of model of Newcomen engine","publisher":"Science Museum Group","url":"https://collection.sciencemuseumgroup.org.uk/objects/co8231705/replica-of-model-of-newcomen-engine"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/07-1.jpg","alt":"James Watt, painted by Carl Frederik von Breda in 1792, resting his head on his hand beside an engine drawing.","file":"Watt James von Breda.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Watt_James_von_Breda.jpg","author":"Carl Frederik von Breda","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/07-2.jpg","alt":"A labelled diagram of Watt's pumping engine, showing the separate condenser and vacuum (air) pump next to the steam-jacketed cylinder.","file":"Watt steam pumping engine.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Watt_steam_pumping_engine.JPG","author":"Robert H. Thurston","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/07-3.jpg","alt":"The roofless stone ruin of James Watt's cottage workshop in the woods behind Kinneil House, Bo'ness, Scotland.","file":"James Watt Cottage ruin, Kinneil House, Bo'ness.JPG","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Watt_Cottage_ruin,_Kinneil_House,_Bo%27ness.JPG","author":"Kim Traynor","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4033},{"n":8,"title":"Boulton & Watt: Selling Power","when":"1769 – 1800","summary":"Watt had a great idea but no money, until cheerful Birmingham factory owner Matthew Boulton became his partner. An ironmaster named John Wilkinson made cylinders so round that the steam stopped leaking. Soon their engines were pumping deep mines, and customers paid them a share of all the coal they saved!","sections":[{"heading":"A Backer Who Ran Out of Luck","paragraphs":["At the end of Part 7 we left James Watt with a patent from 1769, a leaky test engine at Kinneil House in Scotland, and empty pockets. His first backer was John Roebuck, born in Sheffield in 1718, a doctor-turned-industrialist full of big plans. Roebuck had helped start the Carron ironworks near Falkirk in 1759, which became famous for making cast-iron goods and, later, short cannons called carronades. He also leased coal mines and salt works around Bo'ness on the Firth of Forth.","Roebuck's mines had the same problem as so many others in this story: they kept flooding. A pump that used a quarter of the coal of a Newcomen engine sounded like exactly what he needed. In return for paying off Watt's debts and funding experiments, Roebuck took two thirds of the rights to Watt's invention. Watt worked on the engine in a small building behind Kinneil House, Roebuck's home, often travelling back and forth between there and Glasgow.","But the experimental engine would not behave. Its cylinder, made by ordinary methods of the time, was not perfectly round or smooth, so steam leaked past the piston. Watt also had to earn a living, so for several years he spent much of his time surveying routes for canals and harbours in Scotland, tramping through wet fields with measuring chains and instruments. He often felt gloomy about the engine, and in one letter to a friend he grumbled that, of all things in life, nothing was more foolish than inventing.","Then Roebuck's businesses collapsed. His coal mines kept flooding faster than they could be pumped, his debts grew, and in 1773 he went bankrupt. The same year brought sadness for Watt at home: his wife, Margaret Miller, whom he had married in 1764, died, leaving him with two young children. It was one of the hardest times of his life.","Roebuck's creditors, the people he owed money to, looked through his property to see what was worth anything. Watt's patent share was not high on their list; many thought a half-finished engine was nearly worthless. One of those creditors, however, saw things very differently. Roebuck owed him about 1,200 pounds, and he was happy to take Roebuck's share of Watt's patent instead of cash. His name was Matthew Boulton."]},{"heading":"Matthew Boulton, Soho and a New Partnership","paragraphs":["Matthew Boulton was born in Birmingham on 3 September 1728, the son of a maker of small metal goods. In the 1700s Birmingham was famous for what people called toys. These were not playthings, but small metal articles: shoe buckles, buttons, watch chains, snuff boxes and trinkets. As a teenager Boulton came up with a popular way of inlaying enamel in buckles, and after his father died in 1759 he took over the family business and set out to make it much bigger.","In 1761 Boulton leased about 13 acres of land at Soho, just outside Birmingham, which then lay in Staffordshire. There was a stream, a small rolling mill and a house. He built a grand new factory, the Soho Manufactory, finished by about 1765. Visitors were amazed: a long, handsome building looking more like a country mansion than a workshop, with hundreds of workers inside making buttons, buckles, silver and silver-plated tableware, and decorative gilded ornaments called ormolu. Boulton gathered many processes under one roof and organised the work so that each person did a specialised task, an early example of a modern factory.","Soho was so impressive that it became a tourist attraction for nobles, scientists and foreign visitors, and Boulton loved showing them round. He even set up an insurance scheme for workers in the 1770s, in which employees paid a small part of their wages into a fund that helped them if they were sick or injured. He was cheerful, persuasive, generous with credit and always full of new schemes, almost the opposite of the anxious, careful Watt.","Soho had a weakness, though. Its machines were driven by a waterwheel, and the stream was not strong enough, especially in dry summers. Boulton had already been thinking about using a steam engine to pump water back up above the wheel so it could be reused. That made him very interested indeed when he heard about a Scotsman with a much more economical engine.","Boulton was also a member of the Lunar Society of Birmingham, a group of friends who met once a month on the evening nearest the full moon (so they had moonlight to ride home by) to discuss science and invention. Members included the doctor and poet Erasmus Darwin, the potter Josiah Wedgwood, the chemist Joseph Priestley and the doctor William Small, who had once taught in America. Through these friends Boulton and Watt began writing to each other in 1766 and met in 1768, when Watt passed through Birmingham. They liked each other immediately.","After Roebuck's bankruptcy, Boulton took over his share of the patent. In 1774 Watt packed up and moved to Birmingham, bringing the parts of the Kinneil engine with him. At Soho he had skilled metalworkers, tools, space and, above all, Boulton's money and confidence. In 1775 the two men became partners, and the firm of Boulton and Watt was born. It would last for about 25 years in its first form and then carry on under their sons.","There was a problem with time, though. Watt's 1769 patent would normally last 14 years, until 1783. By 1775 six of those years had already gone, and the engine was still not on the market. Boulton argued that it would take years more to develop and sell the engines, and that without longer protection no one would risk the money. He lobbied Parliament hard, and in 1775 an Act of Parliament extended Watt's patent so that it lasted until 1800. This gave the partners 25 more years in which nobody else in Britain could build an engine with a separate condenser without their permission.","The extension was controversial even then. Some people, including mine owners and rival engineers, argued that it gave one firm too much power over a vital technology. You will see later in this chapter that this argument never really went away.","The partners divided their work in a way that suited their personalities. Watt concentrated on designing, calculating and solving technical problems. Boulton handled money, customers, publicity and politics, and kept the anxious Watt going when he felt like giving up. Watt is often quoted as saying he would rather face a loaded cannon than settle an account or make a bargain. Boulton, in contrast, enjoyed exactly that kind of work. It was a partnership of a brilliant engineer and a brilliant businessman, and the engine needed both."]},{"heading":"Iron-Mad Wilkinson and the Perfect Cylinder","paragraphs":["The leaking cylinder had defeated Watt in Scotland. The answer came from an ironmaster with a big personality: John Wilkinson, born in 1728, who ran ironworks at Bersham in Wales and at Broseley and Bradley in the English Midlands. People called him Iron-Mad Wilkinson because he wanted to make everything out of iron. He later built an iron barge, helped start the project that became the famous Iron Bridge over the River Severn, and even had an iron coffin made for himself.","In 1774 Wilkinson patented a new way of boring cannon. Before then, cannon were usually cast with a hollow core, and the inside could end up off-centre or uneven, which made guns inaccurate and sometimes dangerous. Wilkinson cast them solid and then bored the hole out by turning the barrel against a cutting tool. The key to his method was that the long bar holding the cutter was firmly supported, so the cutter could not wobble as it moved forward. The result was a straight, round and smooth hole.","A similar machine, with the boring bar supported at both ends, could bore out a large steam engine cylinder. Instead of a wobbly, egg-shaped tube, Watt could now have a cylinder that was truly round all the way along. In 1776 Boulton wrote that a cylinder about 50 inches (about 1.3 metres) across, bored by Wilkinson, did not err the thickness of an old shilling in any part. In other words, along its whole length it was accurate to about the thickness of a thin coin.","That may not sound like much, but it made all the difference. With a cylinder that accurate, a piston packed with materials such as hemp and tallow could fit snugly enough to keep the steam in. The engine that had wheezed at Kinneil was rebuilt at Soho with a Wilkinson cylinder and finally ran well. Many historians call Wilkinson's boring machine one of the first true machine tools, a machine for making other machines precisely. Wilkinson's cannon patent was later overturned in 1779, but he kept a near-exclusive role supplying cylinders to Boulton and Watt for years.","This is an important lesson about invention. Watt's idea was sound in 1765, but it could not succeed until metalworking caught up. Great machines often depend on many other people's tools and skills. A better engine needed a better cylinder, and a better cylinder needed a better boring machine, and that came from the world of making guns."]},{"heading":"1776: The First Engines Go to Work","paragraphs":["In 1776 the first Boulton and Watt engines started work. The first, completed in March, was at Bloomfield Colliery, a coal mine at Tipton in the Black Country, west of Birmingham, where it pumped water from the pit. Another, finished about a month later, was at Wilkinson's ironworks near Broseley in Shropshire, where it worked the bellows for a blast furnace, blowing air into the fire to make it hot enough to melt iron ore.","The Bloomfield engine's start was turned into a public event. Imagine a crowd of mine owners, engineers and curious locals standing in the cold around a tall brick engine house, its great beam poking out of the wall. There is a hiss, the beam begins to rock, and, according to accounts of the day, the engine quickly began clearing water from the pit. Local newspapers praised it. Boulton wanted everyone to know that this was a new and better kind of engine.","Interestingly, Boulton and Watt did not at first build entire engines in their own factory. Instead, the firm usually supplied the design drawings, sent skilled men to supervise the building, and provided certain special parts such as valves. The customer bought the heavy parts, like the cylinder from Wilkinson and the beam, from other suppliers, and paid for the engine house. Only in 1795 did the firm open its own engine-making works, the Soho Foundry at Smethwick, which was run mainly by the partners' sons, James Watt Junior and Matthew Robinson Boulton.","1776 was also the year of a famous visit. On a tour of Soho, Boulton showed the writer James Boswell, best known for his biography of Samuel Johnson, around the works. Boswell wrote down what Boulton said to him: \"I sell here, Sir, what all the world desires to have, POWER.\" It was a clever joke, because Soho also sold small luxury items, but it was also true. Boulton understood that he was not only selling a machine; he was selling the ability to do work anywhere, whenever you needed it."]},{"heading":"Pay Us a Third of What You Save","paragraphs":["How do you sell an expensive new engine to customers who already own old ones? Boulton and Watt came up with an unusual business model. Instead of charging just a fixed price, they asked customers to pay a yearly royalty equal to one third of the value of the coal the new engine saved, compared with a Newcomen engine doing the same work. The payments would continue for the life of the patent, which meant up to 1800.","This was clever. A customer did not have to believe the advertising; they only had to pay if the engine actually saved them money, and they kept two thirds of the savings for themselves. To measure the work, Watt designed a counter, a little clockwork device fixed to the beam that recorded every stroke, so the number of strokes could be checked and the payments calculated. Engineers compared engines using a measure called duty: how many pounds of water an engine could raise one foot high by burning a set amount of coal.","The biggest market was Cornwall, in the far south-west of England. Cornwall's tin and copper mines were some of the deepest in the world, often hundreds of metres down, and they flooded badly. But Cornwall had no coal of its own. Coal had to be shipped in, mostly from South Wales, which made it expensive. For Cornish mine owners, an engine that used about a quarter of the coal was enormously attractive. In the late 1770s the first engines were set up at Cornish mines such as Ting Tang and Wheal Busy near Chacewater, and many more followed. Within a few years, most of the old Newcomen engines in Cornwall had been replaced.","Watt spent long, unhappy months in Cornwall setting up engines and arguing about money. He suffered from headaches and homesickness, and he did not much enjoy the company of the tough, independent Cornish mine captains. To help, the firm relied on a young Scottish engineer, William Murdoch, who joined Boulton and Watt in 1777 and from 1779 was based in Cornwall, managing engine installations and repairs. Murdoch was practical, energetic and inventive, and later in this story he plays a key part in making engines turn wheels.","The royalties soon caused fierce arguments. Some Cornish mine owners felt they were paying too much, year after year, for an idea. When copper prices fell and mines struggled, many simply stopped paying. By the 1790s the firm was owed large sums in Cornwall that it struggled to collect."]},{"heading":"A Day at a Cornish Engine House","paragraphs":["Let us step into one of those Cornish mines around 1780. On a windy hillside stands a tall, narrow building of granite and slate: the engine house. One thick wall, called the bob wall, carries the weight of the great beam, which sticks out through a high opening above the mine shaft. Beside the engine house rises a brick or stone chimney, trailing coal smoke towards the sea.","Inside, it is warm and noisy. In the boiler house next door, men called stokers shovel coal into the furnace under a large boiler shaped a bit like a wagon roof, which is why it was called a wagon boiler. The steam is not at high pressure; it is only a little above the pressure of the air. A pipe carries it to the cylinder, which stands upright in the engine house, wrapped in its steam jacket and bolted down onto massive stone foundations.","Now watch a stroke. The engineman's valves let steam onto the top of the piston, while underneath it a passage opens to the cold condenser. The steam below the piston rushes into the condenser and vanishes into water, leaving a vacuum. With steam pressing from above and almost nothing pushing back from below, the piston sinks. The beam tilts, and on the outside end, heavy wooden pump rods rise out of the shaft, lifting water up from deep underground through a series of pumps. Then the valves change, steam is allowed to pass to the underside of the piston so pressure on both sides is balanced, and the heavy weight of the pump rods pulls the beam back the other way, ready for the next stroke.","Meanwhile the air pump, driven from the beam, steadily removes the warm water and air from the condenser, and some of that warm water is pumped back into the boiler. A small rod on the beam works the stroke counter, clicking away so the firm can work out the royalty. The engineman's job is to keep the valve gear adjusted, the packing tight and the engine running smoothly, sometimes for weeks at a time without stopping, because if the pumps stop the mine starts to flood.","Hundreds of metres below, miners working by candlelight in the tin and copper lodes depend on this rhythmic rocking for their safety and their jobs. The Cornish engine houses that grew from this period became so typical of the landscape that their ruins are now part of a World Heritage Site, the Cornwall and West Devon Mining Landscape. You will see in Part 11 how later Cornish engineers made these pumping engines even more efficient."]},{"heading":"Rivals, Courtrooms and a Fair Question","paragraphs":["Cornwall was full of skilled engineers, and some of them built engines of their own that Boulton and Watt said copied their invention. Jonathan Hornblower, from a family of Cornish engineers, designed an engine using two cylinders, an early form of the compound engine you will meet in Part 11. Edward Bull built engines with the cylinder placed upside down directly over the mine shaft, doing away with the beam. Both designs used a separate condenser, and Boulton and Watt took the builders to court.","In the 1790s Boulton and Watt took a series of infringers to court. The cases dragged on through the 1790s and were among the most important patent battles of the age. In the end the courts mostly backed Boulton and Watt, and many of the rival engineers and mine owners settled. The firm still never collected everything it believed it was owed.","Here we must be fair. The strong patent, extended until 1800, protected Watt's years of work and gave Boulton the confidence to invest huge sums before any profit came in. Without that protection, the partnership might never have survived. On the other hand, the patent also slowed down other inventors. Watt himself distrusted high-pressure steam as too dangerous, and while his patent lasted, engineers who wanted to try other ideas involving a condenser risked being sued. Some historians and economists argue that steam technology would have advanced faster without such a long monopoly; others say the patent was what made the business possible. It is a debate about inventions and the law that still goes on today, with medicines, software and much more.","What is certain is that after 1800, when the patent expired, inventors such as Richard Trevithick rushed ahead with new designs, especially high-pressure engines. Watt retired from the business that same year, aged 64, and Boulton left soon after, handing the firm to their sons.","By 1800 Boulton and Watt had built roughly 496 engines, according to commonly quoted figures, for mines, ironworks, breweries, waterworks, cotton mills and many other customers. That number tells a story in itself. In 1775 the separate condenser was a clever idea that had never run properly at full size. Twenty-five years later, Watt engines were at work all across Britain and beyond."]},{"heading":"Up and Down Is Not Enough","paragraphs":["Let us look again at those very first Boulton and Watt engines at Bloomfield, Broseley and the Cornish mines. They were magnificent, but they all did basically the same thing as Newcomen's engines: the great beam rocked up and down, and pump rods or bellows moved up and down with it. They were pumping engines. They could lift water out of a mine or blow air into a furnace, but they could not easily spin anything.","Yet look at what else was happening in Britain in the 1770s and 1780s. Flour mills ground grain with turning millstones. Cotton mills ran spinning machines on rotating shafts. Ironworks needed rolling mills and hammers driven round and round. Breweries needed pumps and stirrers. Almost every factory machine needed rotary motion, something that turns in a circle, like a wheel. Until now most of that turning came from waterwheels, and in dry summers, as Boulton knew at Soho, the water could run low.","Some mill owners had even tried a strange halfway solution: they used a steam engine to pump water up into a pond, and then let that water run down over a waterwheel to turn the machines. It worked, but it wasted a lot of power. What industry really wanted was a steam engine that could turn a wheel directly, smoothly and steadily, all day long.","Boulton saw the opportunity clearly. He urged Watt to find ways to make the engine turn, and is often quoted as writing that the people in London, Manchester and Birmingham were steam-mill mad. Making an up-and-down engine turn a wheel sounds simple, but it led to a whole series of clever inventions, a patent puzzle, and one of the most ingenious gears ever built. That is the story of the next part."]}],"deepDive":"Boulton and Watt's royalty system is one of the earliest examples of charging for a technology by the value it creates rather than by the cost of making it. Customers paid one third of the coal savings compared with a Newcomen engine of the same output, for the remaining life of the patent. To make that work, engineers needed a way to compare engines fairly, and the measure they used was duty: the number of pounds of water raised one foot by burning a fixed quantity of coal (a bushel, roughly 84 pounds or about 38 kilograms). Typical figures quoted by historians are around 5 to 7 million for ordinary Newcomen engines, about 9 to 10 million after John Smeaton's improvements, and around 20 million or more for Watt engines, all approximate because measurements varied widely. Later Cornish engines pushed duty far higher, and in 1811 Cornish engineers began publishing monthly reports of engine performance so mine owners could compare them openly. The patent debate is lively among economists and historians. One side argues the 1775 extension to 1800 was essential: Boulton invested heavily for years before profits arrived, and the firm's survival depended on royalties. The other side, associated for example with economists Michele Boldrin and David Levine, argues that Boulton and Watt spent much effort in courts, that the engine's efficiency barely improved during the final years of the patent, and that the spread of steam power and high-pressure designs sped up after 1800. Both views use real evidence, and the truth may be that the patent helped at first and hindered later. Another useful point is that the engine's success relied on a network of suppliers. Wilkinson's boring machine, Soho's skilled workforce, Murdoch's on-site engineering in Cornwall and the Lunar Society's connections were all part of the system. Roughly 496 engines are commonly said to have been built by the firm between 1775 and 1800, a modest number compared with later decades, but enough to show that steam could be a reliable, saleable product rather than a local experiment.","funFact":"John Wilkinson, who bored the cylinders for Boulton and Watt, was so devoted to iron that he had an iron coffin made for himself.","check":{"q":"How did Boulton and Watt charge many customers for using their engines?","a":"A royalty of one third of the coal they saved","wrong":["A fee for every horse the engine replaced","One penny per stroke of the beam","Half of the mine's profits"]},"sources":[{"title":"Boulton and Watt","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boulton_and_Watt"},{"title":"Matthew Boulton","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Matthew_Boulton"},{"title":"John Wilkinson (industrialist)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Wilkinson_(industrialist)"},{"title":"Watt steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watt_steam_engine"},{"title":"James Watt","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Watt"},{"title":"John Roebuck","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Roebuck"},{"title":"Wheal Busy","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Wheal_Busy"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/08-1.jpg","alt":"Matthew Boulton, James Watt's business partner, painted by Lemuel Francis Abbott (Birmingham Museums Trust).","file":"Lemuel Francis Abbott (1760-1803) - Matthew Boulton (1728–1809) - 1908P20 - Birmingham Museums Trust.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemuel_Francis_Abbott_(1760-1803)_-_Matthew_Boulton_(1728%E2%80%931809)_-_1908P20_-_Birmingham_Museums_Trust.jpg","author":"Lemuel Francis Abbott","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/08-2.jpg","alt":"An old etching of the Soho Manufactory near Birmingham, \"belonging to Messrs Boulton & Watt\".","file":"The Soho Manufactory, near Birmingham. Etching. Wellcome V0006177.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Soho_Manufactory,_near_Birmingham._Etching._Wellcome_V0006177.jpg","author":"Wellcome Collection","license":"CC BY 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/08-3.jpg","alt":"John Wilkinson the ironmaster, whose accurate cylinder-boring machine made Watt's engines possible, painted by Lemuel Francis Abbott.","file":"Lemuel Francis Abbott - Portrait of John Wilkinson, The Ironmaster.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lemuel_Francis_Abbott_-_Portrait_of_John_Wilkinson,_The_Ironmaster.jpg","author":"Lemuel Francis Abbott","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3738},{"n":9,"title":"Making It Turn","when":"1780 – 1790s","summary":"Early steam engines could only nod up and down, like a giant pumping handle. James Watt and his clever helper William Murdoch found ways to make engines spin wheels, keep a steady speed and even measure their strength in 'horsepower'. Soon a steam engine was grinding flour in the middle of London!","sections":[{"heading":"A Pump That Wanted to Be a Wheel","paragraphs":["By the late 1770s, Boulton & Watt engines were thumping away at mines all over Britain. Each one did the same basic job: a big beam rocked up and down, and a long rod hanging from the end of the beam worked a pump deep underground. Up, down. Up, down. It was a brilliant way to lift water, and that was exactly what the mines needed.","But the rest of the country wanted something else. Walk into a flour mill, a cotton mill, an ironworks or a brewery in the 1770s and you would see wheels everywhere. Millstones turned round and round to grind grain. Spindles whirled to twist cotton into thread. Grinding wheels, rollers, bellows and hammers were all driven by shafts that spun. Almost every kind of factory machine needed rotary motion, which simply means motion that goes round and round, not back and forth.","Where did that turning motion come from? Mostly from waterwheels, and sometimes from horses walking in circles or from windmills. A waterwheel turns on its own, which is wonderful, but it has to sit beside a fast river, and in a dry summer or a frozen winter it may slow down or stop. Factory owners dreamed of a power source they could put anywhere and that would never run out of river.","There was a clumsy way to get round the problem, and some people really did it. You could use a steam pumping engine to lift water up into a high tank or pond, then let that water pour back down over an ordinary waterwheel. The engine pumped, the wheel turned, and the machinery ran. It worked, but think about how wasteful it is: you burn coal to lift water just so the water can fall again. A lot of the energy leaks away in between, and you need a pond, a wheel and an engine instead of just one machine.","Matthew Boulton, Watt's business partner, could see the size of the opportunity. Mines were a big market, but the mills and workshops of Britain were enormous. In 1781 he is often quoted as writing to Watt that the people of London, Manchester and Birmingham were 'steam mill mad', meaning that everyone wanted engines to turn their machinery. If Watt could make his engine spin a shaft smoothly and reliably, Boulton & Watt could sell engines to almost every industry in the land.","So the challenge was clear. How do you turn the up-and-down nodding of a beam into the steady round-and-round turning of a wheel? The answer seems obvious to us now, because we see it every time someone rides a bicycle. But in 1780 it led to a patent puzzle, a clever escape, and a whole series of inventions that changed what steam engines could do."]},{"heading":"The Crank That Got Away","paragraphs":["Think about pedalling a bicycle. Your legs push up and down, but the pedals go round in a circle. The bent arm that joins the pedal to the axle is called a crank. A crank is one of the oldest tricks in engineering: people had used cranks for centuries on hand-turned grindstones, spinning wheels and pumps. Attach a rod from the end of an engine beam to a crank on a shaft, and every swing of the beam turns the shaft round.","There is one catch. At the very top and the very bottom of each stroke, the rod is pushing straight along the crank arm, so it cannot turn it at all. Engineers call these the dead centres. A cyclist gets past them because the other leg is pushing. An engine gets past them with a flywheel: a heavy wheel on the same shaft that keeps spinning by its own momentum, carrying the crank through the weak points and smoothing out the jerky pushes into a steady turn.","In 1780 a Birmingham button maker named James Pickard took out a patent for using a crank, together with a flywheel, to turn a shaft from a steam engine. A patent is a government grant that gives an inventor the right to stop other people using an invention for a set number of years unless they pay. Pickard's engine was a Newcomen-style atmospheric engine, but his patent meant that, in practice, nobody in Britain could freely use a crank on a steam engine for the next several years.","Watt was furious. He believed that the idea of using a crank had been his first, or at least that it was common knowledge that should never have been patented. A story told by Watt and his circle says that one of the workmen at Soho chatted about Watt's crank plans in a local tavern, and that the idea reached Pickard that way. Historians cannot prove this, and Pickard may well have reached the idea on his own, since cranks were hardly a secret. What is certain is that Watt did not want to pay Pickard for a licence, and Pickard's side reportedly wanted access to Watt's own valuable patent in return.","Watt also had a real engineering worry. An atmospheric or single-acting engine pushes hard on one stroke and only drifts back on the other. Feeding that lopsided push into a crank gives a very uneven turn, and Watt was not sure a flywheel could fully smooth it out. So, partly out of pride, partly out of caution and partly out of patent law, he set out to find another way to make his engine turn."]},{"heading":"Sun, Planet and William Murdoch","paragraphs":["Watt sketched several ways of getting rotary motion without a crank. The one that won was the sun-and-planet gear, and much of the credit for it belongs to a young Scottish engineer called William Murdoch. Murdoch had walked, according to a well-known tale, all the way from Scotland to Birmingham in 1777 to ask for a job at Soho. Boulton & Watt hired him, and he soon became their most trusted engine erector, especially in Cornwall, where he set up and repaired engines at the mines.","Here is how the sun-and-planet gear works. Picture two toothed gear wheels. The sun gear is fixed firmly to the shaft you want to turn, the one that will drive the mill. The planet gear is fixed solidly to the bottom end of the long connecting rod that hangs from the engine beam, so it cannot spin on its own. As the beam rocks, the rod moves the planet gear up and down, and a link keeps it meshed with the sun gear. The planet is forced to travel around the outside of the sun, like a planet orbiting a star, and as its teeth roll around the sun's teeth, it pushes the sun gear round.","The clever part, as far as the law was concerned, was that the planet gear was not a crank. The force went through the meshing teeth, not through a bent arm on the shaft. The little link that held the gears together carried no driving force at all. So Pickard's crank patent did not apply. Watt patented the sun-and-planet gear in October 1781, along with several other ways of producing rotation.","There was also a bonus. When the sun and planet gears have the same number of teeth, the shaft turns twice for every complete up-and-down cycle of the beam, instead of once as it would with a simple crank. That doubled the speed of the output shaft without any extra gearing, which was handy for driving fast mill machinery.","Engines with this gear are called rotative engines. One of the earliest survivors is the Whitbread Engine, built in 1785 for Samuel Whitbread's brewery in London, where it crushed malt and pumped beer and water around the brewery. It is the oldest rotative steam engine still in existence, and today it stands in the Powerhouse Museum in Sydney, Australia, a very long way from the brewery where it first turned.","The sun-and-planet gear was a workaround, and when Pickard's crank patent expired in the 1790s, Boulton & Watt quietly switched to the simpler crank. But for about a decade the gear allowed Watt's engines to break out of the mines and into the mills. Murdoch, who went on to pioneer gas lighting, is a good reminder that famous names often stand on the work of less famous helpers."]},{"heading":"Pushing Both Ways","paragraphs":["Even with a sun-and-planet gear, Watt's early engines had a weakness. They were single-acting. Steam (or rather, the vacuum left when steam condensed) did work on only one stroke of the piston. On the return stroke, the piston was simply pulled back by the weight of the pump rods or by a counterweight. That is fine for pumping, which naturally works on one stroke, but it gives a lumpy, uneven push to a turning shaft: a strong shove, then a lazy coast, then a strong shove again.","Watt's answer, patented in 1782, was the double-acting engine. The idea is easy to say and harder to build. Close both ends of the cylinder. Let steam into the top while the bottom is connected to the condenser, and the piston is driven down. Then swap: let steam into the bottom while the top is connected to the condenser, and the piston is driven up. Now the piston does useful work in both directions, and the engine gives roughly twice the power from a cylinder of the same size.","To make this happen, the engine needed a set of valves that opened and closed at exactly the right moments, worked automatically by rods linked to the moving parts of the engine. Steam had to go in at one end and out to the condenser at the other end, and then reverse, again and again, perhaps twenty or more times a minute. It was a small mechanical dance, and the engine itself was the dancer and the drummer at the same time.","In the same 1782 patent, Watt described another money-saving trick: expansive working. Instead of letting steam flow into the cylinder for the whole stroke, you shut off the steam part of the way through, for example halfway. The steam already inside keeps expanding and keeps pushing the piston, getting weaker as it spreads out. You get a bit less push from each stroke, but you use much less steam, and so much less coal. Watt was cautious and used only modest expansion, partly because it made the force on the piston uneven, but the idea would become very important in later high-pressure engines.","Double-acting engines brought a new problem, though. The old beam engines joined the piston rod to the beam with a chain that wrapped around an arch-shaped end of the beam. A chain is fine when the piston only pulls the beam down. But in a double-acting engine, the piston must also push the beam up, and you cannot push with a chain. Try pushing a door open with a piece of string and you will see the problem. Watt needed a stiff connection that could both push and pull."]},{"heading":"The Linkage Watt Loved Most","paragraphs":["Here is the puzzle Watt faced. The piston rod must move in a perfectly straight line, up and down through the top of the cylinder, or it will bend, jam and let steam leak out around its seal. But the end of the beam does not move in a straight line. It swings in an arc, a curved path, because the beam pivots around its middle like a seesaw. How do you join something that moves in a straight line to something that moves in a curve, using stiff rods that can push as well as pull?","Watt's answer, described in his patent of 1784, was a clever arrangement of hinged bars now often called Watt's linkage, and a related, slightly later version known as parallel motion. In the simplest form, there are three bars joined in a chain. The two outer bars are the same length and pivot on fixed points; the short middle bar connects them. When the outer bars swing, they swing in opposite curves, and the curves cancel each other out at the middle. A point in the centre of the short bar moves up and down in a line that is almost perfectly straight.","It is not exactly straight. If you let the linkage swing very far, the middle point traces a stretched figure-of-eight. But over the range that an engine actually uses, the path is so close to straight that the piston rod slides smoothly in its seal. Watt added extra bars arranged like a pantograph, a drawing tool that copies shapes at a different size, so that the straight-line point could be placed exactly where the piston rod needed it, while the whole thing stayed compact on top of the engine.","Watt was delighted. In a letter to Boulton in June 1784, he called it 'one of the most ingenious simple pieces of mechanics I have invented'. Years later, according to a well-known account, he told his son that he was prouder of the parallel motion than of any of his other mechanical inventions. That is striking, because many people would say the separate condenser was far more important. But engineers love an elegant solution, and this was one: a few bars and pins solving a geometry problem that had no obvious answer.","Parallel motion appeared on beam engines for the next century. If you visit a museum with a working beam engine, look at the top of the piston rod and you will see the bars waving gently as the beam rocks. And the idea has not disappeared. A version of Watt's linkage is still used today in the rear suspension of some cars, to keep an axle moving up and down in a straight line over bumps without sliding sideways."]},{"heading":"Flying Balls Keep the Speed Steady","paragraphs":["Once steam engines started driving mills, a new problem appeared: speed. Imagine a flour mill where an engine turns twenty pairs of millstones. If the miller lifts some stones out of work, the engine suddenly has less to do, and it speeds up. If more stones are added, it slows down. In a cotton mill, threads could snap if machines raced, and cloth could be spoiled. An engine needed something to keep its speed steady even when the load changed.","The solution was borrowed from windmills. Millers had long used a device with spinning weights to help control the gap between millstones as the wind rose and fell. In 1788, after a suggestion from Boulton, Watt adapted the idea for steam engines. The result is called the centrifugal governor, or sometimes the flyball governor, and it became one of the most recognisable symbols of the steam age.","Here is how it works. A vertical spindle is turned by the engine, so it spins faster when the engine runs faster. Hanging from the top of the spindle are two heavy metal balls on hinged arms. When the spindle spins, the balls swing outwards, just as you feel pushed outwards on a fast playground roundabout. The faster the spin, the further out and higher the balls fly. The arms are linked to a sliding collar, and the collar is linked by rods to a valve in the steam pipe, called the throttle.","If the engine speeds up, the balls rise, and the linkage closes the throttle a little, letting less steam into the cylinder, so the engine slows. If the engine slows down, the balls drop, the throttle opens a little, more steam flows in, and the engine speeds up again. Nobody has to stand by the valve and watch. The machine corrects itself, over and over, many times a minute.","This is an early, famous example of what engineers now call feedback control: a system that measures what it is doing and uses that measurement to adjust itself. Your home's heating thermostat, a car's cruise control and the autopilot of an aircraft all use the same basic idea. Watt's simple governor was not perfect. It held the speed within a range rather than at an exact number, and under some conditions it could make an engine 'hunt', speeding up and slowing down in a wobbly rhythm. In 1868 the great Scottish physicist James Clerk Maxwell wrote a paper called 'On Governors', using mathematics to explain when such devices are stable. Many people see that paper as one of the starting points of the modern science of control."]},{"heading":"Inventing Horsepower","paragraphs":["Selling a rotative engine raised a very practical question. If a brewer or a mill owner wanted to replace the horses that turned his machinery, how big an engine should he buy? Customers did not think in terms of steam or coal. They thought in terms of horses. So Watt decided to describe his engines in horses too.","He studied horses at work turning mill gear. By one account he estimated that a mill horse walking round a circular path about 24 feet across (a wheel with a radius of 12 feet), going round about 144 times an hour, pulled with a force of about 180 pounds. Multiplying those numbers out gives about 32,500 foot-pounds of work each minute. A foot-pound is the work done in lifting one pound through a height of one foot. Rounding up generously, Watt and Boulton fixed one horsepower at 33,000 foot-pounds per minute. That is the same as lifting 550 pounds (about 250 kilograms) by one foot (about 30 centimetres) in one second.","Was this a fair measure of a horse? Not exactly. A strong horse can produce well over one horsepower for a short burst, but a typical working horse kept up for a whole day does less. Watt's number was a convenient, slightly generous standard rather than a description of any real horse's day. Some historians think the generosity was deliberate: if a customer bought a '10-horse' engine and found it did more than ten horses' work, he would be pleased, not disappointed.","The horsepower unit spread far beyond Watt's own business. It was used to rate steam engines, then gas and petrol engines, and today car adverts still boast about it. One horsepower in Watt's sense is about 746 watts. There is also a slightly smaller 'metric horsepower', about 735.5 watts, based on lifting 75 kilograms by one metre in one second, which is still used in some countries for cars.","And that word 'watt' is itself a tribute. In 1882 the engineer William Siemens, speaking as president of the British Association for the Advancement of Science, proposed naming the unit of power after James Watt, and the name was soon taken up. In 1960 it became part of the international system of units (SI). One watt is one joule of energy per second. Every time you read '60 W' on a light bulb or '2,000 W' on a kettle, you are using Watt's name, more than a century after his death in 1819."]},{"heading":"The Albion Mill: A Steam Showpiece on the Thames","paragraphs":["Boulton loved a grand demonstration, and in the 1780s he planned one in the heart of London. On the south bank of the River Thames, at the Southwark end of Blackfriars Bridge, rose the Albion Mill, a huge flour mill driven not by water or wind but entirely by steam. Planning started around 1783, and the mill began working in 1786. The young engineer John Rennie, who later became famous for building bridges, designed much of its machinery, using iron gearing where older mills had used wood.","The mill was powered by a Boulton & Watt rotative, double-acting engine of about 50 horsepower, and by 1789 a second engine had been added. They drove 20 pairs of millstones, grinding wheat into flour day and night. The engines' governors kept the stones turning at an even speed. Visitors came to marvel at it. For a while it was one of the sights of London, a working advertisement that said: steam can turn the wheels of any industry, right in the middle of a city, nowhere near a fast river.","It was also a business, and it produced a great deal of flour. By some accounts, in 1790 it was selling more than 6,000 pounds' worth of flour every week. Not everyone was pleased. Traditional millers, who used wind and water, feared they would be put out of business by this giant, and many ordinary Londoners worried that big companies would control the price of bread.","Then, on 2 March 1791, disaster struck. Fire broke out in the mill and spread through the building in a couple of hours, leaving a gutted shell. The official explanation pointed to an overheated bearing, a moving part that had not been greased properly, but many people at the time suspected that someone had set the fire deliberately. Crowds reportedly watched from the bridges, and some local millers were said to have celebrated. More than 500 people lost their jobs, and the owners, including Boulton and Watt, lost a great deal of money.","The blackened ruin stood beside the Thames for years. Some writers have suggested that it was in the mind of the poet William Blake, who lived nearby in Lambeth, when he later wrote of 'dark Satanic Mills'. Nobody can be sure exactly what Blake meant, but the image of the burned-out steam mill is a powerful one. Even though the Albion Mill failed, it had proved its point. A steam engine could now grind, spin, roll, hammer and pump. In the next decades, rotative engines would spread into cotton mills, ironworks, breweries and potteries across Britain and beyond.","So far we have followed the story of steam through the people who built it and the machines they made. But what is really happening inside that cylinder? Why does boiling water push so hard, why does a cold spray make a vacuum, and where does all the energy from the coal go? It is time to pause the history and open up a steam engine, step by step, to see exactly how it works."]}],"deepDive":"Historians of technology often describe the 1780s as the decade when Watt's engine changed from a pump into a 'prime mover', a general-purpose source of power. Four inventions did most of the work. The sun-and-planet gear (patented October 1781, largely Murdoch's design) gave rotation while sidestepping James Pickard's 1780 crank-and-flywheel patent; whether Pickard truly copied Watt is unproven, and cranks were long-established, which is why many historians doubt such a patent should have been granted at all. The double-acting principle and expansive working (patent of 1782) roughly doubled power per cylinder and saved steam. Watt's own figures suggest expansion was used cautiously: cutting off steam at half-stroke raised the theoretical efficiency only modestly, from around 6 percent to about 10 percent, and real engines achieved less. Parallel motion (patent of 1784) is a neat piece of kinematics: the mid-point of Watt's three-bar linkage traces part of a lemniscate-like curve that is nearly straight over a short range, good enough to guide a piston rod through its packing. The centrifugal governor (1788) was adapted from millwrights' practice, so Watt deserves credit for its application rather than its invention. It was proportional control only, so the steady speed depended on the load, and it could oscillate; Maxwell's 1868 paper 'On Governors' analysed such behaviour and is often cited as a founding text of control theory. Horsepower is a marketing unit with a physical definition: 33,000 foot-pounds per minute, equal to about 745.7 watts. Watt's mill-horse estimate gave roughly 32,400 to 32,600 foot-pounds per minute, and the round number gave buyers a little extra. Finally, it is worth remembering how slowly all this spread. By 1800 Boulton & Watt had built a few hundred rotative engines, but waterwheels still supplied most of Britain's factory power for decades, and the Albion Mill's spectacular failure shows that new technology also brought new risks and resentments.","funFact":"Because the sun-and-planet gear makes the drive shaft turn twice for every single rocking cycle of the beam, Watt's early mill engines got a free speed boost just from avoiding someone else's patent.","check":{"q":"What did Watt's centrifugal governor do?","a":"It automatically kept the engine's speed steady by using spinning balls to open or close the steam valve","wrong":["It turned the up-and-down motion of the beam into rotation","It measured how many horses an engine could replace","It let steam push the piston in both directions"]},"sources":[{"title":"Sun and planet gear","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sun_and_planet_gear"},{"title":"Watt steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watt_steam_engine"},{"title":"Watt's linkage","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watt%27s_linkage"},{"title":"Centrifugal governor","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Centrifugal_governor"},{"title":"Horsepower","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horsepower"},{"title":"Watt (unit)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watt_(unit)"},{"title":"Albion Mills, Southwark","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Albion_Mills,_Southwark"},{"title":"Whitbread Engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Whitbread_Engine"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/09-1.jpg","alt":"The sun-and-planet gear on a 1788 Boulton & Watt beam engine at the Science Museum, London, turning up-and-down motion into rotation.","file":"Boulton and Watt sun and planet gear.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulton_and_Watt_sun_and_planet_gear.jpg","author":"Andy Dingley","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/09-2.jpg","alt":"A Watt-type flyball governor (1788) with its two iron balls, on a Boulton & Watt engine at the Science Museum, London.","file":"Boulton and Watt centrifugal governor-MJ.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boulton_and_Watt_centrifugal_governor-MJ.jpg","author":"Dr. Mirko Junge","license":"CC BY 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/09-3.jpg","alt":"Engraving of the steam-powered Albion Mill on the Thames beside Blackfriars Bridge, London, from the European Magazine.","file":"Thomas Prattent - Albion Mill, Blackfriars Bridge - B1977.14.14868 - Yale Center for British Art.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Prattent_-_Albion_Mill,_Blackfriars_Bridge_-_B1977.14.14868_-_Yale_Center_for_British_Art.jpg","author":"Thomas Prattent","license":"CC0"}],"words":3958},{"n":10,"title":"Inside the Machine: How Steam Does Work","when":"Science explained (applies 1700s – today)","summary":"Fire heats water, water turns into steam, and steam pushes. In this part we open up a steam engine and follow the energy from a lump of coal all the way to a spinning wheel. You will learn why steam is invisible, why it pushes so hard, and why you must never touch it!","sections":[{"heading":"Where the Push Comes From: Fire and Energy","paragraphs":["Every steam engine, from Newcomen's nodding giant to a modern power station, starts with the same thing: something hot. In the 1700s and 1800s, that heat almost always came from burning coal, though wood, charcoal and even dried peat were used in places where coal was scarce. To understand what an engine does, we first need to understand what burning really is.","Coal is mostly carbon, left behind by plants that grew in swampy forests hundreds of millions of years ago. Those plants captured energy from sunlight and stored it in the chemical bonds that hold their molecules together. When coal burns, the carbon combines with oxygen from the air to make carbon dioxide, and the stored chemical energy is released as heat and light. So, in a strange way, a steam engine runs on ancient sunshine.","Scientists call the ability to do work energy. Work, in the scientist's sense, means using a force to move something: lifting water, turning a millstone, pulling a train. Energy comes in many forms, such as chemical energy in fuel, heat energy, the energy of motion and electrical energy. A key rule of nature is that energy is never made or destroyed; it only changes from one form to another. A steam engine is simply a machine for changing the chemical energy of fuel into the energy of motion.","But heat on its own does not push anything. A roaring fire on a hearth can warm a room all night without moving so much as a chair. The trick of the steam engine is to use heat to change water into something that pushes. To see how, we need to look closely at what happens when water boils."]},{"heading":"From Water to Steam: The Invisible Gas","paragraphs":["Water is made of tiny particles called molecules, each one made of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom. In liquid water, the molecules jostle and slide past each other, but they stay close together, held by small attractive forces. When you heat water, the molecules move faster and faster. At sea level, once the water reaches 100 degrees Celsius (212 degrees Fahrenheit), the molecules have enough energy to break free of each other completely and fly off as a gas. That gas is steam.","Here is a surprise: real steam is invisible. Look carefully at the spout of a boiling kettle. Right at the opening there is a small clear gap, and only a little further away does the white cloud appear. The clear part is true steam. The white cloud is made of billions of tiny droplets of liquid water, formed when the steam meets the cooler air and condenses back into liquid. So the white 'steam' we see from kettles, pans and the chimneys of old engines is really a mist of water droplets, a bit like a small cloud. The same is true of the huge white plumes rising from power-station cooling towers.","Another surprise is how much heat it takes to boil water away. Heating one kilogram of water from freezing point to boiling point takes about 420 kilojoules of energy. But turning that same kilogram of boiling water at 100 degrees into steam at 100 degrees takes about 2,260 kilojoules more, over five times as much, and the thermometer does not rise at all while it happens. This 'hidden' heat is called latent heat, from a Latin word meaning hidden. The Scottish chemist Joseph Black studied it in the mid-1700s, and as we saw earlier, it helped James Watt understand why the Newcomen engine wasted so much fuel.","Where does all that latent heat go? It is the energy needed to pull the water molecules apart from each other. And when steam condenses back into water, the same energy is given out again. That fact is the secret behind both the power of steam and the danger of it, as we will see.","The most important property of steam for engines is its size. When one litre of water turns into steam at normal air pressure, it becomes roughly 1,700 litres of steam. That is enough to fill a large bathtub several times over, all from one bottle of water. The molecules, now free, zoom around and spread out to take up a vast amount of space. If you trap them in a closed container, they cannot spread out, so instead they push hard against every wall. And if you cool the steam, it shrinks back into a tiny puddle, leaving almost nothing behind."]},{"heading":"Pressure: Steam Pushing on Every Surface","paragraphs":["Pressure means how hard something pushes on each bit of a surface. Inside a closed boiler, countless steam molecules are constantly smashing into the walls. Each hit is tiny, but together they add up to a steady, powerful push on every square centimetre. Heat the boiler more and you make more steam and faster molecules, so the pressure rises.","Air has pressure too. We live at the bottom of an ocean of air many kilometres deep, and its weight presses on everything. At sea level, air pushes with about 1 kilogram of force on every square centimetre, or about 14.7 pounds on every square inch. We do not feel it because it pushes equally from all sides, and the fluids inside our bodies push back. But, as the scientists of the 1600s discovered, if you remove the air from one side of something, the push from the other side becomes very obvious.","Pressure and boiling are linked in a way that surprises many people. Water boils when its molecules can push their way out against the pressure above the water. If the pressure is higher, the molecules need more energy, so the water boils at a higher temperature. In a pressure cooker, the lid traps steam and raises the pressure inside to roughly double normal air pressure, so the water does not boil until it reaches about 120 degrees Celsius. Food cooks faster at that higher temperature. Denis Papin's 'digester' of 1679 used this exact idea.","The opposite happens high up. On the top of Mount Everest, where the air pressure is only about a third of that at sea level, water boils at only about 70 degrees Celsius. Climbers find it very hard to cook pasta or make a properly hot cup of tea.","For engines, the link between pressure and temperature is crucial. High-pressure steam is also very hot steam, and as you will learn later in this story, hotter steam can do more work from the same fuel. But high pressure also means huge forces on the boiler walls. A boiler at ten times normal air pressure has about 10 kilograms of force pushing on every square centimetre of its inside surface. A large boiler has many thousands of square centimetres of surface, so the total force adds up to many tonnes. That is why boilers had to be made strong and carefully inspected, and why every boiler needs a safety valve."]},{"heading":"A Guided Tour: The Parts of a Steam Engine","paragraphs":["Let's walk through a typical steam engine of the 1800s, following the energy from start to finish. Imagine a factory engine house, warm and smelling of hot oil, with the steady 'chuff' of the engine and the slow turn of a huge wheel.","First comes the firebox, the chamber where the fuel burns. A stoker shovels coal through a small iron door onto a grate, a bed of iron bars that lets air flow up from below to feed the flames. Hot gases from the fire then pass along or through the boiler before escaping up a tall chimney, which also helps draw fresh air into the fire.","Next is the boiler, a strong, closed container of water, usually made of riveted iron plates and later steel. In a fire-tube boiler, the hot gases run through tubes that pass through the water, like straws through a drink. In a water-tube boiler, it is the other way round: water flows through tubes surrounded by the fire. Either way, the aim is to give the hot gases as much contact with the water as possible. A glass gauge on the side shows the water level, because a boiler that runs dry can overheat and fail.","On top of the boiler sits the safety valve. In its simplest form, it is a plug held down by a weight or a spring. If the pressure inside rises too high, it lifts the plug and lets steam escape with a loud hiss until the pressure falls. Papin fitted a weighted safety valve to his digester in the 1600s. A safety valve that is stuck, tied down or overloaded can turn a boiler into a bomb, and many boiler explosions in the 1800s happened for exactly that reason.","From the boiler, steam flows through a pipe to the cylinder, a strong metal tube closed at both ends, bored very smoothly on the inside. Inside it slides the piston, a tight-fitting disc that can move back and forth. Rings around the piston seal it so steam cannot leak past. The piston is fixed to a piston rod, which passes out through one end of the cylinder through a tightly packed seal called a gland.","Steam must be let into the cylinder and out again at exactly the right moments. That is the job of the valves. A very common type is the slide valve, a shaped block that slides back and forth over openings called ports. In one position it lets fresh steam into one end of the cylinder while letting used steam out of the other end; in the opposite position it does the reverse. A well-known version, the D slide valve, is credited to William Murdoch around 1799. The slide valve is moved by rods linked to the turning shaft, so the engine works its own valves, perfectly in time.","The piston rod's back-and-forth motion then has to become rotation. On a beam engine, the rod rocks the beam, and the other end of the beam drives a connecting rod. On simpler horizontal engines, the piston rod pushes a connecting rod directly. Either way, the connecting rod turns a crank on the main shaft. On the same shaft sits the flywheel, a massive wheel that stores energy as it spins and carries the crank smoothly past the dead centres. Some mill flywheels were over 6 metres across and weighed many tonnes. Belts or gears then carry the power from the shaft to the machines.","Finally, on many engines, the used steam goes to the condenser, a separate chamber cooled by cold water. There the steam turns back into a small amount of water, leaving a partial vacuum. An air pump removes the water and any air that leaks in. Engines without a condenser simply blow their used steam out into the air, which is where the famous 'chuff' of a steam locomotive comes from."]},{"heading":"Two Ways to Work: Push or Pull","paragraphs":["There are two basic ways steam can move a piston, and the history of the steam engine is partly the story of switching from one to the other.","The first is the vacuum way, used by Newcomen and by Watt's early engines. Steam at only a little above normal air pressure fills the cylinder. Then the steam is condensed, either by a spray of cold water inside the cylinder (Newcomen) or by letting it rush into a separate cold condenser (Watt). Because steam shrinks about 1,700 times when it turns back to water, the space it leaves is almost empty: a partial vacuum. Now the air pressure on the other side of the piston is no longer balanced, and it pushes the piston into the cylinder. In these atmospheric engines, the steam itself does not push the piston at all. Its job is to make the vacuum; the weight of the air does the pushing.","There is a limit to this. The very best vacuum you could ever make would give you a push of only about 1 kilogram per square centimetre, the full weight of the atmosphere, and real engines got less than that. So to get more power, atmospheric engines needed enormous cylinders. Some pumping engines had cylinders about 1.8 metres (72 inches) wide, and later engines were bigger still.","The second way is the pressure way. Here, steam from the boiler is made at a much higher pressure than the air outside, and it pushes directly on the piston. Steam at five times atmospheric pressure pushes about five times harder on each square centimetre than the air could. That means a much smaller cylinder can give the same power, so the engine can be smaller and lighter. As the next part of this story shows, that is what made it possible to put engines on wheels and boats.","The best engines combined both. High-pressure steam pushed on one side of the piston while the other side was connected to a condenser, so the piston had a strong push in front and a vacuum behind. Engineers also learned to let the steam expand as it pushed, and to pass it through two or three cylinders in turn, squeezing out more work before it was finally condensed."]},{"heading":"Following the Energy (and Where It Goes Missing)","paragraphs":["Let's follow the energy all the way through. It starts as chemical energy, stored in the coal. Burning turns it into heat energy in the fire. The heat flows into the water and becomes the energy of hot, high-pressure steam. The steam pushes the piston, turning that into the energy of motion. The crank and flywheel turn it into rotation, and belts and gears carry it to the machines, where it grinds, spins, lifts or hammers. Chemical energy, then heat, then pressure, then motion: that is the energy chain of every steam engine.","If energy is never destroyed, you might expect all of the coal's energy to come out as useful work. But in a steam engine, most of it does not. Hot gases go up the chimney still carrying lots of heat. The boiler and pipes warm the air around them. Some steam leaks. The parts rub against each other and turn energy into friction heat. And, most importantly, the used steam leaving the cylinder still contains a huge amount of heat, including all that latent heat, which is dumped into the condenser water or the open air.","Engineers measure this with a number called efficiency: the share of the fuel's energy that ends up as useful work. Newcomen's engines managed only about 1 percent or less. Watt's improved engines reached a few percent. The best engines of the late 1800s managed perhaps 10 to 20 percent. A modern power station, using steam at very high pressure and temperature, does far better, but even so, more than half of its fuel energy still ends up as waste heat.","Why can't a steam engine use all the heat? This puzzle was so important that it led to an entire new branch of science, called thermodynamics, which we will meet later. For now, here is the simple version: heat can only do work as it flows from a hot place to a cooler place, and some heat always has to be released into the cold side. The bigger the difference between the hot steam and the cold condenser, the bigger the share of heat that can become work. That is why engineers spent two centuries trying to make steam hotter and condensers colder."]},{"heading":"One Stroke, Step by Step","paragraphs":["Let's slow everything down and watch a single turn of a double-acting condensing engine, the kind Watt built in the 1780s and that ran factories for a century afterwards. Imagine the piston resting at the top of the cylinder, and the flywheel creeping round.","Step one: the slide valve moves and opens the top port. Steam from the boiler rushes in above the piston. At the same moment, the bottom port is opened to the condenser, so the space below the piston is connected to a near-vacuum. With steam pressing from above and almost nothing pushing back from below, the piston is driven down.","Step two: part of the way down, on an engine set up for expansive working, the valve shuts off the fresh steam. The steam already trapped above the piston keeps pushing as it expands, but its pressure falls as it spreads into a larger space. The engine squeezes extra work out of steam it has already paid for.","Step three: the piston reaches the bottom. The crank is now passing its lower dead centre, and for an instant the piston rod cannot turn it at all. The heavy flywheel, still spinning, carries the shaft round. Meanwhile the valve has moved again, and now everything reverses: fresh steam enters below the piston, and the used steam above it escapes to the condenser, where a spray of cold water turns it back into water in a fraction of a second.","Step four: the piston is driven back up to the top, the crank passes its upper dead centre, and the cycle starts again. A mill engine might repeat this dance 20 to 60 times a minute, hour after hour, day after day, with each stroke helping to turn the line shafts that ran the machines.","How did engineers know what was really happening inside, where nobody could see? In about 1796, John Southern, an assistant at Boulton & Watt, developed the indicator diagram. A small piston connected to the cylinder moved a pencil up and down as the pressure changed, while a board holding paper moved sideways in step with the engine's piston. The pencil drew a loop. The shape of the loop showed exactly how the pressure rose and fell during each stroke, and the area inside it showed how much work the steam had done. Boulton & Watt kept the device as a trade secret for years. Engineers used indicator diagrams to find leaky valves, badly timed steam and poor condensers, and the same kind of pressure-volume diagram is still used to study engines today."]},{"heading":"See It for Yourself (Safely!)","paragraphs":["You do not need a factory to see the science of steam. Your kitchen is a small steam laboratory. But first, a serious warning: steam burns are dangerous, and you should only ever try these observations with an adult in charge, keeping your hands, face and arms well away from steam, boiling water and hot pans.","Why is steam so dangerous? Remember latent heat. When steam at 100 degrees Celsius touches your skin, it condenses into water, and as it does, it releases all that hidden energy, about 2,260 kilojoules for every kilogram, straight into your skin. Then the hot water it has turned into keeps burning. So a puff of steam can cause a worse burn than a splash of water at the same temperature. Never put your hand or face over a spout or a pan to 'feel' the steam, and never open a pressure cooker before it has fully released its pressure.","With that in mind, here are some safe things to watch, from a sensible distance, while an adult cooks. Watch a pan of water with a loosely fitting lid come to the boil. Soon the lid starts to rattle and lift. That is steam pressure pushing up, in a tiny version of the force that drives an engine. When the steam escapes, the pressure drops and the lid falls again, a bit like a crude safety valve.","Look at the spout of a boiling kettle from the side, not from above. Can you see the small clear gap just in front of the spout before the white cloud begins? That clear gap is the real, invisible steam. The cloud is condensed water droplets. Then notice how drops of water form on a cool window or the inside of a lid. That is condensation, the same process that made the vacuum in Newcomen's and Watt's engines.","If you want to see real engines, many museums run them in steam. The Science Museum in London has a famous collection of early engines, and across Britain, Europe, North America and Australia, volunteers keep old mill engines, pumping engines and traction engines working on special 'steam days'. Watching a giant flywheel turn and hearing the hiss of the valves is the best way to feel what this chapter describes.","Now you know how steam does its work: by pushing with pressure, or by pulling with a vacuum. Through most of the 1700s, engineers like Newcomen and Watt kept their steam gentle, at hardly more than the pressure of the air around us, because strong boilers were hard to build and explosions were terrifying. But a few daring engineers began to ask a dangerous question. What would happen if, instead of using gentle low-pressure steam, they dared to use steam at much higher pressure?"]}],"deepDive":"A steam engine is a heat engine, and its physics can be followed with a few numbers. Water's specific heat is about 4.19 kJ per kilogram per degree, so warming 1 kg from 0 to 100 °C takes about 420 kJ, while its latent heat of vaporisation at 100 °C is about 2,257 to 2,260 kJ/kg. In a condensing engine, most of that latent heat is rejected in the condenser, which is the single largest reason early engines were so inefficient. The volume change is equally dramatic: at 1 atmosphere, saturated steam occupies about 1.67 cubic metres per kilogram, against about 0.001 cubic metres for liquid water, a ratio of roughly 1,600 to 1,700. Atmospheric engines exploited the collapse of this volume; their maximum driving pressure was one atmosphere (about 101 kPa), minus whatever pressure remained in an imperfect vacuum. The boiling point rises with pressure along the saturation curve: about 120 °C at 2 bar absolute, about 180 °C at 10 bar, and at the critical point (about 374 °C and 22 MPa) the distinction between liquid and vapour disappears. This matters because, as Sadi Carnot later showed, the maximum possible efficiency of a heat engine depends on the absolute temperatures of the hot source and cold sink: efficiency cannot exceed 1 minus T(cold) divided by T(hot). For steam at 100 °C (373 K) rejecting heat at 30 °C (303 K), the theoretical ceiling is only about 19 percent; for steam at 600 °C (873 K), it rises to about 65 percent. Real engines fall well short of these limits because of friction, heat leaking through cylinder walls, throttling losses in valves and pipes, and the repeated condensation and re-evaporation of steam on cylinder walls, which engineers identified as the main loss in piston engines. Historians and engineers sometimes argue about how to compare old efficiency figures, because early engineers measured 'duty' (millions of pounds of water lifted one foot per bushel of coal) rather than percentages, and converting between them requires assumptions about coal quality and pump losses. Still, the overall trend from about 1 percent (Newcomen) to a few percent (Watt) to perhaps 10 to 20 percent for the best engines of the late 1800s is widely accepted.","funFact":"The white 'steam' you see puffing from a kettle is not steam at all; true steam is invisible, and the cloud is made of tiny droplets of liquid water.","check":{"q":"In an atmospheric engine like Newcomen's, what actually pushes the piston?","a":"The pressure of the outside air, after the steam inside is condensed to make a partial vacuum","wrong":["High-pressure steam from the boiler","Burning steam inside the cylinder","The spinning flywheel"]},"sources":[{"title":"Steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steam_engine"},{"title":"Latent heat","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latent_heat"},{"title":"Watt steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Watt_steam_engine"},{"title":"Pressure cooking","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pressure_cooking"},{"title":"Slide valve","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Slide_valve"},{"title":"Indicator diagram","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Indicator_diagram"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/10-1.jpg","alt":"A 1908 cutaway of a steam cylinder (A) with steam ports (S) at each end and the sliding valve (V) underneath that sends steam to either side.","file":"Steam engine diagram 1908.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Steam_engine_diagram_1908.png","author":"James E. Homans","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/10-2.jpg","alt":"Water boiling hard in a glass electric kettle, with a white cloud of tiny water droplets drifting up above it.","file":"Boilingkettle.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Boilingkettle.jpg","author":"Plorpy2","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/10-3.jpg","alt":"The huge flywheel, crank and piston rod of the preserved mill steam engine at Ellenroad Engine House, Greater Manchester.","file":"The steam engine at Ellenroad Mill in 2016 (1).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_steam_engine_at_Ellenroad_Mill_in_2016_(1).jpg","author":"Tom Axford 1","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3812},{"n":11,"title":"High Pressure!","when":"1800 – 1850","summary":"When James Watt's patent ended, bold engineers turned the steam up high. Richard Trevithick drove a puffing steam carriage up a Cornish hill on Christmas Eve, and smaller, stronger engines started to move. In the Netherlands, a giant steam engine even helped drain a whole lake!","sections":[{"heading":"Mr Watt's Big Worry","paragraphs":["Imagine you are James Watt in the 1790s. Your engines are famous. They pump water out of Cornish mines, turn the machines of mills and breweries, and earn you and Matthew Boulton a steady stream of royalties. And yet, whenever someone talks about using 'strong steam', you shake your head. Strong steam was the old name for steam kept at a pressure well above the push of the ordinary air around us. Watt thought it was a terrible idea.","His reasons were not silly. Watt's engines worked at a pressure only a little above the atmosphere. Most of the push on the piston came from the vacuum made in his separate condenser, with the air or low-pressure steam pressing on the other side. Boilers in his day were made of small plates of wrought iron or copper riveted together, often in the shape of a wagon or a haystack. They had no pressure gauges in the modern sense, the metal was uneven, and the seams could leak or tear. Pump up the pressure inside a boiler like that, and it might burst, throwing out scalding water, steam and chunks of metal. Watt had seen enough accidents with ordinary equipment to fear what strong steam could do.","There was also business. Boulton and Watt's patent on the separate condenser, extended by Parliament in 1775, lasted until 1800. Any engine that used a separate condenser had to pay them. Watt had no reason to encourage a completely different kind of engine that might compete with his own, and he and Boulton watched rival engine builders closely. When Watt's own assistant, William Murdoch, built a little model steam carriage in about 1784, Watt discouraged him from spending time on it. A road vehicle would need a small, light, powerful engine, and Watt believed that was simply not safe or practical.","So for the last quarter of the 1700s, British steam power mostly stayed low-pressure, slow and huge. A big Boulton and Watt pumping engine could fill a building three or four storeys tall. Its beam could weigh several tonnes. It was wonderful for a mine or a mill, but you could never put it on wheels.","Then the year 1800 arrived, and Watt's patent ran out. Watt himself retired soon afterwards. Almost at once, a group of bold engineers, many of them from the mining country of Cornwall, began to try the very thing Watt had warned against. They raised the pressure, made the engines smaller, and discovered that they could build machines that were light enough to move. This part of the story is about those daring people, the engines they built, and the clever ways they squeezed more work out of every lump of coal."]},{"heading":"The Cornish Giant","paragraphs":["The most daring of them all was Richard Trevithick. He was born in 1771 in the parish of Illogan, near Camborne in Cornwall, a landscape of tin and copper mines, engine houses and spoil heaps. His father was a mine 'captain', which meant a manager in charge of a mine's work. Young Richard did badly at school. One teacher reportedly called him disobedient, slow and obstinate. But he was a natural with machines and numbers, and he was enormously strong. He grew to over six feet tall (about 1.88 metres), and stories from Cornwall describe him lifting heavy pieces of machinery and throwing a sledgehammer over the top of an engine house. Those stories may have grown a little in the telling, but everyone agreed he was a giant of a man.","Trevithick grew up surrounded by steam engines. The Cornish mines were deep and wet, and every one needed pumps. Cornwall had no coal of its own, so coal had to be shipped in from South Wales, which made it expensive. That is why Cornish mine owners cared so much about fuel efficiency, and why they had paid Boulton and Watt for their engines, sometimes grumbling bitterly about the royalties. Trevithick's father was among the Cornish mining men who argued with Boulton and Watt's agents. So from childhood, Richard heard about steam, money and patents at the dinner table.","As a young engineer in the 1790s, Trevithick worked on pumping engines at Cornish mines and became known for being able to fix almost anything. He began to think about strong steam. If you let steam at high pressure push the piston, he reasoned, you would not need a giant cylinder to get a big force. A small cylinder with a strong push could do as much work as a big cylinder with a gentle push. And if the pressure was high enough, you might not need a condenser at all. You could simply let the used steam blow out into the open air.","That second idea is very important. Watt's whole improvement had been the separate condenser, which made a vacuum. A high-pressure engine that just exhausted its steam into the air threw away the benefit of the vacuum, but it also got rid of the condenser, the cold-water tank, the air pump and much of the plumbing. What was left was a compact package: a boiler, a cylinder, a piston and a crank. Trevithick's engines made a loud 'puff' with every stroke as the spent steam escaped, so people called them 'puffers'.","Trevithick's boilers were also a new design. Instead of a separate furnace under a big boiler, he put the fire inside an iron tube that ran through the water, so the hot gases heated the water from within. He made the boiler cylindrical, a shape that is much better at holding pressure, like a can of fizzy drink. Around 1812 he developed this idea into what engineers came to call the 'Cornish boiler'. It was stronger and more efficient than the old wagon-shaped ones, and it is one of the reasons Trevithick could safely use more pressure than his rivals had dared."]},{"heading":"Christmas Eve on Camborne Hill","paragraphs":["Picture Camborne on 24 December 1801. It is winter, dark early, and probably damp, because it is Cornwall. In a workshop near what is now Fore Street, Trevithick and his cousin and partner Andrew Vivian have built something strange: a steam engine on wheels, made not to pump water but to carry people. Local people later called it the 'Puffing Devil', because of the noise and smoke it made.","That evening, the machine set off up Fore Street and then up Camborne Hill, from Camborne Cross towards the village of Beacon. Accounts say it carried six passengers, with Vivian steering. For people who had only ever been pulled by horses, it must have been astonishing and a little frightening to feel a machine pull them uphill by itself, hissing and puffing in the dark. A Cornish folk song, 'Going up Camborne Hill, coming down', is often linked to the event, and the town still celebrates Trevithick Day every year.","The Puffing Devil was not built to last, though, and its career was short. A few days later, it broke down after crossing a gully in the road. The operators pushed it under a shelter and went off to a nearby pub to eat and warm up, but they left the fire burning in the firebox. With nobody adding water, the boiler boiled dry. The iron grew red hot, and the whole machine was destroyed by fire. Trevithick blamed the operators, not the design, and he was probably right: a boiler always needs water covering its hot surfaces.","That accident teaches an important lesson about steam. A boiler is safe only while its plates are kept cool by water on the other side. Once the water level falls below the hot parts, the metal can soften, weaken and fail. Engineers later added glass water gauges so drivers could see the level, and 'fusible plugs', little plugs of soft metal that melt if the metal gets too hot, letting steam into the fire to warn the crew and put it out. Trevithick himself began using such plugs.","Trevithick did not give up. In 1803 he built a second road vehicle, the London Steam Carriage, and drove it through the streets of London with passengers aboard. It looked like a large stagecoach perched on enormous wheels, with the engine at the back. It worked, but nobody wanted to buy one. The roads of the time were rough and muddy, horses were cheap and familiar, and a heavy, noisy steam carriage frightened people and horses alike. Trevithick sold the engine to power a mill instead. Road steam had to wait for better roads and better machines, a story told in a later part of this Deep Dive."]},{"heading":"Puffers at Work, and a Bang at Greenwich","paragraphs":["Trevithick's real success was not road carriages but small stationary engines, meaning engines that stayed in one place. In 1802 he built an experimental high-pressure engine at the Coalbrookdale ironworks in Shropshire, the same valley where Abraham Darby had pioneered smelting iron with coke almost a century earlier. Reports say the boiler there worked at about 145 pounds per square inch, about ten times the pressure of the air around us. For comparison, a Watt engine's boiler usually ran at only a few pounds per square inch above the atmosphere. Trevithick was not just nudging the pressure up. He was multiplying it.","Why does more pressure give more power from a smaller engine? Think about the force on a piston. Force equals pressure times area. If you double the pressure, you get the same force from a piston with half the area. Raise the pressure tenfold, and a cylinder much narrower than Watt's can still deliver a powerful shove. A smaller cylinder means a smaller engine, less iron, less weight and less cost. That is the whole secret of the high-pressure engine: same power, much less bulk.","Puffer engines became popular for jobs where a big beam engine was too expensive or too awkward. They wound ropes up and down mine shafts, crushed ore, drove rolling mills, ran small factories and were even fitted to barges. Because they had no condenser, they did not need a big supply of cooling water, so they could work in places with little water. Some were mounted on wheeled frames and dragged from job to job, early versions of what were later called portable engines. Trevithick also sent engines abroad. In 1816 he sailed to Peru to work with high-pressure engines at the silver mines of Cerro de Pasco, high in the Andes, where thin mountain air made the old atmospheric engines much weaker. He spent about eleven adventurous years in South America and came home almost penniless.","High pressure had a dangerous side, just as Watt had feared. In 1803, one of Trevithick's engines, being used to pump water at Greenwich near London, exploded. Four men were killed. The boy left in charge had reportedly gone off to catch eels and asked a labourer to watch the engine; the safety valve had been fastened down, so pressure built up until the boiler burst. Boulton and Watt pointed to the disaster as proof that strong steam was reckless. Trevithick responded by adding a second safety valve that could not be tampered with easily, and a fusible plug. Over the following century, boiler explosions would remain a serious danger, a story told honestly in Part 15.","So who was right, Watt or Trevithick? In a way, both. Watt was right that high-pressure boilers needed better materials, better workmanship and careful rules. Trevithick was right that high pressure was the future. Almost every steam engine built after the middle of the 1800s, and every modern power-station turbine, uses steam at pressures far higher than anything either of them imagined."]},{"heading":"Across the Ocean: Oliver Evans","paragraphs":["At almost the same time, on the other side of the Atlantic, an American inventor was reaching the same conclusions on his own. Oliver Evans was born in 1755 near Newport, Delaware, the son of a farmer. As a young apprentice to a wagon maker, he read every book on mechanics he could find, sometimes, it is said, by the light of burning wood shavings because candles cost money.","Evans first became famous for something that had nothing to do with steam. In the 1780s he designed an automatic flour mill. Grain was carried up by bucket elevators, moved sideways by screw conveyors, ground, cooled and sifted, with very little human lifting. It was one of the earliest continuous, automated production lines, and it saved millers a great deal of labour. George Washington had the mill on his Mount Vernon estate converted to Evans's system in 1791.","Evans had dreamed about strong steam since his youth. He argued that high pressure would give more power for less fuel and money, and around 1801 to 1804 he built working high-pressure engines in Philadelphia. Around 1806 he set up a factory there called the Mars Works, which built engines for mills, sawmills and, later, steamboats. In 1805 he published a book with a strange title, The Abortion of the Young Steam Engineer's Guide. The odd name was his way of complaining that he could not afford to publish the full guide he had planned. Even so, it spread his ideas.","His most eye-catching machine appeared on 13 July 1805. The Philadelphia Board of Health had asked him to build a steam dredge, a machine to scoop mud and sand from the city's docks. Evans built a flat-bottomed boat about 30 feet (9 metres) long and weighing roughly 17 tons, and he gave it a fancy Greek-sounding name: Oruktor Amphibolos, meaning roughly 'amphibious digger'. Because his workshop was some distance from the river, he fitted the boat with wheels and drove it through the streets, powered by its own engine. The first set of wheels collapsed under the weight and had to be replaced. At the Schuylkill River it was launched and, Evans said, paddled to the harbour.","Here we must be careful. Evans often described the Oruktor as a great success, and he is sometimes called the builder of America's first self-propelled road vehicle. But few eyewitness accounts survive, his own versions of the story changed over the years, and historians doubt it travelled far on land with its small engine. It was not much use as a dredge either, and the Board of Health had it broken up for parts in 1808. What is clear is that Evans proved high-pressure engines could work in America, and that idea took off. On the great rivers of the American West, such as the Mississippi and Ohio, steamboats came to rely on compact high-pressure engines, because they were light, cheap and did not need cold water for a condenser. Evans died in 1819, the same year as James Watt."]},{"heading":"Using Steam Twice: Woolf's Compound Engine","paragraphs":["High-pressure engines that puffed their steam into the air had one big weakness. When the steam left the cylinder, it was still hot and still had plenty of pressure. All that leftover energy simply drifted away in a white cloud. A Cornish engineer named Arthur Woolf asked a clever question: why not use the steam a second time before throwing it away?","Woolf was born in Camborne in 1766, the same town as Trevithick. He went to London as a young man and worked as an engine builder, including in breweries, which used steam engines to pump water and grind malt. In 1804 he patented his idea, now called the compound engine. Steam from the boiler first entered a small high-pressure cylinder and pushed its piston. Then, instead of escaping, it passed into a second, much larger cylinder, where it expanded further and pushed a second piston. Finally it went to a Watt-style condenser, which made a vacuum and squeezed out the last bit of usefulness. In Woolf's design, both cylinders worked together on the same end of the engine's beam.","Why does the second cylinder need to be bigger? Because by the time the steam reaches it, the pressure has dropped. Lower pressure gives less push per square centimetre, so to get a useful force you need more area. That is why compound engines always have cylinders that grow in size, like a family of brothers and sisters lined up by height.","Woolf's engines were tricky to build well, and his early claims about how much coal they saved were too hopeful. But the principle was sound, and it became one of the most important ideas in steam engineering. Later in the 1800s, compound engines with two, three and even four stages, called triple- and quadruple-expansion engines, powered ocean liners and cargo ships across the world, saving huge amounts of coal on long voyages. Woolf himself went home to Cornwall in 1811 and became the chief engineer at Harvey and Company of Hayle, one of the great Cornish engine-building firms. That connection will matter in a moment, when we meet one of the biggest steam engines ever built."]},{"heading":"The Cornish Engine and the Giant of Haarlemmermeer","paragraphs":["Back in Cornwall, the mines kept getting deeper, and mine owners kept wanting to burn less of that expensive Welsh coal. Out of their need grew the Cornish engine, a kind of beam pumping engine that combined Watt's ideas with Trevithick's strong steam. It used a Cornish boiler to make steam at higher pressure than Watt's engines. Its cleverest trick was called expansive working. A valve let steam into the cylinder for only the first part of the piston's stroke, then shut. The trapped steam kept expanding, like a spring uncoiling, and kept pushing the piston the rest of the way. Less steam was used for each stroke, but the work was almost as great. Finally a condenser created a vacuum on the other side.","Cornish engineers also wrapped their cylinders, pipes and boilers in thick layers of insulation, called lagging, and surrounded the cylinder with a jacket of hot steam, so that as little heat as possible leaked away. On a cold day, visitors were surprised to find a Cornish engine house warm and quiet, with the great beam nodding slowly.","How did people know which engine was best? From about 1810 a group of mine owners published monthly figures in a report known as Lean's Engine Reporter, after the engineer Joel Lean who compiled it. Engines were rated by their 'duty', the amount of water they could lift, counted in millions of pounds raised one foot, for every bushel of coal burned. A bushel was a measure of about 94 pounds (roughly 43 kilograms) of coal. Early Watt engines achieved a duty of around 20 million, and later ones over 30 million. Between 1810 and 1840, the average duty of Cornish engines rose from around 20 million to about 90 million, and a few record-setting engines claimed even more. Publishing the numbers created friendly rivalry between mines, and that competition drove engines to become better and better.","Cornish engines were sold all over the world, and the greatest of them all was built not for a mine but for a lake. In the Netherlands, much of the land lies below sea level. Near the cities of Haarlem, Amsterdam and Leiden lay the Haarlemmermeer, a large lake that had grown over centuries as storms ate away its peat shores. In 1836 storms drove its waters right up to the edges of Amsterdam and Leiden. The Dutch government decided the lake had to be drained, and instead of using hundreds of windmills, it chose steam.","Three huge pumping stations were built: the Leeghwater, the Cruquius and the Lynden. The Cruquius, named after an 18th-century surveyor, Nicolaas Kruik, who had promoted draining the lake, was built in 1849 and started regular work in 1850. Its engine was made by Harvey and Company of Hayle in Cornwall, Arthur Woolf's old firm. It was a compound engine with an unusual design: a high-pressure cylinder 2.1 metres (84 inches) across sat inside a ring-shaped low-pressure cylinder about 3.66 metres (144 inches, or 12 feet) across. That outer cylinder is one of the largest steam-engine cylinders ever made. A family could stand inside it. Around the engine, eight great iron beams stretched out like the spokes of a wheel, each one working its own pump.","Together, the three stations pumped the lake dry by 1852, creating a new stretch of farmland that is now home to towns, farms and part of Amsterdam's Schiphol Airport. The Cruquius engine kept pumping until 1933. Today it is a museum, and the giant engine can be seen moving again, driven gently by a hydraulic system rather than steam. It stands as a monument to what Cornish high-pressure steam could do.","High pressure also made something else possible. Because a high-pressure engine was small and light for its power, it could be placed on wheels and made to pull itself along iron rails. In 1804, in Wales, a Trevithick engine became the first railway locomotive to haul a train along rails, and others soon followed. That thrilling story belongs to the separate Railways Deep Dive, so we will leave the rails there. Instead, our story heads north, to the cotton towns of Lancashire, where turning steam engines were about to pull the factory away from the riverbank and plant it in the heart of the city."]}],"deepDive":"Why did high pressure matter so much? The work a steam engine can do depends on how much the steam expands and how big the pressure difference is across the piston. Force on a piston equals pressure times area, so raising boiler pressure from a few pounds per square inch above the atmosphere (typical of Watt engines) to around 50 to 150 psi (as in Trevithick's experiments) let engineers shrink cylinders dramatically for the same power. That made engines lighter, cheaper and movable, opening the way to portable engines, river steamboats and locomotives. But non-condensing 'puffers' threw away the vacuum that gave Watt engines their economy, so in fuel terms they were often wasteful. The really big efficiency gains came from combining both ideas: high-pressure steam admitted for only part of the stroke and allowed to expand (expansive working), followed by condensation. Thermodynamics, which had not yet been invented (see Part 16), later explained why: the efficiency of a heat engine depends on the difference between the temperature at which heat enters and the temperature at which it leaves, and higher-pressure steam is hotter steam. Cornish mine owners measured progress with 'duty', millions of pounds of water raised one foot per bushel of coal (about 94 pounds). Watt engines reached roughly 20 to 30 million; Cornish engines improved from around 20 million in 1810 to about 90 million by 1840, and a few record trials claimed even more. Historians treat such record figures cautiously, because engines were sometimes carefully tuned for tests, coal quality varied, and gains often faded in everyday use. Credit is also debated. Trevithick and Oliver Evans developed high-pressure engines independently and at almost the same time, and both drew on earlier ideas, including Watt's own patent descriptions of expansive working, which Watt rarely used. Arthur Woolf's compound engine was ahead of the materials of its day, and compounding only became widespread later in the century, especially at sea with triple-expansion engines. Finally, the safety argument was real: boiler explosions became a major hazard of the 1800s, and the push for inspection, stronger boiler designs and safety codes grew directly out of the high-pressure revolution.","funFact":"The outer cylinder of the Cruquius engine in the Netherlands is about 3.66 metres (12 feet) across, wide enough for several people to stand inside it.","check":{"q":"What happened to Trevithick's Puffing Devil a few days after its Christmas Eve 1801 run?","a":"It was destroyed by fire when it was left with the fire burning and the boiler boiled dry","wrong":["It exploded while climbing Camborne Hill","It was sold to drive a London mill","It was shipped to Peru to work in a silver mine"]},"sources":[{"title":"Puffing Devil","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puffing_Devil"},{"title":"Oruktor Amphibolos","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oruktor_Amphibolos"},{"title":"Arthur Woolf","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arthur_Woolf"},{"title":"Cornish engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cornish_engine"},{"title":"Museum De Cruquius","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_De_Cruquius"},{"title":"Richard Trevithick","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Trevithick"},{"title":"Oliver Evans","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oliver_Evans"},{"title":"Haarlemmermeer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Haarlemmermeer"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/11-1.jpg","alt":"Portrait of the Cornish engineer Richard Trevithick, painted by John Linnell in 1816.","file":"Richard Trevithick portrait.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Richard_Trevithick_portrait.jpg","author":"John Linnell","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/11-2.jpg","alt":"A working replica of Trevithick's Puffing Devil steam carriage on the street in Camborne, Cornwall, on Trevithick Day 2024.","file":"Puffing Devil replica, Trevithick Day, Basset Street, Camborne, Cornwall - April 2024.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puffing_Devil_replica,_Trevithick_Day,_Basset_Street,_Camborne,_Cornwall_-_April_2024.jpg","author":"Mutney","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/11-3.jpg","alt":"Top of the giant Cornish engine cylinder inside the Cruquius pumping station, built by Harvey & Co of Hayle, Cornwall.","file":"Gemaal de Cruquius - centrale zuiger laag.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gemaal_de_Cruquius_-_centrale_zuiger_laag.jpg","author":"Quistnix at Dutch Wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 2.5"}],"words":3874},{"n":12,"title":"Mills, Factories and Smoky Cities","when":"1770s – 1870s","summary":"Long ago, people spun thread and wove cloth at home. Then giant mills full of machines appeared, first beside rivers and then, thanks to steam engines, right in the middle of cities like Manchester. One huge engine could turn hundreds of machines, and life began to run by the factory clock.","sections":[{"heading":"Spinning by the Fireside","paragraphs":["Step into a farmhouse in Lancashire in about 1750. In the corner, a woman sits at a spinning wheel, drawing out fluffy fibres of cotton or wool and twisting them into thread. Her children card the raw fibre, combing it into soft rolls ready for spinning. In the next room, her husband works a wooden handloom, throwing the shuttle back and forth to weave cloth. Nobody tells them when to start or stop. They work around the farm chores, the seasons and the daylight.","This was the cottage industry, also called the domestic or putting-out system. A merchant, sometimes called a clothier, delivered raw fibre to families in villages, came back later to collect the yarn or finished cloth, and paid them for each piece. Spinning was slow. It took several spinners to keep a single weaver busy, and as weavers got faster with John Kay's flying shuttle, invented in 1733, the shortage of yarn grew worse.","Then came a burst of inventions. James Hargreaves's spinning jenny, from the mid-1760s, let one person spin several threads at once, but it was still small and hand-powered, so it fitted in a cottage. Richard Arkwright's water frame, patented in 1769, was different. It spun strong cotton thread using sets of rollers, and it needed more power than a person could supply. Samuel Crompton's 'mule' of 1779 combined ideas from both and spun very fine, strong yarn. These machines were too big, too many and too power-hungry for a cottage. They needed a new kind of building, filled with machines and workers, driven by one central source of power. They needed a factory."]},{"heading":"Arkwright's Mill by the River","paragraphs":["Richard Arkwright was born in Preston in 1732, the youngest of a large and poor family, and began his working life as a barber and wig-maker. He was not a great inventor in the way Watt was. Historians argue about how much of the water frame he designed himself, and some of his patents were later overturned in court. But he was a brilliant organiser and businessman, and he understood that machines, buildings, workers and money could be combined into a system.","In 1771 Arkwright and his partners built a cotton-spinning mill at Cromford in Derbyshire, in a steep valley of the River Derwent. He chose the spot carefully. A stream of water flowed out of the Cromford Sough, a tunnel that drained the nearby lead mines, and it joined the Bonsall Brook. Because the water came from deep underground, it is said to have rarely frozen in winter, so the waterwheel could turn all year. The wheel turned shafts and gears that drove rows of water frames on several floors.","The first mill was five storeys high. It started with about 200 workers, most of them women and children. The youngest children were reportedly as young as seven, though Arkwright later raised the minimum age to ten. From 1772 the mill ran day and night in two shifts of twelve hours each. The gates were shut at six in the morning and six in the evening, and workers who arrived late were locked out, losing a day's pay and fined another day's pay on top.","Cromford was in the countryside, and there were not enough local people to run it, so Arkwright built houses for workers, which still stand on North Street, as well as a chapel, a market and later a school. Families moved there from far away, and he especially liked families with many children who could work in the mill. By the time Arkwright died in 1792, he employed about 1,000 workers at Cromford and had become one of the richest men in Britain. The Derwent Valley Mills are now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","Notice something about Cromford, though. It was in a narrow valley, miles from big towns, because that is where the falling water was. Water-powered factories had to be built beside fast rivers and streams, often in remote places. Raw cotton had to be hauled in and finished yarn hauled out along bad roads. And in a dry summer, or a hard frost, the wheels might slow or stop. If only a factory could make its own power anywhere it liked."]},{"heading":"The Engine Moves into the Mill","paragraphs":["The first steps were cautious. In the early 1780s Arkwright himself opened a cotton mill, Shudehill Mill on Miller Street in Manchester, close to the markets and the workers. It used a steam engine, but in an indirect way: the engine pumped water up to a reservoir so it could flow back down and turn an ordinary waterwheel. It was inefficient, but it shows how badly mill owners wanted to escape the riverbank.","After 1782, Boulton and Watt's new rotative engines, which could turn a shaft directly using the sun-and-planet gear described in Part 9, made a far better solution possible. In 1785 the Robinson family, who ran cotton mills at Papplewick in Nottinghamshire, installed a Boulton and Watt rotative engine to drive their spinning machinery. It is generally recognised as the first steam engine to power a cotton mill directly. According to local accounts, the Robinsons had a special reason: a local landowner had been changing the flow of water upstream for his ornamental lakes, leaving their waterwheels short of water. Steam gave them independence.","Other mill owners soon followed, including Arkwright and his partners and the new spinning firms of Manchester. By 1800 Boulton and Watt had supplied dozens of engines to cotton and other textile mills, and many cheaper engines built by rival makers were at work too. After the Watt patent expired in 1800, local engineering firms in Lancashire began building mill engines in great numbers.","The change this made was enormous, but simple to state. With a steam engine, a factory could be built wherever there was coal, workers, roads, canals and customers. It no longer needed a waterfall. Mills began to cluster in towns, especially on the coalfields of Lancashire and Yorkshire, and in places served by canals, which could bring coal cheaply. The Bridgewater Canal, opened in 1761 to carry coal from the Duke of Bridgewater's mines at Worsley into Manchester, had already halved the price of coal there. Cheap coal plus steam engines plus thousands of workers turned Manchester into the most famous factory town on Earth."]},{"heading":"One Engine, a Thousand Machines","paragraphs":["How could one steam engine run hundreds of spinning machines spread across five or six floors? The answer was a system of spinning rods and belts called line shafting, sometimes called millwork.","The engine usually lived in its own tall engine house at the side or end of the mill, next to the boiler house and its chimney. Its great flywheel, sometimes many metres across, turned a main shaft. In many mills, the power went up through the building by a vertical shaft with gears at each floor. Later mills often used a 'rope race', a narrow tower where thick cotton ropes ran in grooves from the flywheel up to each floor, like a giant drive belt.","On each floor, a long iron shaft ran along the ceiling, hanging from brackets, turning steadily the whole length of the room. Fixed to it were pulleys, wheels with a flat or curved rim. A loop of leather belt ran down from each pulley to a pulley on a machine below. Big pulleys and small pulleys could change the speed, just as gears on a bicycle do. To stop one machine without stopping the whole mill, a worker shifted its belt sideways onto a 'loose pulley' that spun freely without driving anything.","Line shafting was clever, but it had problems. Every bearing, gear and belt wasted some power through friction, and in many systems a quarter or more of the engine's power was lost before it reached the machines. The belts flapped and slapped, the shafts rumbled, oil dripped from the bearings, and the air was thick with cotton fluff. Worst of all, the moving belts and shafts were unguarded for a long time, and workers, including children, could have hair, sleeves or hands caught, causing terrible injuries.","Imagine standing on the floor of a big spinning room in about 1840. The room is as long as a football pitch, lit by rows of tall windows. Above your head, the shaft turns with a low hum. Dozens of belts slant down to the mules, which roll forward and back across the floor, drawing out and twisting hundreds of threads at a time. The air is warm and damp, because cotton thread breaks less easily in moist air, and fluff drifts like snow. The noise is so loud that many workers learned to talk by lip-reading, a habit some kept for the rest of their lives.","Because the whole mill hung on one engine, the engine was precious. Mill engines were often beautifully painted and polished, with brass fittings and decorative columns, and owners sometimes gave them names, often after members of their family. The engine tenter, the worker who looked after it, was an important person. If the engine stopped, the whole mill fell silent and nobody earned anything. Line shafts stayed in use until the early 1900s, when factories gradually switched to electric motors, often one on each machine, powered by electricity that, as later parts explain, was itself usually made with steam."]},{"heading":"Cottonopolis","paragraphs":["In the 1770s, Manchester was a busy market town with a few tens of thousands of people. By 1851 it had over 300,000, and the surrounding towns had grown just as fast. People poured in from the countryside of Lancashire and Cheshire, from Ireland, from Scotland and beyond, looking for work. In the 1800s Manchester gained a new nickname: Cottonopolis, the city of cotton.","Raw cotton arrived by ship at Liverpool, much of it grown on plantations in the southern United States by enslaved people, a dark connection explored in Part 15. It came up to Manchester by canal, and later by rail, to be spun and woven. Manchester's own cotton mills peaked at more than 100 in the 1850s, and as land in the city grew expensive, many more were built in nearby towns such as Oldham, Bolton, Rochdale, Stockport and Blackburn. Manchester itself became the great trading centre, packed with warehouses, banks and the Royal Exchange where cotton was bought and sold. By 1871, Manchester and its surrounding towns produced about a third of all the cotton goods in the world.","The district of Ancoats, beside the Rochdale Canal, became one of the world's first industrial suburbs. Huge mills such as Murrays' Mills, built from the late 1790s, rose six or seven storeys high, with tall chimneys pouring out coal smoke. A visitor arriving by night saw rows of mill windows blazing with light, and in the day saw a sky stained brown and grey.","Visitors came from all over the world to see this new kind of city. The French writer Alexis de Tocqueville, who visited in 1835, described Manchester as a place where human industry produced astonishing wealth and, side by side with it, shocking poverty, with filthy water running through the streets and the sun seen dimly through the smoke. Many visitors felt both excitement and alarm: this was the future, but was it a good one?","The smoke itself was a sign of steam. Every mill had its own boiler house burning coal day after day, and a mill chimney might stand 60 metres or more tall to carry the fumes away and to create a strong draught for the fire. Hundreds of chimneys together blackened buildings, washing hung out to dry and people's lungs. Rivers such as the Irwell and the Medlock carried dyes, sewage and waste from works along their banks. Cleaner air laws came only much later.","The workers had to live somewhere close, because they walked to work. Builders threw up rows of cheap houses as fast as they could, often 'back-to-backs', where two houses shared a back wall and had windows on only one side. Some of the poorest families lived in damp cellars. Streets were unpaved, drains were few, and many houses shared a single toilet or privy. Diseases such as cholera and typhus spread easily, and in the worst districts many children did not live past the age of five. In 1845 a young German, Friedrich Engels, published a famous description of these conditions. Part 15 looks at that dark side in more detail.","Yet the same cities were also full of energy and new ideas. Manchester produced engineers, scientists, newspapers, libraries, mechanics' institutes where workers could study in the evenings, and political movements that campaigned for votes and fairer laws. The scientist John Dalton, who developed atomic theory, taught in Manchester, and James Joule, whose work you will meet in Part 16, grew up nearby in Salford, the son of a brewer."]},{"heading":"Living by the Clock","paragraphs":["Perhaps the deepest change factories brought was a change in time itself. A handloom weaver at home could work hard for a few days and rest for one, start late, or stop to help with the harvest. In a steam mill, the engine started at a fixed hour and every machine began turning together. Workers had to be at their machines when it did.","So factory life ran by the clock and the bell. Mill bells or, later, steam whistles called workers in the early morning. Some towns had 'knocker-uppers', people paid to tap on bedroom windows with long poles to wake workers before dawn, since few families owned clocks. Gates were locked at the starting time, and latecomers were fined or sent home without pay. Inside, overseers enforced rules with fines for talking, whistling, opening a window or leaving a machine.","Days were long. In the early 1800s, twelve to fourteen hours a day, six days a week, was common, with short breaks for meals. Children were valued because they were cheap and small. 'Piecers' walked up and down beside spinning mules, joining broken threads, while 'scavengers' crawled underneath the moving machines to sweep away fluff. Many children were 'pauper apprentices', orphans or children of very poor families sent from parish workhouses to mills, where they lived in apprentice houses. Laws limiting children's work came slowly, starting in the early 1800s, as Part 15 describes.","Mill owners argued that discipline was necessary. A steam engine burned coal whether the machines were working or not, and expensive machinery earned money only while it was running, so every idle minute cost the owner. That is why owners wanted engines running as many hours as possible and why fines for lateness were so harsh. Some owners, such as Robert Owen at New Lanark in Scotland in the early 1800s, tried a different approach, refusing to employ very young children, providing schools and better housing, and showing that a mill could still make a profit. Owen's ideas inspired reformers, though few owners copied him fully.","Workers did not simply accept all this. They formed friendly societies to help each other when sick, campaigned for a ten-hour day, and organised early trade unions. Over the 1800s, hours slowly fell, the Saturday half-holiday spread, and by the late 1800s many mill towns shut down for a whole 'wakes week' each summer, when families crowded onto trains to seaside resorts such as Blackpool. The rhythm of modern life, with fixed working hours, weekends and holidays, grew partly out of the factory clock."]},{"heading":"Water Doesn't Give Up","paragraphs":["It would be easy to imagine that once steam appeared, every waterwheel stopped. That is not what happened. Water power was free once a wheel was built, while coal cost money every day. For decades, many mill owners stuck with water, and some used both, with a steam engine to help out when the river ran low.","Waterwheels kept getting better, too. Engineers built big iron wheels, some more than 20 metres across, and in 1827 the French engineer Benoit Fourneyron built an efficient water turbine, a forerunner of the turbines in today's hydroelectric dams. In the United States, the great textile city of Lowell in Massachusetts, founded in the 1820s, was built around canals and water power from the Merrimack River. Historians estimate that in Britain water still provided a large share of factory power into the 1830s, and steam did not clearly overtake it until around the middle of the 1800s. In America, water power stayed important even longer.","Many mill owners treated the two kinds of power as partners rather than rivals. A mill might run its waterwheel whenever the river was full and start up a small steam engine in dry summers or icy winters, so the machines never had to stop. Some engines were even used to pump water back up above the wheel, the trick Arkwright had tried in Manchester. In hilly districts of Yorkshire and Scotland, where fast streams were plentiful and coal was far away, water-powered mills kept working well into the late 1800s.","What tipped the balance was a combination of things: steam engines that used less coal, cheaper coal carried by canals and railways, mills in towns with no good rivers, and factories growing so large that no stream could drive them. The Industrial Revolution was a gradual change, not a sudden switch, and steam took over step by step through the 1800s."]},{"heading":"The Giant of Philadelphia","paragraphs":["Steam's spread to America brought fresh ideas. George Henry Corliss was born in New York State in 1817 and set up an engine works in Providence, Rhode Island. On 10 March 1849 he received a US patent for a new kind of valve gear. Instead of one sliding valve doing every job, a Corliss engine had four separate valves, one to let steam in and one to let it out at each end of the cylinder. They snapped open and shut quickly, and, cleverly, the engine's governor decided exactly when to cut off the steam on each stroke. If the load was light, steam was cut off early; if a mill suddenly needed more power, it was cut off later. Some historians note that a similar idea had been developed earlier by another American engineer, Frederick Sickels.","Corliss engines kept a very steady speed, which mattered in mills where an uneven speed could snap threads, and they used roughly 30 percent less fuel than ordinary engines of the time. Corliss even offered to be paid from the coal savings, a sales trick that echoes Boulton and Watt. His engines were copied all over the world and drove mills, waterworks and, later, electric generators.","His most famous engine was built for a party. In 1876 the United States celebrated 100 years since the Declaration of Independence with the Centennial Exhibition in Philadelphia. At its heart was Machinery Hall, filled with lathes, looms, printing presses, pumps and saws. Rather than give each one its own engine, the organisers had a single giant Corliss engine drive the whole hall through about a mile of overhead line shafting.","The Centennial Engine was a twin-cylinder beam engine that stood about 45 feet (14 metres) tall, with a flywheel 30 feet (about 9 metres) across. It produced about 1,400 horsepower. On 10 May 1876, President Ulysses S. Grant and Emperor Pedro II of Brazil turned the controls, and the great beams began to rock and the flywheel to turn, setting hundreds of machines in motion. Visitors were amazed at how quietly and smoothly it ran. Many people said it was the star of the whole exhibition.","After the fair, the engine went back to Providence, and about seven years later it was sold to the railway-carriage builder George Pullman to drive his factory near Chicago, where it worked until 1910 before being scrapped. By then, the world had changed. A hundred years earlier, a factory had been a mill by a stream in Derbyshire. Now steam drove whole halls of machines in a country that had barely existed when Watt patented his condenser.","And steam was not changing only cotton mills. It was blowing air into blast furnaces to make iron, driving hammers and lathes that made more machines, printing newspapers by the thousand, and reshaping whole countries. Historians have a name for that great transformation, and it is where we go next: the Industrial Revolution."]}],"deepDive":"Historians argue about how important steam really was to the early factory system. The first great factories, like Arkwright's Cromford Mill (1771) and Jedediah Strutt's mills at Belper, ran on water, and the factory as a way of organising work, with central power, supervision, shifts and discipline, existed before steam took over. Economic historians who have counted horsepower find that water remained a major source of industrial power in Britain into the 1830s, and steam's share grew decisively only in the middle decades of the century, helped by more efficient high-pressure and compound engines, cheaper coal carried by canals and railways, and ever-larger mills. Some, such as the economic historian Nicholas Crafts, have estimated that steam's measurable contribution to British economic growth was modest before about 1830 and much larger afterwards. So steam did not create the factory, but it set the factory free from geography, letting mills concentrate on coalfields and in cities like Manchester, Oldham and Bolton. That concentration had huge social effects: rapid urban growth, crowded and unhealthy housing, and new patterns of work and time. The historian E. P. Thompson's famous essay on 'time, work-discipline and industrial capitalism' (1967) argued that the factory clock reshaped people's sense of time itself. Engineering-wise, line-shaft drive was a hidden cost: friction in shafts, gears and belts often consumed a quarter or more of an engine's output, which is one reason electric motors, which could be placed on each machine, spread rapidly after about 1900. In America, the Corliss valve gear (patented 1849) was a key step, combining automatic variable cut-off with quick-acting valves to give steady speed and fuel savings of roughly 30 percent over fixed cut-off engines. The 1,400-horsepower Centennial Engine of 1876 became a symbol of American industrial confidence, even as most of its power was spent turning shafting rather than machines.","funFact":"At the 1876 Centennial Exhibition in Philadelphia, a single Corliss steam engine about 14 metres tall powered the machines of the entire Machinery Hall through roughly a mile of overhead shafting.","check":{"q":"Where was a steam engine first used to drive a cotton mill directly, in 1785?","a":"Papplewick, Nottinghamshire (the Robinson family's mill)","wrong":["Cromford, Derbyshire","Lowell, Massachusetts","Ancoats, Manchester"]},"sources":[{"title":"Cromford Mill","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cromford_Mill"},{"title":"Cottonopolis","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cottonopolis"},{"title":"Line shaft","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Line_shaft"},{"title":"Corliss steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Corliss_steam_engine"},{"title":"Cotton mill","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cotton_mill"},{"title":"Richard Arkwright","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Richard_Arkwright"},{"title":"Textile manufacture during the British Industrial Revolution","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Textile_manufacture_during_the_British_Industrial_Revolution"},{"title":"History of Manchester","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_Manchester"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/12-1.jpg","alt":"Richard Arkwright's first cotton mill at Cromford, Derbyshire, built in 1771 and still standing today.","file":"Cromford 1771 mill.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cromford_1771_mill.jpg","author":"chevin","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/12-2.jpg","alt":"An 1835 engraving of Murrays' cotton mills on Union Street, Manchester, with smoking chimneys beside the canal.","file":"Baines 1835-A&G Murray.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baines_1835-A%26G_Murray.png","author":"Baines","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/12-3.jpg","alt":"The huge Corliss Centennial Engine in Machinery Hall at the 1876 Centennial Exhibition in Philadelphia.","file":"The Corliss 'Centennial Engine' at the US Centennial Exhibition (1876).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Corliss_%27Centennial_Engine%27_at_the_US_Centennial_Exhibition_(1876).jpg","author":"Centennial Photographic Company","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3665},{"n":13,"title":"The Industrial Revolution Picks Up Steam","when":"About 1760 – 1900","summary":"Steam engines pumped water out of coal mines, coal melted iron, and iron built even more engines, in a giant loop that kept growing. Soon steam was hammering, printing newspapers and showing off in a huge glass palace in London. Then the new way of making things spread to Belgium, France, Germany, America and Japan.","sections":[{"heading":"A Revolution in Slow Motion","paragraphs":["When you hear the word 'revolution', you might picture something sudden: crowds in the street, a king losing his crown, everything changing in a week. The Industrial Revolution was not like that at all. It was more like a tide coming in. If you stood on the beach and watched for five minutes, you would hardly notice the water moving. Come back after a few hours, and the whole beach has disappeared.","Historians use the name 'Industrial Revolution' for the huge change in how people made things, which began in Britain in roughly the 1760s and is often said to have run until about the 1830s or 1840s, before spreading to other countries. During those years, more and more work moved from hands, homes and small workshops to machines, factories and mines. Instead of most people growing food on farms, more and more people made goods, dug coal, smelted iron and worked in towns. The name became popular in English in the 1880s, when the historian Arnold Toynbee gave lectures about it at Oxford, but French writers had used similar words decades earlier.","Not every historian likes the word 'revolution'. Some, such as John Clapham in the early 1900s and Nicholas Crafts more recently, pointed out that Britain's economy grew fairly slowly in those decades, maybe one or two percent a year, not in a sudden leap. In 1830, most British workers still did not work in factories. Lots of cloth was still woven on hand looms, many mills still turned with waterwheels (as we saw in the last part), and farm work still employed huge numbers of people. So the change was real and enormous, but it built up step by step over a lifetime and more.","Imagine a grandmother born in a Lancashire village in 1760. As a girl she might have spun wool by the fire and walked everywhere. By the time she was an old woman in the 1830s, her grandchildren might be working in a mill with a steam engine thumping in the basement, buying cheap printed cotton dresses, and reading newspapers printed by steam. She would have seen her world transformed, even though no single year felt like an earthquake.","Why did it start in Britain? Historians still argue about this, and there is no single answer. Britain had lots of coal close to the surface and close to the sea or rivers, so it could be moved cheaply. Wages for workers were fairly high compared with the price of coal, which made it worth paying for machines that saved labour. The country had a big trading network, banks, canals that were being dug in the 1760s and 1770s, a large market at home and overseas, and a culture of tinkerers, instrument makers and engineers who shared ideas in clubs and societies, like the Lunar Society of Birmingham that Matthew Boulton belonged to. Part of Britain's trade and wealth also came from its empire and from the enslaved labour that grew cotton and sugar, a hard truth we will look at honestly in Part 15.","Steam was not the only cause of the Industrial Revolution. In fact, the first great spinning mills ran on water. But steam became its beating heart, because it could provide power anywhere, at any time, in any amount you could afford coal for. And steam connected everything else together, as we are about to see in one of the most important loops in history."]},{"heading":"Coal, Iron and Steam: The Great Loop","paragraphs":["Think of three friends who each make the others stronger. That is how coal, iron and steam worked together.","Step one: steam engines pumped water out of coal mines, so miners could dig deeper and bring up more coal. Step two: coal (turned into a fuel called coke) was used to melt iron out of iron ore in furnaces, so iron became cheaper and more plentiful. Step three: that cheaper iron was used to build more steam engines, plus pumps, rails, bridges and machines. And more steam engines meant more pumped mines, more coal, more iron, and round and round the loop went, getting bigger every turn.","The loop needed one big discovery first. For thousands of years, iron had been smelted using charcoal, which is made by slowly burning wood. But making charcoal needed huge amounts of wood, and as we saw in Part 1, Britain's forests were shrinking. In 1709, at Coalbrookdale in Shropshire, an ironmaster named Abraham Darby began fuelling his blast furnace with coke instead. Coke is made by baking coal to drive off its tar and gases, leaving a hard, almost pure carbon fuel. Coke was cheaper, mainly because it took far less labour than cutting trees and burning charcoal, and Britain had plenty of coal.","A blast furnace is a tall tower filled with layers of iron ore, fuel and limestone. To get hot enough to melt iron, it needs a powerful, steady blast of air blown in at the bottom, just as blowing on a campfire makes it blaze. For centuries, that blast came from big leather bellows worked by waterwheels. That meant ironworks had to sit beside rivers, and in a dry summer the furnace might have to stop.","Steam changed that, at first in a roundabout way. At Coalbrookdale in 1742, a steam engine was used to pump water back up into a reservoir above the works, so that the waterwheel could keep turning even when the stream was low. Later, engineers connected steam engines directly to huge blowing cylinders, which were giant air pumps. One of the first Boulton & Watt engines, in 1776, blew air for John Wilkinson's furnace at New Willey in Shropshire. Now an ironworks could be built wherever there was coal and ore, and it could run all year round.","More inventions kept the loop spinning faster. In 1784, Henry Cort patented 'puddling', a way of stirring melted iron in a special furnace to make strong wrought iron using coal instead of charcoal. In 1828, James Beaumont Neilson in Scotland patented the 'hot blast', which heated the air before it was blown into the furnace and cut the fuel needed by about a third.","The results were astonishing. In 1720, British furnaces using charcoal made about 20,000 tons of iron a year. By 1806, furnaces using coke were producing around 250,000 tons. By the 1790s Britain, which once had to import much of its iron from places like Sweden and Russia, had become an exporter of iron instead. Iron began appearing everywhere: in the world's first big cast-iron bridge, the Iron Bridge over the River Severn near Coalbrookdale, put up in 1779 and opened to traffic in 1781; in pipes, rails, pots, machine frames, columns and, of course, steam-engine cylinders. Later in the 1800s, new methods such as Henry Bessemer's converter (1856) made cheap steel, an even stronger metal, and the loop grew bigger again."]},{"heading":"Black Diamonds: Counting the Coal","paragraphs":["If the Industrial Revolution had a fuel gauge, it would be the amount of coal Britain dug out of the ground. People sometimes called coal 'black diamonds', because so much wealth came from it. The numbers tell an amazing story.","Around 1700, Britain mined just under 3 million tons of coal a year. By the 1770s, the figure had climbed to roughly 6 million tons. By 1815 it was about 16 million tons, and by 1830 more than 30 million. Then it really took off. By about 1900, British mines were producing over 200 million tonnes a year, and output reached its all-time peak in 1913, at about 287 million tonnes. That is roughly a hundred times more than in 1700.","Where did all this coal go? Some went into homes for cooking and heating, as it had for centuries. But more and more fed the new industrial world: ironworks and their blast furnaces; factories, mills and workshops with steam engines; gasworks that baked coal to make gas for street lights; brickworks, potteries, glassworks and breweries; steamships and, of course, railway locomotives. Britain also sold huge amounts of coal abroad, shipping it to ports all over the world.","Steam was needed to get the coal out, too. As mines went deeper, steam engines did more than pump water. They wound cages and tubs up and down the shafts, and later they drove big fans that sucked stale air and dangerous gases out of the tunnels. Before steam-powered fans, miners often kept a fire burning at the bottom of one shaft so that the rising hot air would pull fresh air through the mine. In Belgium, steam pumps helped some mines go from about 220 metres deep in 1790 to an average of about 440 metres by 1866, with one pit more than 1,000 metres deep.","More coal meant more miners. Around 1841, Britain had about 216,000 coal miners. By 1920, the industry employed more than 1.1 million people. Mining was hard and dangerous work, and for many years it included children. We will look carefully at that side of the story in Part 15.","Coal gave Britain a special advantage. A single tonne of coal holds more energy than a strong horse could deliver in a couple of years of hard daily work, even though early engines wasted most of it as heat. By digging up the remains of ancient forests that had been squashed underground for hundreds of millions of years, Britain was in a sense borrowing energy from the deep past, and steam engines were the machines that turned that stored sunshine into motion."]},{"heading":"Machines That Make Machines","paragraphs":["Here is a puzzle. To build a good steam engine, you need parts that fit together very accurately: a perfectly round cylinder, a piston that slides snugly inside it, screws and bolts that fit their nuts. But in the 1700s, almost every part was shaped by hand with files, chisels and hammers. Two screws made by the same craftsman might not even fit the same nut. How do you get accurate parts? You build accurate machines that make them. Engineers call these 'machine tools': machines whose job is to cut, drill, plane and shape metal to make other machines.","The star of this story is Henry Maudslay, born in 1771 in Woolwich, near London. As a boy he worked filling cartridges at the Royal Arsenal and then in its blacksmith's shop, where he became famous for his skill with metal. As a young man he worked for the inventor Joseph Bramah, and he helped build Bramah's famous 'unpickable' lock. Bramah put one in his shop window with a sign offering a reward to anyone who could pick it. It stayed unpicked for 47 years, and Maudslay also designed special tools so the locks could be made in large numbers at a sensible price.","Around 1800, in his own workshop in London, Maudslay built one of the first practical screw-cutting lathes. A lathe spins a piece of metal while a sharp cutting tool shaves it into shape. Maudslay's lathe held the cutting tool firmly in a sliding rest, moved along by an accurate master screw and a set of change gears, so it could cut screw threads that were exactly the same every time. He did not invent each of these pieces, but he combined them brilliantly. Suddenly, nuts and bolts could be standardised: any nut of a given size would fit any bolt of that size.","Maudslay also worked with the engineer Marc Isambard Brunel (father of the famous Isambard Kingdom Brunel) to build 42 machines for the Royal Navy's block mills at Portsmouth, finished in the 1800s. They made pulley blocks for sailing ships, which needed huge numbers of them. With the machines, about ten unskilled workers could do the work that had needed about 110 skilled craftsmen. It was one of the earliest examples of a factory where a series of special machines did each step of a job. Maudslay also built a measuring machine, a bench micrometer, accurate to about one ten-thousandth of an inch. His workers nicknamed it 'the Lord Chancellor', because, like a top judge, it settled any argument about whether a piece of work was accurate.","Maudslay's workshop became a kind of engineering school. Among those who trained there were Joseph Whitworth, who in 1841 proposed a standard system of screw threads that was adopted across Britain; Richard Roberts, who built improved machine tools and a self-acting spinning mule; and a young Scot named James Nasmyth.","Nasmyth, born in Edinburgh in 1808, was the son of a painter and had been building model steam engines since boyhood. He set up his own works, the Bridgewater Foundry at Patricroft near Manchester. In 1839 a problem landed on his desk: the engineers building the giant ship Great Britain wanted an iron paddle-wheel shaft about 30 inches (76 centimetres) thick. No hammer in Britain was big enough to forge it. On 24 November 1839, Nasmyth sketched an answer in his notebook: a heavy iron block, the ram, lifted straight up by a steam cylinder above it, then dropped onto the hot metal. The ship ended up with a propeller instead of paddle wheels, so that shaft was never needed, but the idea was too good to waste.","Here the story has a twist about fair credit. In France, an engineer named François Bourdon at the Schneider works at Le Creusot had a similar idea, and he built the first working steam hammer in 1840. When Nasmyth visited Le Creusot in April 1842, Bourdon is said to have taken him to the forge to 'see his own child'. Nasmyth patented his own design on 9 December 1842. He built his first hammer at his own Patricroft works, and in 1843 the Low Moor ironworks in Bradford took an improved version. His works manager, Robert Wilson, later added a clever self-acting valve gear that let the operator choose exactly how hard each blow would be. Nasmyth was a brilliant self-promoter, so for a long time he got most of the credit, but historians now recognise both men.","The steam hammer was astonishingly controllable. Nasmyth loved to show it off by placing an egg in a wineglass and bringing the ram down so gently that it cracked the egg without breaking the glass. Then, with the next blow, he could make the whole building shake. Steam hammers grew to giant sizes: the Krupp works in Germany built one in 1861 nicknamed 'Fritz', known as a 50-ton hammer. They forged ship shafts, anchors, locomotive parts and armour plate. Machines were now making the heavy parts for other machines, and the steam engine was helping to build its own children."]},{"heading":"Printing by Steam","paragraphs":["Picture a London printing shop in 1800. Two men work a wooden hand press. One dabs sticky ink onto the metal type with leather balls on handles. The other lays a sheet of paper on top, slides the bed under a heavy flat plate, and heaves on a long bar to press the paper down. Lift, peel, hang the sheet to dry, repeat. A good team might manage only a few hundred sheets an hour. The press itself was not so different from the one Johannes Gutenberg had used in Mainz about 350 years earlier. For a busy newspaper that needed thousands of copies by morning, that was a real problem.","The answer came from a German printer named Friedrich Koenig, born in Eisleben in 1774. He moved to London in 1806, because Britain had the money and engineering skill he needed, and he worked with a German watchmaker and engineer named Andreas Friedrich Bauer. Koenig received a patent in 1810, and his first trial machine ran in April 1812.","Koenig's big idea was to stop pressing the paper flat. Instead, the sheet was wrapped around a rotating cylinder, which rolled over the inked type as the type bed slid back and forth beneath it. Rollers, rather than leather balls, spread the ink, and a steam engine drove the whole thing, so the workers only had to feed in blank sheets and take away printed ones.","One of Koenig's visitors was John Walter, the owner of The Times newspaper. He ordered two of the new machines. According to the story told by The Times itself, they were set up quietly in a building next door, partly because Walter feared the hand pressmen might protest or damage machines that threatened their jobs. On the morning of 29 November 1814, readers of The Times opened their paper to discover that it told them something remarkable: the very sheet they were holding had been printed by steam-driven machinery. The machines could print about 1,100 sheets an hour, several times faster than a hand press.","Koenig's later machines printed both sides of the paper in one pass. In 1817, after a disagreement with his London business partner, the printer Thomas Bensley, Koenig went home to Germany. He and Bauer set up a factory in an old abandoned monastery at Oberzell near Würzburg. Their company, Koenig & Bauer, still makes printing presses today, more than 200 years later.","The results reached almost every home. As taxes on newspapers were cut (the British stamp duty on newspapers was lowered in 1836 and abolished in 1855), cheap 'penny' papers appeared, and millions of ordinary people could read the news every day. Steam presses also printed cheap books, magazines, school readers, posters, railway timetables and maps. Steam helped spread not just goods, but ideas."]},{"heading":"A Palace of Glass: The Great Exhibition of 1851","paragraphs":["On 1 May 1851, Queen Victoria opened the most extraordinary building London had ever seen. In Hyde Park stood a giant greenhouse of iron and glass, about 563 metres (1,848 feet) long and 138 metres (454 feet) wide, so tall in the middle that full-grown elm trees fitted inside it. A magazine called it the 'Crystal Palace', and the name stuck.","The building was designed by Joseph Paxton, who had started as a gardener and had built huge glass greenhouses for the Duke of Devonshire at Chatsworth. His design was a clever kit. Thousands of identical iron columns, girders and wooden glazing bars, plus hundreds of thousands of panes of glass made mostly in Birmingham and Smethwick, were mass-produced and bolted together on site. From planning to opening took only about nine months. The Crystal Palace was itself a product of the Industrial Revolution: cheap iron, factory-made glass and parts that fitted together because machine tools had made them accurately.","Its official name was the Great Exhibition of the Works of Industry of All Nations. The idea was pushed forward by Prince Albert, Queen Victoria's husband, and the civil servant Henry Cole. It was the first in a long series of world's fairs. Inside were about 13,000 exhibits from Britain, its colonies and 44 foreign countries.","Imagine walking down the long central hall. You might see huge raw lumps of coal and iron ore, then gleaming locomotives and marine engines, printing presses, looms and spinning machines. In the machinery section, many machines were shown actually working, driven by steam piped in from boilers outside the building, so visitors could watch cotton being spun and cloth woven in front of their eyes. From the United States came a mechanical reaping machine for harvesting grain and Samuel Colt's revolvers. From France came fine silks and porcelain.","Between May and October 1851, about six million visits were recorded, at a time when Great Britain had roughly 21 million people. On the busiest day, 7 October, nearly 110,000 people crowded in. Tickets were expensive at first, but from late May there were cheaper 'shilling days', and special trains brought workers and their families from industrial towns all over the country. For many, it was their first trip to London.","The exhibition made a profit of about £186,000. That money helped buy land in South Kensington, where museums grew up, including the Victoria and Albert Museum, the Natural History Museum and the Science Museum, which today displays some of the most important steam engines in the world. The Crystal Palace itself was taken down and rebuilt, even larger, at Sydenham in south London, where it stood until it burned down in 1936.","The Great Exhibition showed Britain at the height of its industrial lead. People began calling Britain 'the workshop of the world'. Around the middle of the 1800s, Britain produced a very large share of the world's manufactured goods: by some historians' estimates about a fifth of everything, and far more of the world's iron, coal and cotton cloth. But visitors who looked closely at the American and European exhibits could already see that other countries were catching up fast."]},{"heading":"Steam Goes Around the World","paragraphs":["Britain tried to keep its industrial secrets at home. For many years it was against the law for skilled workers to emigrate and for some kinds of machinery to be exported. But ideas, like steam, are hard to keep bottled up. Engineers moved abroad, machines were copied, and soon other countries were building their own industries, often with their own brilliant inventors.","Belgium was the first country on the European continent to industrialise on a big scale, helped by rich coalfields and iron ore. An English machine-builder, William Cockerill, moved there in 1799 and built spinning machinery for the wool town of Verviers. His son John Cockerill, born in Lancashire in 1790, set up a huge works at Seraing near Liège in 1817, in a former palace of the Prince-Bishops of Liège. It combined an iron foundry, blast furnaces and a machine factory, and by 1840 it had sixteen steam engines and about 3,000 workers. It built steam engines, ships' engines and, in 1835, Belgium's first steam locomotive.","France followed, with its own engineering tradition. In 1836, the brothers Adolphe and Eugène Schneider took over the ironworks at Le Creusot in Burgundy, which grew into one of Europe's greatest engineering works, building locomotives, ships, bridges and steam engines. That is where François Bourdon built his steam hammer in 1840.","In Germany, the great coal deposits of the Ruhr valley became the engine room of the country. Ruhr coal output grew from about 2 million tons in 1850 to about 60 million by 1900 and more than 110 million by 1913. Companies like Krupp of Essen, famous for its steel and its giant 'Fritz' hammer, grew into industrial empires. After Germany became one united country in 1871, its industry raced ahead, and by around 1900 it was making more steel than Britain.","The United States had vast forests, rivers and coalfields, and plenty of inventors of its own, such as Oliver Evans and George Corliss, whom we have already met. American coal output shot up from about 8 million tons in 1850 to about 270 million tons in 1900. By around 1900, the United States had become the largest industrial economy in the world.","Perhaps the most dramatic change happened in Japan. For more than two centuries, Japan had strictly limited contact with the outside world. In 1853, American steam-powered warships arrived in Tokyo Bay, and the Japanese were shocked to see ships moving against the wind with smoke pouring from their funnels. In 1868, a new government took power in the name of the young Emperor Meiji, and set out to learn from the industrial countries as fast as possible.","Japanese leaders travelled to the United States and Europe on the Iwakura Mission (1871 to 1873) to study factories, schools and governments. Japan hired foreign experts, but always with the aim of training Japanese engineers to take over. In 1872 the government opened the Tomioka Silk Mill in Gunma Prefecture, designed with the help of the French engineer Paul Brunat. A single-cylinder steam engine powered its silk-reeling machines, and it started with about 210 young women workers, many of them daughters of former samurai families. The mill ran until 1987 and is now a UNESCO World Heritage Site.","By 1900, steam was at work on every inhabited continent, in mines, mills, workshops and printing houses. But so far we have mostly seen steam staying in one place, turning machines. The most exciting question was still waiting: could steam carry people and goods across water and along roads, faster and farther than any horse or sail?"]}],"deepDive":"Why did the Industrial Revolution start in Britain, and how important was steam? Historians are still arguing, and the debate is a great example of how history works. The economic historian Robert Allen has argued that Britain had an unusual combination of high wages and very cheap coal. When workers are expensive and energy is cheap, it pays to invent machines that replace muscle with coal-fired power, which is exactly what steam engines did. Joel Mokyr stresses something different: an 'Industrial Enlightenment', a culture in which scientists, instrument makers and craftsmen shared knowledge through societies, books and letters. Kenneth Pomeranz, comparing Britain with the richest parts of China, argued that two strokes of luck mattered a great deal: coal deposits located near where they were needed, and overseas colonies and trade that supplied land-hungry products like cotton, sugar and timber, much of it produced by enslaved people. Most historians now think several of these factors worked together. There is also a surprise about timing. When economists measure growth, steam seems to have contributed only a little to Britain's productivity before about 1830, because engines were still expensive, wasteful of coal and few in number compared with waterwheels, horses and hand tools. In a 2004 study, Nicholas Crafts estimated that steam's biggest economic impact came after about 1850, when high-pressure engines, cheap iron, steamships and railways spread widely. This fits a pattern seen with other 'general purpose technologies' such as electricity and computers: their full effects can take many decades to arrive, because people must invent new ways of organising work around them. Numbers also need care. Early coal and iron figures were collected inconsistently and are estimates, and 'tons' in older British records usually means long tons of 2,240 pounds, slightly more than a metric tonne. Even so, the overall picture is clear: between 1700 and 1913 British coal output grew roughly a hundredfold, and that river of fuel, turned into motion by steam engines, reshaped the world economy.","funFact":"James Nasmyth liked to show how delicately his giant steam hammer could be controlled by using it to crack an egg standing in a wineglass without breaking the glass.","check":{"q":"Which newspaper was printed on Friedrich Koenig's steam-powered press on 29 November 1814?","a":"The Times of London","wrong":["The Manchester Guardian","The New York Herald","The Illustrated London News"]},"sources":[{"title":"Industrial Revolution","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Industrial_Revolution"},{"title":"Blast furnace","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Blast_furnace"},{"title":"History of coal mining","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/History_of_coal_mining"},{"title":"Henry Maudslay","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henry_Maudslay"},{"title":"Steam hammer","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steam_hammer"},{"title":"Friedrich Koenig","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Friedrich_Koenig"},{"title":"The Great Exhibition","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Great_Exhibition"},{"title":"Tomioka Silk Mill","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tomioka_Silk_Mill"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/13-1.jpg","alt":"Colour print of the iron-and-glass Crystal Palace in Hyde Park, London, during the Great Exhibition of 1851.","file":"Crystal Palace from the northeast from Dickinson's Comprehensive Pictures of the Great Exhibition of 1851. 1854.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Crystal_Palace_from_the_northeast_from_Dickinson%27s_Comprehensive_Pictures_of_the_Great_Exhibition_of_1851._1854.jpg","author":"Dickinson Brothers","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/13-2.jpg","alt":"James Nasmyth's own painting of his steam hammer pounding a glowing hot iron bar in a busy forge.","file":"James Nasmyth (1808-1890) - A Steam Hammer at Work - 1957-15 - Science Museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:James_Nasmyth_(1808-1890)_-_A_Steam_Hammer_at_Work_-_1957-15_-_Science_Museum.jpg","author":"James Nasmyth","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/13-3.jpg","alt":"Friedrich Koenig's double-cylinder steam printing press of 1814, marked London 1814 on its top frame.","file":"Koenig's steam press - 1814.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Koenig%27s_steam_press_-_1814.png","author":"Unknown author","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4344},{"n":14,"title":"Steam Takes to Water and Road","when":"1769 – 1920s","summary":"A French army engineer built a huge, wobbly steam wagon, and inventors in France, America and Scotland tried putting engines in boats. Soon steamboats chugged up rivers, giant iron ships crossed oceans, steam tractors ploughed fields and a steam car zoomed faster than 200 kilometres an hour. Steam was on the move!","sections":[{"heading":"Cugnot's Monster on Three Wheels","paragraphs":["Imagine standing in a courtyard in Paris around 1770. In front of you is something nobody has ever seen: a heavy wooden cart with one wheel at the front and two at the back. Hanging out in front of that single front wheel is a big, round copper boiler, hissing and steaming like an enormous kettle. Two brass cylinders, pumping up and down, turn the front wheel. There are no horses. The machine creaks forward at about walking pace, and the crowd gasps.","This was the 'fardier à vapeur', which means 'steam wagon' (a fardier was a heavy cart for hauling big loads). It was built by Nicolas-Joseph Cugnot, a French military engineer born in 1725 in Lorraine. The French army wanted a way to drag heavy cannons without needing teams of horses, which had to be fed, watered and rested. Cugnot tested a small version in 1769, and the full-size machine was built in 1770.","The fardier was a beast. Empty, it weighed about 2.5 tonnes. It was meant to carry around 4 tonnes and travel at almost 8 kilometres an hour, but it never managed that. The best reports suggest it crawled along at a few kilometres an hour. Its biggest problem was its boiler. It had no separate firebox that could be kept going easily, so every quarter of an hour or so the machine had to stop, relight or rebuild the fire, and wait for the pressure to rise again before it could move on.","It was also very hard to control. Because the heavy boiler hung out in front of the single steering wheel, the whole weight sat over the front, and the machine was clumsy to steer and unstable. A famous story says that in 1771 it crashed into a wall, making it the world's first motor accident. But be careful with this tale: no report from the time mentions the crash, and it first appears in writing around 1804, more than thirty years later. It may have happened, or it may be a legend that grew with retelling.","Whatever the truth, the army lost interest, and the project was put away. Cugnot's machine survived, though, and in 1800 it was moved to the Conservatoire national des arts et métiers in Paris, a museum of science and technology, where you can still see it today. It is one of the oldest surviving self-propelled vehicles in the world. In 2010, engineering students in France built a working copy, which was shown at the Paris Motor Show.","Cugnot's wagon was too early. Engines in 1770 were heavy, low-pressure and wasteful, and roads were rutted and rough. But he had proved one huge thing: a fire could make a vehicle move by itself. The next inventors would find that steam was much happier first on water, where a boat could carry a heavy engine without sinking into the mud."]},{"heading":"Paddles on French and American Rivers","paragraphs":["Why put an engine in a boat? For thousands of years, boats had been moved by oars, sails, river currents, or horses walking along a towpath pulling them by rope. Going downstream was easy. Going upstream against a strong current, or across a lake with no wind, was slow and hard. An engine that never got tired, and did not care which way the wind blew, could change all that.","One of the first to try was a French nobleman, Claude de Jouffroy d'Abbans. In 1774, he built a boat called the Palmipède, a word meaning 'web-footed', because it tried to copy a duck's feet with flapping paddles driven by steam. It did not work well. So he tried again. On 15 July 1783, his new boat, the Pyroscaphe (from Greek words meaning 'fire boat'), steamed on the River Saône in eastern France. It was about 13 metres long, and instead of flapping oars it used paddle wheels turned by a steam engine. It made several trips up and down the river, pushing against the current. This was one of the first times a steam engine had driven a boat in a real trial.","Sadly for Jouffroy, he could not win official approval and money to build more, and a few years later the French Revolution turned his world upside down. His invention faded, but he is remembered in France as a steamboat pioneer.","Across the Atlantic, in the newly independent United States, two inventors were racing each other. John Fitch, born in Connecticut in 1743, was a clockmaker, silversmith and surveyor with almost no money but enormous determination. On 22 August 1787, his steamboat Perseverance made a successful run on the Delaware River at Philadelphia. Watching were delegates to the Constitutional Convention, the meeting that was writing the United States Constitution that very summer. Fitch's boat did not use paddle wheels. Instead, the engine worked rows of oars on each side, dipping and pulling like a team of rowers.","In 1790, Fitch built a 60-foot (about 18-metre) boat with steam-driven oars at the stern, and ran a real passenger service on the Delaware between Philadelphia and Burlington, New Jersey, carrying up to 30 passengers at about 6 to 8 miles an hour (roughly 10 to 13 kilometres an hour). That is pretty impressive for 1790! But there were too few customers, the service lost money, and investors gave up.","Fitch's rival was James Rumsey of Virginia, who in December 1787 demonstrated a boat on the Potomac River that used a steam pump to suck water in at the front and squirt it out of the back, a kind of early water-jet. The two men argued bitterly over who deserved the credit. In 1791, the new United States patent office gave Fitch a patent, but it also gave steamboat patents to Rumsey and others on the same day, so Fitch never got the monopoly he hoped for. He died in 1798, poor and disappointed. A steamboat needed more than a working engine. It needed money, the right river, enough customers and good timing."]},{"heading":"Scotland's Canal Tug and the Comet","paragraphs":["Meanwhile, in Scotland, a young engineer named William Symington was working on the same dream. In 1788, he and a wealthy banker named Patrick Miller tried a small steam-driven paddle boat on Dalswinton Loch, a lake on Miller's estate. It worked, but Miller lost interest after further trials, and the work stalled.","Symington's chance came again when Lord Dundas, the governor of the Forth and Clyde Canal Company, backed him. Canal boats were usually pulled by horses walking along the bank, and a powerful steam tug could, in theory, haul many boats at once. A first boat was tried in 1801. Then, in 1803, Symington built a new and better one, named the Charlotte Dundas after Lord Dundas's daughter.","The Charlotte Dundas was about 17 metres (56 feet) long, with a horizontal steam engine turning a single big paddle wheel set inside the stern, so its splashing would not hit the canal banks. In its famous trial in 1803, in a strong headwind that stopped other boats, it towed two loaded barges, each weighing about 70 tons, for roughly 30 kilometres (about 19 miles) along the canal to Glasgow. The trip took about nine and a quarter hours, which may sound slow, but no other boat could have done it against that wind.","Yet the canal owners said no. They feared that the waves from the paddle wheel would wash away the soft canal banks. The Charlotte Dundas was left in a quiet backwater, where it slowly rotted until it was broken up in 1861. Many people consider it the first practical steamboat, because it did a real job well, even though it never went into regular service.","Scotland did get its working steamboat soon after. In 1812, Henry Bell launched the Comet, which carried passengers on the River Clyde between Glasgow and Greenock (the town where James Watt was born). It is usually counted as the first commercially successful passenger steamboat service in Europe. Before long, dozens of little steamers were puffing up and down the Clyde, and Glasgow became one of the greatest shipbuilding cities in the world."]},{"heading":"Fulton's Boat on the Hudson","paragraphs":["Robert Fulton is often called the inventor of the steamboat, but as you have just seen, that is not true. Jouffroy, Fitch, Rumsey and Symington had all made steamboats move before him. Fulton's achievement was different and just as important: he was the first to make a steamboat service that worked as a business, year after year.","Fulton was born in Pennsylvania in 1765 and started out as a painter of miniature portraits. He moved to Europe, became fascinated by canals and engineering, and in 1803 tested a small steamboat on the River Seine in Paris. There he teamed up with Robert Livingston, a rich and powerful American who was then serving as the United States minister (ambassador) to France. Livingston held something very valuable: a legal monopoly from the state of New York, which gave him the sole right to run steamboats on New York's waters, as long as he could produce a working boat.","Back in New York, the partners had a boat built at the Charles Browne shipyard. Its engine was ordered from Boulton & Watt in Birmingham, England, and shipped across the Atlantic. The boat was long and narrow, with a paddle wheel on each side and a tall smokestack. People called it the North River Steamboat, because the Hudson River was then often called the North River.","On 17 August 1807, the boat set off from New York City for Albany, the state capital, about 150 miles (240 kilometres) up the Hudson. Some onlookers expected it to fail or blow up. Instead, it puffed steadily upstream against the current at an average of about 5 miles an hour (8 kilometres an hour). The trip took about 32 hours of travel, plus a stop of about 20 hours at Clermont, Livingston's family estate along the river. Sailing sloops, which depended on the wind, often took several days for the same journey. The return trip to New York took about 30 hours, with only a one-hour stop.","Regular passenger service began on 4 September 1807, with stops along the river. The boat was later widened and rebuilt to about 43 metres (142 feet) long. Many people today call it the Clermont, after Livingston's estate, but that name was not used at the time. It first appeared in a biography of Fulton written in 1817, two years after his death, and it simply stuck.","Fulton's success opened the floodgates. In 1811, a steamboat named New Orleans, built by Fulton and Livingston's partners, set off down the Ohio and Mississippi rivers, the start of the great age of Mississippi riverboats. Within a few decades, hundreds of steamboats were carrying cotton, grain, timber and passengers up and down America's rivers, helping cities like St. Louis and Cincinnati to grow. Because they could travel upstream as easily as down, steamboats turned rivers into two-way highways. They also brought new dangers, especially boiler explosions, which we will look at in the next part."]},{"heading":"Steaming Across the Ocean","paragraphs":["Rivers and canals were one thing. The Atlantic Ocean, nearly 5,000 kilometres of wild water between Europe and North America, was quite another. A ship would need to carry enough coal for the whole trip, and early engines gobbled coal so fast that many experts thought it was impossible.","The first attempt was the American ship SS Savannah. In 1819 it sailed from Savannah, Georgia, to Liverpool, England, in 29 days and 11 hours. But it was really a sailing ship with a steam engine added. Its paddle wheels could even be folded up and stored on deck in about 15 minutes, so they did not drag in the water while the ship sailed. During the crossing, the engine ran for only about 80 hours, roughly a tenth of the voyage, because the ship could not carry more fuel. No passengers dared to buy a ticket, so the voyage was purely an experiment. Still, it was the first time a steam-powered ship had crossed the Atlantic, even partly under steam.","The real race came in 1838. A famous young English engineer, Isambard Kingdom Brunel, had already built the Great Western Railway from London to Bristol. According to a well-known story, he joked that it should simply continue all the way to New York, and he designed a big wooden paddle steamer to do it, also called the Great Western. Brunel understood something clever: if you build a ship twice as long, its cargo space grows much more than the push of water against it, so a really big ship can carry enough coal for an ocean crossing.","Rivals were determined to get there first. They chartered a smaller steamer called Sirius, which left before the Great Western and reached New York on 22 April 1838, almost out of coal. The Great Western, delayed by a fire in its engine room on its first trip, left Bristol on 8 April and arrived the very next day, 23 April, after a crossing of about 15 days, with around 200 tons of coal still in its bunkers. Sirius had won the race, but the Great Western had proved Brunel right: a well-designed ship could cross the Atlantic by steam with fuel to spare. Two years later, in 1840, Samuel Cunard's company began a regular steamship mail service across the Atlantic with its ship Britannia, and Atlantic steamship lines grew rapidly.","Brunel's next ship was a giant leap. The SS Great Britain, floated out at Bristol on 19 July 1843, was about 98 metres (322 feet) long, far larger than any ship before it. It combined two brand-new ideas for an ocean liner: a hull made of iron instead of wood, and a screw propeller instead of paddle wheels. In 1845 it became the first iron-hulled steamship to cross the Atlantic, taking just under 15 days.","Why a propeller? Paddle wheels work by slapping water backwards with flat boards, like someone rowing. They work well on calm rivers, but on the open ocean a ship rolls from side to side, so one paddle wheel lifts out of the water while the other plunges deep, and the ship lurches. Paddle wheels are also big and easy targets in a war. A screw propeller is a set of twisted blades under the back of the ship. As it turns, it pushes water backwards, a bit like a fan or a screw twisting into wood, and it stays underwater whatever the waves are doing.","Not everyone was convinced, so the Royal Navy held a contest. On 3 April 1845, two ships with similar engines, the propeller-driven HMS Rattler and the paddle-driven HMS Alecto, were tied stern to stern and set off in opposite directions in a nautical tug of war. Rattler dragged Alecto backwards at about 2.8 knots. The propeller had won, and navies and shipping lines gradually switched to screws.","The SS Great Britain had an adventurous life. It ran aground in Dundrum Bay in Ireland in 1846, then later carried thousands of emigrants to Australia. It ended up abandoned in the Falkland Islands, before being towed home to Bristol in 1970. Today it sits in the very dry dock where it was built, as a museum ship you can walk through. Brunel went on to design an even bigger ship, the Great Eastern of 1858, about 211 metres long, which later laid the first lasting telegraph cable across the Atlantic in 1866."]},{"heading":"Steam on Roads and Farms","paragraphs":["While ships were conquering oceans, inventors had not forgotten Cugnot's dream of steam on the roads. In Britain in the 1820s and 1830s, engineers such as Goldsworthy Gurney and Walter Hancock built steam carriages and steam buses that carried paying passengers. In 1831, for example, Gurney's steam carriages ran a service between Gloucester and Cheltenham for several months. But many people disliked these noisy, smoky machines. Some turnpike trusts, which ran the toll roads, charged steam carriages huge tolls, and the railways soon offered faster, smoother journeys. The steam-carriage boom faded.","Steam on the roads found its real home in slow, heavy, powerful work. From about the 1850s, makers built 'traction engines': steam tractors with a boiler, a firebox and a big flywheel, two huge driving wheels at the back and two smaller steering wheels at the front. Before that, many farms had 'portable engines', which had wheels but had to be pulled from place to place by horses. A traction engine could drive itself.","On the farm, a traction engine could drive a threshing machine with a long leather belt from its flywheel, separating grain from straw far faster than people with flails. On the road, bigger 'road locomotives' hauled heavy loads such as timber, stone and giant boilers. Travelling fairs used gleaming 'showman's engines', painted in bright colours with twisted brass poles, to haul rides from town to town and then make electricity to light the fair at night.","The law did not make life easy for them. Britain's Locomotive Act of 1865, often called the 'Red Flag Act', limited road steam engines to 4 miles an hour in the country and 2 miles an hour in towns, with a crew of three, one of whom had to walk at least 60 yards ahead carrying a red flag to warn horse riders and carriages. In 1878 the red flag itself stopped being compulsory, though someone still had to walk about 20 yards ahead, and the low speed limits stayed for years.","Ploughing was one of steam's cleverest farm jobs. A heavy engine driving across soft, wet fields would sink in and squash the soil. So in the 1850s, the engineer John Fowler and others developed cable ploughing. Two engines stood at opposite edges of the field, each with a big winding drum underneath and a long steel wire rope. One engine wound in its rope, pulling a special 'balance plough' across the field, while the other paid out its rope. Then they swapped, pulling the plough back again, and the drivers signalled each other with toots on their whistles. Around the time of the First World War, Britain had a little over 600 pairs of ploughing engines at work.","Steam also helped build better roads. In 1867, the Kent company Aveling and Porter produced one of the first successful steamrollers, with a wide, heavy roller at the front instead of wheels. It was tested on roads in Chatham and Rochester and in Hyde Park in London. Steamrollers pressed down crushed stone and, later, hot tar, to make smooth, hard road surfaces, and some were still working in Britain in the 1950s. That is where our word 'steamroller' comes from, meaning to crush any opposition.","By the 1920s and 1930s, lighter and cheaper petrol and diesel tractors and lorries were taking over. The last new British traction engine was sold in 1944. But many were saved by enthusiasts. In 2016, a register listed 2,851 surviving self-moving steam engines in Britain and Ireland, and you can see them puffing proudly at steam rallies every summer.","And of course, the most famous steam vehicles of all ran on iron rails. From the early 1800s, steam locomotives began to pull trains, and within a few decades railways had changed travel, trade and even the way clocks were set. That is a huge story of its own, told in the separate Railways Deep Dive, so we will wave to the trains as they pass and stay on the road."]},{"heading":"The Stanley Steamer and the Rocket","paragraphs":["At the end of the 1800s, a new kind of road vehicle appeared: the motor car. Today we think of cars as running on petrol or electricity, but in the early days, steam was a serious rival. In 1902, of 909 new cars registered in the United States in one count, 485, more than half, were steam cars.","The most famous steam cars were made by identical twins, Francis E. Stanley and Freelan O. Stanley, from Maine. They had made a fortune making photographic plates, which they sold to Eastman Kodak. In 1897 they built their first car. In 1899, Freelan and his wife Flora drove a Stanley to the top of Mount Washington in New Hampshire, the highest mountain in the northeastern United States, the first car to climb its carriage road. Their company, the Stanley Motor Carriage Company, was formed in 1902.","A Stanley Steamer had a compact boiler, usually under the bonnet, heated by a burner that burned kerosene or petrol. The steam drove a small two-cylinder engine connected almost directly to the rear axle. There was no gearbox and no clutch. Steam cars were smooth, quiet and very strong when starting off, because steam pushes hardest when the engine is barely moving.","In 1906, the Stanleys built a special racing car, a long, low, streamlined machine shaped a bit like an upside-down boat, later nicknamed the Stanley Rocket. On the hard, flat sands of Ormond Beach, Florida, near Daytona, their driver Fred Marriott roared through a measured mile in 28.2 seconds, a speed of about 127.7 miles an hour (about 205 kilometres an hour). It was the fastest anyone on Earth had ever travelled in a car. The next year, Marriott tried to go even faster in an improved version, but the car hit a bump at a very high speed, flew into the air and broke apart. Marriott was hurt but survived, and never tried for the record again.","Remarkably, his steam-car record lasted more than a century. It was not until August 2009 that a British team, with Charles Burnett III driving their steam car 'Inspiration', beat it in California, at an average of about 139.8 miles an hour (about 225 kilometres an hour).","So why did steam cars disappear? A steam car had to heat its boiler before it could move, which took several minutes from cold, and it had to carry plenty of water and stop to refill often. Meanwhile, petrol cars got much better. The electric starter motor meant drivers no longer had to crank the engine by hand, and Henry Ford's assembly line made petrol cars like the Model T cheap enough for ordinary families. The Stanley company made its peak of 784 cars in 1908 and about 10,910 in total, but it closed in 1924. The American Doble company built brilliant steam cars that could start from cold in under a minute, but they were very expensive, and Doble stopped making cars in 1930.","In about 150 years, steam had gone from Cugnot's wobbly cannon wagon to ocean liners, farm giants and a car that could beat 200 kilometres an hour. It had shrunk the oceans, opened up rivers and carried millions of people to new lives in new countries. But all this power came with a cost. Behind the speed and the shining brass were children in mills and mines, exploding boilers and smoke-blackened cities. In the next part, we will look honestly at the dark side of steam."]}],"deepDive":"Who deserves credit for the steamboat? This is one of history's classic arguments about 'firsts', and the answer depends on what you mean by 'first'. If you mean the first steam engine to move a boat in a public trial, the honour goes to Jouffroy d'Abbans's Pyroscaphe on the Saône in 1783. If you mean the first regular passenger service, John Fitch ran one on the Delaware in 1790, seventeen years before Fulton. If you mean the first steamboat to do a hard commercial job convincingly, many historians point to Symington's Charlotte Dundas in 1803. Fulton's claim is the first service that was commercially successful and lasting, and in the United States in particular it is often remembered as the beginning of the steamboat age. Engineers today say Fulton's real genius was in system design and business: choosing a reliable Boulton & Watt engine, sizing the boat for the Hudson's deep water, and having Livingston's legal monopoly and money behind him. That monopoly later became the centre of a famous US Supreme Court case, Gibbons v. Ogden (1824), which ruled that only the federal government could regulate trade between states, opening the rivers to competition. On the oceans, the key numbers were fuel and efficiency. Early marine engines worked at low pressure and burned several kilograms of coal for every horsepower each hour, which is why ships like Savannah could steam only a fraction of the way. Brunel's insight was about scaling: a ship's carrying capacity grows roughly with the cube of its size, while the water resistance it must overcome grows more slowly, roughly with the area of its hull. Later, compound and then triple-expansion engines (from the 1880s), using steam two or three times in cylinders of increasing size, cut coal use dramatically and made long voyages to Asia and Australia profitable. Finally, in the early 1900s, ships switched to steam turbines, which Part 17 explores.","funFact":"The SS Savannah, the first ship to cross the Atlantic partly by steam in 1819, is the only ship known to have had paddle wheels that could be folded up and stored on deck.","check":{"q":"In 1906, what kind of power did the car use that set a world land speed record of about 205 km/h at Ormond Beach, Florida?","a":"Steam (it was the Stanley Rocket)","wrong":["A petrol (gasoline) engine","An electric battery motor","A jet engine"]},"sources":[{"title":"Fardier à vapeur","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fardier_à_vapeur"},{"title":"John Fitch (inventor)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Fitch_(inventor)"},{"title":"Charlotte Dundas","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charlotte_Dundas"},{"title":"North River Steamboat","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/North_River_Steamboat"},{"title":"SS Great Western","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Great_Western"},{"title":"SS Great Britain","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SS_Great_Britain"},{"title":"Traction engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Traction_engine"},{"title":"Fred Marriott","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fred_Marriott"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/14-1.jpg","alt":"Cugnot's steam wagon (fardier à vapeur), with its big copper boiler at the front, in the Musée des Arts et Métiers, Paris.","file":"Joseph Cugnot's 1770 Fardier à Vapeur, Musée des arts et métiers, Paris 2015.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joseph_Cugnot%27s_1770_Fardier_%C3%A0_Vapeur,_Mus%C3%A9e_des_arts_et_m%C3%A9tiers,_Paris_2015.jpg","author":"Joe deSousa","license":"CC0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/14-2.jpg","alt":"The iron hull, propeller and rudder of Brunel's SS Great Britain, seen from her dry dock in Bristol.","file":"Propeller of the SS Great Britain in dry dock in Bristol.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Propeller_of_the_SS_Great_Britain_in_dry_dock_in_Bristol.jpg","author":"Nortix08","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/14-3.jpg","alt":"Fred Marriott sitting in the streamlined Stanley Rocket steam car at Daytona Beach, Florida, around 1906.","file":"N041921.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:N041921.jpg","author":"LeSesne, Richard H.","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4138},{"n":15,"title":"The Dark Side of Steam","when":"1780s – 1900s (and later)","summary":"Steam made cloth cheap and cities rich, but it also had a dark side. Children worked long hours in noisy mills and deep, dangerous coal mines, boilers sometimes exploded, and smoke turned the sky grey. Brave people spoke up, and slowly new laws made work safer and fairer.","sections":[{"heading":"Small Hands in the Roaring Mill","paragraphs":["Imagine it is five o'clock on a dark winter morning in a Lancashire town in about 1830. A factory bell clangs. Hundreds of people hurry through the wet streets, and among them are children, some only eight or nine years old, rubbing their eyes. Inside the mill the great steam engine is already hissing in its engine house. When the engineer opens the valve, the long line shafts overhead begin to turn, the leather belts slap into motion, and every spinning machine in the building wakes up at once. From that moment until the engine stops in the evening, the machines set the pace, and the people have to keep up.","Children had always worked. On farms and in cottage workshops they had fed chickens, carded wool and minded babies. But the mill was different. The steam engine did not get tired, and the owners who had paid a lot of money for it wanted it running for as many hours as possible. Twelve, thirteen or even more hours a day were common in the early 1800s, often with only short breaks to eat.","Why children? Partly because they were cheap: a child might earn a small fraction of a man's wage. Partly because their small bodies and nimble fingers suited certain jobs. A 'piecer' walked up and down beside the spinning mules, quickly twisting together threads that had snapped. A 'scavenger' crawled underneath the moving machinery to sweep up fluff and cotton waste, while the heavy carriage of the mule rolled back and forth just above them. Accidents happened. Hair, sleeves and fingers could be caught by unguarded gears, belts and shafts.","The air itself was a problem. Cotton spinning works best in warm, damp air, so many mills were kept hot and humid. Tiny fibres floated everywhere and were breathed in day after day. The noise of hundreds of machines was so loud that workers learned to talk by lip-reading. Long hours of standing could leave growing bodies bent or injured, and doctors at the time wrote about children with deformed legs.","Some of the earliest mill children were 'parish apprentices': orphans or children from very poor families who were sent by local officials to work in distant water-powered mills, often living in a dormitory beside the factory. As steam let mills move into towns, owners could more easily hire children who lived at home with their parents. For a poor family, a child's wage could mean the difference between eating and going hungry, which is one reason many parents felt they had no choice.","Not everyone accepted this. In 1830 a Yorkshire land agent named Richard Oastler wrote a furious open letter to a Leeds newspaper about children working in factories near Bradford. He argued that they were treated worse than enslaved people on sugar plantations, a shocking comparison at a time when Britain was arguing about ending slavery in its colonies. In 1832 a Member of Parliament, Michael Sadler, led a committee that collected evidence from mill workers, some of whom described being beaten to keep them awake at the end of long shifts. Later investigators thought some of the most dramatic stories in Sadler's report were exaggerated, but the report shocked the public and helped push Parliament to act."]},{"heading":"The Factory Act of 1833","paragraphs":["Parliament had already tried to protect mill children. A law of 1802, the Health and Morals of Apprentices Act, limited parish apprentices to 12 hours a day and banned them from working at night. A law of 1819 banned children under 9 from cotton mills. But these early laws had a fatal weakness: almost nobody checked whether they were obeyed. Local magistrates, who were sometimes friends or relatives of the mill owners, were supposed to enforce them, and they rarely did.","The Factory Act of 1833 was different, and it is often seen as a turning point. It applied to textile mills across the United Kingdom, not just cotton mills. Its main rules were simple enough to fit on a poster. Children under 9 could not be employed in most textile mills (silk mills were an exception). Children aged 9 to 13 were limited to 9 hours a day, and no more than 48 hours a week for the younger ones. Young people aged 13 to 18 could work no more than 12 hours a day. Nobody under 18 was allowed to work at night.","The Act also tried to make sure working children learned something. Children aged 9 to 13 were supposed to get two hours of schooling every day, and the mill had to keep a certificate from a schoolmaster to prove it. In practice the 'schools' were sometimes just a corner of the mill with a teacher who could barely read, and the inspectors themselves later called these education rules nearly impossible to carry out properly. Still, it was the start of an important idea: that children's time belonged partly to learning, not only to work.","The most important new idea was the inspector. The 1833 Act created a small team of government factory inspectors, paid by the state and reporting to the Home Office. They had the right to walk into mills, look at records and bring cases against owners who broke the law. At first there were only four inspectors for roughly 4,000 factories, so each one had an enormous area to cover. Clever owners also found ways around the rules. One trick was the 'relay system', where children worked in shifts so that the adults, and the steam engine, could keep going for 15 hours a day.","Even so, the inspectors' reports gave Parliament real information, year after year, and further laws followed. The Factory Act of 1844 required dangerous parts such as flywheels and gearing to be fenced in, stopped children and women from cleaning machinery while it was moving, and said accidents had to be reported. The Ten Hours Act of 1847, pushed for years by campaigners such as Lord Ashley (later the Earl of Shaftesbury) inside Parliament, Oastler outside it, and the mill owner John Fielden, cut the working day of women and young people in textile mills to about ten hours. Change was slow and uneven, but the direction was clear: the power of steam would have to be matched by rules that protected the people working beside it."]},{"heading":"Darkness Under the Ground","paragraphs":["Every steam engine had a hungry firebox, and that firebox needed coal. As engines spread through mills, ironworks, ships and railways, Britain dug more and more coal, and the mines went deeper. The steam engine and the coal mine were tied together in a loop: engines pumped water out of the pits, and the pits fed coal to the engines. But the people doing the digging paid a heavy price.","Picture a coal mine in Yorkshire around 1840. You climb into a basket or cage and are lowered down a shaft hundreds of feet into blackness. At the bottom, narrow tunnels stretch away, some so low that an adult cannot stand up. The only light comes from candles or small lamps. Somewhere ahead, men called hewers are cutting coal with picks. Behind them, the coal has to be moved to the bottom of the shaft, and much of that moving was done by women and children.","The youngest children, sometimes only five or six years old, were 'trappers'. Their job was to sit alone in the dark beside a wooden door and pull it open whenever a coal tub came through, then shut it again. These doors controlled the flow of fresh air through the mine, which was vital for keeping dangerous gases away. A trapper might sit there for twelve hours, mostly in darkness and silence. Older children and women worked as 'hurriers' or 'drawers', pushing or pulling heavy tubs of coal along tunnels too low to stand in, sometimes with a chain running from a belt around the waist.","Mines were dangerous in many ways. Roofs could collapse. Workers could fall down shafts or be crushed by tubs. Water could flood in. Worst of all were explosions. Coal seams can give off methane, a gas miners called 'firedamp'. If enough of it built up and met a naked flame, the result could be a blast that tore through the tunnels, followed by 'afterdamp', a choking mixture of gases left behind. Safety lamps, such as the one designed by Humphry Davy in 1815, helped, but explosions continued throughout the 1800s.","In July 1838, a sudden thunderstorm near Barnsley in Yorkshire caused a stream to overflow into the Huskar Colliery at Silkstone. Water poured into a passage where children were trying to escape. Twenty-six children died: 11 girls aged 8 to 16 and 15 boys aged 9 to 12. The disaster was reported to the young Queen Victoria, and it helped lead to a national investigation.","That investigation, the Children's Employment Commission, sent commissioners into mining districts to interview workers, and its report came out in May 1842. It was full of the miners' own words, and it included drawings, many by the artist Margaret Gillies, showing children crawling through tiny tunnels and dragging coal tubs. Many readers in comfortable homes had simply never imagined such scenes. Lord Ashley led the campaign in Parliament, and in 1842 the Mines and Collieries Act was passed. It banned all women and girls from working underground, and it banned boys under 10. Some mine owners fought hard to weaken the law, and enforcement took time, but it was another step: the coal that fed the steam age would no longer be dug by small children."]},{"heading":"When Boilers Burst","paragraphs":["A boiler is a strong metal container full of hot water and steam under pressure. As long as the metal holds and the pressure stays within safe limits, all that stored energy flows calmly into the engine. But if the boiler fails, the energy is released in an instant. The water inside is hotter than its normal boiling point, so when the pressure suddenly drops, much of it flashes into steam all at once and expands enormously. A boiler explosion could throw heavy metal plates hundreds of feet and wreck a whole building.","Why did boilers burst? Engineers who studied the wreckage found several common causes. Sometimes the water level fell too low, so the fire heated bare metal until it weakened. Sometimes a safety valve, which is meant to let out steam when the pressure gets too high, was stuck, badly adjusted or even deliberately tied down by an operator who wanted more power. Sometimes the boiler had been badly made, with weak joints or thin plates. And very often the metal had simply rusted and thinned over years of use. Inspection records from the late 1800s and early 1900s suggest corrosion was the most frequent cause of all.","The worst boiler disaster in American history happened on the Mississippi River. The Sultana was a large wooden side-wheel steamboat with four boilers, built in 1863 and designed to carry a few hundred passengers. In April 1865, the American Civil War was just ending, and thousands of Union soldiers who had survived terrible prison camps, such as Cahaba in Alabama and Andersonville in Georgia, were waiting near Vicksburg, Mississippi, to go home. Through a mix of haste and corruption, around 2,100 people were crammed aboard, most of them these weakened soldiers. One of the boilers had a leak, and instead of a proper repair that would have taken days, it was quickly patched.","At about two o'clock in the morning on 27 April 1865, a few miles north of Memphis, Tennessee, the boilers exploded. The blast tore through the crowded decks, and fire spread through the wooden boat. Many people who survived the explosion were thrown into the cold, fast-flowing river, swollen by spring floods. Other steamboats hurried to help, but the number of dead was enormous: the most recent estimate is about 1,164 people, and older counts go higher. More people died than in many famous shipwrecks, yet the news was overshadowed at the time by the end of the war and the assassination of President Abraham Lincoln less than two weeks earlier. Investigators blamed the faulty repair, low water in the boilers and the dangerous overloading.","Disasters on land happened too. In the 1860s, nearly 500 boiler explosions were recorded in Britain, killing over 700 people. In March 1905, a boiler explosion at the Grover Shoe Factory in Brockton, Massachusetts, killed 58 people and injured about 150, and the building collapsed and burned.","Out of these tragedies came something we now take for granted: safety inspection. In 1854, engineers and mill owners met in Manchester, including the famous engineer William Fairbairn, and formed what became the Manchester Steam Users' Association, officially founded in 1855. Its idea was simple: trained engineers would visit members' boilers regularly and look for danger signs before an accident happened. The inspections also helped save fuel. In the United States, the Hartford Steam Boiler Inspection and Insurance Company was founded in 1866, combining inspection with insurance. The American Society of Mechanical Engineers, formed in 1880 partly because of worries about boiler safety, published its Boiler Code in 1915. Rules like these, about thickness of metal, safety valves, testing and regular checks, are the ancestors of the safety codes that still protect every boiler and pressure vessel today."]},{"heading":"Smoke, Soot and Stinking Rivers","paragraphs":["Stand on a hill above Manchester in the 1840s and look down. Instead of a clear view you would see a grey-brown haze, pierced by hundreds of tall factory chimneys, each pouring out coal smoke. Soot settled on window sills, washing hung out to dry, buildings, trees and people's lungs. Visitors wrote about the sun looking pale and red through the smoke. Many towns had a distinctive smell of coal fires, and rain could fall dirty.","Coal smoke contains tiny particles of soot along with gases such as sulphur dioxide. Breathed in day after day, it made lung diseases more common, especially in crowded neighbourhoods close to the factories. The sulphur in the smoke could also mix with moisture in the air to make acid, which ate away at stone buildings and harmed plants.","Rivers suffered as well. Factories, dye works, tanneries and slaughterhouses dumped waste into them, and many towns let their sewage flow straight into the nearest river. In London, the Thames became so foul that in the hot summer of 1858 the smell was nicknamed the 'Great Stink'. Curtains in the Houses of Parliament, beside the river, were soaked in chemicals to try to block the smell. Cholera, a deadly disease spread by contaminated water, struck London several times in the 1830s, 1840s and 1850s. In response, the engineer Joseph Bazalgette built a huge new system of sewers between 1859 and 1875, and it used powerful steam engines in pumping stations to move the sewage away. So steam was part of the problem and part of the solution.","One of the most famous descriptions of life in a steam-age city was written by a young German named Friedrich Engels. His father owned textile businesses, and Engels was sent to work in a cotton firm in Manchester in 1842. He spent his spare time walking through the poorest districts and talking to workers. In 1845, at the age of 24, he published a book in German called The Condition of the Working Class in England. He described damp, overcrowded houses, filthy courtyards, polluted streams and families crammed into cellars, and he compared death rates in industrial cities with those in the rest of the country. His book has a strong political point of view, and historians read it alongside other evidence, but it gives a vivid picture of how hard life could be in the world's first great industrial city.","Slowly, laws began to tackle pollution too. The Alkali Act of 1863 targeted chemical works that released clouds of acid gas, and it created inspectors to check them. Laws against factory smoke followed, though they were weak for a long time. The worst air pollution disasters came even later: in December 1952, a thick smog in London, made mainly by coal smoke from homes, power stations and factories, caused thousands of deaths and led to the Clean Air Act of 1956. The smoky skies of the steam age took more than a century to clear."]},{"heading":"Breaking Frames, Building Unions","paragraphs":["Workers did not simply accept everything the new machines brought. The most famous protesters were the Luddites. Their movement began in March 1811 in Arnold, near Nottingham, and spread over the next few years to Yorkshire and Lancashire. They took their name from 'Ned Ludd' or 'General Ludd', a legendary figure who may never have existed, and some of their letters were signed in his name.","It is easy to think the Luddites were smashing steam engines, but that is not really what happened. In the Midlands, most of the machines they broke were stocking frames, hand-operated knitting machines used to make stockings and lace, especially 'wide frames' that produced cheap, poor-quality goods. In Yorkshire, skilled cloth finishers called croppers attacked shearing frames, machines that did their job faster and cheaper. In Lancashire, some protests targeted mills with power looms. The Luddites were not against all machines; they were angry about employers who used new machinery and new methods to cut wages, hire unskilled workers and destroy skilled trades, at a time of war, high food prices and real hunger.","The government's response was harsh. About 12,000 soldiers were sent to the troubled areas. In 1812 Parliament made breaking machines a crime that could be punished by death. One of the few people to speak up for the frame-breakers in Parliament was the young poet Lord Byron. In April 1812, Luddites attacked Rawfolds Mill in Yorkshire and were driven off with gunfire. Later that month, a mill owner named William Horsfall was shot and killed. After trials at York in January 1813, a number of men were hanged and others were sent to the prison colonies in Australia. By about 1816 the movement had faded.","A longer-lasting way for workers to push back was to join together. For many years, laws called the Combination Acts, passed in 1799 and 1800, made it illegal for workers to organise to demand better wages. After those laws were repealed in 1824, trade unions grew, though they were still often treated with suspicion. In 1834, six farm labourers from the village of Tolpuddle in Dorset were sentenced to be transported to Australia for swearing an oath when forming a union. Huge public protests followed, and they were pardoned and allowed to return. Their story became a symbol for the labour movement. Over the rest of the 1800s, unions, campaigners and reformers won shorter hours, safer conditions and, eventually, the right of more working men and then women to vote."]},{"heading":"Cotton, Slavery and Empire","paragraphs":["The steam-powered cotton mills of Lancashire had a hidden connection to a much darker story far away. Cotton does not grow in Britain's cool climate. Most of it had to be imported, and by the middle of the 1800s most came from the southern United States, where it was grown and picked by enslaved African Americans.","In 1793, Eli Whitney designed a cotton gin, a machine that quickly separated cotton fibres from their sticky seeds, a job that had been very slow by hand. Combined with the hungry demand from British and American mills, the gin made growing cotton hugely profitable across the American South. Instead of reducing the need for labour, it increased it, because cotton still had to be planted, hoed and picked by hand. The number of enslaved people in the United States grew from about 700,000 in 1790 to about 3.2 million in 1850. American cotton production rose from 750,000 bales in 1830 to 2.85 million bales in 1850.","In 1860, about 80 percent of the raw cotton arriving in Britain came from America. That meant the steam engines of Manchester, the wages of hundreds of thousands of mill workers and the profits of merchants in Liverpool were all bound up with slavery, even though slavery itself had been abolished in most of the British Empire in 1833. When the American Civil War broke out in 1861, the supply of cotton was cut off, and the Lancashire Cotton Famine left many mill workers unemployed and hungry. Remarkably, at a meeting in Manchester's Free Trade Hall on 31 December 1862, workers voted to support the Union side and its fight against slavery, even though it was costing them their jobs. President Abraham Lincoln wrote back in January 1863 to thank them.","Steam power also changed the balance of power between countries. Britain's cheap, machine-made cloth flooded markets around the world, including India, which had been famous for its fine hand-woven cotton for centuries. Steamships and, later, steam railways helped European empires move soldiers, goods and officials far more quickly, making it easier to control distant colonies and to ship their raw materials home. Historians still argue about how much empire contributed to Britain's industrial success, but it is clear that the benefits of the steam age were not shared fairly. Some people grew very rich, while many others, in Britain and around the world, carried much of the cost."]},{"heading":"The Longest Shadow","paragraphs":["There is one more cost of steam that nobody in the 1800s could see. When coal burns, the carbon in it combines with oxygen from the air to make carbon dioxide, an invisible gas. Every steam engine, every factory chimney and every coal fire released it into the air. For most of human history, scientists now know, the amount of carbon dioxide in the atmosphere had stayed at about 280 parts per million for thousands of years. Once people began burning coal on a huge scale, that number started to climb, and later the burning of oil and gas made it rise even faster.","A few scientists in the 1800s began to wonder about this. In 1896 the Swedish scientist Svante Arrhenius published calculations suggesting that more carbon dioxide in the air would warm the Earth's surface. At the time, it seemed like a distant, almost curious idea. Today the concentration is well over 400 parts per million, more than half as much again as before the Industrial Revolution, and it is the main cause of the climate change the world is now trying to slow down. We will look at that story, and at what it means for the future of steam, in Part 19.","None of this means the steam engine was simply bad. It made goods cheaper, created new jobs, powered printing presses that spread knowledge, and eventually helped raise living standards for billions of people. But an honest history has to look at both sides: the brilliant engineering and the children in the mines, the cheap cloth and the cotton fields, the bright future and the smoky sky. The reformers who wrote reports, passed laws and inspected boilers were part of the steam story too.","And there was one more group of people asking hard questions. Engineers had spent more than a century improving engines by trial and error, saving a little coal here and a little there. Now some began to ask a bigger question: how good could a steam engine ever possibly get? Was there a limit? The search for the answer would lead to a completely new science."]}],"deepDive":"Historians have argued for a long time about how much the early Industrial Revolution helped or harmed ordinary workers, a debate often called the 'standard of living debate'. 'Optimists' point to rising real wages after about 1820, cheaper clothing and food, and growing populations. 'Pessimists' point to long hours, child labour, crowded and unhealthy cities, and the loss of skilled trades. Recent research using records of people's heights (a sign of childhood nutrition and health) and death rates suggests that living conditions for many workers improved only slowly, and in some industrial towns got worse for a time in the early 1800s, even as the economy grew. Life expectancy in big industrial cities like Manchester and Liverpool was well below the national average in the 1840s. The evidence from Parliament has to be read with care too. Sadler's 1832 committee collected testimony to support a cause, and a government commission in 1833 concluded that some of its most dramatic claims were exaggerated, while still agreeing that reform was needed. The factory and mines laws also show how regulation grows: early laws without inspectors failed; laws with professional inspectors, reporting duties and penalties gradually worked. Boiler safety followed a similar path, moving from private inspection associations in Manchester (1854–1855) and Hartford (1866) to engineering codes such as the ASME Boiler Code of 1915 and later government regulation. Finally, the link between British industry and slavery is now an active research field. Scholars debate how much profit from slavery and the Atlantic trade financed British industry, but there is no doubt that Lancashire's mills depended on cotton grown by enslaved people for much of the 1800s. About 80 percent of Britain's raw cotton imports in 1860 came from the United States, which is why the American Civil War caused such a severe crisis in Lancashire.","funFact":"The wreck of the steamboat Sultana was found in 1982 buried under a soybean field in Arkansas, because the Mississippi River had shifted its course since 1865.","check":{"q":"What did the Luddites mostly break?","a":"Knitting (stocking) frames and other textile machines","wrong":["Steam engines in coal mines","Railway locomotives","Steam-powered printing presses"]},"sources":[{"title":"Factory Acts","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Factory_Acts"},{"title":"Mines and Collieries Act 1842","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mines_and_Collieries_Act_1842"},{"title":"Sultana (steamboat)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sultana_(steamboat)"},{"title":"Boiler explosion","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boiler_explosion"},{"title":"Manchester Steam Users' Association","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manchester_Steam_Users%27_Association"},{"title":"The Condition of the Working Class in England","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Condition_of_the_Working_Class_in_England"},{"title":"Luddite","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Luddite"},{"title":"Lancashire cotton famine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lancashire_cotton_famine"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/15-1.jpg","alt":"Engraving of a hurrier pulling a coal tub by a chain while two young thrusters push, in a low mine tunnel.","file":"Hurrier Cobden 1853 (v2).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hurrier_Cobden_1853_(v2).jpg","author":"John C. Cobden","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/15-2.jpg","alt":"The steamboat Sultana, crowded with soldiers, at Helena, Arkansas, on April 26, 1865, the day before the disaster.","file":"Helena, Arkansas. April 26, 1865. Ill-fated Sultana LCCN2013647457.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Helena,_Arkansas._April_26,_1865._Ill-fated_Sultana_LCCN2013647457.jpg","author":"Civil War Glass Negatives","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/15-3.jpg","alt":"An 1835 engraving of a cotton mill spinning room, with workers and a child crouched beside the long spinning mules.","file":"Baines 1835-Mule spinning.png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Baines_1835-Mule_spinning.png","author":"Baines","license":"Public domain"}],"words":4190},{"n":16,"title":"The Science Born From Engines","when":"1824 – 1860s","summary":"People built steam engines long before anyone truly understood how they worked. Then a young French engineer, an English brewer, a German professor and a Scottish-Irish physicist worked out the rules of heat and energy. Their new science, thermodynamics, explains every engine from Watt's to the one in a car.","sections":[{"heading":"Engines First, Answers Later","paragraphs":["Usually we imagine science coming first and inventions following. Scientists discover how something works, and then engineers use that knowledge to build machines. The steam engine turned this upside down. By 1820, steam engines had been working for more than a hundred years. They pumped mines in Cornwall, spun cotton in Manchester and pushed boats along rivers in America. Yet nobody could fully explain what was really going on inside them, or how good they could ever become.","Engineers improved their engines the practical way: by building, testing, measuring and trying again. In Cornwall, mine owners even published monthly reports comparing the 'duty' of their pumping engines, meaning how much water each engine lifted for a fixed amount of coal. These league tables pushed engine builders to compete, and the best Cornish engines became far more economical than the old Newcomen engines. But every improvement raised a question. If you could save a bit more coal by adding a jacket, raising the pressure or using the steam twice, could you keep going forever? Could a perfect engine turn all the heat from its fire into work? Or was there a limit that no amount of cleverness could beat?","Part of the problem was that scientists did not agree on what heat actually was. The most popular idea in the early 1800s was the caloric theory. It said heat was an invisible, weightless fluid called caloric, which flowed from hot things into cold things, a bit like water flowing downhill. A hot cup of tea, in this picture, was full of caloric that slowly leaked out into the air. The theory explained many everyday observations surprisingly well, and many of the best scientists of the time used it.","But some experiments did not fit. Around 1798, an American-born scientist named Benjamin Thompson, known as Count Rumford, was supervising the boring of cannon barrels in Munich. He noticed that the drilling made the metal very hot, and that the heat seemed to keep coming as long as the horses kept turning the drill. In one test, the friction heated enough water to make it boil in about two and a half hours. If heat were a fluid stored inside the metal, surely it would eventually run out. Rumford suggested instead that heat was connected with motion. Not everyone was convinced at the time, but he had planted a seed.","So, at the start of the 1820s, engineers had working engines but no theory, and scientists had a theory of heat that might be wrong. The person who first tied these threads together was a quiet young French army engineer."]},{"heading":"A Young Frenchman's Little Book","paragraphs":["Nicolas Léonard Sadi Carnot was born in Paris in 1796. His father, Lazare Carnot, was a famous mathematician and a leading figure in the armies of the French Revolution, sometimes called the 'Organizer of Victory'. Sadi studied at the École polytechnique, France's top school for engineers and scientists, and then became an officer in the army's engineering corps. He was shy and thoughtful, interested in music, mathematics and, above all, in machines.","Carnot was struck by something that worried many French people after the long wars with Britain ended in 1815. Britain had thousands of steam engines driving its mines and mills, and France had far fewer. Carnot believed steam power was a big part of Britain's strength, and that France needed to understand it better. Yet when he looked at the engineering books of the time, he found that they described particular engines but offered no general theory. Why did some engines use less coal than others? Would another substance, such as air or alcohol vapour, work better than steam? Nobody knew.","In June 1824, Carnot published a short book called Reflections on the Motive Power of Fire. 'Motive power' meant the ability to make things move, in other words to do work. The book was only about 118 pages long, and Carnot paid to have 600 copies printed. He was just 28 years old. The book was clear and elegant, written so that non-specialists could follow it, but it attracted very little attention at the time.","Carnot's central idea was beautifully simple. He compared a heat engine to a waterwheel. A waterwheel does work because water falls from a high place to a low place. The higher the fall, the more work each bucketful can do. In the same way, Carnot argued, a heat engine does work because heat 'falls' from a hot body to a cold body. In a steam engine, the hot body is the boiler and its fire. The cold body is the condenser, or the surrounding air. Without a cold place for the heat to flow into, there could be no work at all, no matter how hot the fire.","This was a startling claim. It meant the cold part of an engine is just as essential as the hot part. It explained why Watt's separate condenser had been such a good idea, and why engines with a bigger difference in temperature between the hot and cold sides could, in principle, do more work from the same heat. Carnot also realised that what mattered was the temperatures themselves, not whether the engine used steam, air or something else.","Sadly, Carnot did not live to see his ideas recognised. In 1832 a cholera epidemic swept through Paris. Carnot caught the disease and died on 24 August 1832, aged only 36. Because people feared the infection could spread, many of his belongings are said to have been buried with him, and his brother Hippolyte later destroyed most of his personal papers. Some of his private notes did survive, and when Hippolyte published extracts from them in 1878, decades later, they showed that Carnot had begun to doubt the caloric theory himself and to suspect that heat and work were connected."]},{"heading":"The Perfect Imaginary Engine","paragraphs":["To find the limit of all engines, Carnot did something clever. Instead of studying a real, leaky, noisy engine, he imagined a perfect one, with no friction, no leaks and no heat wasted by flowing where it should not. Today we call it the Carnot engine, and the series of steps it goes through is called the Carnot cycle.","Here is the idea in simple terms. Imagine a cylinder with a piston, filled with some gas or steam. First, the cylinder touches the hot source and takes in heat while the gas expands slowly, pushing the piston and doing work. Next, the cylinder is cut off from the heat, and the gas keeps expanding on its own, cooling down until it reaches the temperature of the cold side. Then the cylinder touches the cold side, and the piston pushes back in, squeezing the gas while it hands its leftover heat to the cold side. Finally, the cylinder is cut off again, and the gas is squeezed further until it heats up back to the starting temperature. The cycle can then repeat. Because more work comes out during the hot expansion than goes in during the cool squeeze, the engine produces useful work overall.","Carnot showed that this ideal engine has a special property: it is reversible. Run it backwards, putting work in, and it would carry heat from the cold side up to the hot side, like a refrigerator. Using this idea, he argued that no engine working between the same two temperatures could ever beat it. If some super-engine could do better, you could hook it up to a reversed Carnot engine and get work out of nothing, which seemed impossible.","Later scientists turned Carnot's insight into a formula. The best possible efficiency of any heat engine depends only on the temperatures of the hot and cold sides, measured on an absolute scale that starts at absolute zero (we will meet that scale soon). In words: the maximum efficiency equals one minus the cold temperature divided by the hot temperature.","Let's try it. An early low-pressure steam engine might have steam at about 100 °C, which is about 373 kelvin, and a condenser at about 30 °C, which is about 303 kelvin. Divide 303 by 373 and you get about 0.81. One minus 0.81 is 0.19, so even a perfect engine at those temperatures could turn at most about 19 percent of the heat into work. Real engines of the time managed far less. Now imagine a modern power station with steam at about 600 °C (about 873 kelvin) and the same cold side. The limit jumps to about 65 percent. That single calculation explains two hundred years of engineering: to make better engines, make the hot side hotter and the cold side colder, and waste as little as possible along the way."]},{"heading":"The Brewer Who Measured Heat","paragraphs":["While Carnot's little book sat almost forgotten, a boy in northern England was growing up in a family of brewers. James Prescott Joule was born on 24 December 1818 in Salford, right next to Manchester, the steam-powered heart of the cotton industry. He was a shy, often unwell child and was taught at home. For a time he and his brother were tutored by John Dalton, the famous chemist who developed the modern idea of atoms. Joule later helped run the family brewery, but his real passion was experiments, which he carried out at home with great care.","Around 1840, Joule became excited about electric motors, which were brand new. Could they replace the brewery's steam engines? To find out, he needed to compare carefully how much work he could get from burning coal in a steam engine, and from the chemicals used up in a battery to drive a motor. This led him to measure how electric currents heat the wires they flow through. In 1841 he showed that the heat produced depends on the resistance of the wire and on the square of the current, a rule now called Joule's law. (Coal won the contest: batteries of the time were far too expensive.)","Joule then asked a deeper question. If you use work to do something, such as stirring water or pushing a current through a wire, and you get heat, is there a fixed exchange rate? In other words, does a certain amount of work always produce exactly the same amount of heat? If so, heat could not be a separate fluid; it must be another form of the same thing as work.","His most famous experiment used a paddle wheel inside a container of water. A weight hung from a string wound around the axle. As the weight slowly fell, it turned the paddles, which churned the water. The friction of the paddles against the water warmed it, but only very slightly. Joule had to measure temperature changes of a fraction of a degree, using some of the most sensitive thermometers in the world, while making sure the heat of his own body or the room did not spoil the result. He repeated the experiment many times, then calculated how far a weight had to fall to warm a pound of water by one degree Fahrenheit.","His answer, from experiments in the 1840s that he refined by 1850, was that about 772 foot-pounds of work produced that much heat (his earlier runs had given somewhat different numbers, such as 770 and 819). In plain words: if you lifted a one-pound weight about 772 feet into the air and let it fall, all that work turned into heat would warm one pound of water by just one degree Fahrenheit. This number became known as the mechanical equivalent of heat.","When Joule first announced his ideas, at a British Association meeting in Cork, Ireland, in August 1843, the audience reportedly listened in silence. Many scientists thought a young brewer was not a serious researcher. Joule was not the only one with this idea, either. A German doctor, Julius Robert Mayer, had published a similar claim in 1842, based partly on his observations as a ship's doctor, and in 1847 the German scientist Hermann von Helmholtz published a powerful general statement of the conservation of energy. There was a bitter argument later about who deserved the most credit. Today historians give credit to several people, while recognising that Joule's careful measurements did the most to convince other scientists."]},{"heading":"The Laws of Energy","paragraphs":["In 1847 Joule presented his work again, this time at a British Association meeting in Oxford. In the audience was a brilliant young professor named William Thomson. Thomson had been born in Belfast in 1824 and became professor of natural philosophy (physics) at the University of Glasgow in 1846, when he was only 22, the same university where James Watt had once repaired a model Newcomen engine. Thomson was impressed by Joule, though at first he was not sure how to fit Joule's ideas together with Carnot's, which he admired deeply. The two men later became friends and worked together on experiments from 1852 to 1856. Joule married Amelia Grimes in August 1847, and a well-known story says that when Thomson met the couple in the Alps soon afterwards, Joule was planning to measure whether the water at the bottom of a waterfall was slightly warmer than at the top, as his theory predicted. The plan turned out to be impractical, but it shows how completely his idea filled his thoughts.","In 1848 Thomson proposed an absolute temperature scale based on Carnot's ideas about heat engines, instead of on the behaviour of any particular substance like mercury. The scale starts at absolute zero, the coldest possible temperature, at about −273.15 °C, where things would have the least possible heat energy. Each step on the scale is the same size as a degree Celsius, so water freezes at about 273 kelvin and boils at about 373 kelvin. Thomson later became Lord Kelvin, taking his title from the River Kelvin that flows past the University of Glasgow, and the unit of this scale is now called the kelvin in his honour. Thomson also used the word 'thermodynamic' in 1849, from Greek words meaning 'heat' and 'power'.","Meanwhile, in Germany, Rudolf Clausius had been reading Carnot's work through an 1834 paper by the French engineer Émile Clapeyron, who had explained Carnot's ideas with graphs and equations. Clausius was born in 1822 in Köslin, in Prussia. In 1850 he published a paper on the moving force of heat that showed how Carnot's reasoning and Joule's discovery could both be true at once. Carnot had been right that work needs heat to flow from hot to cold. But he had been wrong that the heat arrives at the cold side unchanged, like water at the bottom of a waterfall. Some of the heat is actually used up, converted into work. Thomson reached very similar conclusions in a paper of 1851.","Out of this work came the laws of thermodynamics, the science of heat, energy and work. The first law says that energy is never created or destroyed; it only changes form. Chemical energy in coal becomes heat, heat becomes the motion of a piston, motion becomes electricity, and so on, but if you add up all the energy at the end, including the waste heat, the total is the same as at the start. This is why no machine can produce endless work from nothing. A 'perpetual motion machine' is not just difficult to build; the first law says it is impossible.","The second law is subtler, and in some ways more surprising. One simple version, close to how Clausius put it, says that heat does not flow by itself from a colder body to a hotter one. Your cold drink warms up in a hot room; the room never gets hotter by sucking heat out of the drink. Another version, from Thomson's work, says that no engine working in a cycle can take heat from a hot source and turn all of it into work. Some heat must always be passed on to something colder. That is why every steam engine, car engine and power station has an exhaust, a radiator, a condenser or a cooling tower. It is not just poor design. It is a law of nature.","In 1865 Clausius gave a name to the quantity at the heart of the second law: entropy, from a Greek word for 'transformation'. Roughly speaking, entropy measures how spread out and disordered energy is. Heat flowing from hot to cold spreads energy out and increases entropy, and in any real process the total entropy goes up. Clausius summed up the two laws in one famous pair of statements: the energy of the universe is constant, and the entropy of the universe tends to a maximum."]},{"heading":"Keeping Score: How Efficient Were Engines?","paragraphs":["With the new science, engineers finally had a way to measure engines against a perfect standard. Efficiency means the share of the fuel's energy that becomes useful work, instead of waste heat. Looking back with modern measurements, we can give each stage of steam history a rough score.","Newcomen's atmospheric engines, from 1712 onwards, turned only about 1 percent of the energy in their coal into useful work, or even less. Most of the rest went into heating up the cylinder again after every cold spray. Watt's engines, with their separate condenser, used far less coal for the same work, and their efficiency was a few percent. Arthur Woolf's compound engines of the early 1800s reached about 7.5 percent, nearly twice as good as Watt's. Each step was a big gain in coal saved, even though the numbers still look small.","In the second half of the 1800s, engineers used the new science to guide them. A Scottish engineer, William Rankine, also at the University of Glasgow, worked out the ideal cycle for engines that boil a liquid and then condense it again. It is still called the Rankine cycle, and it is the basic cycle of every steam power station today. Boilers became stronger, so pressures rose. Marine engines in the 1870s worked at about 45 to 60 pounds per square inch; by 1891, triple-expansion engines, which used the steam three times in three cylinders of increasing size, ran at about 160 pounds per square inch. They burned about 1.5 pounds of coal for each horsepower for an hour, compared with 2 to 2.5 pounds for the earlier compound engines. The best large engines of the late 1800s reached roughly 10 to 20 percent efficiency.","Modern power stations do much better. A large coal-fired station using steam turbines with very hot, high-pressure steam can turn about 40 to 46 percent of its fuel's energy into electricity. Combined-cycle gas plants, which use a gas turbine and then make steam from its hot exhaust to drive a steam turbine, can approach or exceed 60 percent. All of these numbers sit below the Carnot limit for their temperatures, just as the science predicts. No engineer has ever beaten it, and the second law says no one ever will."]},{"heading":"Every Heat Engine Since","paragraphs":["Thermodynamics began with steam, but it soon became one of the most important sciences of all. Any machine that turns heat into motion is a heat engine, and the same rules apply to every one of them. A petrol car engine burns fuel inside its cylinders; it has a very hot side (the burning gas) and a cold side (the outside air), and a typical car engine turns only about a quarter of its fuel energy into motion. The rest leaves as heat through the exhaust pipe and the radiator. The largest diesel engines, used in giant ships, can reach just over 50 percent. Jet engines in aeroplanes, gas turbines in power stations and the rocket engines that launch spacecraft are all heat engines too, and their designers use exactly the same laws first worked out from steam.","The laws also work in reverse. A refrigerator, an air conditioner or a heat pump is a heat engine running backwards, like Carnot's reversible engine. It uses work, usually from an electric motor, to pump heat from a cold place to a warmer place. The second law explains why this always needs energy: heat will not flow uphill on its own.","The new science changed how we talk about energy and power, too. The unit of energy, the joule, is named after James Joule. The unit of power, the watt, is named after James Watt, and one watt means one joule of energy every second. So a 60-watt light bulb uses 60 joules every second, and one horsepower, Watt's old selling unit, is about 746 joules every second. The unit of absolute temperature, the kelvin, honours William Thomson. In a way, the history of the steam engine is written into the units scientists use every day.","Thermodynamics even reached far beyond engines. Chemists use it to predict which reactions can happen. Biologists use it to understand how living cells use energy from food. Astronomers use it to study stars, and Thomson himself wondered whether the whole universe would one day run down as its heat spread evenly everywhere, an idea sometimes called the 'heat death' of the universe. All of this grew out of a practical question about how to save coal in a pumping engine.","There was one more lesson in the new science. If the best engines need the hottest possible steam and the smoothest possible flow of energy, then the old design of a heavy piston sliding back and forth, stopping and reversing at the end of every stroke, was not ideal. What if steam could simply rush through a machine and spin a wheel directly, with no piston at all? That was exactly the kind of spinning that Hero of Alexandria had shown with his little steam ball almost 1,800 years earlier, and a young engineer in England was about to make it work on a giant scale."]}],"deepDive":"Carnot's theorem states that no heat engine working between a hot reservoir at absolute temperature Th and a cold reservoir at Tc can be more efficient than a reversible engine, whose efficiency is 1 − Tc/Th. Remarkably, Carnot reached this conclusion in 1824 while using the caloric theory, which treated heat as a conserved fluid. His key arguments, that work requires a temperature difference, that engines should be analysed as complete cycles, and that reversible processes set the upper limit, survived the collapse of caloric theory. Clausius's achievement in 1850 was to keep Carnot's principle while replacing the conservation of caloric with the conservation of energy, which Joule, Mayer and Helmholtz had established in the 1840s. Thomson's absolute temperature scale (1848) was designed so that it did not depend on the properties of any substance; it was defined through ideal engines, which is why it fits Carnot's formula so naturally. Entropy, introduced by Clausius in 1865, is defined so that a reversible transfer of heat Q at temperature T changes entropy by Q/T. In a Carnot engine, the entropy taken in from the hot side equals the entropy dumped to the cold side, which leads directly to the efficiency formula. In any real, irreversible engine, entropy is created by friction, leaks and heat flowing across finite temperature differences, so real efficiency falls below the Carnot limit. Historians also discuss the 'priority dispute' over energy conservation: Mayer published first (1842), but in a less convincing form, and supporters of Joule, including Thomson, defended his claim; later, the physicist John Tyndall argued publicly for Mayer's importance in 1862. Today credit is usually shared. Another interesting point is the direction of influence: thermodynamics did not invent the steam engine, but once formulated it guided later engineering, from Rankine's analysis of steam cycles to the very high temperatures and pressures of modern supercritical power plants and combined-cycle gas turbines.","funFact":"Sadi Carnot paid to print only 600 copies of his 1824 book, and it was almost ignored, yet it is now seen as the founding work of thermodynamics.","check":{"q":"According to Carnot, what does every heat engine need in order to do work?","a":"A hot place and a colder place, so heat can flow from hot to cold","wrong":["Water that is kept below its boiling point","A fuel that burns without any smoke","A piston made of iron rather than brass"]},"sources":[{"title":"Nicolas Léonard Sadi Carnot","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nicolas_L%C3%A9onard_Sadi_Carnot"},{"title":"Reflections on the Motive Power of Fire","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reflections_on_the_Motive_Power_of_Fire"},{"title":"James Prescott Joule","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/James_Prescott_Joule"},{"title":"Mechanical equivalent of heat","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mechanical_equivalent_of_heat"},{"title":"Rudolf Clausius","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rudolf_Clausius"},{"title":"William Thomson, 1st Baron Kelvin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_Thomson,_1st_Baron_Kelvin"},{"title":"Thermal efficiency","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermal_efficiency"},{"title":"Compound steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compound_steam_engine"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/16-1.jpg","alt":"Young Sadi Carnot, aged about 17, in his École polytechnique uniform, painted by Louis-Léopold Boilly in 1813.","file":"Sadi Carnot.jpeg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sadi_Carnot.jpeg","author":"Louis-Léopold Boilly","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/16-2.jpg","alt":"Old engraving of Joule's apparatus: a falling weight turns paddles inside a tank of water, warming it slightly.","file":"Joule's Apparatus (Harper's Scan).png","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Joule%27s_Apparatus_(Harper%27s_Scan).png","author":"Jacob Abbott","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/16-3.jpg","alt":"Title page of Carnot's 1824 book 'Réflexions sur la puissance motrice du feu', printed in Paris.","file":"Réflexions 1824.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:R%C3%A9flexions_1824.jpg","author":"Unknown typesetter","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3935},{"n":17,"title":"Parsons and the Spinning Turbine","when":"1880s – 1910s","summary":"A clever engineer named Charles Parsons found a way to make steam spin a wheel directly, with no piston thumping back and forth. His turbine whirled thousands of times a minute, perfect for making electricity. Then he built a tiny boat called Turbinia that zoomed past the whole Royal Navy and amazed the world.","sections":[{"heading":"The Trouble With Pistons","paragraphs":["By the 1880s, steam engines were everywhere: in mills, mines, ships and factories. They were bigger, safer and far more efficient than Newcomen's nodding giant had ever been. But almost all of them still worked in the same basic way. A piston slid one way inside a cylinder, stopped, then slid back the other way. Engineers call this reciprocating motion, which simply means moving back and forth. A crank and a heavy flywheel then turned that back-and-forth shove into a turning motion.","This worked, but it had problems. Imagine a heavy metal piston, perhaps as wide as a dinner table, racing to one end of a cylinder, slamming to a stop, then racing back again, many times every minute. Every stroke shook the engine, the floor and the building around it. Big engines needed massive stone or brick foundations to stop them from rattling themselves to pieces. The faster you tried to run them, the worse the shaking became, and the more the moving parts wore out.","Then a brand-new customer for steam power appeared: electricity. In 1882 Thomas Edison opened his Pearl Street Station in Lower Manhattan, New York, one of the first central power stations in the world. It lit about 400 lamps for 82 customers when it opened on 4 September 1882, and within two years it was serving more than 500 customers and over 10,000 lamps. The power came from coal-fired boilers and piston steam engines driving dynamos (early generators). Edison's team even had to swap out their first engines, because the speed regulators on them were not steady enough for the dynamos.","Here was the difficulty. A generator works best when it spins fast and very smoothly. Inside, a coil of wire and a magnet move past each other, and the faster they move, the more electricity you get from a machine of a given size. Piston engines, though, liked to run at a few hundred turns per minute at most. To speed things up, engineers used belts and gears, which wasted energy and needed constant care. Any wobble in the engine's speed made electric lights flicker.","So engineers had a puzzle. Could you make steam produce a smooth, fast, steady spin directly, with no piston at all? Remember Hero of Alexandria's aeolipile from Part 2, the hollow ball that whirled around as jets of steam shot out of it. That ancient toy showed that steam could make something spin. The challenge, nearly 1,800 years later, was to turn that idea into a powerful, efficient machine that could run day and night. Two engineers, one in Sweden and one in England, were about to crack it in two different ways."]},{"heading":"A Boy From a Castle Full of Science","paragraphs":["Charles Algernon Parsons was born in London on 13 June 1854, but he grew up mostly at Birr Castle in central Ireland. That was no ordinary home. His father, William Parsons, the 3rd Earl of Rosse, was a famous astronomer who had built a gigantic telescope in the castle grounds, nicknamed the Leviathan of Parsonstown. For decades it was the largest telescope in the world, and with it Lord Rosse saw the spiral shapes of distant galaxies (then called nebulae) more clearly than anyone before him.","Charles was the youngest son, and the Parsons children were taught at home by private tutors, some of them serious scientists. The castle had workshops where the boys could tinker with tools, lathes and metal, and they built all sorts of contraptions. It was the perfect training ground for a future engineer. Growing up surrounded by giant machines, careful measurements and the idea that you could build something no one else had built must have shaped how he thought.","Parsons went on to study mathematics at Trinity College Dublin and then at St John's College, Cambridge, graduating in 1877. At that time it was unusual for a wealthy young man with a university degree to go and get his hands dirty in a factory. But that is exactly what he did. He became an apprentice at the huge Elswick engineering works of William Armstrong in Newcastle upon Tyne, in the north-east of England, a place that built cranes, bridges, ships and guns.","After a few years working on other engine ideas, in 1884 Parsons joined the Newcastle firm of Clarke, Chapman and Company as a junior partner, in charge of a new department making electrical equipment for ships. The firm wanted a way to light ships with electricity, and that meant needing a compact, fast-spinning engine to drive a generator. This was exactly the problem Parsons had been thinking about. Newcastle, by the way, was a great place to be an engineer: it was surrounded by coal mines, shipyards and ironworks, and the River Tyne was full of ships.","Parsons was quiet, stubborn and extremely practical. He liked to test ideas by building them, measuring them and building them again. Colleagues remembered him as shy in company but fearless in the workshop. Those qualities would matter, because the machine he was about to build would have to spin faster than almost anything engineers had ever tried."]},{"heading":"Two Ways to Push a Wheel: Impulse and Reaction","paragraphs":["Before we look at Parsons's machine, let's understand the two main ways steam can spin a turbine. A turbine is a machine where a moving fluid, like steam, water or air, pushes on rows of blades to spin a shaft directly. A windmill and a waterwheel are simple relatives. In a steam turbine, the blades are small, curved and set around the rim of a wheel, a bit like the teeth of a comb bent into a circle.","The first way is called impulse. Picture a garden hose spraying a strong jet of water at the paddles of a toy waterwheel. The jet hits each paddle, pushes it, and the wheel spins. In an impulse turbine, steam is first squirted through a fixed nozzle, a specially shaped narrow opening. As it squeezes through, its pressure drops and it speeds up enormously, becoming a fast jet. That jet then slams into curved blades on a wheel, which turn the steam around and are pushed in return. All the speeding-up happens in the nozzle; the moving blades just catch the jet.","The second way is called reaction. Think of letting go of a balloon that is not tied: air rushes out the back, and the balloon shoots forward. Hero's aeolipile worked like this, and so does a spinning lawn sprinkler. In a reaction turbine, the moving blades themselves are shaped so that the steam speeds up as it passes between them, a bit like a nozzle. As the steam rushes out of the gaps, it pushes back on the blades, just as escaping air pushes a balloon. In Parsons's design, the pressure drops a little in the fixed blades and a little in the moving blades, so each moving row gets both a push from the incoming flow and a kick from the steam leaving it.","The Swedish engineer Gustaf de Laval, born in 1845 in Orsa, Sweden, chose the impulse route. De Laval was already well known for building a centrifugal machine that separated cream from milk by spinning it very fast, and he was an expert at making things whirl at high speeds. He worked out his impulse turbine idea in the early 1880s and built a small working model in 1887. His turbines had a single wheel of blades hit by steam jets, and they could reach astonishing speeds, up to about 30,000 revolutions per minute (rpm) in small versions.","To get the most out of the steam, de Laval designed a nozzle that first narrows and then widens again. This shape lets steam speed up beyond the speed of sound. It is still called the de Laval nozzle, and remarkably, the same basic shape is used today on the exhaust nozzles of rocket engines. De Laval's turbines were simple and could work with steam at many different pressures, but because all the steam's energy was used in one fast jet, the wheel had to spin incredibly quickly, and he had to invent special reduction gears to slow the motion down for useful machines."]},{"heading":"Inside Parsons's Turbine: Many Small Steps","paragraphs":["Parsons had a different insight. If you let high-pressure steam expand all at once, it rushes out so fast that the wheel would have to spin at a frightening speed to keep up, and a lot of energy is wasted. So why not let the steam drop its pressure in many small steps instead of one big one? Each step would take only a little energy from the steam, so each row of blades could move at a sensible speed, yet all the steps added together would capture most of the energy.","His turbine was a long shaft, called the rotor, covered in ring after ring of small blades sticking outwards. Around it was a tube-shaped casing with rings of fixed blades sticking inwards, fitting neatly between the moving rows. Steam entered at one end. First it passed through a ring of fixed blades, which steered it at just the right angle. Then it rushed through a ring of moving blades, pushing them round. Then another fixed ring, then another moving ring, and so on, along the whole length of the machine.","At each step, the steam lost a little pressure and spread out a little more. Because steam takes up more space as its pressure falls, Parsons made the blades and the passages slightly bigger towards the exhaust end, so there was room for the swelling steam. Engineers call this arrangement multi-stage or compounding, and it is the reason modern turbines look like a long cone or a series of drums that grow fatter towards one end. Parsons also made the steam flow along the shaft, parallel to it, which engineers call axial flow.","His first turbine, built at Clarke Chapman in 1884, was small. It drove a dynamo that Parsons had also designed himself, and together they produced about 7.5 kilowatts of electricity, roughly enough today to run a few electric kettles at once. But the speed was the real headline: about 18,000 revolutions per minute, which is about 300 turns every single second. Most steam engines of the day turned fewer than 200 times a minute. Running at that speed meant every part had to be balanced perfectly, and Parsons had to design new kinds of bearings that could cope with a little wobble without shaking themselves apart.","This historic machine survives. It is kept in the collection of the Science Museum in London, a small, plain-looking engine that started an enormous family. What made it special was not raw power, but the fact that it turned smoothly, without any back-and-forth thumping, and could be connected straight to a generator without belts. There was no heavy piston to stop and start. The steam simply flowed through and the shaft spun. It was, in a way, Hero's spinning dream made practical, but with dozens of carefully shaped steps instead of two bent nozzles."]},{"heading":"Lighting Up Newcastle","paragraphs":["A brilliant invention still has to find customers. In 1889 Parsons left Clarke Chapman to set up his own business, C. A. Parsons and Company, at Heaton in Newcastle, to build turbines joined to generators, a combination called a turbo-generator. There was a catch. His old firm kept the rights to his original patents for the axial design, where steam flows along the shaft. Rather than give up, Parsons designed a different kind of turbine in which the steam flowed outwards from the centre, called a radial-flow turbine, and sold those instead. A few years later, in 1894, he managed to buy back his original patents and returned to the axial design he preferred. It is a good example of how inventors sometimes have to invent around their own earlier inventions.","His first big break came in his home city. In 1890 the Newcastle and District Electric Lighting Company opened Forth Banks Power Station, and it was the first power station in the world to make its electricity using turbo-generators. Instead of rows of thumping engines and slapping belts, the engine room held compact machines humming at high speed. Visitors noticed how much less space they needed and how little they shook.","Parsons kept improving his machines. One important step was adding a condenser, a chamber where the used steam is cooled back into water. You met the idea in Part 7: James Watt's separate condenser. Condensing the exhaust steam creates a partial vacuum at the end of the turbine, so the steam rushes through all the blades faster and gives up more of its energy before it leaves. For a turbine, which can let steam expand all the way down to very low pressure, a good condenser made a big difference to efficiency. Parsons turbines soon used noticeably less steam for each unit of electricity than the best piston engines.","Sizes grew quickly. His first turbine produced about 7.5 kilowatts. In 1899 his company built a turbo-generator of about 1,000 kilowatts (one megawatt) for a power station in Elberfeld, Germany. That is more than a hundred times as much power in about fifteen years. Within a few more years, turbines of several thousand kilowatts were being built, and other companies in Europe and America were making their own versions, often under licence from Parsons. In the United States, Westinghouse built Parsons-type turbines, while Charles Curtis developed an impulse design that the General Electric Company built in large numbers. In France, Auguste Rateau developed a multi-stage impulse turbine from the late 1890s.","Why did turbines win the race to drive generators? Imagine you are a power-station manager in 1905. For the same power, a turbine was much smaller and lighter than a piston engine, so your building could be smaller. It had far fewer rubbing parts, so it needed less oil and fewer repairs. It ran smoothly, so it did not need huge foundations, and it could spin a generator directly at high speed. And as turbines got bigger, they got more efficient, while giant piston engines got clumsier. By the 1910s, most new large power stations were choosing turbines."]},{"heading":"Turbinia Crashes the Party","paragraphs":["Parsons believed his turbine could do something even more exciting: drive ships. Ships of the time were powered by enormous piston engines that filled their insides with clanking metal and shook the whole hull. To prove his point, he and a group of friends formed a company and built an experimental vessel. They called her Turbinia. She was built at Wallsend on the River Tyne and launched on 2 August 1894. She was long and narrow, about 32 metres (105 feet) long but only about 2.7 metres (9 feet) wide, shaped like an arrow for speed.","At first, Turbinia was a disappointment. With a single turbine driving a single propeller, she was nowhere near as fast as Parsons hoped. The propeller was spinning so fast that it churned the water into a froth of bubbles. Engineers now call this cavitation: when a propeller spins very fast, the water pressure behind its blades drops so low that little pockets of vapour form, and the propeller loses its grip on the water. Parsons was one of the first people to study this properly. He even built a small water tunnel with a glass side, where he could watch model propellers spinning and see the bubbles form.","Parsons rebuilt Turbinia's machinery. He fitted three turbines, the steam passing from one to the next, each driving its own propeller shaft. Each shaft carried three propellers, so the little boat had nine propellers in total. Now her engines produced about 2,000 horsepower, a huge amount for a boat that weighed only about 44 tonnes. On trials she reached about 34.5 knots, roughly 64 kilometres per hour. That made her the fastest ship in the world.","Then came the most famous stunt in engineering history. On 26 June 1897, the Royal Navy held a grand review of its fleet at Spithead, the stretch of sea near Portsmouth, to celebrate Queen Victoria's Diamond Jubilee (60 years on the throne). Picture it: line after line of mighty battleships and cruisers, flags flying, dignitaries watching, including the Prince of Wales, along with foreign admirals and crowds of spectators. Turbinia had not been invited.","Suddenly the slim little boat appeared and raced up and down between the lines of warships, trailing smoke and spray, faster than anything else afloat. A Navy patrol boat set off to stop her, but it could not catch her. Whether the Navy chiefs were annoyed or impressed, everybody had seen what a turbine could do. Newspapers reported it around the world. It was a daring piece of showmanship, but it was backed by careful science: Parsons had done the tests and knew his boat could outrun anything in the review."]},{"heading":"Turbines Rule the Waves","paragraphs":["The Royal Navy did not take long to be convinced. Parsons's company built turbine engines for two experimental destroyers, HMS Viper and HMS Cobra, launched in 1899. Sadly, both ships were lost in separate accidents in 1901, one running onto rocks and one breaking apart in heavy seas, but neither loss was blamed on the turbines. Turbine-powered passenger ferries also appeared, starting with the Clyde steamer King Edward in 1901, and passengers loved how smooth and quiet they were compared with the shuddering old steamers.","The big moment for navies came with HMS Dreadnought. In January 1905 a design committee decided she would use turbines rather than piston engines, which saved about 1,100 tonnes of weight. Dreadnought was built astonishingly quickly: laid down in October 1905, launched in February 1906 and completed by the end of that year. Her Parsons turbines produced about 23,000 horsepower and drove four propeller shafts, giving a top speed of about 21 knots. She was so fast and so heavily armed that she made every older battleship look out of date overnight. Soon navies spoke of 'dreadnoughts' as a whole new class of ship, and nearly all of them used turbines.","Ocean liners followed. The British Cunard Line built two huge, fast liners, Lusitania and Mauretania. Mauretania was launched in 1906 at the Swan Hunter yard in Wallsend, just down the Tyne from where Turbinia had been built, and made her first voyage in November 1907. She had four Parsons turbines, driving four propellers, with a total of about 68,000 horsepower, and could steam at about 25 knots. Feeding her boilers was hard work: she burned about 1,000 tonnes of coal a day, shovelled by hundreds of stokers in hot boiler rooms deep inside the ship.","Mauretania became famous for speed. In those days, the ship with the fastest Atlantic crossing held an honour called the Blue Riband. Mauretania won the eastbound record in 1907 and the westbound record in 1909, and she kept the Blue Riband until 1929, when the German liner Bremen finally beat her. Twenty years at the top made her one of the best-loved ships ever built.","Not every ship went all-turbine. The Titanic, launched in 1911, used a clever mix. Her two outer propellers were driven by two huge four-cylinder triple-expansion piston engines producing about 30,000 horsepower together. But instead of throwing away the steam that came out of those engines, it was fed into a low-pressure Parsons turbine in the middle of the ship, which drove the centre propeller and added about 16,000 horsepower more. This combination squeezed extra work out of the same coal. It shows that, around 1910, engineers were still choosing the best tool for each job, mixing old and new.","One problem remained. Turbines like to spin fast, but big ship propellers work best spinning slowly, often below a few hundred turns a minute, or they cavitate. Early turbine ships drove their propellers directly and accepted the compromise. In the 1900s and 1910s, Parsons and others developed strong reduction gears that let a fast turbine turn a slow propeller, and geared turbines became standard in many large ships for decades."]},{"heading":"From a Small Machine to Giant Turbines","paragraphs":["It is worth pausing to see how fast the turbine grew. In 1884 Parsons's first machine made about 7.5 kilowatts. Around 1899 his company built a one-megawatt set. By the 1910s, single turbo-generators of many thousands of kilowatts were running in power stations in Britain, Germany and the United States, and the biggest ships had turbines producing tens of thousands of horsepower. In just about thirty years the turbine went from a curious experiment to the main way of turning heat into large amounts of electricity.","Parsons himself became one of the most honoured engineers of his time. He was elected a Fellow of the Royal Society in 1898, was knighted in 1911, and received the Order of Merit in 1927, one of Britain's highest honours. He never stopped tinkering. His company also made searchlight mirrors and large telescope mirrors and optical glass, a nice link back to his father's giant telescope at Birr Castle. He died in 1931 while on a cruise, aboard the ship Duchess of Richmond.","He was not a lone genius, though. De Laval, Curtis, Rateau and many engineers at Westinghouse, General Electric, Brown Boveri and other companies all helped shape the turbine. Metallurgists developed steels that could cope with heat and the huge forces at high speed. Mathematicians and engineers worked out better blade shapes. And the science of thermodynamics from Part 16 told them why squeezing more energy from steam needed higher temperatures and lower exhaust pressures. Turbines were a team effort spread across several countries.","Today Turbinia herself is on display at the Discovery Museum in Newcastle upon Tyne, while her original engines are at the Science Museum in London. If you ever stand next to her, notice how small she is: a slim boat that changed the navies and power stations of the world. The engineering society ASME has named her a historic engineering landmark, noting that her success helped turbines replace piston engines both in ships and in power stations.","And here is the surprise. The great-great-grandchildren of Parsons's turbine have not gone away. They are spinning right now, in huge halls all over the world, many times larger than anything Parsons imagined, turning generators that make much of the electricity flowing into the reader's home. The lamp you are reading by, or the screen in front of you, may well be powered by steam. In the next part, we will step inside one of these modern power stations and follow the steam on its journey."]}],"deepDive":"Why did the multi-stage turbine beat both the piston engine and the single-wheel impulse turbine? The answer lies in blade speed. When steam expands from high pressure to a near-vacuum in one go, it can reach speeds well over 1,000 metres per second. For a single row of blades to capture that energy efficiently, the blade tips need to move at roughly half the jet speed. That is why de Laval's single-stage machines had to spin at tens of thousands of rpm and needed heavy reduction gearing, and why their efficiency was limited. Parsons's solution, pressure compounding, divides the total pressure drop among many stages, so the steam velocity in each stage is modest and the blades can run at practical speeds. Later designs mixed approaches: Curtis used velocity compounding (several rows of moving blades catching one jet in turn), Rateau used a series of impulse stages, and modern turbines often have an impulse-type control stage at the high-pressure end followed by many reaction-type stages. Engineers describe a stage by its degree of reaction: 0 percent means a pure impulse stage, where all the pressure drop happens in the fixed nozzles, while Parsons's classic design is about 50 percent reaction, with fixed and moving blades of mirror-image shape sharing the drop. Turbines also exploited thermodynamics better than pistons. A piston engine's cylinder is alternately filled with hot and cooler steam, so heat is lost to the walls each cycle, and expanding steam to very low pressure needs an impractically huge cylinder. A turbine has steady flow: each part of the machine always sees the same temperature, and the low-pressure end can simply be made wider, letting steam expand all the way down to the condenser vacuum. That extra expansion is where much of the gain comes from. Historians debate how much credit to give each pioneer. De Laval's nozzle and high-speed work were important and his nozzle lives on in rockets, but Parsons's multi-stage reaction design, combined with his direct-drive generator and condenser, became the template for large power stations. Both men also showed something typical of this era: engineering moved from craft knowledge toward careful testing, with Parsons building one of the first cavitation tunnels to study propellers scientifically.","funFact":"Charles Parsons's father, the Earl of Rosse, built a telescope so large it was nicknamed the Leviathan, and for decades it was the biggest telescope in the world.","check":{"q":"What did Turbinia do at the Spithead naval review in 1897?","a":"She appeared uninvited and raced between the lines of warships at about 34 knots, faster than any ship there","wrong":["She towed Queen Victoria's royal yacht into harbour","She sank after her boiler exploded","She won a sailing race using only wind power"]},"sources":[{"title":"Charles Algernon Parsons","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Algernon_Parsons"},{"title":"Turbinia","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Turbinia"},{"title":"#73 Turbinia (Historic Mechanical Engineering Landmark)","publisher":"ASME","url":"https://www.asme.org/about-asme/engineering-history/landmarks/73-turbinia"},{"title":"Steam turbine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steam_turbine"},{"title":"Gustaf de Laval","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gustaf_de_Laval"},{"title":"Pearl Street Station","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Pearl_Street_Station"},{"title":"HMS Dreadnought (1906)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HMS_Dreadnought_(1906)"},{"title":"RMS Mauretania (1906)","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/RMS_Mauretania_(1906)"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/17-1.jpg","alt":"Turbinia racing at full speed around 1897, smoke pouring from her funnel and a big wave at her bow.","file":"Turbinia At Speed.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turbinia_At_Speed.jpg","author":"Alfred John West","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/17-2.jpg","alt":"Portrait photograph of Sir Charles Parsons, inventor of the steam turbine, by Russell and Sons of London.","file":"Portrait of Charles Parsons (1854-1931), Engineer (2551040213).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_Charles_Parsons_(1854-1931),_Engineer_(2551040213).jpg","author":"Smithsonian Institution from United States","license":"No restrictions"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/17-3.jpg","alt":"The spinning part (rotor) of a 1930 Parsons ship turbine, showing its many rows of small blades.","file":"Wirnik turbiny parowej ORP Wicher.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wirnik_turbiny_parowej_ORP_Wicher.jpg","author":"Topory at Polish Wikipedia","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"}],"words":4102},{"n":18,"title":"Steam in Power Stations Today","when":"1950s – today","summary":"Steam engines did not disappear. Inside most big power stations, a fire, a nuclear reactor or even the hot rocks of the Earth boil water into steam, and that steam spins giant turbines to make electricity. When you switch on a light, there is a good chance a steam turbine somewhere is helping.","sections":[{"heading":"Follow the Steam: A Tour of a Power Station","paragraphs":["Imagine putting on a hard hat, ear defenders and safety boots and walking into a large thermal power station. A thermal power station is any power plant that makes electricity from heat. From the outside you might see a tall chimney, huge grey cooling towers and a tangle of pipes. Inside, it is like a giant version of the machines you have met in this story, all connected in one big loop of water and steam. Let's follow a single drop of water around that loop.","Our drop starts as very pure water. Power stations clean their water carefully, because even tiny amounts of minerals could leave crusty deposits inside the pipes, just like the scale that forms inside a kettle in a hard-water area. Powerful feed pumps push the water into the boiler at enormous pressure, often more than a hundred times the pressure of the air around us.","In the boiler, the water flows through thousands of metres of steel tubes lining the walls of a tall furnace. In a coal or gas plant, fuel burns in the middle of that furnace. In a big coal plant the boiler can be as tall as a large office building. Our drop heats up, turns into steam, and then passes through more tubes called the superheater, which heat the steam far beyond its boiling point, often to well over 500 °C. Superheated steam is completely dry and invisible, carrying a huge amount of energy.","Now the steam rushes along thick pipes to the turbine. It first enters the high-pressure turbine, where it pushes through rows of small blades and loses some of its pressure and temperature. In many plants it is then sent back to the boiler to be reheated before entering the intermediate-pressure turbine, and finally it flows into one or more low-pressure turbines with much bigger blades. All of these sit on one long shaft, which can be tens of metres long. As the steam pushes the blades, the whole shaft spins, exactly as in Parsons's design from Part 17, only far bigger.","At the end of the shaft is the generator. Inside it, a rotating electromagnet called the rotor spins inside fixed coils of copper wire called the stator. As the magnet's field sweeps past the wires, it pushes electric current through them, which is the principle Michael Faraday discovered in 1831. The electricity goes to a transformer, which raises its voltage so it can travel long distances along power lines with less waste, and then out into the electricity grid.","Meanwhile our drop, now as low-pressure steam that has given up most of its energy, leaves the last turbine and enters the condenser. This is a big chamber filled with thousands of tubes carrying cold water from a river, the sea or a cooling tower. The steam touches the cold tubes, condenses back into liquid water, and shrinks enormously, which creates a partial vacuum that helps pull steam through the turbine. That is James Watt's separate condenser idea, still at work. The drop is now water again, and the feed pumps send it back to the boiler to begin the loop once more. The same water goes round and round, again and again."]},{"heading":"Giants That Spin in Step","paragraphs":["Modern turbines are enormous. Parsons's first turbine in 1884 produced about 7.5 kilowatts. A single large modern turbine-generator can produce more than 1,000 megawatts, which is over a hundred thousand times as much. Among the biggest are the two turbine-generators planned for the Hinkley Point C nuclear station being built in England, each rated at about 1,600 megawatts. A single machine like that can supply as much electricity as a few million homes use on average.","The blades change size along the shaft. At the high-pressure end, where the steam is dense and squeezed, the blades are short, only a few centimetres long. By the low-pressure end, the steam has spread out hugely, so it needs much more room. The last row of blades in the biggest turbines can be well over a metre long, and in some machines about as long as a tall adult. Imagine a ring of steel blades as long as your teacher is tall, whirling around many times a second. The tips of these blades can move faster than the speed of sound, so they have to be made from very strong steel or titanium alloys.","Why do these machines spin at such exact speeds? Electricity from the grid is alternating current (AC), which means it rapidly changes direction back and forth. In Britain, Europe, most of Asia and Africa and Australia, it does this 50 times a second, called 50 hertz. In North America and some other places, it is 60 hertz. A typical large generator makes one complete AC cycle for each turn of its two-pole magnet. So in a 50-hertz country, the turbine shaft spins at exactly 3,000 revolutions per minute (50 turns per second), and in a 60-hertz country it spins at 3,600 rpm. Some nuclear plants use generators with four magnetic poles, so their turbines spin at half those speeds, 1,500 or 1,800 rpm, which lets the huge low-pressure blades be even longer.","Every generator connected to a grid spins in step with all the others, like a giant team of rowers keeping the same beat. If lots of people suddenly switch on kettles, for example during the break in a big televised football match, the extra demand tries to slow all the generators down a tiny bit. Control systems open the steam valves a little wider to keep the speed steady. This is a modern great-grandchild of Watt's flyball governor from Part 9, now run by computers and sensors.","These machines are built to run for decades. A large turbine-generator might be overhauled every few years, when engineers open the casing, inspect every blade for cracks or erosion, and rebalance the rotor. A steel rotor weighing many tonnes must be balanced so precisely that it spins smoothly at thousands of rpm, just as Parsons had to balance his small rotor at 18,000 rpm."]},{"heading":"Many Fires, One Big Kettle","paragraphs":["Here is a surprising idea: a power station is, in a way, a gigantic kettle. What changes from one type of plant to another is mostly the heat source, the thing that boils the water. After that, the steam, turbine, generator and condenser work in much the same way. Engineers call this a steam cycle, or the Rankine cycle, after the Scottish engineer William Rankine who described it in the 1850s.","Coal was for a long time the biggest heat source. Coal is ground into a fine powder, almost like flour, and blown into the furnace with air, where it burns in a huge fireball. Coal still made about a third of the world's electricity in 2024, more than any other single source. Oil-fired steam plants work in a similar way, though they are less common today.","Natural gas is used in a clever way. The most efficient gas power plants are called combined-cycle plants. First, the gas is burned in a gas turbine, a machine much like a jet engine bolted to the ground, which spins its own generator. But the hot exhaust coming out of the gas turbine is still very hot, roughly 450 to 650 °C. Instead of wasting it up a chimney, it passes through a heat recovery steam generator, a boiler that uses that leftover heat to make steam. The steam then spins a steam turbine and a second generator. Typically about one third of the plant's electricity comes from the steam part. By using the same fuel twice, the best combined-cycle plants turn more than 60 percent of the fuel's energy into electricity, and one has been officially certified at just over 62 percent, while makers claim up to about 64 percent for their newest designs.","Biomass power stations burn wood pellets, crop waste or other plant material instead of coal, and some old coal plants have been converted to do this. Waste-to-energy plants burn rubbish that cannot be recycled and use the heat to make steam. Concentrated solar power plants use huge fields of mirrors to focus sunlight and heat a fluid, which then makes steam for a turbine; you will hear more about them in Part 19. And some industrial factories, like paper mills and sugar refineries, burn their own leftovers to make steam both for electricity and for their manufacturing processes.","Because so many kinds of power plant rely on steam turbines in the end, steam still plays a starring role in the world's electricity. In 2024, coal and gas together made over half of the world's electricity, and most of that, plus nearly all nuclear power, came from generators turned by steam turbines. So roughly half or more of all the world's electricity still comes from steam. In the United States alone, power-plant steam turbines made about 42 percent of the country's electricity in 2022, not counting the steam part of combined-cycle plants. Hero's spinning ball, Newcomen's pump and Watt's condenser all have descendants in this story."]},{"heading":"Splitting Atoms to Boil Water","paragraphs":["Nuclear power sounds like science fiction, but at its heart a nuclear power station is another way of boiling water. Inside the reactor, atoms of uranium are split apart in a process called nuclear fission. Each split releases a burst of energy, mostly as heat, and also sends out tiny particles called neutrons that can split more atoms, keeping a controlled chain reaction going. Control rods, made of materials that soak up neutrons, are moved in and out to keep that reaction steady. A small amount of uranium fuel can release an enormous amount of heat, far more than the same weight of coal.","That heat has to be carried away by a coolant. In many reactors, ordinary water flows around the fuel. In a boiling water reactor, the water boils right inside the reactor and the steam goes straight to the turbine. In a pressurised water reactor, the most common type in the world, the water around the fuel is kept at such high pressure that it cannot boil even at around 300 °C. It flows to a steam generator, a big heat exchanger, where it heats a separate loop of water that does boil. That second loop's steam spins the turbine. Keeping two loops means the water that touches the fuel never goes into the turbine hall.","The first nuclear power station to send electricity into a public grid was at Obninsk, a science town south-west of Moscow in what was then the Soviet Union. It was connected in June 1954. Its single reactor, called AM-1, produced about 30 megawatts of heat and around 5 megawatts of electricity, a tiny amount by today's standards, but it showed the idea worked. Obninsk kept making electricity until 2002.","In Britain, Calder Hall in Cumbria, on the site now called Sellafield, was opened by Queen Elizabeth II on 17 October 1956. It is often described as the world's first full-scale nuclear power station. Its four reactors were cooled by carbon dioxide gas rather than water. The hot gas flowed through heat exchangers that boiled water into steam, and that steam drove turbines. Calder Hall's main job was actually to make plutonium for Britain's nuclear weapons programme, and electricity was treated as a useful by-product. It ran until 2003, and its four cooling towers were demolished in 2007.","Since then, hundreds of nuclear reactors have been built around the world, and almost all of them do the same final job: they make steam to spin a turbine. That is why nuclear stations need the same big turbine halls, condensers and often the same giant cooling towers as coal plants. The reactor is simply a very powerful and very carefully controlled heat source for an old idea."]},{"heading":"Steam From the Earth: Larderello","paragraphs":["Sometimes nature makes the steam for us. Deep underground, the rocks are hot, and in some places, especially near volcanoes and where the Earth's crust is thin, rainwater seeps down, gets heated and bubbles back up as hot water or steam. This is called geothermal energy, from Greek words meaning 'earth heat'.","One of the most famous places for it is Larderello, in Tuscany in central Italy. The area is full of steaming vents and bubbling pools, and the Romans used its hot springs for bathing. In the 1800s a businessman named François Jacques de Larderel found a way to use the natural steam to help extract boric acid, a useful chemical, from the mud, and the village was named after him.","On 4 July 1904, Prince Piero Ginori Conti tried something new at Larderello. He used natural steam to drive a small engine connected to a dynamo, and it lit four light bulbs. That modest glow was the first electricity ever made from geothermal steam. A small commercial geothermal power station followed in the area in the early 1910s. Today Larderello's power plants produce about 10 percent of all the geothermal electricity in the world, nearly 5,000 gigawatt-hours a year.","Other places followed. In 1960, The Geysers in northern California began producing geothermal electricity, and it grew into one of the largest geothermal fields on Earth. Some countries rely on Earth heat a lot. In 2021, geothermal power made about 29 percent of Iceland's electricity and about 43 percent of Kenya's, where power stations tap the hot rocks of the Great Rift Valley.","There are a few kinds of geothermal plant. The simplest, used at Larderello and The Geysers, is a dry steam plant, where natural steam from wells goes straight into a turbine. Flash steam plants take very hot water under pressure and let its pressure drop suddenly so some of it flashes into steam. Binary plants use cooler water to boil a different liquid with a lower boiling point, which then spins the turbine. In every case, the turning still comes from a hot gas pushing turbine blades."]},{"heading":"Hotter, Harder, Better: Supercritical Steam","paragraphs":["Remember Sadi Carnot from Part 16? He showed that a heat engine works better the bigger the difference between its hot side and its cold side. Power-station engineers take that lesson very seriously. The hotter they can make the steam going into the turbine, and the colder the condenser at the end, the more of the fuel's energy can become electricity.","But making steam hotter means raising its pressure too, and something strange happens to water at extreme conditions. Above about 374 °C and about 22 megapascals of pressure (roughly 220 times the pressure of the air around us), water reaches what scientists call its critical point. Beyond it, water no longer boils at all. There are no bubbles and no clear difference between liquid and gas; the water simply gets thinner and thinner as it heats up, smoothly becoming a hot, dense fluid. This is called supercritical water.","Supercritical boilers do not need the big steam drum that older boilers used to separate steam from water, so engineers call them once-through boilers. The idea was patented by Mark Benson in 1922, and the first commercial supercritical power-station unit started running at the Philo plant in Ohio in 1957. Today, ultra-supercritical plants push steam to around 600 °C or more, and researchers are testing materials for even hotter conditions, above 700 °C. The challenge is metal: at such temperatures and pressures, ordinary steel slowly creeps and weakens, so engineers use special alloys containing chromium, nickel and other metals.","How much difference does this make? Compare the numbers from Part 16: Newcomen's engines managed around 1 percent efficiency. Modern coal plants turn roughly 33 to 45 percent of their fuel's energy into electricity, with older, subcritical plants at the lower end and the best ultra-supercritical plants near the top. Gas combined-cycle plants can pass 60 percent. Each extra percentage point means less fuel burned for the same electricity, which saves money and cuts pollution and carbon dioxide.","Nuclear plants usually run their steam at lower temperatures, around 280 to 300 °C, partly for safety and because of what the reactor materials can cope with. That is why a typical nuclear plant's efficiency is only about 33 percent, and why its low-pressure turbines are so huge: they have to handle enormous volumes of cooler steam."]},{"heading":"Clouds That Are Not Smoke","paragraphs":["Because no engine can turn all its heat into work, as the second law of thermodynamics says, every thermal power station has to get rid of a lot of leftover heat. In a typical plant, more heat is thrown away than becomes electricity. This waste heat is removed by the condenser's cooling water. Plants beside the sea or a large river may use that water directly and return it slightly warmer. Many inland plants use cooling towers.","The giant curved cooling towers you see near many power stations are called natural-draft hyperboloid towers, from the smooth hourglass-like curve of their shape. Large ones can be up to about 200 metres tall and about 100 metres wide at the base. The design was patented by two Dutch engineers, Frederik van Iterson and Gerard Kuypers, in 1916, and the first reinforced-concrete one was built at a coal mine in Heerlen in the Netherlands in 1917–1918. The curved shape is strong and stable while using relatively little concrete.","Inside, warm water from the condenser is sprayed or trickled over packing near the bottom of the tower. Air flows in through openings around the base. As some of the water evaporates into the air, it takes heat with it and cools the rest of the water, the same way sweat cools your skin. The warm, damp air rises up the tower like the hot air in a chimney, pulling in fresh cool air from below, no fans needed. The cooled water is collected and reused, while fresh water is added to replace what evaporated.","Now look at the top of the tower. That big white plume is not smoke, and it is not true steam either. Remember from Part 10 that real steam is invisible. The plume is warm, moist air that has hit the cooler outside air, and its water vapour has condensed into billions of tiny droplets, making a cloud. It is mostly just water, the same thing you see when you breathe out on a cold day. The smoke and gases from burning fuel, if any, leave through a separate tall chimney, and nuclear plants have no smoke at all, even though their cooling towers make clouds."]},{"heading":"Steam All Around Us","paragraphs":["Power stations are steam's biggest job today, but they are far from its only one. Walk into any hospital and somewhere there will be an autoclave, a strong steel chamber that uses high-pressure steam to sterilise surgical instruments and equipment. It was invented by Charles Chamberland, who worked with Louis Pasteur, in 1879, and it is a direct descendant of Denis Papin's steam digester from Part 4. A typical autoclave holds instruments in saturated steam at 121 °C, at about one atmosphere above normal pressure, for 15 minutes or more, which kills bacteria and even their tough spores. Steam works better than dry hot air because, as it condenses on cool objects, it releases its latent heat straight into them.","In kitchens, steam cooks vegetables, puffs up bread and powers pressure cookers. Restaurant espresso machines use hot water and steam under pressure, and their steam wands froth milk. Factories use steam to heat chemicals, dry paper, cook food in huge batches, and clean equipment. Some cities, including New York, even have networks of underground pipes that carry steam to heat buildings; New York's steam system dates back to the 1880s. Steam cleaners blast dirt and germs off floors and tiles without harsh chemicals.","And then there are the old engines themselves. Across the world, volunteers and museums keep historic steam engines working. At places like the Cruquius pumping station in the Netherlands, mill engines in England, and steam fairs where traction engines and steam rollers chug across the fields, you can still see pistons slide, beams rock and flywheels turn. Heritage railways run steam locomotives (their story is told in the Railways Deep Dive). These volunteers clean, oil, repair and inspect the machines, and boilers must still pass strict safety tests, a lesson learned from the explosions you read about in Part 15.","So steam is not a museum piece. It is quietly at work in hospitals, factories and kitchens, and above all in the turbine halls that light up cities. The water cycle inside a modern power station, from pump to boiler to turbine to condenser and back, links directly to every inventor in this story.","But there is a big question hanging over that picture. Much of the world's steam is still made by burning coal and gas, which release carbon dioxide and warm the planet. Countries are now trying hard to cut those emissions, building wind turbines and solar panels that make electricity with no steam at all. So what will happen to steam as the world tries to stop burning fossil fuels? Will the great turbines fall silent, or will they find new sources of heat? That is the story of the next part."]}],"deepDive":"Engineers describe a power station's steam loop as the Rankine cycle: water is pumped to high pressure, heated into steam, expanded through a turbine, and condensed back to water. Carnot's work sets the ceiling. The best possible efficiency of any heat engine is 1 minus the ratio of the cold temperature to the hot temperature, measured in kelvin. With steam at about 600 °C (873 K) and a condenser near 30 °C (303 K), the Carnot limit is roughly 65 percent. Real coal plants reach about 40 to 45 percent at best, because heat must pass through boiler walls, steam is not heated at a single temperature, turbines have friction and leakage, and the boiler's flue gases carry heat away up the chimney. Engineers attack each loss in turn. Reheating steam partway through the turbine raises the average temperature at which heat is added. Feedwater heaters use small amounts of steam bled from the turbine to preheat the water going back to the boiler. Going supercritical removes the boiling stage, where a lot of heat is added at a relatively low temperature. The combined-cycle gas plant is a neat way around the materials limit. A gas turbine can take in combustion gases at well over 1,300 °C, far hotter than any steam boiler tube could stand, but it throws out exhaust at around 450 to 650 °C. Adding a steam cycle underneath uses that exhaust, so the combination captures heat over a very wide temperature range, which is why such plants can exceed 60 percent. Nuclear plants are limited differently: water-cooled reactors produce steam at only around 280 to 300 °C, so their efficiency is about a third. There is a real debate about how to count steam's share of world electricity. Coal and gas together made over half of the world's electricity in 2024, but not all gas power uses steam (simple gas turbines and engines do not), while nuclear, most biomass, geothermal and concentrated solar do. Most estimates therefore land at roughly half to two thirds of global electricity passing through a steam turbine, which is why sources disagree and why the careful phrase is roughly half or more. Grid frequency adds another twist: because large turbine-generators spin in synchrony, their heavy rotors store energy that naturally steadies the grid for a few seconds when demand jumps, a service called inertia that grid operators now have to replace in other ways as fewer big spinning machines run.","funFact":"The very first electricity made from Earth's natural steam, at Larderello in Italy on 4 July 1904, lit just four light bulbs.","check":{"q":"In a typical nuclear power station, what does the heat from the reactor actually do?","a":"It boils water into steam, which spins a turbine connected to a generator","wrong":["It turns directly into electricity with no moving parts","It is used to burn coal more quickly","It melts ice to make a waterfall that spins a waterwheel"]},"sources":[{"title":"How electricity is generated","publisher":"U.S. Energy Information Administration","url":"https://www.eia.gov/energyexplained/electricity/how-electricity-is-generated.php"},{"title":"Electricity generation","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Electricity_generation"},{"title":"Combined cycle power plant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Combined_cycle_power_plant"},{"title":"Supercritical steam generator","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supercritical_steam_generator"},{"title":"Obninsk Nuclear Power Plant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obninsk_Nuclear_Power_Plant"},{"title":"Calder Hall nuclear power station","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Calder_Hall_nuclear_power_station"},{"title":"Geothermal power","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Geothermal_power"},{"title":"Autoclave","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Autoclave"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/18-1.jpg","alt":"A real low-pressure steam turbine rotor from a German power station, its large blades on show outdoors.","file":"Low-pressure turbine rotor from the Greifswald Nuclear Power Plant.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Low-pressure_turbine_rotor_from_the_Greifswald_Nuclear_Power_Plant.jpg","author":"Siarhei Besarab","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/18-2.jpg","alt":"Cooling towers at the Larderello geothermal power plant in Tuscany, Italy, where natural underground steam makes electricity.","file":"Larderello Cooling Towers.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Larderello_Cooling_Towers.jpg","author":"User:Goth nespresso","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/18-3.jpg","alt":"Calder Hall in Cumbria, the world's first commercial nuclear power station, with its four steaming cooling towers.","file":"Calder Hall nuclear power station (11823864155).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Calder_Hall_nuclear_power_station_(11823864155).jpg","author":"ENERGY.GOV","license":"Public domain"}],"words":3919},{"n":19,"title":"The Future of Steam","when":"Today – the coming decades","summary":"Burning coal and gas to boil water has a hidden cost: it fills the sky with carbon dioxide that warms our planet. Now engineers are hunting for cleaner ways to make steam, using giant mirrors, hot rocks deep underground, new kinds of nuclear reactors and maybe one day the power of the Sun itself. Some are even trying to replace steam with a strange, squashed form of carbon dioxide!","sections":[{"heading":"The Invisible Problem in the Smoke","paragraphs":["Imagine standing next to a huge coal-fired power station on a cold winter morning. You can see the white clouds pouring from the cooling towers, and, as you learned in the last part, those clouds are only harmless water droplets. But from the tall, thin chimney comes something you cannot see at all. It has no colour and no smell. It is carbon dioxide, a gas made of one carbon atom joined to two oxygen atoms, and it is the reason the story of steam has reached a turning point.","Here is the chemistry, step by step. Coal is mostly carbon, the remains of ancient swamp plants that were squashed and baked underground for millions of years. When coal burns, each carbon atom grabs two oxygen atoms from the air. Carbon atoms are fairly light, but oxygen atoms are heavier, so the gas that comes out weighs much more than the carbon that went in. In fact, every kilogram of pure carbon becomes about 3.7 kilograms of carbon dioxide. Real coal is not pure carbon (it also contains water, ash and other substances), so burning about 1 kilogram of coal releases roughly 2 to 3 kilograms of carbon dioxide. It sounds like magic, making more weight than you started with, but the extra weight simply comes from the oxygen taken out of the air.","Power stations burn coal by the trainload. In the United States in 2023, according to the government's Energy Information Administration, coal-fired power plants released on average about 2.31 pounds (just over 1 kilogram) of carbon dioxide for every kilowatt-hour of electricity they made. A kilowatt-hour is about the energy needed to run a microwave oven for an hour. Gas-fired plants did better, releasing about 0.96 pounds (about 0.44 kilograms) per kilowatt-hour, because natural gas contains lots of hydrogen, which burns into water instead of carbon dioxide, and because modern gas plants are more efficient. But better is not the same as zero.","Why does this matter? Carbon dioxide acts a bit like a blanket around the Earth. Sunlight passes through the air and warms the ground; the warm ground then gives off invisible heat rays (infrared light), and carbon dioxide absorbs some of that heat and sends part of it back down. A little carbon dioxide is good: without any greenhouse gases our planet would be frozen. The trouble is the amount. Since about 1750, around the time Watt was experimenting in Glasgow, the amount of carbon dioxide in the air has risen by roughly half. Over the decade from 2016 to 2025, the world's average surface temperature was about 1.3 degrees Celsius warmer than in the late 1800s, and 2024 was the warmest year ever recorded.","One or two degrees may not sound like much. But this is an average for the whole planet, all year round, and even small changes in that average mean more heatwaves, heavier downpours, melting glaciers and rising seas. Scientists around the world agree that the main cause of the warming is burning fossil fuels: coal, oil and natural gas. In 2015, almost every country signed the Paris Agreement, promising to try to keep warming well below 2 degrees Celsius.","This is where our story gets awkward. For about 300 years, from Newcomen's engine in 1712 to the giant turbines of today, the easiest way to make steam was to burn coal. Steam built the modern world, and coal was its favourite food. So the big question for the coming decades is not really whether we will keep using steam. It is: can we keep the steam and lose the smoke? Engineers are attacking the problem from many directions at once, and some of their ideas might have amazed Hero, Watt and Parsons."]},{"heading":"Electricity Without Boiling Water","paragraphs":["First, an honest surprise: some of the fastest-growing ways of making electricity do not use steam at all. They skip the kettle completely.","Take a wind turbine. Its giant blades are turned directly by moving air, the same wind that once spun the windmills you met in Part 1. The spinning shaft turns a generator, and out comes electricity. No fire, no boiler, no condenser. Hydroelectric dams work in a similar way, except that falling water spins the turbine instead of wind. Waterwheels powered mills long before steam did, and their modern descendants are still the world's biggest source of low-carbon electricity.","Solar panels are even stranger, because they have no moving parts at all. A solar panel, or photovoltaic panel, is made of thin slices of silicon, the same material used in computer chips. When light hits the silicon, it knocks tiny charged particles called electrons loose, and wires collect them as an electric current. Sunlight in, electricity out, and nothing spins.","These technologies have grown astonishingly fast. According to Ember, an energy research group that tracks the world's electricity, in 2024 about 41 percent of all the world's electricity came from low-carbon sources. Hydropower supplied about 14.3 percent, nuclear about 9 percent, wind about 8.1 percent and solar about 6.9 percent. Solar was the fastest-growing source of electricity for the twentieth year in a row: its output rose by 29 percent in a single year and had doubled in just three years. The world's solar panels together could produce about 2 terawatts at full sun in 2024, twice as much as in 2022. (A terawatt is a million megawatts, or the output of roughly a thousand large power-station turbines.)","So does this mean steam is about to disappear? Not quite. Look at that list again. Nuclear power, which supplied 9 percent, works by boiling water. So do most geothermal plants and biomass plants, which burn wood chips or crop waste. And the other 59 percent of the world's electricity in 2024 still came mostly from coal and gas, and much of that passes through a steam turbine at some point. Steam turbines once made an even bigger share of the world's electricity; as gas turbines, wind and solar spread, the share has fallen to roughly half or a little more, and it is still falling as wind and solar grow.","There is another reason steam is not going away soon. The wind does not always blow and the Sun sets every evening, so electricity grids need some power stations that can run whenever they are needed. Batteries are getting cheaper and are helping a great deal, but many experts think a mix will be needed: wind, solar and batteries alongside low-carbon power stations that can run day and night. Many of those round-the-clock stations will still make steam. The difference is the heat source. Instead of coal, the fire under tomorrow's kettle could be sunlight, the heat of the Earth itself, or the nucleus of an atom."]},{"heading":"Catching Sunlight in a Mirror Forest","paragraphs":["Picture the Mojave Desert in California, near the border with Nevada. The ground is dusty and flat, and spread across about 3,500 acres (roughly 14 square kilometres) are 173,500 mirror units, each one carrying two mirrors and each one slowly tilting to follow the Sun across the sky. Rising from the middle of three huge mirror fields are three towers, each about 140 metres (459 feet) tall, roughly the height of the Great Pyramid of Giza. At the top of each tower sits a boiler glowing so brightly it is hard to look at. This is the Ivanpah Solar Electric Generating System, which formally opened in February 2014.","Ivanpah is a kind of power station called concentrated solar power, or CSP. It does not use solar panels. Instead, the mirrors, called heliostats, act like an enormous magnifying glass. Each one reflects sunlight onto the same small spot: the receiver at the top of the tower. There, the concentrated sunlight heats water in pipes until it becomes steam at about 550 degrees Celsius. That steam then does exactly what steam has done since Parsons: it races through a turbine, spins a generator and makes electricity. In other words, Ivanpah is a steam power station whose firebox is the Sun.","At full power, Ivanpah's three units can generate about 392 megawatts, enough on a sunny afternoon for well over a hundred thousand homes. But the plant has also taught engineers some hard lessons, and it is fair to tell them. It burns natural gas every morning to warm up its equipment, more than its designers expected. Birds flying through the concentrated sunlight near the towers can be burned, and estimates of how many die each year have varied widely and been argued over. Its output has been lower than first hoped. And while it was being built, ordinary solar panels became much cheaper. In 2025 its owners proposed closing the plant, and late that year California's regulators decided that two of its three units should keep running for the time being. Its future is a reminder that a new technology can be clever and still struggle against cheaper rivals.","Yet concentrated solar power has one superpower that ordinary solar panels lack: it can store heat. Heat is far cheaper to store than electricity. The trick is molten salt, a mixture of about 60 percent sodium nitrate and 40 percent potassium nitrate. At room temperature it looks like white granules, but heated past a couple of hundred degrees it melts into a clear liquid that flows like water and can hold a great deal of heat.","In a typical design there are two giant insulated tanks. During the day, cooler molten salt is pumped up the tower, heated by the concentrated sunlight to well over 500 degrees Celsius, and poured into the hot tank. Whenever electricity is needed, even hours after sunset, hot salt is pumped through a heat exchanger, where it boils water into steam for the turbine. The cooled salt goes back to the cold tank to wait for tomorrow's sunshine. It is a giant thermos flask for sunlight.","Spain's Gemasolar plant near Seville, which started running in 2011, showed how well this can work. Its 2,650 heliostats surround a 140-metre tower, and its salt tanks hold enough heat to keep generating for about 15 hours without any sunlight. In the summer of 2013 it ran 24 hours a day for 36 days in a row, something no solar plant had managed before. Similar plants now stand in other sunny deserts, such as the Noor Ouarzazate complex in Morocco and Cerro Dominador in Chile's Atacama Desert, which opened in 2021 with a 110-megawatt tower. Not every project has gone smoothly, and CSP is still more expensive than solar panels, but storing sunshine as heat to make steam at midnight is a genuinely new chapter in the steam story."]},{"heading":"Hot Rocks and New Reactors","paragraphs":["Deep under your feet, the Earth is hot. Dig down far enough almost anywhere and the rock gets warmer by roughly 25 to 30 degrees Celsius for every kilometre. In a few lucky places, such as Larderello in Italy (which you visited in Part 18), Iceland or New Zealand, hot water and natural steam come close to the surface by themselves. But most of the planet's heat lies in dry, solid rock with no water flowing through it. Could we turn that rock into a boiler?","That is the idea behind an enhanced geothermal system. Engineers drill a deep well, then pump water down at very high pressure. The pressure widens tiny cracks that already exist in the rock, creating a hidden maze of passages. A second well is drilled nearby. Cold water goes down the first well, soaks up heat as it trickles through the hot cracks, and comes up the second well hot enough to drive a steam turbine or to heat another fluid that drives a turbine. The cooled water is then sent back down to be heated again. It is like building your own underground hot spring.","People have experimented with this for decades. A research site at Soultz-sous-Forêts in Alsace, France, began work in the 1980s and now feeds a small 1.5-megawatt demonstration plant into the grid. In the United States, the government funds a research site called Utah FORGE, and in 2023 a company called Fervo Energy tested a project in Nevada using drilling methods borrowed from the oil and gas industry, while starting work on a much larger plant called Cape Station in Utah. Supporters hope that enhanced geothermal could one day work in many more places than ordinary geothermal can. Whether it becomes cheap enough is one of the open questions of the coming decades.","Nuclear power is the other big round-the-clock source of low-carbon steam. As Part 18 explained, a nuclear reactor is really just a very unusual firebox: splitting uranium atoms releases heat, the heat boils water, and the steam spins a turbine. The newest idea is to make reactors smaller and build them in factories, like aeroplanes, instead of constructing each one as a giant one-off project. These are called small modular reactors, or SMRs, usually defined as reactors making less than 300 megawatts of electricity each.","Some are already running. Russia's Akademik Lomonosov is a floating nuclear power station, a barge carrying two small reactors, which has supplied electricity to the Arctic town of Pevek since 2020. China connected a small high-temperature reactor called HTR-PM to its grid in 2021; it is cooled by helium gas and filled with fuel shaped like billiard balls, called pebbles. China is also building a 125-megawatt water-cooled reactor called Linglong One. In 2020 the United States approved the design of the NuScale reactor, a small module meant to be grouped in sets, though no NuScale plant has yet been built.","Here is the interesting part for our story. Some of these new designs are cooled not by water but by helium gas, liquid sodium metal or molten salt. Yet almost all of them still pass their heat to water at the end, to make steam for a turbine. A few gas-cooled designs might one day send hot helium straight through a gas turbine instead, skipping steam altogether. But for now, even the most futuristic reactors on the drawing board mostly end up doing what Newcomen's engine did in 1712: boiling water."]},{"heading":"A Star in a Bottle, Still Boiling Water","paragraphs":["The Sun shines because of nuclear fusion. Deep in its core, hydrogen nuclei are squeezed together so hard and so hot that they fuse into helium, releasing enormous energy. For more than 70 years, scientists have dreamed of building a small star on Earth to make electricity. The fuel would be forms of hydrogen called deuterium, which can be taken from seawater, and tritium, which can be made from the metal lithium. It would release no carbon dioxide.","The most famous attempt is ITER (pronounced 'eater'), an enormous machine being built at Saint-Paul-lès-Durance in southern France by a partnership of China, the European Union, India, Japan, Russia, South Korea and the United States. ITER is a tokamak, a doughnut-shaped chamber surrounded by giant magnets that hold a cloud of fuel heated to over 100 million degrees Celsius, around ten times hotter than the centre of the Sun. The plasma (a gas so hot that its atoms are torn apart) must never touch the walls, so the magnets hold it floating in place. Its vacuum vessel alone, with shielding and ports, weighs over 5,000 tonnes.","ITER's goal is to show that fusion can give out about ten times more energy than is used to heat the fuel: about 500 megawatts of fusion heat from about 50 megawatts of heating. But ITER is an experiment, not a power station. It is not designed to make any electricity at all; its heat will simply be carried away. It has also taken far longer than planned. Under a schedule revised in 2024, experiments using the real deuterium-tritium fuel are not expected to begin until about 2039.","Meanwhile, a different approach made headlines. On 5 December 2022, at the National Ignition Facility in Livermore, California, 192 powerful lasers fired at a fuel capsule smaller than a peppercorn. The lasers delivered 2.05 megajoules of energy, and the fusion released 3.15 megajoules, the first time a controlled fusion experiment had given out more energy than was delivered to the fuel. Later shots did even better. But there is an honest catch: making that laser pulse took around 300 megajoules of electricity from the grid, so the whole facility was still far from producing more energy than it used.","Now for the twist that connects fusion to our story. Suppose fusion works. How do you get electricity out of a miniature star? In most designs, the fusion reactions fire out very fast particles called neutrons, which carry most of the energy. The neutrons fly out of the magnetic cage and crash into a thick surrounding layer called a blanket, which contains lithium. The blanket gets very hot (and, as a bonus, the neutrons striking lithium make new tritium fuel). Then a coolant carries the heat away, boils water, and the steam spins a turbine connected to a generator.","So the most advanced energy experiment in human history, if it succeeds, will probably end with the same step as a 1712 mine pump: turning water into steam. A few researchers are exploring ways to turn fusion energy into electricity directly, without a turbine, especially with unusual fuels that release charged particles instead of neutrons. Those ideas are much further from reality. Nobody can promise when, or even whether, fusion power stations will supply our homes. It might be in your lifetime; it might take longer. But if it happens, there is a good chance a steam turbine will be humming somewhere inside."]},{"heading":"New Tricks: Super Carbon Dioxide, Captured Carbon and Wasted Heat","paragraphs":["Steam has had a long run as the favourite working fluid, the substance that carries heat into motion. But some engineers are asking a bold question: is water really the best choice? One surprising rival is carbon dioxide, the very gas that causes so much trouble when it escapes from chimneys. In a closed loop, sealed inside the machine, it could become a helper instead.","Squeeze carbon dioxide hard enough and heat it above about 31 degrees Celsius at a pressure of about 7.4 megapascals (roughly 73 times normal air pressure) and it becomes supercritical. It is no longer clearly a gas or a liquid; it is something in between, as dense as a liquid but flowing like a gas. Because it is so dense, a turbine using supercritical carbon dioxide can be tiny compared with a steam turbine of similar power. General Electric built a prototype about one tenth the size of a matching steam turbine. In 2023 a test plant called STEP was completed in San Antonio, Texas; its turbine, designed for 10 megawatts, is described as about the size of a desk.","Smaller machines could mean cheaper power stations and better efficiency, and these turbines could someday be paired with nuclear reactors, concentrated solar plants or fusion. A related idea, called the Allam cycle, burns natural gas in pure oxygen with carbon dioxide as the working fluid, so that the carbon dioxide produced can be drawn off in a pure stream instead of escaping into the air. These ideas are still being tested. If they succeed, the 300-year reign of water as the king of working fluids could finally be challenged.","That leads to another approach: carbon capture. Instead of letting carbon dioxide float up the chimney, why not catch it? At the Boundary Dam power station in Saskatchewan, Canada, one coal-fired unit was rebuilt and reopened in 2014 with equipment designed to capture about 90 percent of its carbon dioxide, roughly a million tonnes a year. The gas is separated from the exhaust, squeezed, and piped away, mostly to an oil field where it is pumped underground. The project proved that capture can be done, but it also showed the difficulties. It cost about 1.5 billion Canadian dollars, it broke down often in its early years, it captured less than hoped, and running the capture equipment used up a big slice of the plant's own power, cutting its output from about 139 to 110 megawatts. Engineers still argue about whether carbon capture will become cheap and reliable enough to matter on a large scale.","The last new trick is the oldest lesson in the whole story: stop wasting heat. Remember that even the best power stations throw away more than a third of their fuel's energy as warm water and warm exhaust. Combined-cycle gas plants already catch the hot exhaust from a gas turbine in a heat recovery steam generator, a kind of boiler with no fire of its own, and use it to make extra steam. Factories that make steel, glass or cement could do the same with their hot exhaust. For gentler heat, under about 100 degrees Celsius, engineers use an organic Rankine cycle, which swaps water for a liquid that boils at a lower temperature, so even lukewarm waste heat can spin a small turbine. The efficiency is very low, often well under 10 percent, because, as Sadi Carnot showed in 1824 (Part 16), an engine with only a small temperature difference cannot do much work. Some cities pipe leftover power-station heat through underground pipes to warm homes, a system called district heating. James Watt became famous for noticing that Newcomen's engine wasted steam. The engineers of the future may become famous for finding uses for the heat we still throw away."]},{"heading":"Crystal Balls and Honest Forecasts","paragraphs":["If you read news stories about energy, you will see lots of bold promises: fusion in ten years, solar everywhere, coal gone by a certain date. It is worth remembering how hard it is to predict technology. In 1884, when Charles Parsons built his first small turbine, few would have guessed that its descendants would make most of the world's electricity. And in the 1950s, some people predicted that nuclear power would make electricity 'too cheap to meter', which did not happen.","So here is what we can say with confidence, and what remains uncertain. Confident: the world is trying hard to cut carbon dioxide emissions; wind and solar power are growing very quickly; and the share of electricity made by burning coal will need to fall a long way if countries are to meet their climate goals. Also confident: steam turbines will not vanish soon. Nuclear, geothermal, biomass and concentrated solar plants all rely on them, and many coal and gas stations will keep running for years while cleaner power is built.","Uncertain: how fast all this will happen; whether new nuclear reactors can be built cheaply and on time; whether fusion will ever work as a power source; whether carbon capture will become affordable; and whether supercritical carbon dioxide turbines will replace steam in some new plants. Different experts give different answers, and they change their minds as new results arrive. That is not a weakness of science. It is how science is supposed to work.","One possibility is that, a few decades from now, steam will make a smaller share of the world's electricity than it does today, but will still be doing important jobs: in nuclear plants running day and night, in geothermal plants drawing on the Earth's heat, and in solar towers making steam from stored sunlight long after dark. Another possibility is that fusion or new turbines will surprise everyone. Your generation will find out which.","Either way, the engineers working on these problems are doing the same thing Newcomen, Watt, Trevithick and Parsons did: looking at a machine, measuring where the energy goes, and asking how to do better. The problems are bigger now, because the whole planet is involved. But the method has not changed.","And that makes this a good moment to step back. We have travelled a long way, from a spinning ball of steam in ancient Alexandria to mirror forests in the desert and miniature stars in France. In the final part, we will look back at the whole 2,000-year journey, from Hero's toy to a power network that wraps around the planet, and see what it can teach us."]}],"deepDive":"Why do engineers keep chasing higher temperatures, and why might carbon dioxide beat water? It comes back to Carnot's limit (Part 16): the best possible efficiency of any heat engine is 1 minus the ratio of the cold temperature to the hot temperature, both measured in kelvin. With a hot side of 550 °C (about 823 K) and a cold side of 30 °C (about 303 K), the ceiling is about 63 percent; real plants reach much less because of friction, heat leaks and the practical shape of the cycle. Raising the hot temperature raises the ceiling, but steam at very high temperatures and pressures demands thick, expensive alloy pipes, and much of a steam plant's size comes from the huge volume of low-pressure steam leaving the turbine. Supercritical carbon dioxide is attractive because it stays dense through the whole cycle, so the turbomachinery is compact and less energy is spent pumping. GE reported about 50 percent conversion efficiency from a test with CO2 heated to 700 °C, but pilot plants such as the 10-megawatt STEP facility in Texas are still proving whether these machines can run reliably for decades. Carbon arithmetic is simpler: carbon has an atomic mass of 12 and carbon dioxide 44, so each kilogram of carbon burned yields 44/12, or about 3.67 kilograms of CO2. Depending on its carbon content (roughly 50 to 90 percent, by type), a kilogram of coal yields about 2 to 3 kilograms of CO2. Debates continue on several fronts. Concentrated solar power can store heat cheaply but has been undercut by low-cost photovoltaics plus batteries; projects such as Ivanpah have struggled financially, while plants with molten-salt storage such as Gemasolar have shown round-the-clock operation. Carbon capture works technically, as Boundary Dam shows, but uses a large share of a plant's output (an 'energy penalty') and costs remain high. For fusion, scientists distinguish 'scientific breakeven' (more fusion energy out than energy delivered to the fuel, achieved at the National Ignition Facility in 2022) from 'engineering breakeven' (more electricity out than the whole plant uses), which no experiment has yet reached. Honest forecasting means treating all these as research paths, not guarantees.","funFact":"Spain's Gemasolar solar plant can keep making steam for about 15 hours with no sunlight at all, and in 2013 it ran around the clock for 36 days in a row, thanks to tanks of molten salt.","check":{"q":"How does a concentrated solar power plant like Gemasolar keep making electricity after sunset?","a":"It stores the Sun's heat in tanks of hot molten salt, then uses that heat to make steam for its turbine","wrong":["It switches to burning coal every night","It uses moonlight reflected by its mirrors","It stores the electricity in giant rechargeable car batteries"]},"sources":[{"title":"How much carbon dioxide is produced per kilowatthour of U.S. electricity generation?","publisher":"U.S. Energy Information Administration","url":"https://www.eia.gov/tools/faqs/faq.php?id=74&t=11"},{"title":"Global Electricity Review 2025","publisher":"Ember","url":"https://ember-energy.org/latest-insights/global-electricity-review-2025/"},{"title":"Ivanpah Solar Power Facility","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ivanpah_Solar_Power_Facility"},{"title":"Gemasolar Thermosolar Plant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gemasolar_Thermosolar_Plant"},{"title":"ITER","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/ITER"},{"title":"National Ignition Facility","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Ignition_Facility"},{"title":"Supercritical carbon dioxide","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Supercritical_carbon_dioxide"},{"title":"Boundary Dam Power Station","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Boundary_Dam_Power_Station"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/19-1.jpg","alt":"Ivanpah from the air in 2014: three solar towers, each ringed by huge fields of mirrors in the Mojave Desert.","file":"Ivanpah Solar Power Facility from the air 2014.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ivanpah_Solar_Power_Facility_from_the_air_2014.jpg","author":"Craig Butz","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/19-2.jpg","alt":"Gemasolar near Seville, Spain: a glowing central tower surrounded by a giant circle of sun-tracking mirrors.","file":"Gemasolar Thermosolar Plant 1.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gemasolar_Thermosolar_Plant_1.jpg","author":"kallerna","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/19-3.jpg","alt":"Wide-angle drone view of the ITER fusion construction site at Cadarache, France, in 2017, with cranes and buildings.","file":"Iter aerial 2017 Halfway to first plasma (45588568915).jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iter_aerial_2017_Halfway_to_first_plasma_(45588568915).jpg","author":"Oak Ridge National Laboratory","license":"CC BY 2.0"}],"words":4350},{"n":20,"title":"Full Steam Ahead: The Whole Story","when":"1st century CE – today","summary":"It took about 2,000 years and hundreds of clever, stubborn people to turn a spinning toy into the machines that light our homes. Steam changed how people worked, travelled, read the news and even talked, and it left words like 'horsepower' and 'letting off steam' in our language. The next big idea could come from someone who simply notices what is being wasted and asks 'why?'","sections":[{"heading":"From a Spinning Ball to Pressing on the Air","paragraphs":["Let's take one last journey through the whole story, very fast, like flipping through a photo album. Each snapshot is a moment when somebody looked at hot water and saw something new.","Snapshot one: Alexandria, in Roman Egypt, around the first century CE. Hero, a teacher and engineer, describes a hollow metal ball sitting on two pivots over a pot of boiling water. Steam rushes up into the ball and shoots out of two bent nozzles, and the ball whirls round. It is the aeolipile, a delightful demonstration of action and reaction. It spins fast but does almost no useful work, and nobody in Hero's world has a reason, or the metalworking skill, to turn it into a working engine. The idea sits on the shelf for well over a thousand years.","Snapshot two: the 1500s and 1600s. Scattered across the world, people start dreaming about steam again. In 1551, the Ottoman scholar Taqi al-Din describes a roasting spit turned by a jet of steam. In 1606, Jerónimo de Ayanz y Beaumont receives a royal privilege from King Philip III of Spain for a steam device to raise water, linked to draining silver mines. Salomon de Caus in France, Giovanni Branca in Italy and the Marquess of Worcester in England all publish their own ideas. None of them yet becomes a reliable everyday machine, but the dream is spreading.","Snapshot three: the mid-1600s, and a discovery that changes everything. It turns out that the key is not steam pushing, but air pushing. Evangelista Torricelli's mercury tube in 1643 shows that air has weight. Blaise Pascal's family experiment on the Puy de Dôme mountain shows that air pressure is lower higher up. Otto von Guericke's two copper half-spheres in Magdeburg, held together by nothing but air pressure, defeat teams of straining horses. Robert Boyle and Robert Hooke build a better air pump. And Denis Papin, inventor of a pressure cooker with a safety valve, suggests in 1690 that steam could fill a cylinder under a piston; cool the steam, it shrinks back into a few drops of water, and the air outside pushes the piston down. That simple layout, a piston in a cylinder, will drive the next 200 years of engineering.","Snapshot four: 1698. Thomas Savery, an English military engineer, patents a pump with no piston and no beam, his 'Miner's Friend'. Steam fills a vessel, cold water on the outside condenses it, the vacuum sucks water up, and then fresh steam pushes the water higher. It works best in houses and gardens rather than deep mines, and its high-pressure steam is dangerous for the weak boilers of the time. But it is the first steam pump that people actually buy.","Snapshot five: 1712, near Dudley Castle in the West Midlands. Thomas Newcomen, an ironmonger from Dartmouth, and his partner John Calley set up a great wooden beam rocking slowly up and down, about 12 times a minute, draining a coal mine. Inside its cylinder, a spray of cold water condenses the steam, and the weight of the atmosphere, not the push of steam, drives the piston down. It wastes enormous amounts of coal, turning only about 1 percent of the fuel's energy into useful work, but at a coal mine coal is cheap. Within a few decades, Newcomen's 'nodding giants' are working across Britain and Europe."]},{"heading":"From Watt's Walk to the Spinning Turbine","paragraphs":["Snapshot six: Glasgow, 1763 to 1765. James Watt, a young instrument maker at the university, is repairing a little model of a Newcomen engine. He notices how much steam it gulps, because the cylinder is cooled and reheated on every stroke. Helped by Joseph Black's discovery of latent heat, he understands the waste. On a Sunday walk on Glasgow Green, as he later remembered it, he sees the answer: condense the steam in a separate vessel, so the main cylinder can stay hot all the time. His patent comes in 1769. Watt did not invent the steam engine, but his improved engines used roughly a quarter of the coal of a Newcomen engine for the same work.","Snapshot seven: Birmingham, 1775 onwards. Matthew Boulton becomes Watt's partner at the Soho Manufactory, and John Wilkinson's cannon-boring machine makes cylinders accurate enough to stop the steam leaking. Boulton tells a visitor that he sells what all the world desires: power. Then the engineers teach the engine to turn wheels: the sun-and-planet gear, the double-acting engine, parallel motion and the spinning-ball governor. Watt invents the horsepower as a sales unit, and the watt is later named in his honour.","Snapshot eight: the early 1800s. Once Watt's patent ends in 1800, Richard Trevithick in Cornwall and Oliver Evans in the United States dare to use high-pressure steam. Their engines are small and light enough to move around, opening the way for road carriages and, as the separate Railways story tells, locomotives. Arthur Woolf's compound engine uses the same steam twice. Mighty Cornish engines drain deep mines, and in the Netherlands the Cruquius engine, with an outer cylinder about 3.7 metres across, helps pump a whole lake dry; it starts work in 1850.","Snapshot nine: smoky mill towns, busy harbours and great exhibitions. Steam engines drive whole factories through long shafts and leather belts, turning Manchester into 'Cottonopolis'. Steamboats ply rivers and steamships cross oceans. Steam presses print newspapers, steam hammers forge iron, and Britain becomes known as 'the workshop of the world', with other countries soon building their own engines and training their own engineers. There is a darker side too: crowded slums, child labour, mining dangers, boiler explosions and air thick with coal smoke.","Snapshot ten: a new science. Engineers had built engines first and understood them later. In 1824 a young French engineer, Sadi Carnot, explains that every heat engine needs a hot place and a cold place. James Prescott Joule shows that heat and work are two forms of the same thing, energy. Rudolf Clausius and William Thomson (Lord Kelvin) set out the laws of thermodynamics. Now engineers know the rules of the game, and they know no engine can ever turn all of its heat into work.","Snapshot eleven: 1884, Newcastle upon Tyne. Charles Parsons, the youngest son of an astronomer earl, builds a turbine in which steam pushes past many rows of little blades, spinning a shaft at about 18,000 revolutions per minute, with no piston going back and forth at all. In 1897 his little ship Turbinia races through a naval review at about 34 knots, astonishing the admirals. Within a few years, turbines drive battleships, ocean liners and electrical generators.","Snapshot twelve: today. Coal, gas, nuclear, geothermal and solar heat all boil water into high-pressure steam that spins giant turbines, some producing more than 1,000 megawatts each. Even now, roughly half or more of the world's electricity comes from generators turned by steam. And as Part 19 showed, the engineers of tomorrow are trying to keep the steam while losing the smoke."]},{"heading":"Many Hands, Not One Genius","paragraphs":["If someone asks you, 'Who invented the steam engine?', you now know that the honest answer is: nobody, and lots of people. That is not a trick answer. It is the most important lesson in this whole story.","Think about it like a relay race. Hero showed that steam could make something move. Torricelli, Pascal, von Guericke and Boyle showed that air has weight and that a vacuum can be made. Papin put a piston in a cylinder and described how condensing steam could let the air push it. Savery built the first steam pump people paid for. Newcomen and Calley built the first engine that worked for years on end, a real machine with a piston and beam. Watt made it efficient. Each runner needed the baton from the one before.","Some of the runners have been nearly forgotten. Denis Papin, who came up with the piston-and-cylinder idea in 1690, died in 1713 in poverty and obscurity in London; for about 300 years nobody knew where he was buried, until a record of his burial at St Bride's Church in Fleet Street was found in 2016. Thomas Newcomen had to work under Savery's very broad patent, so he never got the fame or fortune his engine deserved. John Calley, his partner, is barely remembered at all.","William Murdoch, a Scottish engineer who worked for Boulton and Watt, is usually credited with developing the sun-and-planet gear, and he also pioneered gas lighting; yet his name is far less famous than Watt's. Richard Trevithick built some of the first high-pressure engines and steam road vehicles, but he ended his life poor. Joseph Black's science of latent heat helped Watt understand the waste he wanted to cut. John Wilkinson's precise boring machine made Watt's cylinders possible. Without the skilled workers who made the parts, the miners who dug the coal and the engine-men who kept the fires going, none of these inventions would have worked at all.","There is another fair point to make. The story is often told as a British one, and Britain really was where the first practical engines were built and spread. But ideas about steam came from Egypt, the Ottoman Empire, Spain, France, Italy, Germany and the Netherlands, and the later story was shaped by Americans like Oliver Evans, Robert Fulton and George Corliss, the Swedish engineer Gustaf de Laval, and many others. Inventions are almost always built by many people, in many places, over long periods of time. The 'lone genius' makes a good story, but it is rarely a true one.","Patents show both sides of this. Savery's patent and Watt's extended patent helped their owners earn money from their work, which encouraged investment. But they also blocked others: Newcomen had to share with Savery's partners, and high-pressure engines had to wait until Watt's patent ran out in 1800. Societies are still debating how to reward inventors without slowing everyone else down."]},{"heading":"How Steam Changed an Ordinary Day","paragraphs":["Imagine a girl named Mary living in a Lancashire village in 1750, before steam engines were driving the mills. Her family spins and weaves cotton at home. They work hard, but they decide when to start and when to stop, and the rhythm of the day follows the daylight, the seasons and the farm. Few people travel more than a day's walk from home. A book is precious and a newspaper is rare. Almost everything Mary owns was made by hand, by someone she might know.","Now imagine her great-granddaughter in Manchester in 1850. She works in a cotton mill where one huge engine in the engine house drives hundreds of machines through spinning shafts and belts. The engine does not get tired, so the mill can run from early morning to evening, and her day is ruled by a bell and a clock instead of the Sun. Her family lives in a crowded street of terraced houses, close to the mill, under a sky stained by coal smoke. Life is hard, and for many families, especially children, it is harder than village life had been.","But some things are new and exciting. Cotton cloth is far cheaper than it was, so even poor families can afford more clothes. Steam presses print cheap newspapers, so she can read (or hear read aloud) news from across the country. Steamships bring food and goods from distant lands. And by her old age, steam travel has made journeys that once took days possible in hours. Her world is bigger, faster, noisier and more crowded than Mary's could ever have been.","Historians still argue about how steam changed people's lives, and the honest answer is: in many ways at once, some good and some bad. Over the long run, machines powered by steam helped make goods much cheaper and, eventually, made many people far better off than their ancestors. But in the early decades the gains were shared very unequally. Factory owners and merchants grew rich while many workers lived in poverty, and the gap between rich and poor became painfully visible in the new industrial cities. It took decades of campaigning, new laws and trade unions before working hours shortened, children were sent to school instead of the mill, and city streets got clean water and sewers.","Steam also changed time itself, in a way. Factories needed workers to arrive together, so clocks and timetables became part of everyday life. Steam-powered travel later pushed many countries to agree on standard times so that schedules made sense from one town to the next. When you check the time before school, you are living with one of steam's quiet inventions: a world organised by the clock."]},{"heading":"Steam Words Hiding in Your Mouth","paragraphs":["Steam engines are no longer puffing in every town, but they have left fingerprints all over the English language. You probably use some of these words and phrases without knowing where they came from.","Horsepower is the most famous. James Watt needed a way to tell customers how many horses his engine could replace, so he defined one horsepower as 33,000 foot-pounds per minute: enough power to lift 33,000 pounds by one foot in one minute. Today we know that equals about 746 watts. It is a measure of power, not a promise about any real horse, since a horse can work much harder for a short burst, while its steady output over a long working day is far lower. We still measure car engines and lawnmowers in horsepower, more than 200 years later.","The watt itself is the other great steam word. In 1882, at a meeting of the British Association, the engineer William Siemens proposed it as the name for a unit of power, in honour of James Watt. One watt is one joule of energy per second (the joule, of course, is named after James Prescott Joule, the scientist of Part 16). Every light bulb, phone charger and power station in the world is rated in watts, kilowatts or megawatts, so Watt's name appears on billions of objects.","Then there are the everyday phrases. 'Full steam ahead' comes from steamships, where the captain would order the engines to drive the ship forward as hard as they could; today it means going forward with all your energy. 'Letting off steam' comes from the safety valve, which releases extra steam so a boiler does not burst; when you run around the playground after a long lesson, you are letting off steam. 'Running out of steam' describes an engine whose fire is dying, so it slows down and stops, just like you at the end of a very long day.","A 'steamroller' was originally a heavy steam-powered road roller that flattened gravel and tarmac. Now, if a team 'steamrollers' its opponents, it crushes them completely. People also talk about something 'picking up steam' or 'gathering steam' when it gets faster and stronger, like an engine building up pressure. Even 'getting steamed up' about something, meaning getting angry or excited, borrows the picture of a boiler building pressure inside.","Not every machine word lasts so long. Most people today have never seen a working steam engine in daily use, yet these words survive because the pictures behind them are so vivid: a boiler about to burst, a ship racing forward, a giant roller flattening everything in its way. Language remembers what people once saw every day."]},{"heading":"Full Circle: Hero's Idea Comes Home","paragraphs":["Here is one of the most beautiful twists in the whole history of technology. Hero's aeolipile, the spinning ball from Alexandria, worked by reaction: steam rushing out of the nozzles pushed the ball the opposite way, just as a garden sprinkler spins or a rocket rises. For centuries after Hero, that spinning idea led nowhere useful. Instead, engineers went in a completely different direction, building engines with pistons that slid back and forth, beams that rocked up and down, and cranks that turned the back-and-forth motion into rotation.","Then, in 1884, Charles Parsons came back to spinning. His turbine had no piston, no beam and no crank. Steam flowed through rows of blades, some fixed and some on a spinning shaft, pushing the moving blades round directly. In Parsons's design, part of the push came from reaction, the same principle that had spun Hero's ball. About 1,800 years separated the two machines, yet they were cousins. The idea that had seemed like a toy turned out to be the right idea all along; people simply needed metals, precision engineering, science and a real job (making electricity) before it could work.","There is another link across the centuries. Newcomen's first engine in 1712 used water turned to steam to do its work. In 1954, the Obninsk nuclear power plant in the Soviet Union became the first nuclear power station to supply electricity to a power grid, using the heat of splitting atoms. And how did it make that electricity? By using water turned to steam. The fire had changed beyond recognition, from burning coal to splitting uranium, but the middle of the story, water boiling into steam, was the same.","Today, as Part 18 described, steam turbines in power stations are vast: some of their last blades are about as long as an adult is tall, and the whole machine may weigh hundreds of tonnes. Yet if Hero could step into a modern power station, he might recognise the basic trick at once. Hot steam, rushing out, makes something spin."]},{"heading":"Your Turn, Young Inventor","paragraphs":["What made James Watt's great idea possible? Not a lucky accident, and not a kettle (that story is a legend). It came from careful noticing. Watt saw that a small model engine was gulping far more steam than it should, measured the waste, and kept asking why until he understood it. Newcomen noticed that mines were flooding and that existing pumps could not cope. Parsons noticed that piston engines shook and could not spin fast enough for electrical generators. Again and again in this story, progress began when someone noticed a problem or a waste and refused to accept it.","You can practise the same habit anywhere. Why does the bathroom mirror steam up? Where does the heat from the back of the fridge go? Why does your bike chain squeak, and how much effort is lost? Write down your questions. Measure things if you can. Some of the greatest inventors started exactly this way, with a notebook and a nagging question.","One of the best ways to explore steam is to see it working. In London, the Science Museum displays historic engines, including early pumping and mill engines. In Birmingham, the Thinktank science museum holds the Smethwick Engine, a Boulton and Watt beam engine that began work in 1779 and is thought to be the oldest working steam engine in the world. In west London, the London Museum of Water and Steam at Kew Bridge has the world's largest collection of Cornish engines, including the Grand Junction 90-inch engine, which is over 12 metres (40 feet) tall, weighs about 250 tons and was once the largest working beam engine in the world; the museum holds special 'Steam Up' days when some of its engines run. In the Netherlands, you can stand inside the Cruquius pumping station, a museum since 1933, and look up at the giant engine that helped drain the Haarlemmermeer. Many other countries have heritage railways, steam fairs and engine houses kept alive by volunteers.","You can also build or run a small model steam engine, but only with an adult, because even a toy engine has a real boiler, real flames and real steam. Remember from Part 10 that steam can scald worse than boiling water, since it releases extra hidden heat as it condenses on skin. Never heat a closed container, never block a safety valve, and never point steam at anyone. Watching a pan lid rattle on the stove, with an adult nearby, is the safest steam experiment of all, and it shows the whole idea: heat makes water into steam, and steam can push.","Remember too that the steam story is not finished. Part 19 showed engineers trying to make steam from sunlight, hot rocks and atoms, testing new working fluids like supercritical carbon dioxide, and hunting for ways to use the heat we waste. Those problems need new minds, from every country, and many of the people who will solve them are children today.","So the next time you see a pan lid rattling on the stove, look closely. You are watching the same push that drained Newcomen's mines, turned Watt's wheels and spun Parsons's turbines, the push that is spinning a turbine somewhere right now so that, when you flick a switch, your lights come on. Two thousand years ago, Hero made steam spin a ball. Today, steam spins the world."]}],"deepDive":"How do historians weigh the steam engine's importance? It is tempting to say steam 'caused' the Industrial Revolution, but the picture is more complicated. Economic historians have shown that steam spread slowly: for decades after Watt's improvements, water wheels still supplied much of the power in British industry, and steam's biggest effects on productivity came in the mid to late 1800s, once high-pressure engines, compound engines and better machine tools had made it cheaper and more reliable. Some economists, such as Nicholas Crafts, have estimated that steam's contribution to British economic growth was modest before about 1830 and much larger afterwards. Others emphasise that the engine was part of a bigger system: cheap coal, iron, skilled mechanics, patent law, banking, canals and later railways, and global trade (including cotton grown by enslaved people in the American South). The 'many hands' view also applies to credit. Patents give a legal 'inventor', but technical history shows long chains of borrowing: Papin's piston-and-cylinder layout (1690), Savery's pump (1698), Newcomen's engine (1712), Watt's separate condenser (patented 1769), and later Trevithick's high-pressure engines and Parsons's turbine (1884). Each solved a specific limit of the one before. Thermodynamics adds a final perspective. Newcomen engines converted roughly 1 percent of their fuel's energy into work, Watt's engines a few percent, good late-1800s engines around 10 to 20 percent, and modern steam-turbine plants about 33 to 45 percent (or above 60 percent when a steam cycle is combined with a gas turbine). That is a rise of roughly forty times in about 300 years, achieved by hundreds of improvements rather than one breakthrough. It also explains steam's long survival: once you can make heat, from coal, uranium, the Earth or the Sun, water is cheap, safe and well understood as the fluid that carries that heat into motion.","funFact":"The Smethwick Engine, built by Boulton and Watt and put to work in 1779 pumping water for a canal, is thought to be the oldest working steam engine in the world, and it can still be seen at Thinktank in Birmingham.","check":{"q":"Which modern machine works on the same reaction idea as Hero's spinning aeolipile and still helps make much of the world's electricity?","a":"The steam turbine","wrong":["The solar panel","The Newcomen beam engine","The Magdeburg hemispheres"]},"sources":[{"title":"Steam engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steam_engine"},{"title":"Charles Algernon Parsons","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Charles_Algernon_Parsons"},{"title":"Obninsk Nuclear Power Plant","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Obninsk_Nuclear_Power_Plant"},{"title":"Smethwick Engine","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smethwick_Engine"},{"title":"London Museum of Water & Steam","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/London_Museum_of_Water_%26_Steam"},{"title":"Museum De Cruquius","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Museum_De_Cruquius"},{"title":"Denis Papin","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Denis_Papin"},{"title":"Horsepower","publisher":"Wikipedia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horsepower"}],"photos":[{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/20-1.jpg","alt":"The 1779 Smethwick Engine at Thinktank, Birmingham: its wooden beam above and big iron cylinder below.","file":"Smethwick Engine at ThinkTank Museum.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Smethwick_Engine_at_ThinkTank_Museum.jpg","author":"IMechE1","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/20-2.jpg","alt":"Old book illustration (1910) of Hero's aeolipile: a steam ball on a boiling cauldron, with someone tending the fire.","file":"Heron's Eolipile.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Heron%27s_Eolipile.jpg","author":"Sue Clark","license":"Public domain"},{"src":"img/deep/steam-engine/20-3.jpg","alt":"Turbinia on display at the Discovery Museum in Newcastle upon Tyne, seen from above in 2022.","file":"Turbinia in the Discovery Museum September 2022.jpg","url":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Turbinia_in_the_Discovery_Museum_September_2022.jpg","author":"Nick-D","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0"}],"words":3734}],"words":78844};
